HT_DATA.load("detail", {"yYAw79386WI":{"wy":"Held up as a marvel of pedagogy and the best explanation of differentials, recommended again and again.","q":{"x":"this video explaining differentials is from 1937 and is a marvel of pedagogy","i":19980625,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":41300169,"author":"mdorazio","month":"2024-08","story":"Basic Mechanisms In Fire Control Computers (1953) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite old video on vehicle differentials.","quote":"My favorite is probably this one on vehicle differentials","text":"Not just military training videos, older ones in general are often superior to what gets made today. My favorite is probably this one on vehicle differentials: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40896743,"author":"nixass","month":"2024-07","story":"The Basics of How Engines Work (2021) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Still one of the best how-it-works videos ever.","quote":"It's almost 100 years old by now but still one of the best \"how\" videos ever.","text":"One of the best \"how this mechanical thing works\" for me is about differential/steering by Chevrolet (1937.). It's almost 100 years old by now but still one of the best \"how\" videos ever. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28256409,"author":"AussieWog93","month":"2021-08","story":"Patterns in Confusing Explanations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Example of an amazingly powerful way of explaining things.","quote":"Done properly, it's an amazingly powerful way of explaining things.","text":"First thing I thought of when you said that: [VIDEO] Done properly, it's an amazingly powerful way of explaining things."},{"id":23268455,"author":"sajforbes","month":"2020-05","story":"Calculus Made Easy (1914) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best explanation of differential steering the commenter has seen.","quote":"the best explanation I've seen about how cars' differential steering works comes from 1937.","text":"A similar thing - the best explanation I've seen about how cars' differential steering works comes from 1937. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21358669,"author":"smogcutter","month":"2019-10","story":"South-Pointing Chariot","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Terrific GM-produced explanation of how differential gears work.","quote":"Here’s a terrific explanation of how differential gears work, from a film produced by GM in the 30s","text":"Here’s a terrific explanation of how differential gears work, from a film produced by GM in the 30s: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19980625,"author":"dTal","month":"2019-05","story":"Five Hundred and Seven Mechanical Movements (1908)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"1937 differential explainer praised as a marvel of pedagogy.","quote":"this video explaining differentials is from 1937 and is a marvel of pedagogy","text":"Not just the 50s/60s - this video explaining differentials is from 1937 and is a marvel of pedagogy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6231787,"author":"aa0","month":"2013-08","story":"Five Hundred and Seven Mechanical Movements","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Thorough 1937 explanation of how differentials work.","quote":"This 1937 video about differentials is also enlightening","text":"That was a great video, thank you! Knowing basic gear mechanics helps a lot in understanding how cars function. This 1937 video about differentials is also enlightening: [VIDEO] Why is it that old tutorial videos are so thorough and informative?"},{"id":38519255,"author":"deaddodo","month":"2023-12","story":"1962 Military Innstructional Video on Magnetic Cores","stance":"rec","why":"One of the pretty good old government educational films.","quote":"All of the old US gov't/mil funded education films are pretty good.","text":"All of the old US gov't/mil funded education films are pretty good. * [VIDEO] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] Etc."},{"id":36900291,"author":"bramhaag","month":"2023-07","story":"Hydraulic Steering – Principles of Operation (1956) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among similar old educational car videos.","quote":"Similar videos about cars","text":"Similar videos about cars: Chevrolet Suspension (1938): [LINK] How Differential Steering Works (1937): [VIDEO] How A Transmission Works (1936): [LINK] And as a bonus, an explanation about transistors from 1953: [LINK]"},{"id":29540002,"author":"ironmagma","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: What practical skills do you recommend?","stance":"rec","why":"Starting point for learning how car components work.","quote":"I would maybe start with the individual components. How a differential works","text":"I would maybe start with the individual components. How a differential works: [VIDEO] There are a number of videos of transparent engines: [LINK] But if that doesn’t work, searching YouTube for “how an engine works” should give lots of content. Once you’ve figured out how the engine turns the flywheel, you can investigate how the flywheel applies different speeds to the wheels via a transmission. Manual transmissions are simpler, so I would start there: [LINK] then figure out how the clutch fits in. After that, you can move on to torque converters (coolest component imo, basically a fluid coupling) and automatic transmissions, which can be a mind-bender. Feel free to reach out to me at pc.peterso at Mail of g dot common TLD, happy to share what I learned and save you some time. Lastly, there is a wealth of tidbits on Engineering Explained and howacarworks.com, although the latter goes into far more detail than you probably need."},{"id":20584776,"author":"ohaideredevs","month":"2019-08","story":"Ask HN: Your favourite tutorial for total beginners?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed as a favorite tutorial for total beginners.","quote":"How a car differential works","text":"How guns work: [LINK] How a car differential works: [VIDEO] Downright the best zero-to-programmer course in the world. Wish I knew about it when I was starting out: [LINK]"},{"id":19979558,"author":"lqet","month":"2019-05","story":"Five Hundred and Seven Mechanical Movements (1908)","stance":"rec","why":"Extremely interesting 1930s differential explainer with a key insight at minute 5.","quote":"It also is extremely interesting to watch. Minute 5 is very illuminating.","text":"A few months ago, this video [0] from the 30ies explaining a differential was posted on HN. It also is extremely interesting to watch. Minute 5 is very illuminating. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18136053,"author":"foobarian","month":"2018-10","story":"Honda will use GM’s self-driving technology, invests in Cruise","stance":"rec","why":"Classic 1937 video on differentials and multi-shaft mechanisms.","quote":"Classic video about differentials from 1937 (!)","text":"This kind of mechanical device where there are more than two shafts are always cool. Classic video about differentials from 1937 (!): [VIDEO]"},{"id":18276223,"author":"Raphmedia","month":"2018-10","story":"Space Transportation System","stance":"rec","why":"Example of instructive old videos that prioritized function over form.","quote":"Videos were very instructive back then too.","text":"Videos were very instructive back then too. At some point, we started to prioritize form over function. E.g. [VIDEO] [Around The Corner - How Differential Steering Works (1937)]"},{"id":16771844,"author":"sheltron","month":"2018-04","story":"Walt Disney's MultiPlane Camera (1957) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Liked explainer on differential steering.","quote":"Heres a couple of others that I like. Differential Steering","text":"Wow these are really great thanks for sharing. Heres a couple of others that I like. Differential Steering: [VIDEO] Turbo Encabulator: [LINK]"},{"id":12373278,"author":"japhyr","month":"2016-08","story":"4WD vs. AWD. What's the Difference?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows why differentials are needed and how they work.","quote":"There's also a great video from 1937(!) that shows why we need differentials, and how they work.","text":"Here are the key takeaways for me: \"Thanks to that differential between your axles, an AWD car will send your engine’s power down the path of least resistance—the wheel with the least grip. Where a two-wheel drive car can only choose between two wheels, an AWD system looks for that least resistance across all four wheels.\" \"4WD works by locking the front and rear axles together, splitting torque 50:50 between them. This provides great traction, but a vehicle locked in 4WD cannot safely be operated on dry pavement because its front and rear axles are forced to rotate at the same speeds.\" I remember being scared when I bought my first 4wd vehicle, a '91 Toyota pickup. I knew I shouldn't have the wheels locked in 4wd on dry pavement, and I was worried about going from snowy roads to dry pavement. When I moved on to an Outback (AWD, not 4wd) I wondered why I didn't have to worry about transitioning between slippery roads and dry pavement. Now I understand. There's also a great video from 1937(!) that shows why we need differentials, and how they work. Here's a direct link to the video if you're going to skip the article: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9902341,"author":"aunty_helen","month":"2015-07","story":"How a car works (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Example of explaining core concepts with simpler older technology.","quote":"is a great example of this.","text":"This is great. As for the criticism that a lot of the content is old, most of the core concepts can be explained better with older tech as it's simpler. This allows you to focus on what is happening and leave the extra details about what technology a part or process has for a case by case basis. [VIDEO] is a great example of this. Saying that, an article on direct injection, common rail diesels, efficient turbo charging, variable valve timing, MAF VS MAP EFI would be worth considering."},{"id":27638412,"author":"paulkrush","month":"2021-06","story":"3D Printed South Pointing Chariot","stance":"reference","why":"Commonly encountered 1937 differential video.","quote":"","text":"I am surprise I have not seen this, with how many times I have run across the Around The Corner - How Differential Steering Works (1937): [VIDEO]"},{"id":22809143,"author":"Arnavion","month":"2020-04","story":"How to Use a Differential Analyzer","stance":"reference","why":"Pointed to as the non-clickbait-title version from a channel of recovered films.","quote":"is the non-clickbait-title version, hosted by the channel with other popular recovered films in the same genre.","text":"[VIDEO] is the non-clickbait-title version, hosted by the channel with other popular recovered films in the same genre."},{"id":22941683,"author":"throw1234651234","month":"2020-04","story":"Why are Soviet math textbooks so hardcore in comparison to US textbooks? (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Offered as an example of focus on basics without filler.","quote":"","text":"I find this to be true. It seems like there was more focus on the very, very basics. Now there is a ton of filler and the content gets lost. E.g.: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["elvOZm0d4H0",1],["vc4yL3YTwWk",1],["MO0r930Sn_8",1],["cQO2XTP7QDw",1],["kc9HwsxE1OY",1],["tyixMpuGEL8",1],["wFpeM3fxJoQ",1],["AvEqfg2sIH0",1],["xP5-iIeKXE8",1],["JOLtS4VUcvQ",1]]},"YSfCdBBqNXY":{"wy":"HN readers recommend it as the best primer for understanding ADHD at a neurological level, often suggesting that even the first 10 to 20 minutes are enlightening.","q":{"x":"You don't need to watch the entire 3 hours. The first 10 minutes should be fairly enlightening.","i":30867547,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":42606155,"author":"jeromie","month":"2025-01","story":"Ask HN: My 12 year-old nephew just got diagnosed with ADHD, any advice?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Research on ADHD, peer rejection and emotional impulsiveness","quote":"I can't recommend this video enough.","text":"I can't recommend this video enough. I wish this research and understanding was available when I was a kid. I figured out systems and tools to succeed in school and work (I was largely a C student testing in the top 0.1% of CA school kids on the standardized tests -- found that tidbit cleaning out my mom's things). It was the rejection I experienced in peer friendships that scarred me deeply, and as someone who has been unmedicated into my 40s, still impacts my adult friendships and professional relationships. ADHD: Essential Ideas for Parents - Dr. Russell Barkely [VIDEO] Some key tidbits to sell you (and your nephew's parents) on spending the full two hours with this: \"... it also helps families to understand some of the other life course risks 50 to 70% of ADHD children are utterly rejected by close friendships by second grade it is in fact one of the more devastating consequences of this disorder is this inability to make and keep close sustained friendships with others their children and it is heartbreaking for parents to see this happening that their child is not as liked as other children that the sleepovers the going to the movies and the other social events in which other children celebrate their peer relationships are shut off for this child why is it there the single best predictor of peer rejection is that symptom the emotional impulsiveness friends forgive you your distractability your forgetfulness your working memory problems and even your restlessness they wil"},{"id":22136011,"author":"mr_woozy","month":"2020-01","story":"ADHD, a Lifelong Struggle (2018)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Essential for understanding ADHD neurologically","quote":"is essential to understanding ADHD at the neurological level.","text":"If you haven't already watched it: ADHD: Essential Ideas for Parents - Dr. Russell Barkely is essential to understanding ADHD at the neurological level. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40013585,"author":"01HNNWZ0MV43FF","month":"2024-04","story":"Childhood Loneliness Linked to Later Psychosis","stance":"rec","why":"Matches commenter's experience of executive function problems","quote":"the stuff described in that video very much matches my experience.","text":"The newer ADHD meds are not stimulants and are not addictive. Maybe ADHD has \"boomed\" in \"\"\"popularity\"\"\" because a disorder that used to be diagnosed as hyperactivity and a deficit of attention actually turned out to be a quantifiable problem with executive function which exists in a known part of the brain, can be quantifiably treated with medication and therapy, and if untreated results in very confusing problems with emotions and motivation for as long as you live. It's not as if ADHD doesn't exist. I'm speaking off of what was apparently state of the art 10 years ago. [VIDEO] I'm hoping today, in a matter of hours, to get prescribed one of these new non-stim drugs, because the stuff described in that video very much matches my experience. Maybe thinking that ADHD is made up has boomed in popularity because of the South Park and King of the Hill episodes that downplay it. Imagine learning medicine from cartoons."},{"id":38276533,"author":"positus","month":"2023-11","story":"Hacking ADHD: Strategies for the modern developer","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley lecture offered as counterpoint","quote":"I strongly disagree and think you should consider watching the following","text":"I strongly disagree and think you should consider watching the following: ADHD: Essential Ideas for Parents - Dr. Russell Barkley [VIDEO]"},{"id":33080203,"author":"kayodelycaon","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: How to find what I am really good at?","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed look at what ADHD is and how it affects children","quote":"I found it quite fascinating and it helped me understand some of my symptoms.","text":"ADHD is quite well studied. If you're up for it, there is a 2.5h video that goes into some detail about what it is and how it affects children. I found it quite fascinating and it helped me understand some of my symptoms. (Mine is comorbid with bipolar. Yay.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":30865512,"author":"kitsune_","month":"2022-03","story":"Developers spend most of their time figuring the system out","stance":"rec","why":"Good primer on ADHD","quote":"The video of the talk by Russell Barkley that is linked elsewhere is a good primer","text":"It is possible, the condition is a bit poorly named. The video of the talk by Russell Barkley that is linked elsewhere is a good primer [VIDEO] ). People with ADHD can have episodes of hyper focus. However this is a bit a misnomer, it's called Perseveration and is not really a good thing, and has more to do with one's inability to task switch ( [LINK] ). However it also allows you to completely zone into a code base or a problem. The problem is you don't really have much control over what will catch your attention. But of course you can develop quite some deep technical expertise this way. Now, that said, there is a new movie, Everything Everywhere All At Once, and if you take this title as a stand in for ADHD, maybe you can see that while this way of living can be overwhelming it also allows you to have a pretty good overview over systems and how their components interacts very fast. I think that's what many ADHD'ers are actually very good at, grokking code bases or the gist of things very quickly. Systems thinking in general. Also quick recall / association chains... Also, on average, the higher your IQ, the later in your life your ADHD diagnosis will be (see this video on adult ADHD [LINK] ). Your other abilities can compensate somewhat for your ADHD. Also of course when battling this condition you will build many personal tools and habits that neuro-typical people will never have to do, so in that sense you also might have a leg up in certain situations. I'd say professio"},{"id":30867547,"author":"kayodelycaon","month":"2022-03","story":"Developers spend most of their time figuring the system out","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what ADHD is; first 10 minutes enlightening","quote":"You don't need to watch the entire 3 hours. The first 10 minutes should be fairly enlightening.","text":"The whole ADHD as an adaptation theory has been throughly debunked. Your confusion is very understandable because most people don't know what ADHD is. Some of the symptoms overlap with immaturity, so people who are not qualified to tell the difference will frequently misdiagnose the problem. If you want to understand ADHD, this video for parents of ADHD children will explain. [VIDEO] You don't need to watch the entire 3 hours. The first 10 minutes should be fairly enlightening."},{"id":27872355,"author":"rvanlaar","month":"2021-07","story":"Show HN: I built an app to create repeatable checklists to help with ADHD","stance":"rec","why":"Helps understand ADHD behavior is not intentional","quote":"I can recommend to great resources. One is a YouTube series by Russel Barkley","text":"Hey, that sounds rough, very rough. It's not his fault. It's not your fault either. Speaking from experience myself, it's not that he purposely wants to speak its just that he can't not talk. Like people in a wheelchair would like to stand and walk but can't, or only very short distances. I can recommend to great resources. One is a YouTube series by Russel Barkley [1] The other are the books and podcast by Dr Hallowell [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19016373,"author":"afiori","month":"2019-01","story":"Most People With Addiction Grow Out of It (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"30 essential things parents should know about ADHD","quote":"There is a very good video about 30 essential things parents should know about ADHD","text":"> Contrast that will ADD/ADHD. I'm not convinced it has anything to do with maturity or a lack thereof. Sure, mindfulness meditation helps quite a bit. But I think you can be mature, self sufficient, polite, etc, and yet still have ADHD, or immature, etc. and have ADHD. There is a very good video about 30 essential things parents should know about ADHD [VIDEO] One of them is that when unmedicated on average a person with ADHD has the maturity of someone 2/3 of its age. Its is just an average, but is relevant to think how different a 14 years old and a 21 years old are (or 20 and 30)."},{"id":18668213,"author":"giggles_giggles","month":"2018-12","story":"ATDT relief","stance":"rec","why":"Shows ADHD is real","quote":"Maybe watch this if you don't think ADHD is real.","text":"If you're not trying to sound like a douche, don't put ADHD in scare quotes. Yes, it was over-diagnosed in the 90s, but I brought myself in and was diagnosed as an adult in 2009. Maybe watch this [VIDEO] if you don't think ADHD is real. No, I don't know for certain if this is related to my ADHD. I might just be inconsiderate and annoying. I'm speculating, because it's a behavior that I have spontaneously manifested, observed, criticized myself for, and yet still do. It fits in the category of \"executive control\" and \"impulse control\" and those are the things that ADHD impairs. And I already wear headphones most of the day when I'm in the office because music helps me focus and otherwise I start listening to the conversations around me. Listening to music while I work (and when I studied when I was in school) is one way I have managed my disorder throughout my life."},{"id":17889156,"author":"TaylorAlexander","month":"2018-08","story":"Procrastination is more about managing emotions than time: study","stance":"rec","why":"Fascinating lecture reframing ADHD beyond attention deficit","quote":"I recommend watching the first 20 minutes, and continuing with it if it strikes you as relevant","text":"So I recently found this fascinating lecture on ADHD. It turns out, it’s poorly named and doesn’t really have to do with an attention deficit. Based on what you’ve said, I recommend watching the first 20 minutes, and continuing with it if it strikes you as relevant: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16413024,"author":"sosuke","month":"2018-02","story":"Ask HN: Is it 'normal' to struggle so hard with work?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains ADHD motivation and short-term memory issues","quote":"you'll likely feel yourself nodding along thinking of you.","text":"If you’re still here looking at replies feel free to contact me. And or watch/listen to this video [VIDEO] Wish you'd left some way to contact you. Now that I'm at a computer I can say more but you're certainly not uncommon and there is lots of help that you can have. Cognitive behavior therapy shows great results in adult adhd/add patients. The video I linked talks about children but you'll likely feel yourself nodding along thinking of you. Money quote for me was something like the ADHD child has zero self motivation. All motivation comes from the outside world. Which is why kids can play video games for hours because there is instant feedback. But when you finish a problem on your homework nothing happens. And that there is a extremely poor short short term memory problem. Tons of stuff."},{"id":34473724,"author":"pas","month":"2023-01","story":"I've procrastinated working on my thesis for more than a year","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] [LINK] what ultimately works is asking for help, accepting that yes, it might not be what you want, but it gets you through this hurdle. maybe the next one will be easier. and get up to speed and then work on doing it alone, and then work on doing your best."},{"id":26755856,"author":"ReactiveJelly","month":"2021-04","story":"Embrace the Grind","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm not gonna disagree but I want to add a couple things. 1. Kids with ADHD probably can't develop executive function as fast or as far as other kids. I'm pretty sure I have it, it explains the repeated performance reports of \"You're good when you apply yourself and useless when you don't.\" Unfortunately I struggle to _choose_ to apply myself. Willpower doesn't seem to come naturally to me, even after 10 years as a professional programmer. Adderall didn't help - I had to take it in the morning, after breakfast. So I was still late for work, because eating breakfast is not something I'm good at, and I couldn't start my day until I had finished breakfast. Then after work the stimulants wore off and I felt like shit and reverted to my normal do-what-i-want executive function. But it made me feel normal without caffeine. So I quit the Adderall and just cutting caffeinated soda with non-caff every morning, as though I was lowering my dose on a prescription. So far it's working. I still never clean my room, which is status quo for the last 20 years, and work is still pretty easy. The phrase \"idiot savant\" comes to mind. All I want is for people to stop thinking that I'm doing this on purpose. I don't enjoy constantly feeling like a moron and being behind on simple household chores despite making decent money at a job that is considered (by other people) to be difficult. And that might even be the case for the kids with detailed exercise programs. I don't exercise at all because it'"}],"r":[["SqFu5O-oPmU",1],["UmKf8smvGsA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1],["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["biYciU1uiUw",1],["EpRrD58Ah3Q",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["zb7Bs98KmnY",1],["-s5szfPYKY4",1],["CM0aohBfUTc",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["hx7BXih7zx8",1],["tTBWfkE7BXU",1],["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["iph500cPK28",1],["wo84LFzx5nI",1],["rzQEIRRJ2T0",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["JLb5hZLw44s",1]]},"ERbvKrH-GC4":{"wy":"HN commenters post it in threads about purpose, ambition, burnout and career anxiety as a reframing that life is a dance, not a march toward eventual happiness.","q":{"x":"You haven't been failing, you've been dancing.","i":3365751,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":1992930,"author":"dstein","month":"2010-12","story":"Ask HN: Finally have loose change and spare time, but no sense of purpose. Help.","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Eloquently shows career and wealth don't bring happiness","quote":"eloquently describes exactly what you have figured out","text":"The following video, narrated by Alan Watts, and animated by Trey Parker and Matt Stone, eloquently describes exactly what you have figured out: [VIDEO] You essentially have come to the realization that the things you have been lead to believe (career, wealth, social status, possessions) are not what will make you happy. This realization can go down a dark path of depression if you're not careful, so be careful and stay level headed. Definitely take time off, pursue interests you never had time for and completely stop worrying about the future. That's the important part, to stop thinking there's some sort of end goal that you're working toward."},{"id":32358404,"author":"stakkur","month":"2022-08","story":"How to choose your college degree (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Life is a dance, not a march toward eventual happiness","quote":"It's a dance, not a long march towards 'eventual' happiness","text":"Well-intentioned, I suppose, but this seems more of a scattershot blog post than a thoughtful answer to 'how to choose your college degree'. I've heard a lot recently about 'picking the right college major'. It's normal to worry about such things. I remember. But I think obsessing over picking the 'right' college degree is a waste of time. If you have particular interests, follow them in college, but waste no more time worrying if you've made the 'right' choice. I'm in my 50s. I've heard hundreds of stories of the twists and turns of lives and the role of college in them--including my own. I was dead set on being an electrical engineer, and the first year and a half of college was in that subject. I was certain I was destined to invent the next power source and change the world with it. I ended up with an undergrad in history, and (years later) a graduate degree in urban planning. I've been in the 'tech' business for 30-odd years as a technical writer, then usability engineer, then software developer, then analyst/product owner/whatever. The best 'engineer' I know has an undergrad in fine arts. They own three software patents; they also make pottery. My 'old guy' advice to those staring down college decisions: The road doesn't matter very much. Taking steps matters. Just keep taking action and learning and adjusting and, for god's sake, enjoy your life. It's a dance, not a long march towards 'eventual' happiness: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24439154,"author":"stakkur","month":"2020-09","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite motto?","stance":"rec","why":"Two-minute description of how to live","quote":"here's what I'm striving to live like, described in two minutes","text":"I don't have a single favorite motto, but here's what I'm striving to live like, described in two minutes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11477840,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2016-04","story":"The rituals we perform and the games we play","stance":"rec","why":"Puts well why school mindset doesn't help enjoyable learning","quote":"I think this video puts it well","text":"You're welcome. :-) Thank you for your answer, I think I understand your reasoning a little better. With the example of the mathematician, you can read it a number of different ways, but one of the things I took from it was the difference between a healthy curiosity and an unhealthy curiosity. It sounds to me that you have a healthy curiosity, and enjoy the mental stimulation of exploring the big questions. As long as it remains something you enjoy and you're still open to enjoying other things then you can't really go wrong. If you'd like to find kindred spirits that also enjoy thinking about the big questions, you can find plenty in the world of philosophy. I can recommend The School of Life as an enjoyable way to explore philosophy. As some examples, here are a couple of my favourites from TSoL: [LINK] [LINK] Learning can be done for your own enjoyment, but what I would suggest is the mindset that many of us used to get us through school isn't likely to help with that. I think this video puts it well: [VIDEO] Related to this, here's a discussion about deschooling you may find interesting: [LINK] One last thing... [LINK] Best of luck!"},{"id":10643462,"author":"codemac","month":"2015-11","story":"Our Father’s Retirement and the Shared Fear Of Uselessness","stance":"rec","why":"Brings perspective on fear of dying and uselessness","quote":"the Alan Watts talk that brings some perspective for me.","text":"\"Fear of uselessness\" seems to be a euphemism for the fear of dying, both the process and the ending. I'm not sure there is a great answer here, so I'm just going to post the Alan Watts talk that brings some perspective for me. Alan Watts - Music and life: [VIDEO] \"[..] but we miss the point the whole way along, it was a musical thing. You were supposed to sing or dance while the music was being played\"."},{"id":7987863,"author":"chris123","month":"2014-07","story":"Matt Cutts: Gadgets, Google, and SEO","stance":"rec","why":"Alan Watts on living in the present","quote":"This is what Alan Watts has to say about it","text":"Glad to hear Matt is finally questioning his priorities and taking corrective action. Better late than never. Work-life-balance means now, in the present moment, not 15 years from now. The younger we are when we realize that, the better. This is what Alan Watts has to say about it: \"Music & Life\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":7183583,"author":"daGrevis","month":"2014-02","story":"Life is a game, this is your strategy guide","stance":"rec","why":"Nicely shows life is about happiness, not achievement","quote":"In my opinion, this video[1] nicely shows what I'm talking about.","text":"I would like to share my opinion on living life. What is the main goal? Some may think that it's getting better at something to get a raise and more money. First there is a school, then there is the 1st job, the you try to get a better one and you are trying and trying and trying to get higher. In my opinion, that's not the main point and it's just a side-effect of the life. Article suggests that 1st 15 years of life is a training or a tutorial, but I disagree. They are as much as important as any other time period. Person should do what makes he or she happy. If it's playing video-games or spending time with friends at a bar, it's okay. Of course, it won't affect your skills on getting a better job (so more money), but if it makes you a happier person, do it! Life is all about being happy and doing what you love to do. There is no magic-prize at the end of the life, only memories. What will be the point of having much money and good CV in you will sacrifice your happiness on it. In my opinion, this video[1] nicely shows what I'm talking about. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7105224,"author":"tripzilch","month":"2014-01","story":"How a Math Genius Hacked OkCupid to Find True Love","stance":"rec","why":"Animated Watts lecture: life is music to dance along to","quote":"check out this cool youtube animation made by the Southpark folks from his lecture","text":"Alan Watts said: (paraphrased) Life is not a journey pilgrimage with an important goal at the end, it is a musical thing and you are supposed to laugh and sing and dance along the way. For the full text, check out this cool youtube animation made by the Southpark folks from his lecture: [VIDEO] (animation style is Southpark-like, content is Alan Watts)"},{"id":5201037,"author":"dcolgan","month":"2013-02","story":"Dream, but don't forget to live","stance":"rec","why":"Encourages relaxing and being happy now","quote":"I really liked this video by Alan Watts called \"Life and Music","text":"Having grown up as a Christian in the Midwestern US, we were always taught that heaven lasts forever, and therefore I never felt any time pressure. This life can be spent building character and struggling with self improvement and you can enjoy yourself in the next life. As I've come to consider the possibility that life ends when it is over, that mindset no longer makes nearly as much sense to me. Yes I want to improve myself, since self improvement and achievement brings increased opportunities, but why am I striving to find more and more opportunities and achievement? Is there some mystical end point after which I can finally relax and be happy? Why not just decide to relax and be happy now? And so I've tried to do so and I think I'm enjoying life more because of it. I really liked this video by Alan Watts called \"Life and Music:\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":3973799,"author":"chubbard","month":"2012-05","story":"Are Stanford Students Just (Really Excellent) Sheep?","stance":"rec","why":"Watts articulated this theme with more panache and charm","quote":"he did it with much more panache and charm.","text":"No generation ever knows what its all for until they're 40 possibly 35 because you're outside the holy grail of advertising demographic. Coincidence? Yea probably. If you haven't figured it out by then your not paying much attention anyway. There's prescript of zoloft by the door for you. Anyway, This essay is much the same that Allan Watts articulated in the 60s and I think he did it with much more panache and charm. [VIDEO] I find its best to laugh because it really has been a well executed practical joke."},{"id":3365751,"author":"blhack","month":"2011-12","story":"I'm turning 30 and I've produced no amazing art.","stance":"rec","why":"Reframes life as dancing, not failing; stop waiting for payoff","quote":"You haven't been failing, you've been dancing.","text":"Alan watts has something important to say about this... [VIDEO] You haven't been failing, you've been dancing. This is the point. Enjoy it and stop waiting for the payoff."},{"id":2010077,"author":"noidi","month":"2010-12","story":"Winning Isn't Normal","stance":"rec","why":"Food for thought on life goals","quote":"which is great food for thought when pondering your goals in life","text":"This blog post reminded me of Alan Watts' Music and Life, which is great food for thought when pondering your goals in life: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27547774,"author":"Crazyontap","month":"2021-06","story":"On Depression and Founders","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the worry that dream chasing ends empty","quote":"What if the bag of dreams we're all chasing is just empty like this Alan watts video(1).","text":"Man this comment is scary af for me. I mean I don't know you from adam but getting to the success at the end of the rainbow is what makes me jump out of bed each morning and not want to go to bed each night. It's my dream that consumes me and keeps me happy more than anything else. But I too sometimes feel that \"what happens after it's done\" and it is not everything I imagine it to be. What if the bag of dreams we're all chasing is just empty like this Alan watts video(1). Call it denial but I just try not to think about it a lot as I don't know what the future holds and it's pointless to speculate right now as every person is different. But reading your comment did scare me a little tbh! (1) [VIDEO]"},{"id":24645736,"author":"stakkur","month":"2020-10","story":"On Old Age","stance":"reference","why":"Alan Watts on Buddhism and life as dance","quote":"Alan Watts--who had a thing or two to say about Buddhism--disagrees with you","text":">>It's really odd of you saying that for Buddhism, life a \"dance\". This is really not what Buddhism is about. Alan Watts--who had a thing or two to say about Buddhism--disagrees with you: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12448913,"author":"randomsearch","month":"2016-09","story":"Hunter S. Thompson on Finding Your Purpose (1958)","stance":"reference","why":"Further video underlining the commenter's point","quote":"A further video that underlines my point","text":"\"A lifestyle merely describes and/or quantifies the way one lives.\" This is correct. I don't draw the conclusion from the post, I make this statement to directly contradict the post, which I believe is unwise. A further video that underlines my point: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12145767,"author":"ivm","month":"2016-07","story":"Ask HN: When you feel stuck in life","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the quoted passage about life as music","quote":"It was a musical thing and you were supposed to sing or to dance while the music was being played.","text":"> We thought of life by analogy with a journey, a pilgrimage, which had a serious purpose at the end, and the thing was to get to that end, success or whatever it is, maybe heaven after you’re dead. But we missed the point the whole way along. It was a musical thing and you were supposed to sing or to dance while the music was being played. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3757498,"author":"peteretep","month":"2012-03","story":"Dokkodo by Miyamoto Musashi ","stance":"reference","why":"Alan Watts quote as antidote to austere self-discipline","quote":"I find this quote by Alan Watts particularly poignant, and perhaps an antidote to the original post","text":"> A lot of this can be interpreted as a way to shun yourself from the world and not face reality. But can't anyone do that with anything helpful? There are lots of texts on rising above worldly pleasures, and some are very good. I'm a particular fan of Ekhart Tolle's work, and I enjoyed the Tibetan Book of Living and Dying... The Tao of Pooh, and the Te of Piglet got me through a difficult adolescence. As with any idea, though, simplifying it too far tends to lose important subtleties. The mental austerity mentioned in the original post is likely to strike a resonant chord with many men who wished they had better self-control, and who wished they were acting with more general agency in their life. Men are complete suckers for certain ideals of manhood, certain archetypes that are well presented. If this is something that interests you, I can strongly recommend the books Iron John by Robert Bly, King Warrior Magician Lover by Robert Moore, and the excellent Way of The Superior Man by David Deida. I find this quote by Alan Watts particularly poignant, and perhaps an antidote to the original post: \"We thought of life by analogy with a journey, a pilgrimage, which had a serious purpose at the end and the thing was to get to that end, success or whatever it is, maybe heaven after you’re dead. But we missed the point the whole way along. It was a musical thing and you were supposed to sing or to dance while the music was being played.\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":2674971,"author":"lhnz","month":"2011-06","story":"Ask HN: Overcoming an Inferiority Complex","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I completely understand how you feel. You sound successful: academically and career wise. You realise this, yet it's never enough and you compare yourself to the cream of the crop or 'what you could be'. You need to stop this. Constantly wishing for something more while feeling unhappy with where you are 'in the moment' is a bad state of mind. It is not how you want to experience your life and it is certainly not reflective of what you have already achieved. (Many happy people achieve a lot less!) There will always be people that are more intelligent or more successful, and so on. No matter how hard any of us try, there will always be something more to attain and, truly, if you wished to feel superior to your peers then Hacker News would not be the best place to be. Other than at a few elite universities and companies it is difficult to find people as intelligent, refined and ambitious as those found here. (This is not a bad thing! Being around these kinds of people lifts you up.) I am not saying 'give up on trying to attain', and the advice which has been given by many in this thread on how to do so or how to free yourself from tedium is brilliant. But remember that life is not a journey in which the only thing important is moving-up and getting to the 'end'. There is not going to be a top to the ladder you're climbing, and the only 'end' is when you die. [1] As for: gaining confidence in your abilities, feeling better about yourself, having a better thought process, and ove"},{"id":2531260,"author":"wattsbaat","month":"2011-05","story":"What happens to all the Asian-American overachievers when the test-taking ends?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Then the progression stops, and the advice runs out. All your life the entire raison d'etre for you has been The Next Step, but now there is none. You've done it, you've hit the end, trumpets are supposed to blare, and... and... And then what? You must have either seen this or you don't need to. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["jVoSQP2yUYA",1],["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["KgzQuE1pR1w",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["R5Gppi-O3a8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["iFDe5kUUyT0",1],["Hy4QjmKzF1c",1],["49kBWM3RQQ8",1],["obmt_PkIfBE",1],["nnR8fDW3Ilo",1],["NOAgplgTxfc",1],["mn4L_aJ1rV4",1],["PmTUW-owa2w",1],["RcU7FaEEzNU",1]]},"mw2z9lV3W1g":{"wy":"HN cites it repeatedly as the root-cause argument that money in politics must be fixed before other issues, and as background for his Mayday PAC.","q":{"x":"This TED lecture[0] is only 18 minutes long and it offers rather a chilling perspective on any sensible reform.","i":48242530,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":13427913,"author":"mtgx","month":"2017-01","story":"What I Wish I'd Known About Equity Before Joining a Unicorn","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lessig says fix money in politics first.","quote":"Larry Lessig has been right all along when he said this needs to be fixed before anything else [1].","text":"All of that, plus tax software companies, such as Intuit lobbying against simplifying the tax code. [LINK] And once again we reach the conclusion that corporate lobbying and donations are the \"root of all evil\" in American politics, and everything is broken because of it. Larry Lessig has been right all along when he said this needs to be fixed before anything else [1]. Because once this is fixed, everything else should be a lot easier and a lot more in tune with what the People want [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] It's just so easy and so cheap for corporations to buy votes right now. Why wouldn't they do it, when the upside is billions of dollars and there's no penalty for it? They can literally buy a vote with a few thousand dollars \"donation\". Set a limit of $200 (maybe $500 for presidential candidates) political donation per year per person and imprison (6-36 months) anyone who dares to do it any other way, fast and furious, no matter who he is, wealthy billionaire or former president. It's the only way to escape this corruption in the system. Also, I think the U.S. would need a special agency whose sole mission is to look for this type of corruption. In this case, \"mission creep\" and the purpose of maintaining their jobs would work in the People's favor. The more the agency would do (catch corrupt donors or politicians), the more it could justify its existence. Similar agencies have seen a lot of success in Europe, trying to catch corrupt politicians."},{"id":8857302,"author":"tim_hutton","month":"2015-01","story":"Ask HN: What can we do to show we don't support the response to Aaron Swartz?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Root cause of political problems is money in politics.","quote":"Support Lawrence Lessig in his efforts to solve this root problem!","text":"The root cause of this is the influence of money on our politicians. Symptoms include laws written for the rich and the corporations rather than for the people, causing the continued growth of inequality and a drag on the economy. Support Lawrence Lessig in his efforts to solve this root problem! [VIDEO] He departs on the second of his New Hampshire Rebellion walks on Sunday."},{"id":7082181,"author":"PythonicAlpha","month":"2014-01","story":"Obama’s Changes to Government Surveillance","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lessig on money in politics.","quote":"But you should better watch this one, I don't know if you know Lessig (I think, he is a real patriot)","text":"I already do :( I think, it's in best interest of the people in power, that the campaign is so costly. When you look at the presidents of the last decades (I don't know to much US history, to go back to far), they all where already millionaires when they where elected. But you should better watch this one, I don't know if you know Lessig (I think, he is a real patriot): [VIDEO] In our country, the campaigns are less costly, but until anybody comes to the top of any major party, he must be inwardly corrupted already. I see it this way, that nearly no current leading politician in our country is not already totally corrupted by money and power. I think, Mark Twain had this one right, but I don't think, that it had to be exported, it seems to lay inside the human kind. I think it was already visible in the first republics like Rome. Maybe we are in a similar position today as in Caesars or even Neros time. You should know, that some years ago, even in Europe, rich people started building places to live for millionaires -- apart and secure from the \"plebs\". They provide for times, when the 99% will no longer hold still. Of course, millionaires are only third class \"rich\" people in our world. Second class are billionaires."},{"id":48242530,"author":"maratc","month":"2026-05","story":"Steve Wozniak cheered after telling students they have AI – actual intelligence","stance":"rec","why":"Short 18-minute lecture with chilling perspective on reform.","quote":"This TED lecture[0] is only 18 minutes long and it offers rather a chilling perspective on any sensible reform.","text":"In 2016, just 400 American families were the source of 50% of all election money that year. If your \"candidate\" is going to \"rein in big tech,\" how the hell are they supposed to raise money from them? And if they aren't going to do that, where will that money go? Your views seem to be rather ... idealistic. This TED lecture[0] is only 18 minutes long and it offers rather a chilling perspective on any sensible reform. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43448338,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2025-03","story":"California Attorney General issues consumer alert for 23andMe customers","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig rebuts the point that money isn't corporate influence.","quote":"It feels like we have a government that governs on behalf of those with money","text":"Here is a Harvard law professor and tech darling (of creative commons), Lawrence Lessig, rebutting your overall point. [VIDEO] Summary: It feels like we have a government that governs on behalf of those with money because those with money vote first in the fundraising \"election\" that happens even before a primary election. More money is more votes in the \"election\" that mattes the most. Those with money get to filter candidates first. edit: Just because money comes from an individual and not directly from a corporation doesn't mean it isn't a pro-corporate/\"corporate\" contribution."},{"id":34843610,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2023-02","story":"Big Tech lobbyist language made it verbatim into NY’s hedged repair bill","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig talk on the topic of lobbying influence.","quote":"A Ted Talk by Harvard Law professor and Copyright Law reform advocate Lawrence Lessig on the topic","text":"Political movement related to this study: [LINK] A Ted Talk by Harvard Law professor and Copyright Law reform advocate Lawrence Lessig on the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7681999,"author":"slantedview","month":"2014-05","story":"The Launch of the Mayday Citizens' SuperPAC","stance":"rec","why":"Background on Mayday campaign and financial incentives.","quote":"This TED talk from Lessig gives more background","text":"I'd argue that Congress has not done a good job of anything (at least not anything that the majority of Americans would care about) because our motivations are not aligned. That's the point of this campaign - to remove the motivation for Congress to act in ways that our not in the best interest of the general public, by removing the financial incentives for them to do so. This TED talk from Lessig gives more background: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7314921,"author":"PythonicAlpha","month":"2014-02","story":"Privacy Protects Bothersome People (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Speech addressing corruption and democracy.","quote":"Good speech about it: Lawrence Lessig: We the People, and the Republic we must reclaim","text":"That is a valid question. Good speech about it: Lawrence Lessig: We the People, and the Republic we must reclaim: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6011505,"author":"lifeguard","month":"2013-07","story":"Prism Break","stance":"rec","why":"Argues US government corruption.","quote":"America's government is so corrupt it is no longer democratic. Watch this video","text":"Is it a crime for a hungry man to steal bread? None of your proposed solutions would address his needs. America's government is so corrupt it is no longer democratic. Watch this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6137296,"author":"liscovich","month":"2013-07","story":"The entire US Code is now online in XML","stance":"rec","why":"Lessig's TED talk on money in politics.","quote":"Larry Lessig has a great book on this","text":"There is very little outright corruption in Congress. Special interests exert most of the influence through campaign contributions that are publicly disclosed. Larry Lessig has a great book on this: [LINK] . And here is the link to his TED talk on the same topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44331541,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2025-06","story":"How malicious AI swarms can threaten democracy","stance":"reference","why":"Lessig argues fundraising filters candidates before primaries.","quote":"fundraising is structurally an election where money counts as votes","text":"> the candidate who spent less won. That is entirely unsubstantiated. There is no reasonable way to measure this and any measurements taken are inherently political. I am amenable to the idea of that being true for official spending, but unless the twitter purchase, for example, were tabulated, or spending on our American \"pravda\" (Truth social which was clearly influenced by [LINK] ), I would be extremely suspicious of those numbers. > so at least at presidential level, there is neither apathy, complacency, or the ability to buy elections. Again, I completely disagree, and so does Harvard Law Professor Lawrence Lessig, who argues that it is extremely hard to win a primary without fundraising, and fundraising is structurally an election where money counts as votes, and therefore nearly all candidates who make it to the primary have already been filtered through by those with money: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36103786,"author":"bostik","month":"2023-05","story":"Reflections on Ten Years Past the Snowden Revelations","stance":"reference","why":"Recorded Lessig talk making the same point about democracy.","quote":"Lessig has made the same point in his \"Country of Lesters\" talk.","text":"I keep saying that what we are currently living through is a surprisingly accurate reflection of how democracy began. In the city state of Athens, all free men had the vote. The definition of \"free man\" just happened to exclude approximately 97% of the population. Our governments have since figured out that you can merrily hand out the privilege, as long you remain in control of what is allowed to be put on the vote in the first place. I'm not alone with my view, btw. Lessig has made the same point in his \"Country of Lesters\" talk.[0,1] 0: [LINK] (overview, writeup) 1: [VIDEO] (recorded talk)"},{"id":18185384,"author":"startupfounder","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What can be done to prevent a climate catastrophe?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"...governments can be counted out of taking the leadership needed to solve this within 12 years.\" This is the issue.[0] Governments are the middlemen between citizens that we the people decide to put in power through voting to represent us and our issues like climate change. But democracy has been hijacked by putting additional middlemen between we the people . The second middleman is the capital election , specifically ~132 Americans who donated 60% of the SuperPAC money. So our representatives spend a significant portion of their time in office calling these people to get funding for the next election cycle and when they vote on issues they think of these donors. Follow the money. Yes we can fix data centers or cargo ships or deploy nuclear power or utility regulation, but at some point regulation for these as well will be dependent on the people we vote to put into power to represent us. At the end of the day, corporations have a fiduciary duty to their shareholders to maximize profits and shareholder value. If leaders lead, companies will follow. I have an idea! Why don't we, the very large community of Hacker News with 3-3.5M+ monthly uniques[1] petition one of the most innovative and powerful institutions to fund more projects that will fix broken governments around the world? \"Government\" is only mentioned one time in the YC RFP[2], maybe it should have it's own category. Send them an email: hn@ycombinator.com or [LINK] or contact your friends who are in the YC networ"},{"id":16280549,"author":"mtgx","month":"2018-02","story":"Amazon Health","stance":"reference","why":"Lessig argues money in politics is root cause.","quote":"Larry Lessig was right when he kept saying that this is the root cause that needs to be fixed before anything else","text":"And that's because the US government no longer responds to the needs and wants of the people, but to the needs of corporations and rich people, which happened because of legalized bribery, or the ability to buy a politician's vote with \"donation money\". This is why Larry Lessig was right when he kept saying that this is the root cause that needs to be fixed before anything else . People should be supporting such \"single issue\" politicians that want to reform this system all over, because this is what will fix everything else in the long term, whether it's economic, healthcare, or even social issues (which tend to be turned into issues to distract from the real harm they're doing somewhere else). [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15905436,"author":"mtgx","month":"2017-12","story":"Net Neutrality’s Holes in Europe May Offer Peek at Future in U.S","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for claim that money in politics is the biggest issue.","quote":"It's the biggest issue that affects all the other issues","text":"It's not about what the people want, because the U.S. is no longer a democratic country. When more people start admitting that and seeing that way, then they'll be ready to change that at the detriment of everything else . It's the biggest issue that affects all the other issues, but some still believe if you support this change at the detriment of everything else then you're a \"purist\" or a \"single issue candidate\". Of course, usually the people who call you those names are also the ones who benefit from the current system. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":13548656,"author":"bostik","month":"2017-02","story":"Tech firms are departing from their see-no-evil stance on society and politics","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I am going to get flamed to oblivion for this, but nonetheless: You are not looking far enough in the past. The very textbook definition [0] of fascism (or to be precise - one of the basic definitions) is that it is a political movement that was directly linked with corporatism. Or in other words, a form of government where the major political power is wielded by powerful corporations and furthers primarily their interests.[1] Quoting from [0]: \"fascism, especially once in power, [attracts] support primarily from what in a classical sense is called the 'far right' or 'extreme right'.\" Authoritarianism and police state mentality are both drivers, but also the results of this mentality. Considering how the politics in the western world, US included, have been going for at least the recent half a century... I am going to say that fascism didn't go away with the deaths of Hitler and Mussolini. It has been a dark undercurrent in the US politics (as well as UK and the Scandinavian countries, at least) all this time. We have ALL been on the road back to fascism for decades. It didn't start with G.W. Bush in the US, or with Thatcher in the UK, or with True Finns in Finland, or with whatever the French populist party happens to be called this week. Each one of these is just a manifestation of a more generic right-wing, very much misandry-fuelled swing in public opinion. US has been on its way to fascism for a long time. But you are not alone, your country has just been the most powerf"},{"id":13533651,"author":"mtgx","month":"2017-01","story":"Request for Startups: News, Jobs, and Democracy","stance":"reference","why":"Linked among resources on money in politics reform.","quote":"","text":"For startups working on improving democracy, I'd suggest you start helping these three groups in any way you can: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Creating a more fair representation in government that doesn't only involve the duopoly parties as well as getting money out politics should be the two main priorities before anything else. Unless these two things are fixed first, everything else will continue to be broken. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9473201,"author":"higherpurpose","month":"2015-05","story":"Big companies are stopping Congress from fixing the patent system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Term limits should be set. I think 12 years in Congress is about the optimum number: not too long to become your life, but also long enough to get \"enough experience\" and pass it along to the newcomers as they come in. That would be 6 terms for House Representatives and 2 for Senators. Of course it's not a magic bullet. Many other things need to be changed: 1) banning corporations, PACs or any organization from giving money to politicians - only individuals should be able to donate and the sum should be limited to maybe $1,000 (allowing \"NGO's\" and such to give money is just a loophole looking to be abused) 2) making votes in Congress anonymous [1]. Votes shouldn't have \"transparency\". There's a reason why anywhere else voting is anonymous: it's much harder to get influenced this way, whether it's by money or peer/leader pressure (see invasion of Iraq, passing the \"Patriot\" Act, etc). The lobbyists can give you money, and then you can still vote as you wish, which over the long term it will seem like a less and less viable strategy for lobbyists. 3) switching to a much more democratic voting system (\"approval voting\" [2] would be ideal, but even the 2-voting rounds system that's used in some European countries would be a HUGE improvement, as it would fix the \"spoiler effect\" somewhat - but still nowhere near enough as approval voting does) 4) proportional party representation in Congress (seems like a no-brainer to me). The idea that 2 parties can dominate Congress/Parliament"},{"id":5864415,"author":"Datsundere","month":"2013-06","story":"NSA leaker is a patriot, not a traitor","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The voting system in America is just plain wrong.","text":"Hobbes had said that humans give up some of their rights to be protected by the government. That's what the western philosophy is built on. You may try to deny it but needing security is already a loss of freedom. Examples of peaceful countries like sweden or switzerland can be given, but what did they do to stop hitler? Poland started to crack the enigma code and later turing with his friends in secrecy completed it in England. What we need is people that can be trusted to be in power. The voting system in America is just plain wrong. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["WTYet-qf1jo",1],["tk862BbjWx4",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["LMbI6sk-62E",1],["lr4MmmWQtZM",1],["qp0HIF3SfI4",1],["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["v3pK0dccQ38",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["1gEz__sMVaY",1],["amJ-ev8Ial8",1],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["PJy8vTu66tE",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["9WcHsT97suU",1],["9tocssf3w80",1],["Eghl19elKk8",1],["d0nERTFo-Sk",1],["GTQnarzmTOc",1],["AxCo2bE9Gtk",1]]},"2pWv7GOvuf0":{"wy":"Frequently recommended as an excellent, clear introductory RL course from the lead of AlphaGo, pairing well with Sutton and Barto.","q":{"x":"Really clear explanations that went hand in hand when I was reading Sutton and Barto's introductory book.","i":22754933,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":44280555,"author":"briandw","month":"2025-06","story":"Q-learning is not yet scalable","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent introductory RL course for those wanting depth","quote":"this intro course from David Silver (Deep Mind) is excellent","text":"This papers assumes that you know quite a bit about RL already. If you really want to dig into RL, this intro course from David Silver (Deep Mind) is excellent: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22754933,"author":"colmvp","month":"2020-04","story":"ACM Prize in Computing Awarded to AlphaGo Developer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear RL explanations pairing well with Sutton and Barto","quote":"Really clear explanations that went hand in hand when I was reading Sutton and Barto's introductory book.","text":"I almost knew exactly who it was going to be when they mentioned AlphaGo Developer. For those who aren't that well versed in RL, I recommend watching his lectures at UCL ( [VIDEO] ). Really clear explanations that went hand in hand when I was reading Sutton and Barto's introductory book."},{"id":23880387,"author":"confuseshrink","month":"2020-07","story":"Powerful AI Can Now Be Trained on a Single Computer","stance":"rec","why":"Series showing what RL can and cannot do","quote":"I would start with David Silvers (DeepMind) youtube series to get an idea of what's possible or not.","text":"I would start with David Silvers (DeepMind) youtube series to get an idea of what's possible or not. Running an already trained reinforcement learning agent is relatively cheap (unless your model is massive). I suspect the reason people aren't using it yet is because it's a) really difficult to get right in training, even basic convergence is not guaranteed without careful tuning b) really difficult to guarantee reasonable behavior outside of the scenarios you're able to reach in QA. edit: Link to lecture series [VIDEO]"},{"id":19170582,"author":"alkhatib","month":"2019-02","story":"Dopamine framework: Fast prototyping of reinforcement learning algorithms (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Full lecture playlist on RL from a DeepMind researcher","quote":"Dave's UCL Course on RL[2] is great too","text":"I came across this a few days ago: For Reinforcement Learning specifically, the standard text is Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction[1]. Dave's UCL Course on RL[2] is great too (playlist of all lectures)[3]. Source: Julian Schrittwieser works on Deepmind at Google [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19196264,"author":"colmvp","month":"2019-02","story":"Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction (2018) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Lectures covering the material of the Sutton and Barto book","quote":"I also recommend interested people to watch David Silver's RL lectures at UCL on YouTube.","text":"I also recommend interested people to watch David Silver's RL lectures at UCL on YouTube. He covers material from the book. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18400214,"author":"visarga","month":"2018-11","story":"Philosophy Has Made Plenty of Progress","stance":"rec","why":"Introductory RL course that reframes philosophy of mind through agents, rewards and environments","quote":"There is a great RL course on YouTube by David Silver","text":"The notions of agent, environment and reward are encountered in Reinforcement Learning which is a sub-field of ML but also relevant to biological agents. There is a great RL course on YouTube by David Silver (one of the creators of AlphaGo, which is probably the most famous application of RL so far). [VIDEO] The course can properly set the perspective of RL and shed new light on the philosophy of mind, if you take what it says and then extrapolate to humans and other agents. What I find fascinating about RL is that it can be defined concretely. Consciousness can only be defined by reference to other words, in a less exact and concrete way. RL can also explain how meaning appears, based on future reward prediction. The rich sensations we have can be explained by an encoding-decoding architecture based on reconstruction error. Many difficulties in RL map back to difficulties humans have in choosing how to act - the exploration-exploitation tradeoff, instinct vs reason (two different ways to perform RL, one based purely on rewards and the other based on an internal model of the world and rewards). Some of the problems related to multi-agent RL are also covered in Game Theory, such as the prisoner's dilemma. Regarding philosophy of language: we have today numerical representations of meaning in words and phrases. They are usually represented as vectors in high dimensional space or sets of vectors. For example it is customary to use 300-1000 dimensions for representing words. Thes"},{"id":17010814,"author":"crisbal_","month":"2018-05","story":"Reinforcement Learning – Bandit Problems","stance":"rec","why":"Lessons by AlphaGo contributor David Silver","quote":"I would also like to suggest these lessons","text":"As someone who is doing his bachelor thesis on Reinforcement Learning this is some useful information. OT (but not really) question: does anyone here use Reinforcement Learning techniques at work? For the thesis I am working on black-box optimization of 2 variable functions with Reinforcement Learning (and comparing it with Bayesian Optimization techniques). As someone else suggested the Sutton & Barto book is really great knowledge but I would also like to suggest these lessons ( [VIDEO] ) by David Silver (who worked on AlphaGo)"},{"id":16140387,"author":"igravious","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: Best introductory video courses on ML and Deep Learning?","stance":"rec","why":"One of several recommended ML course resources","quote":"DeepMind. Reinforcement Learning Course by David Silver[4]","text":"fast.ai Making neural nets uncool again. fast.ai is dedicated to making the power of deep learning accessible to all.[0] Udacity. Intro to Machine Learning: Pattern Recognition for Fun and Profit[1] MIT 6.S099: Artificial General Intelligence[2] Lecture Series on Artificial Intelligence by Prof. P. Dasgupta, Department of Computer Science & Engineering, Indian Institute of Technology, Kharagpur[3] DeepMind. Reinforcement Learning Course by David Silver[4] Stanford Winter Quarter 2016 class: CS231n: Convolutional Neural Networks for Visual Recognition.[5] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":15863115,"author":"magoghm","month":"2017-12","story":"Mastering Chess and Shogi by Self-Play with General Reinforcement Learning","stance":"rec","why":"Reinforcement learning course by AlphaZero lead","quote":"You can check this Reinforcement Learning Course by David Silver on YouTube","text":"You can check this Reinforcement Learning Course by David Silver on YouTube: [VIDEO] By the way, I believe David Silver was the lead programmer for AlphaZero."},{"id":13900946,"author":"deepnet","month":"2017-03","story":"Ask HN: Best books on AI?","stance":"rec","why":"Course based on Sutton and Barto's RL book","quote":"David Silver's Reinforcement Course is based on Sutton & Barto","text":"Sutton & Barto's Reinforment Learning complete this triumvirate [LINK] David Silver's Reinforcement Course is based on Sutton & Barto [VIDEO]"},{"id":47925841,"author":"aanet","month":"2026-04","story":"A Man Behind AlphaGo Thinks AI Is Taking the Wrong Path","stance":"reference","why":"Popular RL lectures by David Silver","quote":"His RL lectures [1] are quite popular","text":"Not \"A Man Behind AlphaGo...\" but rather \"The Man Behind AlphaGo...\" On David Silver's Ineffable Intelligence startup and their $1 Billion (!) seed round (!!) His RL lectures [1] are quite popular [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["JAcR7kqOb3o",1],["qv6UVOQ0F44",1],["CZIINXhGDcs",1],["NfnWJUyUJYU",1],["lhYGXYeMq_E",1],["-KxjVlaLBmk",1],["iOWamCtnwTc",1],["iDulhoQ2pro",1],["Gh-Tff7DdzU",1]]},"3kEfedtQVOY":{"wy":"HN commenters call it one of the best security talks and the standard introduction to LANGSEC, which they use to argue for simpler, well-specified input formats.","q":{"x":"The best talk on computer security I have seen is Meredith Patterson's Science of Insecurity at 28c3","i":11624132,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11624132,"author":"sedachv","month":"2016-05","story":"Remote code execution vulnerability in ImageMagick","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best computer security talk, using grammar theory for security.","quote":"The best talk on computer security I have seen is Meredith Patterson's Science of Insecurity at 28c3","text":"> 1. Simpler formats for file representation and data interchange. When someone tries to add an extra bitfield option, say no. When they keep trying, get a wooden stick with \"no\" written on it. Part of the disease of modern computing is bloated specs. > 2. Restrictive not permissive code bases. Exit and bail out early. Tell the user \"file corrupted\". Push back. The best talk on computer security I have seen is Meredith Patterson's Science of Insecurity at 28c3: [VIDEO] It is really interesting that the techniques needed to achieve these security goals (theory of grammars) is one of the oldest and best explored areas of computer science."},{"id":5512326,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2013-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best technical talk you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Systematic proposal for secure computing using linguistics and formal grammars.","quote":"It's a tour de force, presenting a systematic and practical proposal for how we can build an open and secure future for computing.","text":"Meredith Patterson's astonishing CCC talk on computing and linguistics. It's a tour de force, presenting a systematic and practical proposal for how we can build an open and secure future for computing. I can't imagine a better example of the joys of inter-disciplinary thinking. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40536829,"author":"holmesworcester","month":"2024-05","story":"Civil society in Latvia, Lithuania, and Poland targeted with Pegasus spyware","stance":"rec","why":"Parser complexity underlies exploits; use well-described data and generated parsers.","quote":"Folks should watch this panel and other talks by Meredith L. Patterson","text":"I think a lot of people are mis-seeing these Pegasus attacks as a problem of political will (\"we need sanctions on NSO/Israel!\") or memory safety (\"rewrite it in Rust!\") when there is a deeper problem: parser complexity. Specifically, we're using context-sensitive and Turing-complete parsers up and down the software stack, which (at the Turing-complete level of complexity, says Gödel) guarantees that determined attackers can expect to discover an exploitable \"weird machine.\" Folks should watch this panel and other talks by Meredith L. Patterson (co-collaborator and wife of the late Len Sassaman) and Sergey Bratus (previously at DARPA, now Dartmouth) to get a sense of the problem and the solution. [LINK] These folks are saying that, just as the industry handled over-the-wire insecurity with TLS and e2ee (and not rolling our own crypto) we can handle device exploitability with well-described data and automated parser generation from those descriptions (and not rolling our own parsers, which these folks say should be verboten for the same reason as rolling one's own crypto: parsing is too hard for non-specialists to get right). Moreover, we don't have to redo the whole stack for it to be meaningful: big players can start using this tooling (e.g. [LINK] ) for parsers handling data from very untrustworthy inputs, such as message payloads sent to a phone number. Apple's Lockdown mode is a step in this direction, though in such a crude, \"burn the village\" way that it makes the phone"},{"id":10567521,"author":"hannob","month":"2015-11","story":"We need less powerful languages","stance":"rec","why":"Older talk that could be a gamechanger in IT security.","quote":"this older talk by Meredith Patterson is still worth watching a lot","text":"It's kinda interesting that he writes a long blogpost with many ideas that are quite similar to the Langsec ideas, however in the comments he says that he wasn't aware of Langsec. I am not surprised. Langsec could really be a gamechanger in many aspects of IT security, yet many people in the Infosec community are not aware of these concepts. If you're curious, this older talk by Meredith Patterson is still worth watching a lot: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9947897,"author":"ShaneWilton","month":"2015-07","story":"Hammer: Parser Combinators for C","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on context-free formats to avoid memory corruption bugs.","quote":"Meredith Patterson and Sergey Bratus gave an excellent talk on the subject at 28c3","text":"Hammer is a nifty parser combinator library from the people behind Language-Theoretic Security [1]. Their goal is to promote the use of context-free formats and protocols, to avoid falling into the trap of trying to parse recursively-enumerable languages, a problem that reduces to the halting problem. The idea is that by sticking to context-free grammars, protocols will be easier to parse, and the bulk of memory corruption errors can eliminated. Meredith Patterson and Sergey Bratus gave an excellent talk on the subject at 28c3 [2], and they've put together a video series on using Hammer to build a secure parser for JSON RPC [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":8874007,"author":"noblethrasher","month":"2015-01","story":"Useful old technologies: ASN.1 (2013)","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to LANGSEC.","quote":"here is a video that works a bit better as an introduction to LANGSEC","text":"Yes, I should have said “unbounded length field”. But, with respect to the discussion, a 32 or 64-bit integer is only bounded in the academic sense. Also, here is a video that works a bit better as an introduction to LANGSEC: [VIDEO] (around 19:00 is especially entertaining)"},{"id":6730044,"author":"majika","month":"2013-11","story":"Bytecode","stance":"rec","why":"Why undecidable input recognition makes protocols unsecurable.","quote":"","text":"This is related to Meredith Patterson's talk at the 28th CCC, \"The Science of Insecurity\": [VIDEO] > Why is the overwhelming majority of common networked software still not secure, despite all effort to the contrary? Why is it almost certain to get exploited so long as attackers can craft its inputs? Why is it the case that no amount of effort seems to be enough to fix software that must speak certain protocols? > The answer to these questions is that for many protocols and services currently in use on the Internet, the problem of recognizing and validating their \"good\", expected inputs from bad ones is either not well-posed or is undecidable (i. e., no algorithm can exist to solve it in the general case), which means that their implementations cannot even be comprehensively tested, let alone automatically checked for weaknesses or correctness. The designers' desire for more functionality has made these protocols effectively unsecurable."},{"id":6149105,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2013-08","story":"Comments in JSON","stance":"rec","why":"Sloppy grammars and bad parsers make data cross into Turing-complete code.","quote":"","text":"> \"Code\" requires some notion of an execution platform/environment, which does not exist for arbitrary data. Arbitrary data don't exist without some notion of an execution (or interpretation) platform. We tend to use \"code\" as a word for \"commands telling some execution process what to do\" and \"data\" as a word for \"information that is meant to be transformed\" but in reality this distinction is meaningless; both are fundamentally the same thing, and even our \"code\" vs. \"data\" words have blurry borders. It's very apparent when you start reading configuration files. For example, aren't Ant \"configuration files\" essentially programs[0]? We all know what we usually mean in context by saying what is \"code\" vs. what is \"data\", but one has to remember, that in fact they are the same - minding it leads to insights like metaprogramming. Forgetting about it leads to dumb languages and nasty problems, and is generally not wise. [0] - the answer is: yes, they are, see [LINK] for more. ETA: Questions to ponder: - are regular expressions code, or data? - is source written in Prolog code, or data? Also I recommend watching [VIDEO] to learn how what would be data, as defined by formal grammars of some real-world protocols, can - by means of sloppy grammars and bad parser implementation - cross the threshold of Turing-completeness and become code."},{"id":3751600,"author":"mkn","month":"2012-03","story":"Tanenbaum’s advice for building product, networks, and living life.  ","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for accepting only regular or context-free input grammars.","quote":"She contends, and it seems sound to me, that the time has passed to be \"tolerant when accepting,","text":"Item [8] was mentioned in this talk: [VIDEO] by Meredith Patterson. She contends, and it seems sound to me, that the time has passed to be \"tolerant when accepting,\" and that you should instead only accept regular grammars or, at worst, context-free grammars as input languages on the open Web."},{"id":17263095,"author":"sedachv","month":"2018-06","story":"I Quit My Job to Live on Donations to Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Inputs and protocols can be described by grammars.","quote":"All communication protocols, any kind of input, can be described in terms of grammars, and that is important","text":"Did your programs take input? If you had studied parsing theory, you would know that \"strings\" do not mean ASCII/Unicode text strings, they mean sequences of symbols from a set. All communication protocols, any kind of input, can be described in terms of grammars, and that is important: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14384188,"author":"dom0","month":"2017-05","story":"Language-agnostic schemas based on Haskell's type system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] i.e. it's kinda unwise in some applications (e.g. arbitrary / untrusted schemas) to have to implement & run languages that are powerful enough to perform pretty much arbitrary computation."},{"id":10326639,"author":"noblethrasher","month":"2015-10","story":"Language-theoretic Security","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker is an original langsec author discussing type theory.","quote":"","text":"Langsec knows all about type theory: See [VIDEO] (about 90 seconds) n.b. that Merideth Patterson, the speaker in that video, is one of the original authors of langsec."},{"id":4193681,"author":"zitterbewegung","month":"2012-07","story":"MVC, MOVE - Or Simply A State Machine?","stance":"reference","why":"Security proofs for protocols via state machines.","quote":"You can basically make security proofs in your protocol.","text":"An even better argument for a state machine (FSM or PDA) * [LINK] You can basically make security proofs in your protocol. [VIDEO] I was thinking about extending this to bottle.py and replacing the routing elements with an fsm library. Then I wanted the views to be a bunch of regexps or simple includes."}],"r":[["7rNY7xKyGCQ",1],["ydqReeTV_vk",1],["f8FAJXPBdOg",1],["ObvxPSQNMGc",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["jAhjPd4uNFY",1],["2o8MDCIlOEk",1],["tMP5Pbx8I4s",1],["FV8n_E_6Tpc",1],["bx3KuE7UjGA",1],["ne99laPUxN4",1],["yvFKh_3evC8",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["ckBfbvOXDvU",1],["n-AbPav5E5M",1],["HCUct4NlxE0",1],["XXBxV6-zamM",1]]},"Uhx-26GfCBU":{"wy":"HN links it whenever someone claims cold or snow rules out cycling, as the concise rebuttal that infrastructure and maintenance decide it.","q":{"x":"winter cycling has nothing to do with the weather, and everything to do with safe cycling infrastructure.","i":27674209,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":38893055,"author":"kiba","month":"2024-01","story":"The Dubious Economics of Deep-Sea Mining","stance":"rec","why":"Described as a good video on the subject.","quote":"Not Just Bike has a good video on it.[1]","text":"Not Just Bike has a good video on it.[1] Of course, if you don't think youtube videos can't ever be credible sources, then I am not going to be able to convince you of anything. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32813284,"author":"hddherman","month":"2022-09","story":"Bikes, not self driving cars, are the technological gateway to urban progress","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of what makes winter bicycling possible.","quote":"properly designed infrastructure and first-class maintenance results in people actually choosing to opt for the bicycle, even during winter.","text":"Not Just Bikes made a great overview of what makes driving a bicycle in the winter possible: [VIDEO] tl;dw: properly designed infrastructure and first-class maintenance results in people actually choosing to opt for the bicycle, even during winter."},{"id":32538770,"author":"eesmith","month":"2022-08","story":"If everyone bicycled like the Danes, we’d avoid a UK’s worth of emissions","stance":"rec","why":"Oulu proves winter is no barrier given safe infrastructure.","quote":"winter cycling has nothing to do with the weather, and everything to do with safe cycling infrastructure.","text":"\"Why Canadians Can't Bike in the Winter (but Finnish people can)\" - [VIDEO] > When talking about bicycle infrastructure in Canada, the number one excuse I hear is \"winter.\" Many Canadians see the cold and snow as a fundamental barrier to year round cycling. But one city, Oulu in Finland, with winter weather worse than most Canadian cities, shows that winter cycling has nothing to do with the weather, and everything to do with safe cycling infrastructure."},{"id":32233413,"author":"sunflowerfly","month":"2022-07","story":"The 15-minute city (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why some places have winter cycling and others don't.","quote":"did a great episode on why some places have winter cycling and others do not","text":"The Not Just Bikes Youtube channel did a great episode on why some places have winter cycling and others do not: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29506392,"author":"Rendello","month":"2021-12","story":"How do you fix a stroad?","stance":"rec","why":"Finnish winter bike infrastructure examples.","quote":"some places in Finland have done a great job.","text":"For anyone interested in what _winter_ biking infrastructure could look like, some places in Finland have done a great job. Not for everyone, but popular nonetheless: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29097352,"author":"danny_codes","month":"2021-11","story":"Waymo will start testing self-driving cars in New York City","stance":"rec","why":"Covers winter biking and US transport policy impact.","quote":"This NotJustBikes video covers this subject nicely.","text":"This NotJustBikes video covers this subject nicely. [VIDEO] Watching his channel has reinforced the dramatically negative impact American transportation policy has had on urban life."},{"id":28985862,"author":"noahtallen","month":"2021-10","story":"Barcelona’s Bicibús: hundreds of families biking to school together","stance":"rec","why":"Biking in snow is fine with proper infrastructure.","quote":"biking in the snow is fine when the infrastructure is equipped to make it an effective mode of transportation.","text":"Here’s a video with more about why biking in the cold and snow isn’t as bad as we make it out to be: [VIDEO] The gist is, like usual, biking in the snow is fine when the infrastructure is equipped to make it an effective mode of transportation. It would suck most places in North America because there is next to no dedicated bike infrastructure besides painted gutters. Those obviously don’t get plowed correctly, so it’s terrible. When the major problems like that are handled, it’s fine."},{"id":27674209,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2021-06","story":"Cities Are Not Loud: Cars Are Loud, and Places Without Cars Are Quiet [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Oulu shows winter cycling depends on infrastructure, not weather.","quote":"winter cycling has nothing to do with the weather, and everything to do with safe cycling infrastructure.","text":"> I’m an avid cyclist, but here in Ottawa for half the year it’s -20C and the roads are treacherous. The Not Just Bikes channel, produced by an ex-pat Canadian, has a video asking why can't people in Canada cycle in the winter if the folks in Finland can? > When talking about bicycle infrastructure in Canada, the number one excuse I hear is \"winter.\" Many Canadians see the cold and snow as a fundamental barrier to year round cycling. But one city, Oulu in Finland, with winter weather worse than most Canadian cities, shows that winter cycling has nothing to do with the weather, and everything to do with safe cycling infrastructure. […] * [VIDEO]"},{"id":26927167,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2021-04","story":"E-Scooters roll into New York","stance":"rec","why":"Safe maintained paths enable cycling in cold places.","quote":"they just have safe paths to ride on, and those paths get maintained.","text":"Can't speak to e-scooters in particular, but cold weather gets cited as a problem for bikes as well, so thought I'd bring this up: for bikes at least it's as much of a question of infrastructure priorities as it is the weather itself. Plenty of people bike in places with much colder weather than New York all the time: [VIDEO] ...they just have safe paths to ride on, and those paths get maintained. (So: not a painted bike lane that all the snow gets shoveled into.)"},{"id":26068077,"author":"ZeroGravitas","month":"2021-02","story":"Don’t Underestimate the Power of a Walk","stance":"rec","why":"Weather matters less than infrastructure for cycling.","quote":"The scene with the line of tiny bikes parked outside the school in the middle of winter is particularly striking.","text":"Weather isn't the biggest determinant of cycling usage (and that probably applies to walking too): \"Why Canadians can't bike in the winter but Finnish people can\" [VIDEO] The scene with the line of tiny bikes parked outside the school in the middle of winter is particularly striking. I've biked through all weathers but even for me that seems like something that wouldn't be possible, but apparently is with a bit of organisation."},{"id":47189737,"author":"Mawr","month":"2026-02","story":"Cardiorespiratory fitness is associated with lower anger and anxiety","stance":"reference","why":"Oulu stands out for infrastructure, which drives cycling.","quote":"infrastructure and its maintenance is clearly the defining factor.","text":"It's utterly fascinating you wrote that and yet could not make the right conclusion. \"In a small town, which is 12x6 miles judging by google maps yet has 590 miles of bike paths.\" + \"not all, as the author of the video himself mentions that Oulu stands out among Finnish cities in this regard\" The right conclusion here is that infrastructure and its maintenance is clearly the defining factor. This really shouldn't be surprising. Consider this: how many trains do you think passed through the areas rail tracks are at before the tracks were built? Or: how many trains need to pass through an area before we can justify the cost of building train tracks there? You simply can't point to just any winter cycling stats without first making sure the infrastructure is there. \"Cycling in winter just ain't working out!\" — no, you literally are not putting in the minimum of effort — \"we've tried nothing and are all out of ideas\" vibes. And here's the kicker: You assumed these statistics are from a city that's a cycling paradise, but I'm willing to bet Oulu is a car-infested shithole, just like all of the Netherlands is. No, I'm not kidding in the slightest. And it's pretty much confirmed in the video the parent linked: [1]. Sure, those areas are as good as it gets at this time, but they're nothing to what things should look like, so since your conclusions are based on faulty assumptions, they are automatically invalid. What these cycling-friendly areas are doing is slowly grinding away at th"},{"id":38192031,"author":"Qwertious","month":"2023-11","story":"Spain lives in flats: why we have built our cities vertically","stance":"reference","why":"Counters claim winter prevents cycling.","quote":"Myth.","text":">On the other hand, for four to six months of the year, most areas of Canada, and even a good portion of the US, definitely don't. Myth. Why Canadians Can't Bike in the Winter (but Finnish people can): [VIDEO]"},{"id":37415024,"author":"kogepathic","month":"2023-09","story":"Norway rolls back EV incentives while boosting walking and cycling","stance":"reference","why":"Oulu residents cycle through winter.","quote":"Somehow the residents of Oulu find a way to cycle in winter","text":"> It's not \"they\", it's winter. Somehow the residents of Oulu find a way to cycle in winter: [VIDEO] > If you don't move, you're frozen. Good thing cycling and walking involve moving."},{"id":37151231,"author":"Gwypaas","month":"2023-08","story":"July 2023 was the hottest month on record","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Or simply build adequate infrastructure.","text":"Or simply build adequate infrastructure. Why Canadians Can't Bike in the Winter (but Finnish people can) [VIDEO]"},{"id":35833963,"author":"menus","month":"2023-05","story":"Squad Solar City Car","stance":"reference","why":"Finns cycle in winter, so Americans could too.","quote":"If Finns can cycle in the winter (1), why can't Americans?","text":"If Finns can cycle in the winter (1), why can't Americans? If it is possible for snowplows can clear giant 14 lane highways, they can clear small 2 lane bike paths 1) [VIDEO] Yes the answer is more complicated due to suburban sprawl and lack of infrastructure, but it is not impossible. It is always possible."},{"id":32457742,"author":"danuker","month":"2022-08","story":"Electric vehicles are way more energy-efficient than internal combustion","stance":"reference","why":"Oulu shows infrastructure shapes how people live in snow.","quote":"Oulu, Finland is an example of how infrastructure defines the way people live","text":"> real humans occasionally need to move a mattress And real humans can occasionally rent a car. Maybe it is not worth holding thousands of dollars' worth of rusting metal for that occasional odd job. As for snow and ice, Oulu, Finland is an example of how infrastructure defines the way people live: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29920359,"author":"stephen_g","month":"2022-01","story":"To stop building heat islands, stop overbuilding parking lots","stance":"reference","why":"Very cold places where many people bike.","quote":"there are very cold places where much of the population happily bikes.","text":"Of course, there are very cold places where much of the population happily bikes. See here for example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28660591,"author":"Symbiote","month":"2021-09","story":"Free Parking Is Killing Cities","stance":"reference","why":"Cold places where cycling is practical.","quote":"there are places with similar weather where it is practical","text":"I assume it's not practical at present, but there are places with similar weather where it is practical: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27671001,"author":"estebank","month":"2021-06","story":"Cities Are Not Loud: Cars Are Loud, and Places Without Cars Are Quiet [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Year-round maintained bike infrastructure regardless of conditions.","quote":"infrastructure for bikes should be well-kept year round, regardless of local conditions[2].","text":"> I’m unwilling to have someone who forgot to put winter tires on slide over me and my bike Part of the argument is that traffic should be segregated[1] and infrastructure for bikes should be well-kept year round, regardless of local conditions[2]. If a car can slide in snow and run you over, a distracted driver can do that on ideal conditions as well. If the roads are not in a ridable condition, that means that bike infrastructure is not being prioritized, usually because it is not considered a valid commuting mechanism. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30760653,"author":"oblio","month":"2022-03","story":"Two-minute battery changes push India’s delivery riders to switch to e-scooters","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Science has proved it!","text":"Agreed! It's impossible to use anything but cars in places with bad weather. Science has proved it! [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["K3lP3BhvnSo",1],["ORzNZUeUHAM",1],["ipVxxxqwBQw",1],["WVDQEoe6ZWY",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1],["jN7mSXMruEo",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["ASXJgvy3mEg",1],["1FXuMs6YRCY",1],["wdgULBpRoXk",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["-ZDZtBRTyeI",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["lBVvnDfW2Xo",1],["7zrx-b2sLUs",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["4j_UjIshzMc",1],["NSaRb-iFwPA",1],["YtfzjehDg74",1]]},"x7cQ3mrcKaY":{"wy":"HN calls it the famous talk for understanding why React rejected separation of concerns by technology and for newcomers puzzled by JSX.","q":{"x":"See Pete Hunt's famous talk","i":9556521,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":23144686,"author":"insin","month":"2020-05","story":"What if we'd had better HTML-in-JS syntax all along?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk that solved problems the commenter struggled with.","quote":"I was totally floored; they'd solved everything I had been trying to do with UI as code and much more","text":"How far back can we go? insin/DOMBuilder was initially based on a 2006 library of the same - but differently-cased - name by Dan Webb ( [LINK] ) and the earliest variant I can recall was Mochikit.DOM ( [LINK] ) By the end of DOMBuilder's life it could generate DOM or HTML from the same code and I'd tried unsuccessfully to implement a way to rehydrate HTML by hooking up events using the same code on the client. Not long after, I happened to be in the audience for Pete Hunt's React: Rethinking best practices talk at JSConf EU ( [VIDEO] ) and I was totally floored; they'd solved everything I had been trying to do with UI as code and much more, and JSX was the icing on the cake, as using nested object/array syntax has always been rife with comma-management hell when you're maintaining UI code."},{"id":16353878,"author":"EdwardMSmith","month":"2018-02","story":"How and why we moved to Vue.js","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great overview of the reasoning behind React.","quote":"gives a great overview of the reasoning behind react","text":"If you’re not sure of the “why” of react, I’ve found that Pete Hunt’s talk “Rethinking Best Practices” gives a great overview of the reasoning behind react. Especially from about 15:15. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32918202,"author":"yCombLinks","month":"2022-09","story":"React I love you, but you're bringing me down","stance":"rec","why":"Early React talk making a good point.","quote":"I think this very early react talk makes a very good point","text":"I actually much prefer how react handles this, I think this very early react talk makes a very good point [VIDEO]"},{"id":14172445,"author":"vmasto","month":"2017-04","story":"An experienced Javascript developer’s account of learning React","stance":"rec","why":"Good starting point for newcomers puzzled by JSX.","quote":"this talk by Pete Hunt is a good start","text":"I was very intrigued by the title: Things nobody will tell you about React.js Immediately had high hopes that someone is going to try and give a well researched, novel critique that maybe can spur out some constructive conversation. And then, sadly, the author goes on to write exactly what countless other articles have already covered and discussed to the death for years now. \"HTML in JS\", \"JSX is bad\", \"Redux is complicated\", \"The tooling is too much\". And then here we are again in the front page of HN for some reason. To me, this is, again, an extremely common case of \"trying to learn everything at once\". Still, a couple of points I'd like to try to mention: JSX: Has been argued about so much it's pretty much useless to try and add anything to this discussion at this point honestly. For whomever likes it, fine, for those who don't, thankfully the JavaScript ecosystem provides with a multitude of alternatives. For new-comers still trying to reason about it, this talk by Pete Hunt is a good start [VIDEO] Redux: > Anytime I work with redux I need to go back to its documentation because its core concepts are so complicate that my mind struggles retrieving them back puking them out as soon as I stop working with it. While I agree that working with Redux at scale can be complicated, the core concepts are extremely simple: Async Action -> Action -> Reducer -> Connect data to your components -> Display -> Async Action -> enter an extremely simple data flow paradigm (flux). (state, "},{"id":11789671,"author":"firasd","month":"2016-05","story":"Thinking in React","stance":"rec","why":"Conceptual intro to React's building views approach.","quote":"","text":"This is a very useful walkthrough. Some links for those who are also interested in \"Thinking in React\" in a more conceptual way (#4 is a presentation from the same person who wrote the walkthrough): 1) React — Basic Theoretical Concepts: “A description of the conceptual model of React without implementation burden.” [LINK] 2) Pure UI: “A lot has been written about the merits of React as a framework. Today I’m compelled to write about the benefits of a programming model it enables and its implications to the design and creation workflow.\" [LINK] 3) Removing User Interface Complexity, or Why React is Awesome: “I’m mostly concerned with the core problems of data binding and componentizing UIs. After much research and usage of the technologies mentioned above, I found React to provide the best solution.” [LINK] 4) Pete Hunt: React: Rethinking best practices (youtube): “building views with JavaScript, ‘re-​rendering’ your entire application when your data changes, and a lightweight implementation of the DOM and events.” [VIDEO] 5) Nothing new in React and Flux except one thing: “What is it that makes React so innovative and compelling?… The Virtual DOM allows us to convert a mutable retained mode graphics API to an immutable functional immediate mode API.” [LINK] (I collected them to go along with something I wrote in the same vein: [LINK] )"},{"id":9556521,"author":"ChrisGaudreau","month":"2015-05","story":"How I learned to stop worrying and love React","stance":"rec","why":"Famous talk distinguishing concerns from technologies.","quote":"See Pete Hunt's famous talk","text":"Separation of concerns and separation of technologies are a different thing. It would appear you haven't done your research. See Pete Hunt's famous talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8725870,"author":"paulshen","month":"2014-12","story":"Build with React – ReactJS articles and tutorials","stance":"rec","why":"","quote":"Check out or this JSConf talk","text":"You're definitely not the only one. [LINK] as well as other HN comment threads. Check out [LINK] or this JSConf talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":8407351,"author":"munro","month":"2014-10","story":"Mistakes AngularJS Developers Make","stance":"rec","why":"Great React video on organizing by concept rather than technology.","quote":"There's a great React video on this concept, as well as some other good stuff!","text":"#1 -- Separating by concerns is much preferable to me, organizing by controllers/directives/services/... is a separation of technology, not the high level concept. There's a great React video on this concept, as well as some other good stuff! [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7421374,"author":"versusdotcom","month":"2014-03","story":"Angular 2.0","stance":"rec","why":"React founder points to weaknesses of Angular.","quote":"Here a pretty good video of React's founder pointing on some weakness of Angular","text":"Totally agree and also recommend to look at React. While Angular is just way too much of everything React is pretty much thought-out: Here a quick comparison of both: [LINK] Here a pretty good video of React's founder pointing on some weakness of Angular: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43855051,"author":"code_biologist","month":"2025-05","story":"All four major web browsers are about to lose 80% of their funding","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"explaining in 2013 why the styling separation of concerns for document display doesn't make sense for applications","text":"I understand the sentiment, but disregarding the nuance does the situation a disservice. HTML/CSS serves two separate but related use cases: a document layout and display language, and as a display layer for applications. I remember Pete Hunt's talk \"React: Rethinking best practices\" [1] explaining in 2013 why the styling separation of concerns for document display doesn't make sense for applications. Has opinion on best practice se-sawed back and forth? No, we've merely gone from web content being document centered to being massively application centered, and the discourse on best practices follows that proportional shift. Would it be better if there was a different web application display technology, not retrofitted on top of HTML/CSS? Like maybe, but HTML/CSS is... fine. Even separated from the success of Javascript, it's an archetypal example of \"worse is better\" [2] leading to market success. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":40033681,"author":"whoknowsidont","month":"2024-04","story":"Interview with Senior JavaScript Developer 2024 [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here's Pete Hunt talking about static webpages with React, in a best practices talk from 2013","text":"Your quote doesn't state anything to support what the OP said. It doesn't even say what you said it says. Here's Pete Hunt talking about static webpages with React, in a best practices talk from 2013: [VIDEO] There is no need to imbue meaning into quotes or history when there is a clear, well defined timeline with striking examples of how React was used to generate static website all the way from its initial release."},{"id":29323158,"author":"acemarke","month":"2021-11","story":"Remix – A framework focused on web fundamentals and modern UX","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It seems like you're conflating a few different concerns here: - JSX as a paradigm for defining component display output - Drag and drop capability for UI design - How web apps are built tooling-wise - The actual display layer being used (HTML, canvas, etc, or at least that's what I think I'm reading) - Whether the browser is where these apps run at all (?) Those are all entirely different questions and concerns. Which of these are you actually asking about? JSX has been argued about since React was first introduced [0]. It exists because React tries to stay \"just JS\" and use JS logic for flow control, rather than having a completely separate templating layer. Some people love that, some people hate it. Drag and drop for laying out an app exists - there's multiple projects out there that allow a very VB-like GUI design experience for React components. Modern JS build tooling exists to solve the multiple problems I listed earlier: insufficient standard library, building full-size apps with tens of thousands of SLOC and large teams, and optimizing deployment bundle and asset sizes. HTML and CSS are the native language of browsers. You can certainly use canvas/WebGL to draw pixels on screen (and that's what \"compile-native-desktop-to-WASM\" demos do), but you lose all the built-in accessibility of HTML, and still have to reinvent all the widgets from scratch. As for \"breaking free of browser constraints and web fundamentals\"... well, we _are_ talking about still delivering code t"},{"id":26134801,"author":"smhg","month":"2021-02","story":"The complexity that lives in the GUI","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Around 20:00 in that video he compares React with the Doom3 engine.","text":"> There is also another way of making GUIs called Immediate Mode that is commonly used for drawing user interfaces in games. In this mode the GUI components are no longer subscribing and waiting for events to come, but are instead a part of the main loop that runs at 60 fps and re-render themselves based on the current “global” state. > Immediate Mode GUIs somehow never reached the critical mass and you are probably not going to find it outside of the games industry. Isn't this what React is built on? I think this was part of the 'original' selling point by Pete Hunt: [VIDEO] (2013). Around 20:00 in that video he compares React with the Doom3 engine."},{"id":24981252,"author":"antris","month":"2020-11","story":"React Fundamentals: What I’m Learning","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Pete Hunt from Facebook admits that \"we weren't communicating what we were doing\".","text":"Yes I agree, React broke that mould and web apps are better for it. But it was controversial. The React team even got ridiculed early on: [VIDEO] And the person who wrote that tweet wasn't alone. It's perhaps easy to forget how much opposition there was to React. Facebook was quite a bit further than many in the industry, and Pete Hunt from Facebook admits that \"we weren't communicating what we were doing\". It was a bit much to swallow for many at first, but in the end, the VDOM was such a powerful idea that React was destined to take off sooner or later."},{"id":23144405,"author":"hombre_fatal","month":"2020-05","story":"What if we'd had better HTML-in-JS syntax all along?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"where he makes the case against the \"separation of concerns\" cargo cult.","text":"Link? You might be misremembering Pete Hunt's talk about React's design decisions: [VIDEO] where he makes the case against the \"separation of concerns\" cargo cult."},{"id":21348062,"author":"PKop","month":"2019-10","story":"React Concurrent Mode","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Rethinking best practices absolutely addressed misapplication of separation of concerns.","text":"Rethinking best practices absolutely addressed misapplication of separation of concerns. [VIDEO] Components have always had JSX markup along with data manipulation logic. CSS in JS and styling co-located with component extended this consolidation of cross-cutting concerns. These, along with not manually updating DOM state as you mention, all fall under declarative aspects of React.. which concurrent rendering and Suspense are consistent with and essentially an extension of."},{"id":17467008,"author":"DougBTX","month":"2018-07","story":"Vue.js: the good, the meh, and the ugly","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it boils down to asking why client side development isn't like like HTML templating","text":"React was advertised from the start as the V in MV-whatever, so that seems like an overly restrictive definition of \"idiomatic\". > At which point, JSX is basically just another fancy HTML templating library. JSX is optional syntax sugar; my team uses React and Redux heavily, but we've opted not to use it. HTML templating is great actually, all the rendering is data -> UI. If you go back to the early Rethinking Best Practices presentation[0], it boils down to asking why client side development isn't like like HTML templating. The answer is that updating the entire page DOM on every change is unworkable, there needs to be support for partial updates. That's what React offers, a return to the data -> UI paradigm, without having to write a secondary code path for partial updates. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13153735,"author":"kentor","month":"2016-12","story":"Why we chose Vue.js over React","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"points out how separating templates and logic is merely a separation of technologies, not concerns","text":"Yes and this is a tired argument by now. The talk by Pete Hunt aptly titled \"React: Rethinking best practices\"[0] given in 2013 points out how separating templates and logic is merely a separation of technologies, not concerns. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11216408,"author":"MalcolmDiggs","month":"2016-03","story":"Ask HN: Long held tech taboos being broken?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"When React was released it pushed against some long-held (and still widely held) beliefs about separation of concerns","text":"When React was released it pushed against some long-held (and still widely held) beliefs about separation of concerns, etc. [VIDEO] Years later, it's still a major point of contention, and a bit of a taboo."},{"id":7701219,"author":"danabramov","month":"2014-05","story":"The Case for React.js and ClojureScript","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Pete Hunt talks about this in Rethinking Best Practices video[1].","text":"Pete Hunt talks about this in Rethinking Best Practices video[1]. This is one concern, not two. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["_P9HqHVPeik",1],["WUvTyaaNkzM",1]]},"2Vrhk5OjBP8":{"wy":"HN considers it the best experimental rebuttal and clarification of the Veritasium wire debate, with real measurements.","q":{"x":"Yeah I really liked the AlphaPheonix video[0] where he actually built the thing in a field and explained the entire effect properly.","i":38780364,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":40887462,"author":"reaperman","month":"2024-07","story":"Mechanical computer relies on kirigami cubes, not electronics","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Experiment measuring the speed of electricity","quote":"AlphaPhoenix did an amazing experiment to measure the speed of electricity FWIW.","text":"[VIDEO] AlphaPhoenix did an amazing experiment to measure the speed of electricity FWIW. His other videos are incredible as well and explain EM physics in an absolutely outstanding way."},{"id":38780364,"author":"moffkalast","month":"2023-12","story":"Veritasium: The SAT Question Everyone Got Wrong [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Built the experiment in a field and explained the effect properly","quote":"Yeah I really liked the AlphaPheonix video[0] where he actually built the thing in a field and explained the entire effect properly.","text":"Yeah I really liked the AlphaPheonix video[0] where he actually built the thing in a field and explained the entire effect properly. I'm not entirely sure if Derek didn't really understand what he was presenting in that video or deliberately based it on a really misleading bullshit gotcha to troll people and stir debate for clicks, and neither reflects very well on him. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37638444,"author":"jerf","month":"2023-09","story":"How the Most Useless Branch of Math Could Save Your Life","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best rebuttal to the Veritasium wire video, with a real experiment","quote":"the best \"bunking\" video, if I may call it that, is probably AlphaPheonix's","text":"The video in question for reference: [LINK] Interestingly, when I saw that I figured this channel for a goner because he was going to turn to the darkside, but quite contrary to expectations I feel like his channel has actually blossomed since then. His older videos are fine, but often just explaining concepts that you can find a lot of other explanations on, but some of his recent videos have rocked the boat even among physicists and IMHO he was ultimately proved to be correct for them. I particularly point at his videos about how nobody has measured the one-way speed of light ( [LINK] ), which was well-known among people very very deep in the field of relativity but not very well known outside of that (even amongst physicists in other fields, let alone lay people) and the electricity videos he's done in the last year ( [LINK] , [LINK] ) which attracted a whole whackload of \"debunking\" videos, which were themselves wrong (the best \"bunking\" video, if I may call it that, is probably AlphaPheonix's: [VIDEO] , since he actually set up the experiment, albeit on a smaller scale). They aren't all of that caliber, but the knot theory video was very good too and not something I've seen a hundred times elsewhere, and the airships video that preceded that one clearly had a high degree of research put into it beyond just the hype."},{"id":47827472,"author":"sbuttgereit","month":"2026-04","story":"Any Color You Like: NIST Scientists Create 'Any Wavelength' Lasers","stance":"rec","why":"Experiment on electricity propagation with good comment discussion","quote":"Good discussion in the comments there as well.","text":"Looking at the discussion below this comment, I'd just add this video by AlphaPheonix: [VIDEO] Good discussion in the comments there as well."},{"id":43151809,"author":"Spivak","month":"2025-02","story":"But good sir, what is electricity?","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstration of electrical signal propagation speed","quote":"you can see one of the best people do a demonstration of it","text":"Yes, you can see one of the best people do a demonstration of it [VIDEO] It's close enough to c that you should just use c but it can be observed that it's less."},{"id":36920346,"author":"Manuel_D","month":"2023-07","story":"U.S. Marine Corps Antenna Handbook (1999) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Actual test showing energy mostly takes the wire path","quote":"Someone actually went out and tested this","text":"Not really in the intuitive sense: [LINK] Someone actually went out and tested this: [VIDEO] The electric field is very tightly constrained around the wire. The amount of energy that reaches the light through the gap is incredibly small, not nearly enough to turn on a real light bulb. The bulk of the energy does indeed have to take the path through the wire. I find that video usually leads people to an even less correct understanding than the marbles-in-a-tube analogy. The Veritasum video can essentially be summarized, \"fun fact: two parallel wires act like an antenna, and an extremely small amount of energy reaches the other end before the rest of the energy takes the long path through the wire."},{"id":35607664,"author":"bertil","month":"2023-04","story":"How do electrons find the “Path of Least Resistance”? [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies how electricity moves in circuits as part of creator series","quote":"The author has a fascinating video","text":"The author has a fascinating video (part of a wider effort of many science YouTubers) that tries to clarify all that. [VIDEO] I’m actually not sure if that makes his explanation more accurate because it’s informed by that, or more regrettably forced metaphor. But the whole series of videos from different creators is worth watching if you want to understand the whole “electrons are moving” thing."},{"id":29923728,"author":"pja","month":"2022-01","story":"5.5 mm in 1.25 nanoseconds","stance":"rec","why":"Small-scale demonstration of the effect with an oscilloscope","quote":"Take a look at the video made by YouTuber AlphaPhoenix where he constructs the entire thing on a smaller scale","text":"His presentation is not very clear & imo the way he answers the question is not actually helpful in actually understanding what’s going on, but he is correct that current will flow in the far side almost immediately. As is made clearer in the documents he shared which contain his discussions with actual physicists, that current will be much smaller than the final current that flows all the way round the circuit, but it is there nevertheless. (In fact that current will initially flow whether or not the two halves of the flat ring are connected - if you think about it things have to work like this, because the middle of the system cannot “know” whether the far ends are connected until light has had time to get there & back. Relativity is absolute.) Take a look at the video made by YouTuber AlphaPhoenix where he constructs the entire thing on a smaller scale and demonstrates the effect with an oscilloscope: [VIDEO] One way of viewing what’s going on here is to consider the two sides as a pair of large dipole antennas. When the switch (or in AlphaPheonix’s case, a transistor) is turned on, a pulse of electrons is pushed down one antenna, setting up a transient wave in the electromagnetic field around it which induces a current in the opposite direction in the “receiving” antenna on the other side. This happens regardless of whether the wired are connected in a loop or not! Physics is great."},{"id":49107452,"author":"perpetuallunch","month":"2026-07","story":"A.I. Companies Are Recruiting Electricians and Carpenters by the Thousands","stance":"reference","why":"AlphaPhoenix's response to Veritasium","quote":"AlphaPhoenix’s response to Veritasium","text":"One cannot simply bring up Veritasium without also honouring his arch nemesis ElectroBOOM. [LINK] Others that deserve a mention: styropyro [LINK] The Angry Pixie herder, Uncle Bumblefuck aka AvE: [LINK] And that guy with the multi gigawatt 150,000 volt Lorentz Cannon: [LINK] I’m sure I forgot someone? AlphaPhoenix’s response to Veritasium: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41558247,"author":"kragen","month":"2024-09","story":"How to succeed in MrBeast production (Leaked PDF)","stance":"reference","why":"Experimental results showing a more complicated signal than a single delay","quote":"the result is more complicated than just a single time delay","text":"well, the truth turned out to be quite complicated; see also [VIDEO] and [LINK] . the result is more complicated than just a single time delay, and the details of the experimental setup (like the wire diameter! and load impedance relative to transmission-line pulse impedance) matter a lot. in alphaphoenix's first experiment, putting 5 volts into a kilometer of wire, he got 0.2 volts across his resistor immediately, then after about 1.6μs, a jump up to about 2 volts, and then a gradual rise to 1.7 volts, some overshoot peaking after another 1.6μs, and then settling back down to the 1.7-volt level (derek's results in the linked video, which incidentally links to one of the two i linked above, were quantitatively different but qualitatively similar) the really unintuitive thing about this i think is that people think of electrical energy as flowing inside wires, when actually almost all of it flows around the wires, as veritasium explained quite ably. this is something people doing high-speed pcb layout have to deal with a lot in order to avoid emi problems as i understand it, derek has a ph.d. in physics, or actually in physics education research [LINK] . that doesn't mean he knows everything about physics, but generally my experience with people in ph.d. programs is that they're good at listening to counterarguments and admitting when they're wrong, and also seeking out experts before publishing (i think the veritasium video does contain a minor error in that it says electrons"},{"id":33679174,"author":"voidwtf","month":"2022-11","story":"Computer proof ‘blows up’ centuries-old fluid equations","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the discussion that arose between AlphaPhoenix and Veritasium YouTube channels concerning the speed of electrons along a closed loop of wire","text":"This sounds similar in some ways to the discussion that arose between AlphaPhoenix and Veritasium YouTube channels concerning the speed of electrons along a closed loop of wire. [VIDEO] With a relevant illustration at 9:41 at where I think these topics intersect conceptually."},{"id":30561720,"author":"autokad","month":"2022-03","story":"We're building computers wrong [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I believe this is the same video, the author theorized (probably correctly) that it was because there was residual 'power' on the wire, as it acts like a giant capacitor, among other things. this is because the wire that immediately showed up was extremely small and dissipated very quickly. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29672786,"author":"YetAnotherNick","month":"2021-12","story":"Electricity Is Magnetism","stance":"reference","why":"Km-long wire experiment measured with oscilloscope","quote":"This guy performed the same experiment using a km long wire and measured the voltage in oscilloscope.","text":"This guy performed the same experiment using a km long wire and measured the voltage in oscilloscope. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vs41JrnGaxc",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["t-O-Qdh7VvQ",1],["oI_X2cMHNe0",1],["XEVlyP4_11M",1],["bvim4rsNHkQ",1],["yY9GAyJtuJ0",1],["bHIhgxav9LY",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["r6Rp-uo6HmI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["gB9n2gHsHN4",1],["bL6hp8BKB24",1],["CaasbfdJdJg",1],["yL_-1d9OSdk",1],["kPRA0W1kECg",1],["cefnmjtAolY",1],["ar9WRwCiSr0",1],["GFMyMxMYDNk",1]]},"l7rce6IQDWs":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear, from-scratch look at how video hardware works, understandable with just basic logic knowledge.","q":{"x":"Anyone with a basic understanding of logic circuits has enough background to understand it. Worth a watch.","i":22330486,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22077951,"author":"Diederich","month":"2020-01","story":"Show HN: 1.5kb Microkernel for Ben Eater's 6502 Computer","stance":"strong_rec","why":"How VGA signals work and timing circuit.","quote":"His stuff is just fantastic.","text":"I'm actually more excited to see a new Ben Eater video on youtube than anything else. His stuff is just fantastic. Even though it's lower level than I've ever worked, it's extremely entertaining and educational. His 6502 series is just great, but I want to highlight another couple of videos he did: \"Let's build a circuit that displays an image on a VGA monitor! In this video, I talk about how VGA signals work and build a circuit that provides the correct timing of sync signals so that a monitor recognizes the signal.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":20854082,"author":"dimator","month":"2019-09","story":"CPU Made from 74HC Chips Is a Glorious Mess","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Building VGA display from breadboard circuit.","quote":"He builds up to it in his usual amazing teaching style, highly recommend","text":"Ben Eater did a video on driving a VGA display using a breaboard circuit. He builds up to it in his usual amazing teaching style, highly recommend: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27429954,"author":"caymanjim","month":"2021-06","story":"An interactive look at how a video signal is made","stance":"rec","why":"Video card from gates and how VGA is generated.","quote":"Ben Eater has a great video[1] about building a video card from simple gates and other basic components.","text":"Ben Eater has a great video[1] about building a video card from simple gates and other basic components. It goes into some detail about how a VGA signal is generated. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27194474,"author":"fowl2","month":"2021-05","story":"The Graphics Gremlin – An Open Source Retro ISA Video Card","stance":"rec","why":"Graphics card on breadboard with discrete logic.","quote":"Fascinating.","text":"Even further to that end of the spectrum is \"The World's Worst Graphics Card\"[1] on a breadboard with mostly discrete logic. Fascinating. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25288636,"author":"vishnuharidas","month":"2020-12","story":"NandGame – Build a Computer from Scratch","stance":"rec","why":"Video card from ICs on a breadboard.","quote":"He made a video-card using ICs on a breadboard that fascinated me","text":"Reminds me of Ben Eater's Youtube videos where he builds everything using basic gates on a breadboard. He made a video-card using ICs on a breadboard that fascinated me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24661764,"author":"el_oni","month":"2020-10","story":"A trip through the Graphics Pipeline (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Fundamental graphics card on a breadboard.","quote":"Maybe something like Ben Eater's video card series?","text":"Maybe something like Ben Eater's video card series? [VIDEO] If i remember correctly he assembles a gfx card on a bread board. So its fairly fundamental"},{"id":24662852,"author":"dahart","month":"2020-10","story":"A trip through the Graphics Pipeline (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Framebuffer display built from scratch on breadboard.","quote":"you’ll understand what every transistor does at a high level in an hour","text":"This one might be interesting to you: [VIDEO] “The world’s worst video card?” Ben builds a framebuffer display device on a bread board from scratch, you’ll understand what every transistor does at a high level in an hour (don’t miss part 2). That is abstractly what first principles graphics looked like some time ago - pixels were represented by a block of main memory, and the video system simply scanned that memory at the same frequency as the CRT monitor’s beam. I’d say it’s pretty important to remember that what counts as “first principles” graphics has changed over time, and there was a time before frame buffers (the first Pong game was displayed on an oscilloscope) and the way we use framebuffers today is completely different - there is no more direct access to pixels in the same way old terminals used to work. The article here is detailing how we use a higher level API to draw higher level primitives, and the hardware takes care of the pixels. I’d love to hear a little more about your goal... what’s driving you to study the old terminals?"},{"id":22330486,"author":"MarioMan","month":"2020-02","story":"Brown: color is weird [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Building a VGA video card understandable with basic logic.","quote":"Anyone with a basic understanding of logic circuits has enough background to understand it. Worth a watch.","text":"Not the same thing, but you reminded me of this since it's in a similar vein. Ben Eater went through the entire process of building a VGA \"video card.\" Anyone with a basic understanding of logic circuits has enough background to understand it. Worth a watch. Part 1: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK]"},{"id":42319934,"author":"tetha","month":"2024-12","story":"Show HN: My C compiler compiled itself","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates how messy breadboarding gets.","quote":"","text":"Back in university, we took the steps of: - Breadboarding some CPU components - Breadboarding a very, very minimal CPU. More like a simple Adding + Multiplication machine with 4 registers or so, with a few premade components. - Eventually moving up to microcoding a simulated CPU - Eventually writing binary code to control the microcoded CPU - At that point I kinda cheated and wrote my own assembler because I got sick of checking so many bits. - This project then stopped at an assembly level. - But then we implemented our own ML-variant interpreter and later on ML-variant compiler in OCaml - And later on we had that ML-Compiler compile itself and extended it from there. Pick your poison where to start. Just be aware that breadboarding stuff becomes very... messy very quickly[1]. And I do include Hardware in this, because you needed simpler CPUs to design more complex CPUs. In practice, you want to pick the best combination of a familiar language that is as high level as it can be. But back in the day, that would've been assembly, binary. In the case of C, it went from BCPL, in which a compiler for B was written, in which a compiler for New B (NB) was written, which then turned into C. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41600543,"author":"bonoboTP","month":"2024-09","story":"The Cheating Device (ChatGPT on a TI-84) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Example of calm technical video style.","quote":"Compare it with Ben Eater building a video card","text":"> I’m not quite sure what you mean by YouTubeisms The unending barrage of memes, the animations, floating emojis, the music constantly changing pace, the bright colors, \"Youtube-voice\", etc. etc. Compare it with Ben Eater building a video card: [VIDEO] (of course the level of detail is higher but just focus on the style, voice, lack of animations and memes. He cracks a joke here and there, but isn't full of Youtubeisms.) And yes, it's a different genre. And yes, some people hate the genre that youtube is filled with these days."},{"id":35073671,"author":"johannes1234321","month":"2023-03","story":"Appler: Apple ][ emulator for IBM PC, written in 8088 assembly","stance":"reference","why":"VGA graphics card build with resistor calculations.","quote":"Ben Eater's video series on building a VGA graphics card.","text":"Some what related: Ben Eater's video series on building a VGA graphics card. In there he calculates the resistors to show colors. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32841750,"author":"lozenge","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: How do I write a Linux driver for my 15 year old photo printer?","stance":"reference","why":"Using chips and breadboards to meet protocols.","quote":"","text":"Ben Eater has some videos showing the USB protocol on an oscilloscope. [LINK] [LINK] And some videos on using chips and breadboards to meet protocols- [VIDEO] And a digital protocol too- [LINK]"},{"id":26134153,"author":"ChuckNorris89","month":"2021-02","story":"Nyuzi – An Experimental Open-Source FPGA GPGPU Processor","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a hobby GPU built for education.","quote":"","text":"To clarify further, Nvidia and AMD (and probably other small players like ARM, Quallcomm, Imagination) own the patents on core shader tech, which are the building blocks of any modern GPU design. If you want to design a GPU IP that works around all their patents, you probably can, but unless you're a John Carmack x10, your resulting design would be horribly inefficient and not competitive enough to be worth the expensive silicon it will be etched on and probably not compatible to any modern API like Vulcan or DirectX. But if you just want to build your own meme GPU for education/shits and giggles, that doesn't follow any patents or APIs, then you can and some people already did: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24824592,"author":"cromwellian","month":"2020-10","story":"Why Apple II Is the World's Best Selling Personal Computer (1978)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows building video hardware from TTL chips.","quote":"Here, learn to built one yourself","text":"> Chips that didn't exist in 1978. The overall point you're making is that technology from several years later was better. Not relevant. Years later, even the Apple IIc (1984) wasn't close to a C64, not to mention an Amiga. The thing you're missing is, Commodore had purchased MOS Technology, just like Apple purchased PA Semi to make their own iPhone SoCs, and had spent years investing in custom silicon going back to the VIC in 1979. The Apple II did not have a \"video chip\". Wozniak had cobbled together a video chip out of a bunch of 74LS TTL chips. Here, learn to built one yourself: [VIDEO] That's what I meant by \"superior design\". Commodore actually had integrated CMOS chips for video, audio, I/O, that they had designed in house and fabbed themselves. The Apple 2, well into the IIc days, was built like a semi-decent Arduino project. Even the Mac had gfx and DMA cobbled out of TTL chips."}],"r":[["aircAruvnKk",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["WrxZ4AZPdOQ",1],["LUFp6sjKbkE",1],["0Rnq1NpHdmw",1],["hQVTIJBZook",1],["1-dUkyn_fZA",1],["vT1JzLTH4G4",1],["k_5M-z_RUKA",1],["Q1lL-hXO27Q",1],["HI09kat_GeI",1],["kTKn1aSOyOs",1],["AcS3NOQnsQM",1],["ZXtimhT-ff4",1],["frAEmhqdLFs",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["t_rzYnXEQlE",1],["uqY3FMuMuRo",1]]},"BFld4EBO2RE":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as the best demonstration of procedural graphics from a master, accessible to beginners yet deep enough for advanced viewers.","q":{"x":"My favorite video of his is Painting a Landscape with Maths.","i":37105168,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37105168,"author":"fasterik","month":"2023-08","story":"OP drew these corals with mathematical equations","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Quilez paints a landscape using only math","quote":"My favorite video of his is Painting a Landscape with Maths.","text":"Indeed, Inigo Quilez is \"the guy\" for this kind of stuff. He co-created ShaderToy and has worked on effects and modeling for multiple Pixar films. My favorite video of his is Painting a Landscape with Maths. [VIDEO] He deserves to have at least as many subscribers as other math YouTube channels like Verisatium and 3Blue1Brown."},{"id":42590568,"author":"thewanderer1983","month":"2025-01","story":"VoxelSpace: Terrain rendering algorithm in less than 20 lines of code (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Quilez's landscape rendering with shader code","quote":"you should check out Inigo Quilez","text":">It looks really good for such a simple algorithm. I love this! If you like that, you should check out Inigo Quilez [VIDEO] Here is the shadertoy link with code [LINK]"},{"id":41350496,"author":"tombh","month":"2024-08","story":"Programming terrain from scratch using C++ and OpenGL by Shamus Young (2006)","stance":"rec","why":"Live coding of a ray-marched landscape using only math in a shader","quote":"I just finished watching a live-coding session (from 2 years ago) by Inigo Quilez (ex Pixar)","text":"I just finished watching a live-coding session (from 2 years ago) by Inigo Quilez (ex Pixar), where he constructs an entire ray-marched landscape using only maths (mostly SDFs) in pure GPU shader code. The stream: [VIDEO] The demo: [LINK]"},{"id":38036460,"author":"sph","month":"2023-10","story":"Interactive Intro to Shaders","stance":"rec","why":"Showcase of what masters can do with shaders","quote":"let me introduce you to Inigo Quilez and his shader art","text":"Welcome to the Internet, superMayo! If you want to see what the Masters can do with shaders, let me introduce you to Inigo Quilez and his shader art: [VIDEO] EDIT: I did not notice you are the author of this article. It's very well done, and I've been looking for more approachable and interactive tutorials on the arts of shader coding."},{"id":37530172,"author":"antegamisou","month":"2023-09","story":"Painting with Math: A Gentle Study of Raymarching","stance":"rec","why":"Companion video on painting a landscape with maths","quote":"be sure to check out, as the author cites, Inigo Quilez's videos Painting a Landscape with Maths -","text":"Good to see high quality articles gaining traction. If the article looks too verbose, be sure to check out, as the author cites, Inigo Quilez's videos Painting a Landscape with Maths - [VIDEO] Painting a Character with Maths - [LINK] Bonus: Painting with Maths in Google Sheets - [LINK]"},{"id":31637408,"author":"djmips","month":"2022-06","story":"Source code for 4kb demoscene production “Elevated” released (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Advanced instructional video on procedural graphics from the author of Elevated","quote":"Inigo Quilez has many instructional videos on his channel in how to code procedural graphics from the very beginner to the advanced.","text":"Thankfully for the graphics the very person who did the graphics in this Elevated demo is very generous with their time. Inigo Quilez has many instructional videos on his channel in how to code procedural graphics from the very beginner to the advanced. I link a couple of examples. You can watch the more advanced video even as a beginner but trying yourself is easier at the beginner level. Beginner. [LINK] Advanced. [VIDEO] Website. [LINK]"},{"id":31235637,"author":"lifthrasiir","month":"2022-05","story":"Making graphics in 4 kilobytes (2008) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth videos by Quilez, more accessible than slides","quote":"Inigo Quilez, the author of this slide, also has made in-depth videos [1] [2]","text":"Inigo Quilez, the author of this slide, also has made in-depth videos [1] [2] which might be more accessible to who aren't familiar with shader languages. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38826339,"author":"zengid","month":"2023-12","story":"Genuary 2024: Generative Art / Creative Coding Month","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Edit: Hey my bad the prompts for Genuary are all for making visuals, but I suppose they could be interpreted for audio too. My bad, but I'll leave my music-nerd rant below in tact in case anyone finds it useful ---------------- This is awesome, I didn't know about this but I've been subconsciously preparing for it over Christmas break. Here's what I've found useful: Music: - Eli Fieldsteel's intro to SuperCollider course is fantastic. SuperCollider language is kind of nice. Function literals are simply curly braces, `{}`, and the last expression is returned. It has pipe's for function arguments, so if you squint it kind of feels like Ruby or Rust. // a silly function. Note that paren \"blocks\" make evaluating multiple lines easier ( x = {|num| num.squared + 42 }; y = x.(0); ) If you've only ever used JavaScript, there's a lot of \"wtf is this shit\" moments, but otherwise it's really nice. I feel like SCLang is super powerful and want to use it for some projects. Here's Eli's course playlist: [LINK] - Sonic Pi is built on-top of SuperCollider, but it's MUCH easier to get started with making bleeps and bloops. Sam Aaron, who originally created Overtone (a Clojure front-end for SuperCollider) created Sonic Pi initially to teach kids computer programming and music, but now it's turning into a pretty nice live-coding setup. The language is basically a DSL extension of Ruby, and although it's very elegant, I feel like it's a little nerfed in terms of a full language when compared to "},{"id":36378981,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2023-06","story":"Infinite Photorealistic Worlds Using Procedural Generation","stance":"reference","why":"Example of art generated by math without assets","quote":"","text":"Could you define \"assets\", please? What would you consider the assets in these videos by Inigo Quillez, for example? [VIDEO] [LINK]"}],"r":[["8--5LwHRhjk",2],["Kvsvd67XUKw",1],["xUezfuy8Qpc",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["ia8Q51ouA_s",1],["zBgCRJluWTc",1],["jpNU72dny2s",1],["mb3uK-_QkOo",1],["RTB5XhjbgZA",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["8k2AbqTBxao",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["SqayDnQ2wmw",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["w70Xc9CStoE",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["bRCNenhcvpw",1],["pSHj5UKSylk",1]]},"T71ibcZAX3I":{"wy":"HN points people to it as the clearest single video on simple, long-term index fund investing.","q":{"x":"This video is probably the most clear resource I can think of that has all the information in one place.","i":28718427,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28718427,"author":"f0e4c2f7","month":"2021-10","story":"Redditors are right about the unfairness of the market","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clearest single resource on index fund investing","quote":"This video is probably the most clear resource I can think of that has all the information in one place.","text":"> buy real things that appreciate over time Companies are real things that appreciate over time. Stock is ownership of that. I wouldn't characterize it as a scam exactly. I do think people underestimate how clever markets are and would advise against trying to outsmart them. There is a different kind of bet you can make which is to buy a small part of the entire US economy on the assumption that it will continue to grow during your lifetime. Thats what an index fund is. It's a boring bet with modest returns. A lot has been written about index funds. This video is probably the most clear resource I can think of that has all the information in one place. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46390514,"author":"qnleigh","month":"2025-12","story":"Ask HN: What skills do you want to develop or improve in 2026?","stance":"rec","why":"Case that index funds beat active trading","quote":"Here's a good video that makes a case for this.","text":"I'm not downvoting you, but I think you are probably getting downvoted because \"Stock trading can be quite lucrative\" is statistically just not true. Even professional financial advisors almost never beat the market [0]. Only hedge funds that pay for huge amounts of priveledged financial information do, and even then not always. Here's a good video that makes a case for this. Even if you don't agree, you might find some of the points he makes interesting. But tl;dr, he argues that index funds basically always outperform other methods, so one should primarily invest in things like that. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31564971,"author":"ggop","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: How to invest savings?","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to simple long-term investment rules","quote":"Good introduction","text":"JL Collins has some good advice. Unbiased and simple to follow rules for long term investment: [LINK] Good introduction: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27659397,"author":"murm","month":"2021-06","story":"How do I start investing?","stance":"rec","why":"Simple explanation of retirement investing with index funds","quote":"JL Collins lays out how to invest for your retirement using index funds, explained in simple and commonsensical terms.","text":"I think this video[0] fits nicely to your situation. JL Collins lays out how to invest for your retirement using index funds, explained in simple and commonsensical terms. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26430084,"author":"mister_tee","month":"2021-03","story":"The Joy of Fuck-You Money (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"One-hour video intro to JL Collins's investing ideas","quote":"Or for a video intro, he gave this 1hr Talk@Google","text":"JL Collins was I think my introduction to the financial independence concept though I'd \"accidentally\" been investing in index funds and for retirment before. That series of posts (the \"Stock Series\") is great and it's available in edited book form called \"The Simple Path to Wealth.\" Or for a video intro, he gave this 1hr Talk@Google: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21904863,"author":"caro_douglos","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: How do you consume noisy financial news?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on not watching markets too closely","quote":"JL Collins [1] has a great talk at Google","text":"JL Collins [1] has a great talk at Google I discovered while perusing youtube[2]. He taps into Warren's advice of not watching the markets too closely (mentioned in your second link). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21582661,"author":"patrickk","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: What's the most valuable thing you can learn in an hour?","stance":"rec","why":"Covers the basics of the simple path to wealth","quote":"this talk covers the basics","text":"Reddit FIRE is also worth reading: [LINK] Also JL Collins' book \"The Simple Path to Wealth\" is well worth a read, on the same topic. If you don't want to get it, this talk covers the basics: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PTUY16CkS-k",1],["gmn7tjSNyAA",1],["np_ylvc8Zj8",1],["RxabLA7UQ9k",1],["Kxryv2XrnqM",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["BDqvzFY72mg",1],["GOGru_4z1Vc",1],["taZJblMAuko",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["XRr1kaXKBsU",1],["8YUWDrLazCg",1]]},"-fny99f8amM":{"wy":"Strongly and repeatedly recommended as a visually distinctive documentary and a guide to postmodern ideas on politics, truth and technology.","q":{"x":"he's essentially cross cut the last 40 years into a 3 hour cliff notes guide to some of the darker corners of postmodern philosophy.","i":13263201,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":24467447,"author":"onion2k","month":"2020-09","story":"Do We Have Victorians to Thank for Consumerism?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Brilliant filmmaker; this and Bitter Lake worth watching.","quote":"Adam Curtis is a brilliant film maker.","text":"Adam Curtis is a brilliant film maker. \"Hypernormalisation\" ( [VIDEO] ) and \"Bitter Lake\" ( [LINK] ) are well worth watching too."},{"id":18086481,"author":"bartcobain","month":"2018-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Visually appealing, evolving documentary unmatched by others.","quote":"I have been looking for another documentary as visual appealing, as evolving as this one","text":"Hypernormalisation from Adam Curtis. I have been looking for another documentary as visual appealing, as evolving as this one but haven't found any other documentary. [VIDEO] Do you know another documentary as good as this one?"},{"id":13350220,"author":"alistproducer2","month":"2017-01","story":"Ask HN: Will automation generate social massive protests?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Awesome documentary covering this subject.","quote":"Hypernormalisation is an awesome documentary that actually covers this subject. If you've got a couple hours to kill I highly recommend it.","text":"Hypernormalisation is an awesome documentary that actually covers this subject. If you've got a couple hours to kill I highly recommend it. It can be watched on youtube [VIDEO]"},{"id":13263201,"author":"mcphilip","month":"2016-12","story":"A bigger problem than ISIS: The Mosul Dam is failing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Condenses 40 years into a bleak guide to postmodern philosophy.","quote":"he's essentially cross cut the last 40 years into a 3 hour cliff notes guide to some of the darker corners of postmodern philosophy.","text":"No joke, just finished watching HyperNormalisation[1]. The most depressing part to me is that Adam Curtis isn't off on an easily dismissed tangent in this documentary, he's essentially cross cut the last 40 years into a 3 hour cliff notes guide to some of the darker corners of postmodern philosophy. Specifically, Baudrillard. His 2002 essay, The Violence of the Global[2] has some interesting food for thought: >We believe that the ideal purpose of any value is to become universal. But we do not really assess the deadly danger that such a quest presents. Far from being an uplifting move, it is instead a downward trend toward a zero degree in all values. In the Enlightenment, universalization was viewed as unlimited growth and forward progress. Today, by contrast, universalization exists by default and is expressed as a forward escape, which aims to reach the most minimally common value. This is precisely the fate of human rights, democracy, and liberty today. Their expansion is in reality their weakest expression. HyperNormalism is bleak and offers no solutions for course correcting international affairs, and there's the rub: universal solutions may not exist. For me, my mind's left reeling by the complexity of the times, leaving me at a loss for how to integrate information from sources like Baudrillard into my worldview guiding day-to-day behavior. My typical response is a hyperlocal focus on the people around me in meatspace and trying to at least get the basics right like t"},{"id":24968987,"author":"m_ke","month":"2020-11","story":"South Park creators have new political satire series with AI-generated deepfakes","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how badly this can be abused by political operatives.","quote":"I recommend watching Adam Curtis' documentary called Hypernormalisation to get a sense of how badly this can get abused by political operatives.","text":"I recommend watching Adam Curtis' documentary called Hypernormalisation to get a sense of how badly this can get abused by political operatives. Especially this clip: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13167568,"author":"krenoten","month":"2016-12","story":"Leonardo Da Vinci Lost Drawing Discovered","stance":"rec","why":"Adam Curtis documentary on truth under fire.","quote":"There's a great documentary about this by Adam Curtis","text":"There's a great documentary about this by Adam Curtis called [HyperNormalisation]( [VIDEO] ), discussing how truth is seriously under fire right now."},{"id":17473463,"author":"dghughes","month":"2018-07","story":"Japan Tests Silicon for Exascale Computing in 2021","stance":"reference","why":"Mentions BlackRock's Aladdin risk system at 1:12:00.","quote":"at 1:12:00 it was mentioned Larry Fink built one of the first \"supercomputers","text":"I was watching the documentary HyperNormalization and at 1:12:00 it was mentioned Larry Fink built one of the first \"supercomputers\" (at least a large system for its time). It was used for risk analysis it was built in 1986. The company was BlackRock and the computer was called Aladdin. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16329224,"author":"bstrand","month":"2018-02","story":"John Perry Barlow has died","stance":"reference","why":"Examines Barlow's utopian cyberspace rhetoric starting at 40m 35s.","quote":"examined by Adam Curtis in his 2016 film Hypernormalisation (starting at 40m 35s)","text":"It is worthwhile to consider Barlow's legacy--particularly this utopian rhetoric about cyberspace--as examined by Adam Curtis in his 2016 film Hypernormalisation (starting at 40m 35s):- [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-ZxPhDC-r3w",1],["l7TONauJGfc",1],["MNXypBxNGMo",1],["MijmeoH9LT4",1],["BI-old7YI4I",1],["41VJudBdYXY",1]]},"f7CW7S0zxv4":{"wy":"HN commenters link it repeatedly in colonial history threads as an eloquent, fact-dense short speech.","q":{"x":"This talk has many facts not even commonly known to Indians.","i":19184648,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":34596990,"author":"parthdesai","month":"2023-01","story":"UK expected to be only major economy to shrink in 2023 – IMF","stance":"rec","why":"Mini speech on what the British Empire did in India","quote":"here's a 8 minute mini speech of things British empire did in India.","text":"Yeah, but we are talking about India under british empire. Not Rwanda and Haiti. Since you're so oblivious of what the british empire did, here's a 8 minute mini speech of things British empire did in India. India wasn't some poor country, it was actually one of the richest countries in the world before brits came along and stole everything. This was my entire point in the initial post, it's both hilarious and a bit sad how people are so unaware what the british empire did in India. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33387861,"author":"hawk_","month":"2022-10","story":"Exploring the richness and variety of letters sent to the East India Company","stance":"rec","why":"More detailed debate on the British empire","quote":"This one has more details if anyone's interested","text":"The video linked by parent is light on details. This one has more details if anyone's interested (A debate on the empire but many of the things apply to EIC) [VIDEO]"},{"id":32715468,"author":"vivegi","month":"2022-09","story":"Some things to learn from the British East India Company's growth and demise","stance":"rec","why":"Eloquent short speech arguing for reparations from Britain","quote":"He is a very eloquent speaker and writer.","text":"Mr. Tharoor is a current Member of Parliament of India and previously India's Ambassador to the UN. He is a very eloquent speaker and writer. Here's his famous short speech at the Oxford Union where he made a case for reparations from Britain for its excesses during its reign of the Indian subcontinent. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29159110,"author":"rramadass","month":"2021-11","story":"Shooting an Elephant (1936)","stance":"rec","why":"Shorter speech on what British colonialism did to India","quote":"Shorter version of above at Britain Does Owe Reparations","text":"This argument is somewhat disingenuous with cherry-picked examples which are highly dependent on Time and Context. Colonialism ruined whole countries leaving them poor, rewrote cultural narratives, uprooted entire social structures and in a word, destroyed peoples histories and a sense of their own independent identity. In the bloody aftermath some flourished and some did not; but this cannot be used as an example of the \"superiority\" of certain forms of government when the existing forms had been destroyed. A good starting point on further research is the book; Why Nations Fail: The Origins of Power, Prosperity, and Poverty by Acemoglu and Robinson (be sure to read the Critical Reviews section) - [LINK] The following two videos though dealing specifically with India under British Colonialism are equally valid as to what had happened to other countries under the British Empire; 1) W.r.t. what British Colonialism did to India, see Dr. Shashi Tharoor's longer speech Looking Back at the British Raj in India - [LINK] 2) Shorter version of above at Britain Does Owe Reparations [to India] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":29239938,"author":"nonamechicken","month":"2021-11","story":"Indian Railways Works Manual (2000) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Speech listing damages done by British rule","quote":"Dr Shashi Tharoor did a nice speech about the British rule in India listing out the damages they did","text":"Regarding 'helped unify India as a nation', they are not the first. Referring to [LINK] , India's map looked close to the present day multiple times in the past. Maurya Empire-284 BCE [1:57] Delhi Sultanate (Tughlaq)-1345 [8:45] Mughal Empire-1692 [10:24] As for 'they also brought manufactured goods back', they exported raw materials from India and imported back finished goods, thereby destroying the local industries in India. >By the mid-19th century, the Indian economy had been fully transformed into a colonial trade pattern, where its export consisted mainly of agricultural commodities such as foodgrains, and the home charges were met by exporting such commodities, while the imports consisted of manufactured goods, especially cotton textiles and machinery (Siddiqui, 2015c). After the 1880s, the trade imports became more diversified, due to the growing penetration of foreign manufactured goods such as cotton textiles, capital goods, machinery etc. (Siddiqui, 1996) This in turn further undermined the position of the local producers and hastened the decay of indigenous industries and handicrafts. [LINK] Dr Shashi Tharoor did a nice speech about the British rule in India listing out the damages they did: [VIDEO] . Text version of this speech available here: [LINK] . His book on the same topic: [LINK] Then there is the famines. Famines during british rule is estimated to have killed from 20-50 million Indians: [LINK] . Google image search of 'british india famines' should show "},{"id":21104966,"author":"shripadk","month":"2019-09","story":"Before the East India Company","stance":"rec","why":"Counter to rosy views of the East India Company","quote":"I'll stick this here for anyone wanting to know the real history of EIC","text":"I'll stick this here for anyone wanting to know the real history of EIC and not some fancy, all-so-rosy version presented by EIC apologists. As a constant reminder of the atrocities, plunder, destruction of culture and tradition carried by EIC in the garb of \"Industrialization\" and establishing \"Democracy\" for which Britain has never apologized: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19184648,"author":"rainhacker","month":"2019-02","story":"The Rest of Us Always Knew Churchill Was a Villain","stance":"rec","why":"Contains facts not commonly known even to Indians","quote":"This talk has many facts not even commonly known to Indians.","text":"If anyone is interested to learn more; the author of this post has debated \"Britan does owe reparations\" in Oxford Union Society here : [VIDEO] This talk has many facts not even commonly known to Indians."},{"id":17053540,"author":"astatine","month":"2018-05","story":"The Last Slave Ship Survivor Gave an Interview in the 1930s","stance":"rec","why":"Presents reparations for historical wrongs in an interesting way","quote":"the question of reparations for historical wrongs is presented in an interesting manner.","text":"There was a debate about reparations in quite another context - the British colonisation of India. Though not connected with the topic being discussed, the question of reparations for historical wrongs is presented in an interesting manner. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30459191,"author":"teatree","month":"2022-02","story":"How old are democracies across the world?","stance":"reference","why":"Tharoor's point about UK democracy and colonial history","quote":"It is a bit rich to celebrate UK's democracy as 102 years old","text":"This reminds me of Shashi Tharoor's [0] speech at Oxford Union. It is a bit rich to celebrate UK's democracy as 102 years old when they were practicing colonization and slavery less than a human lifetime ago (75 years), isn't it? [VIDEO]"},{"id":26749974,"author":"lordvegazo","month":"2021-04","story":"Prince Philip has died","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for argument that the monarchy should pay reparations","quote":"","text":"It was Queen Victoria who gave patents to explore. They came and plundered our lands and created unrest first in the name of east india company and then the British Empire. We just dont know how the subcontinent would have evolved without their interference - because of their divide and conquer policy we have an extreme distrust between the muslims and the hindus that had resulted in one of the worst (and ridiculus) land partitions in 1947 and created a conflict that will go on for generations. They interfered where they had no business to be in and made a mess of everything. People argue that his hands are clean because all the bad stuff was done by his mother-in-law and the PMs. But he directly benifitted from the gains (as will his decendents) of the genoide and plunderings and won't even acknowledge. The monarch should pay the subcontinent back for the damages they have caused[0, 1]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":25670954,"author":"s_dev","month":"2021-01","story":"Scandinavian 'Nabob' of the British Empire: Discovery of a New Colonial Archive","stance":"reference","why":"Ex-colonies' view on British development claims","quote":"","text":">A lot of people praise British for developing India. A lot of British people praise Britain for developing India. Indians themselves and other ex-colonies (much of the world that was Victorian pink) aren't so convinced: [VIDEO] British railways were basically Roman roads. They served to move the army -- the fact the citzens could use them for trade was a massive incidental benefit."},{"id":24154968,"author":"arrayjumper","month":"2020-08","story":"Mahatma Gandhi is set to become the first non-white person on British currency","stance":"reference","why":"Tharoor's rebuttal that India was denied democracy","quote":"We were denied democracy sir! We had to snatch it, seize it from you!","text":"> That it exists at all, as a democracy, is due to the immense efforts of the British....Indian citizens should be grateful to all of the above groups of people who have given them democracy as it is today What? Are you serious with this line of thought that Indians should be grateful to the British for democracy? I'd like to quote Shashi Tharoor's arguments here from an Oxford Union debate [0] - > It's a bit rich to oppress, enslave, kill, torture, maim people for 200 years and then celebrate the fact that they're democratic at the end of it. We were denied democracy sir! We had to snatch it, seize it from you! With the greatest of reluctance it was conceded. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23602778,"author":"teatree","month":"2020-06","story":"The Importance of India","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as reference in argument about colonial oppression","quote":"","text":"May I know how many years your grandmother was kept as a slave and abused ? Indian experienced it for 200+ years. People (Americans dare I say ?) have no problems accepting blacks are behind whites on most development indices EVEN TODAY because of slavery and oppression, but somehow can't extend the same logic to former colonial countries like India. Recent economic hardships of a particular class has threatened democracy and made a joke of fabled American institutions, despite high levels of education and wealth. But a country which was oppressed for centuries and is still reeling in poverty, for benefit of the Western world, now is in a position to help them with a little bit of help and you still can't extend a helping hand in good faith. Maybe your grandmother would have been a bicycle if she had wheels, but you despite having a brain are a simpleton. REF: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20764991,"author":"dragonsh","month":"2019-08","story":"Why so many Chinese students can’t understand the Hong Kong protests","stance":"reference","why":"Source for argument that Britain owes reparations","quote":"","text":"> The consequence of continuing would have been far worse than abandoning. The fact remains that they were moving to put in place democratic reforms. Prior to the Chinese revolution HK was an economic backwater - most of its population being related to the transhipment port and military presence. Kowloon and New Territories were only added as buffer zone for the port. Growth was nearly all post Chinese revolution. The consequences of continuing will be worse for Britain as they will not be able to milk revenue or profits from Hong Kong. By extending it until 1997 they were able to milk Hong Kong, until the very day when it was about to become a liability and lead to economic expense. All the wealth of Hong Kong in those days from 1980's to 1997 was due to growth of China. Hong Kong has never paid any revenue or money to China, but on the contrary Britain extracted money from Hong Kong since they set foot and continued it for 150 years. On top of it in 1997 moved all the profits to London when they left. These all facts are part of Hong Kong history. Probably Britain should pay reparations to Hong Kong. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18685235,"author":"rvn1045","month":"2018-12","story":"New research on the cost of colonization to India","stance":"reference","why":"Tharoor demanding British apology for colonial events","quote":"here is a talk by Tharoor who has demanded that the British apologize","text":"Shashi Tharoor an Indian politician has written a book about this as well. There is this narrative within India (among many hindus and advocated by hindu nationalist political parties such as the bjp) that the muslim empire of the Mughals harmed India and robbed it of some kind of growth. But people like Tharoor are trying to create this new narrative that the British were much more harmful for India than the 'muslim' rulers of the Mughal empire. here is a talk by Tharoor who has demanded that the British apologize for many of the events that took place during that period: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16769078,"author":"smoothpenguin","month":"2018-04","story":"What a skull in an English pub says about India's 1857 mutiny","stance":"reference","why":"Linked in support of colonial exploitation claim","quote":"","text":"There was always unrest, true, though it was no exception for the time considering its size. Either way the colonialists did nothing to help the situation but were focused on milking the cash cow. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14992358,"author":"nonamechicken","month":"2017-08","story":"Body Count of the Roman Empire","stance":"reference","why":"Supporting evidence on British rule's impact on India","quote":"","text":"Regarding British in India, according to [LINK] , there are estimates of 20-50 million people died to famines which happened because of British looting. And from [LINK] , In 1600, when the East India Company was established, Britain was producing just 1.8% of the world’s GDP, while India was generating some 23% (27% by 1700). By 1940, after nearly two centuries of the Raj, Britain accounted for nearly 10% of world GDP, while India had been reduced to a poor “third-world” country, destitute and starving, a global poster child of poverty and famine. The British left a society with 16% literacy, a life expectancy of 27, practically no domestic industry and over 90% living below what today we would call the poverty line. Also, [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["n8Dd5aVXLCc",1],["144uOfr4SYA",1],["x5Efg42-Qn0",1],["r1ZLGqL1FMo",1],["ffkIQrq-m34",1],["GcJxRqTs5nk",1],["pQ0CvjAJXz4",1],["yErKTVdETpw",1],["A-fxij3zM7g",1]]},"4bM3Gut1hIk":{"wy":"Regarded as one of the coolest talks ever, with a futuristic binary visualization demo that has inspired later tools.","q":{"x":"really blew my mind when I saw it.","i":24793787,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":49218924,"author":"vanderZwan","month":"2026-08","story":"Assembly Hall of Shame","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Domas's cantor.dust presentation, one of the coolest talks","quote":"Chris' presentation of it in 2012 is still one of the coolest talks I've ever seen.","text":"Also came up with the original ..cantor.dust.. binary visualization tool, which is a tool I never used directly but Chris' presentation of it in 2012 is still one of the coolest talks I've ever seen. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":49288983,"author":"nerdsniper","month":"2026-08","story":"Spaghettifying DRAM","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Futuristic binary visualization UI still ahead of its time","quote":"which completely blew me away. It still feels futuristic today, 13 years later.","text":"My introduction to his work was \"The future of RE Dynamic Binary Visualization\"[0] which completely blew me away. It still feels futuristic today, 13 years later. Novel UI/UX paradigms like this are slow to find widespread adoption, even when they're so clearly demonstrated to be such an ideal fit for their purpose. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24793787,"author":"LargoLasskhyfv","month":"2020-10","story":"Reverse Engineer Amazon's Whispersync","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Cantor Dust 3D binary visualization demo","quote":"really blew my mind when I saw it.","text":"Like CantorDust by Christopher Domas@Batelle? [VIDEO] really blew my mind when I saw it. Btw. about 8 years later they finally delivered, by releasing it as plug-in for Ghidra! Yay! [LINK]"},{"id":7456608,"author":"e12e","month":"2014-03","story":"Introducing Ring -3 Rootkits: BIOS rootkit targeting vPro chipsets (2009) [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Domas on dynamic binary visualization for reverse engineering","quote":"Skip to ~14-15 minutes to get to the good stuff, then go back and watch the whole thing :)","text":"You might want to have a look at Bossard's presentation from defcon20 (2012?), \"Hardware backdooring is possible\": [LINK] He's done a few of these, so I might be getting them mixed up -- but the message is basically that x86 (and by extension amd64) is hopelessly broken due to legacy crap that sort of hides between the cpu, ram and devices (the software running on your cdrom drive, on the network or sound card...). Also related: 4 2 1 Christopher Domas The future of RE Dynamic Binary Visualization [VIDEO] [ed2: Skip to ~14-15 minutes to get to the good stuff, then go back and watch the whole thing :) software home page and demo download (I don't think there's anything new published): [LINK] [LINK] ed: More recent presentation, looks to be the same: [LINK] ] Again related to (but as far as I remember does not reference [edit: I was wrong, he does indeed reference this]): Black Hat USA 2010: Voyage of the Reverser: A Visual Study of Binary Species 1/5 [LINK] #HITB2013AMS D2T1 Sebastien Kaczmarek - Dreamboot - A UEFI Bootkit [LINK]"},{"id":38642287,"author":"mk_stjames","month":"2023-12","story":"Sci-Fi Interfaces: Hackers (1995)","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on binary visualization by its originator","quote":"a talk about binary visualization by the legend Christopher Domas","text":"Those are trigrams of all 3-byte sequences of binary executables arranged in a cube. Here are some more examples of that type of viz: [LINK] And here is a talk about binary visualization by the legend Christopher Domas, which is where I first saw it used: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26267914,"author":"stack_underflow","month":"2021-02","story":"Plain Text Protocols","stance":"rec","why":"Domas presentation on cantor.dust binary visualization","quote":"(very interesting presentation)","text":"Your post reminded me of the presentation on cantor.dust: [LINK] [VIDEO] - Christopher Domas The future of RE Dynamic Binary Visualization (very interesting presentation) Looks like there's even been a recently open sourced plugin for Ghidra released by Battelle: [LINK]"},{"id":24793789,"author":"rckoepke","month":"2020-10","story":"Reverse Engineer Amazon's Whispersync","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite version of 3D binary visualization concept","quote":"This is my favorite version of it","text":"This is my favorite version of it: [VIDEO] Some people have taken this concept very far. Batelle's CantorDust (was?) a pretty fully developed 3D visualization software for binary packages utilizing many different views and filters with convenient UX for selecting blocks of code graphically and zooming in on the corresponding hex, or vice-versa. Edit: I looked very hard for this software about 6-12 months ago and couldn't find anything...it appeared to be vaporware. However, 'LargoLasskhyfv posted in this thread tree at the exact same time I did and noted that it was just released about 3 months ago as a plugin for Ghidra. I'm very excited to play around with it."},{"id":24823876,"author":"rckoepke","month":"2020-10","story":"Visualizing binaries with space-filling curves (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Demo explaining how to use binary visualizations","quote":"This demo does a fairly decent job of explaining how you'd use these kinds of visualizations","text":"This demo does a fairly decent job of explaining how you'd use these kinds of visualizations: [VIDEO] Batelle's CantorDust combines the visualization concept with a convenient UX for selecting blocks of code graphically and zooming in on the corresponding hex, or vice-versa. The \"devil is in the details\" with respect to the UX for these kinds of tools. The visualization or 2D image by itself is somewhat less useful without being able to snap to the corresponding part of the hex or IDA/Ghidra disassembly. I do think adding a 3rd dimension to the visualization probably adds somewhat more utility as well. The recently released open source package for CantorDust seems to omit the 3D visualizations which were shown in the demo linked here."},{"id":29270052,"author":"LargoLasskhyfv","month":"2021-11","story":"Ergonomics of the Symbolics Lisp Machine (2014)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Thank you for the explanation. But initially I posted that in answer to [LINK] where someone asked about if it would make sense to clone/reimplement somthing like Genera. And I pointed out that maybe the next best thing would be [LINK] With the intent to not having to start from scratch, not necessarily using it as is, but to take inspiration from. Or not, and really pushing it forward instead, but that depends on so many factors. However, it is possible. Besides that, did you really have the impression 'innocent people' had to be warned of the consequences, wasted time, whatever? ;-) Furthermore, either Common Lisp is Common Lisp, or not. So what does it matter, if only few people ever used that? As soon as it's getting graphical there is nothing besides the two commercial vendors(so proprietary), and old McCLIM. So who really cares? There is Movitz and Mezzano, why should there be no effort to make Medley modern? Would McCLIM on steroids be better for SBCL and Clozure? Who knows? I for my part like to look deep into such things, to see how I can adapt the concepts(not necessarily code) into my dabblings on FPGAs. Sometimes I had my mind blown, by things I haven't seen before, didn't even know the possibility thereof. Learning new concepts, applying them. Like this [VIDEO] (not related to topic) Anyway, I like to go back into the roads not taken, to at least partially re-branch from there. There is much to learn from. From that what we have now, not so much."},{"id":23266634,"author":"LargoLasskhyfv","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Why isn’t visual programming a bigger thing?","stance":"reference","why":"3D visualization of binaries with no source","quote":"(skip to about 14m30sec, the show starts there)","text":"Most visual programming is two-dimensional. What if it were three-dimensional instead, arranged in some sort of [LINK] with [LINK] 's representing your modules/nodes, chosen according to the amount of connections they have with other modules/nodes, the connections styled and color coded, maybe even unobtrusive/discreetly animated to show the direction/speed/volume of signals/dataflow, width of datatypes/buses, all in an abstract fashion similar to the way large metro/rapid-transit systems are mapped? Able to encapsulate other modules/nodes, folding large graphs into one, popping into an [LINK] only on demand? Turnable/viewable from any direction you like, but usually snapping to some virtual 3D-grid, in say steps of 45° ? Zoomable? This is even usable the other way around for reasoning about stuff you have NO source code for. Like in [VIDEO] (skip to about 14m30sec, the show starts there) and [LINK] (same guy) and others, but similar [LINK] and even more so [LINK] That looks messy, but that is because it is in reverse, with no known sources. Now imagine how tidy it could look when done originally, from first principles. Your own cybernetic Zettelkasten/Memex/Dynabook/whatever in a world of common and open (architecture neutral) data & document formats, automagically tagging, indexing and sorting all your stuff. OFFLINE if you like to. Usable on conventional desktops, tablets, large surfaces, overlaid onto some augmented reality, VR, holographics, i don't care. With the end go"},{"id":15165703,"author":"tekacs","month":"2017-09","story":"Binary data visualization","stance":"reference","why":"Talk that inspired Veles visualizations","quote":"Veles visualizations were inspired by Christopher Domas’ talk from Derbycon 2012.","text":"Yes. If you follow the link at the top of the page to the explanation post[1], it says at the bottom: > Veles visualizations were inspired by Christopher Domas’ talk from Derbycon 2012. > Check out the video of his presentation [VIDEO] . [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":11791347,"author":"luch","month":"2016-05","story":"I wrote a display server, a desktop environment, and a debugging/reversing tool","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Senseye really looks like \"Cantor.Dust","text":"Senseye really looks like \"Cantor.Dust\" ( [VIDEO] ). I wonder if c.d. has been an inspiration for it."},{"id":8577657,"author":"fenollp","month":"2014-11","story":"Visualizing Entropy in Binary Files","stance":"reference","why":"Presentation of Cantor Dust visual reverse-engineering tool","quote":"Have a look at Cantor Dust [1], a visual reverse-engineering program that is promising.","text":"Have a look at Cantor Dust [1], a visual reverse-engineering program that is promising. The author says in a presentation [2] that CD is based on Cortesi's work. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] Here is a demo: [LINK]"},{"id":6221636,"author":"e12e","month":"2013-08","story":"Google confirms Java and OpenSSL crypto PRNG on Android are broken","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I realized that you probably did have a product -- unfortunately that doesn't mean that the product works (not trying to imply anything about your product/company here; it's just (as you probably know) there many companies selling security solutions; and very few that seem to be selling security solutions that work...). Are you aware of: \"Overcoming TPM by exploiting EFI overflow\" [VIDEO] and: \"Coreboot/bios malware\": [LINK] It's an interesting use of TPM -- and sounds like a sound approach, assuming there aren't any bugs in the TPM software... which might be too big an assumption. I don't suppose any of your software is available as open source? Where can I/we learn more?"}],"r":[["3-son3EJTrU",1],["6y8XgGhXkTQ",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["WwslBPj8GgI",1],["IAF8DjrQSSk",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["7g1Acy5eGbE",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["HxSHIpEQRjs",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["Ai2HmvAXcU0",1],["Wpv-Qb-dB6g",1],["C8--cXwuuFQ",1],["sWgDk-o-6ZE",1],["p6K4iIMlouI",1],["eX9m3g5J-XA",1],["LiWlvBro9eI",1]]},"Eq3bUFgEcb4":{"wy":"Repeatedly recommended as the definitive, well-made explanation of why attempts to reinvent music notation fail and what tradeoffs the current system makes.","q":{"x":"excellent deep-ish dive into music notation by the inimitable Tantacrul","i":39270436,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39270436,"author":"PaulDavisThe1st","month":"2024-02","story":"Analysis of Bach's information-dense music","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive into music notation by Tantacrul.","quote":"excellent deep-ish dive into music notation by the inimitable Tantacrul","text":"[VIDEO] \"Notation Must Die\" ... excellent deep-ish dive into music notation by the inimitable Tantacrul"},{"id":48496386,"author":"Slow_Hand","month":"2026-06","story":"Ear Training Practice Exercises","stance":"rec","why":"History and tradeoffs behind failed new notation proposals and the current system.","quote":"Tantacrul has made an excellent video describing the history and tradeoffs that lead us to our current system","text":"If you want an excellent explanation of why proposals for new notation systems like you’re suggesting have been developed (and failed) Tantacrul has made an excellent video describing the history and tradeoffs that lead us to our current system: [VIDEO] He’s product lead at MuseGroup developing notation software and his expertise lies at the intersection of music composition, UX design, and programming."},{"id":45844755,"author":"midenginedcoupe","month":"2025-11","story":"Show HN: See chords as flags – Visual harmony of top composers on musescore","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed look at piano roll and other notation styles.","quote":"Tantacrul (designer on MuseScore) has done a great video.","text":"Pro jazz trombonist here. All there is to usefully say about piano roll notation, with or without colours has pretty much already been said. And if you want more (much more) detail, then Tantacrul (designer on MuseScore) has done a great video. [VIDEO] The only useful head's up I can give is the current position marker on your playback is quite a long way behind the audio, around 1-2 beats on the one piece I tried."},{"id":40963499,"author":"PaulDavisThe1st","month":"2024-07","story":"Musical Notation for Modular Synthesizers","stance":"rec","why":"Long-form video on notation systems.","quote":"Obligatory (long-form) watch if you're going to continue wondering about this","text":"Obligatory (long-form) watch if you're going to continue wondering about this: [VIDEO] \"Notation must die\" by Tantacrul"},{"id":41009585,"author":"javier123454321","month":"2024-07","story":"Playing guitar tablatures in Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Breakdown of different notation systems and how they came to be.","quote":"here is an excellent breakdown of different systems which have been developed, and how they came to be","text":"Super cool, great job. Looks like a really fun project. This really highlights why sheet notation is so much better at describing music than tablature. The advantage of tabs is that they tell you which version of the same note to play (You can play the same note in up to 6 places in the guitar). However, sheet notation tells you precisely the duration of each note, and can often include the intended articulation. Writing music is fascinating, here is an excellent breakdown of different systems which have been developed, and how they came to be: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39196583,"author":"oriolid","month":"2024-01","story":"Apple Vision Pro review","stance":"rec","why":"Explains attempts to make music easier to read and why they fail.","quote":"This is by the way a great explanation of all the attempts to make music easier to read and how they fail","text":"The problem is that once you get past the very initial stage, you want to read ahead of what you play at the moment. At that moment Synthesia-style falling blocks notation becomes difficult to read because the blocks don't have any position reference besides the keys and no time values. Even the scrolling sheet music or tab is distracting compared stationary black and white. With guitar there's the additional problem that you can't see both hands at the same time, and usually you won't be looking at your hands at all. And if you ever want to play without help from the app, you need to learn to find the notes anyway. This is by the way a great explanation of all the attempts to make music easier to read and how they fail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38193256,"author":"james-revisoai","month":"2023-11","story":"Minimalist Affordances: Making the Right Tradeoffs","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on the history and alternatives of music notation systems.","quote":"for an amazing \"documentary\" on notation systems","text":"I really like the points about symbols to the end, but am not sure the Music notation hits the right note. The symbols - besides Quarter note - for a rest/not playing are intuitive to almost every learner. They are just so different, so square, so alien compared to the notes in melody, that you don't make the mistake of playing a rest note in general. Yes, it's hard to learn the symbols that involve playing something (especially clefs and timing not being linear - see: [VIDEO] for an amazing \"documentary\" on notation systems), but often finding the pitch/right note is the larger challenge (for beginners) than understanding the meaning of notes. Is this because we do not teach people to play songs with only one note, but many rhythms? I don't know. But I do not think the point of affordances in notation is a great example, especially given all the alternatives (see the video above) which, although in some cases clearer (e.g. a instrument-morphic notation), struggle in other ways (too much space used, too unable to represent timing, not transferrable between instruments, etc). The reality is also that our mind can adapt easily in such distinct systems as music notation to shifted realities. Sheet music for different instruments sometimes uses the same clefs, bars and all, but is several notes a part (eg... the music of Violin and Viola are notated differently, such that the notes of the respective instruments more or less span the whole bar without going too far above or below "},{"id":41440817,"author":"gcr","month":"2024-09","story":"Chromatone – Visual Music Language","stance":"reference","why":"Includes a segment on Farbige Noten, colored note notation.","quote":"mapped notes to colors as a novel notation method","text":"There’s some previous work from the 1800s and earlier that feels relevant, especially Farbige Noten, which mapped notes to colors as a novel notation method. There’s a short segment dedicated to it at 1h5m [VIDEO]"},{"id":38839692,"author":"QVVRP4nYz","month":"2024-01","story":"Notation Must Die: The Battle for How We Read Music [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Memorable segment on the jubilate.","quote":"the thing that I will forever remember from it was the \"jubilate","text":"I watched it before and the thing that I will forever remember from it was the \"jubilate\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":38968071,"author":"miggol","month":"2024-01","story":"Helio: Libre Music Composition Software","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses piano rolls and why they are inefficient for musicians.","quote":"The tl;dw is that piano rolls are intuitive to some degree but are inefficient at presenting information that musicians are interested in.","text":"In this video he speaks about piano rolls at large: [VIDEO] The tl;dw is that piano rolls are intuitive to some degree but are inefficient at presenting information that musicians are interested in."}],"r":[["bnoUBfLxZz0",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["eVtCO84MDj8",1],["IlU-zDU6aQ0",1]]},"A9UmdY0E8hU":{"wy":"HN uses it as a counterintuitive data point in debates on welfare states, social mobility and entrepreneurship.","q":{"x":"In these kinds of discussions I like to promote this interesting TEDx talk","i":16977594,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":27514953,"author":"karencarits","month":"2021-06","story":"Survey shows people no longer believe working hard will lead to a better life","stance":"rec","why":"Short talk with points on social mobility, discussed from 8:30.","quote":"This short tedxoslo had some nice points","text":"Well, it depends. This short tedxoslo had some nice points (\"Where in the world is it easiest to get rich?\" Hint: not the US): [VIDEO] He's talking about social mobility from 8:30"},{"id":20035098,"author":"StavrosK","month":"2019-05","story":"It's 2059, and the Rich Kids Are Still Winning – Ted Chiang","stance":"rec","why":"Surprising answer on where it is easiest to get rich.","quote":"I watched a very interesting TED talk related to this the other day.","text":"I watched a very interesting TED talk related to this the other day. The premise is the question \"Where in the world is it easiest to get rich?\". The answer won't surprise you if I tell you the answer will surprise you. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16977594,"author":"willvarfar","month":"2018-05","story":"Both in rich and poor countries, universal health care brings huge benefits","stance":"rec","why":"Nordics lead in millionaires per capita thanks to safety net.","quote":"In these kinds of discussions I like to promote this interesting TEDx talk","text":"In these kinds of discussions I like to promote this interesting TEDx talk: [VIDEO] \"Where in the world is it easiest to get rich?\" Spoiler: the talk shows that statistically the Nordic countries lead the world in millionaires and billionaires / capita, and in rags-to-riches. The talker offers the opinion that this is because its of the strong social safety net, education and healthcare. Living in Scandinavia of course I like to hear this ;)"},{"id":16946603,"author":"willvarfar","month":"2018-04","story":"Yanis Varoufakis: Marx predicted our present crisis – and points the way out","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on proportion of rich people in Nordic countries.","quote":"Here is an interesting related tedx talk about the proportion of rich people in Nordic socialist countries","text":"Here is an interesting related tedx talk about the proportion of rich people in Nordic socialist countries [VIDEO]"},{"id":14641686,"author":"btilly","month":"2017-06","story":"A tale of two Canadas: Where you grew up affects your income in adulthood","stance":"rec","why":"Data on rich people per capita and mobility in Canada, Nordics, US.","quote":"which had some other interesting data points as well.","text":"It was a tidbit that I ran across in [VIDEO] which had some other interesting data points as well. Such as the fact that when you look at rich people per capita, it seems that social democracies like Canada, and Scandinavia create rich people more easily than the USA does. For example if you look for people worth $30million+ per million people, Canada has 186 while the USA has 126. That's about a third more per capita. And watching the video, you find that 42% of the children of the bottom 20% in the USA wind up in the bottom 20%. Very little of Canada does anything like that poorly."},{"id":11646104,"author":"okket","month":"2016-05","story":"GiveDirectly Planning to Give $30M in Basic Income to East Africa","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why Nordic countries make it easy to get rich.","quote":"This and a good explanation can be found in","text":"Just giving money will not work, see Saudi Arabia. They have all the money they can dream of and did not evolve. If you want it to work it needs 3 things: 1) Free Education 2) High Taxes 3) Strong Unions Sounds leftist? It may astonish you which countries have the highest ratio of rich persons per capita live. This and a good explanation can be found in \"Where in the world is it easiest to get rich? (TEDxOslo)\" [VIDEO] Just giving money may help in short term anyway, but I doubt it is one solution for everything."},{"id":38218990,"author":"vages","month":"2023-11","story":"Swedish painters trade union to stop all work with Tesla brand cars","stance":"reference","why":"Why getting rich is easy in the Nordic countries.","quote":"it’s very easy to get (filthy) rich in the Nordic countries","text":"Tesla may go to Germany, but Scandinavia and the Nordic countries are still very good places to create factories. Highly skilled labor is easy to come by, and the top earners make less than their colleagues in the US. These are only a few of the many reasons why it’s very easy to get (filthy) rich in the Nordic countries: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35496857,"author":"vages","month":"2023-04","story":"I wish GPT4 had never happened","stance":"reference","why":"Nordics are easiest place to become a millionaire due to welfare.","quote":"The Nordic countries are actually the easiest place in the world to become a dollar millionaire","text":"The Nordic countries are actually the easiest place in the world to become a dollar millionaire (or billionaire for that case) exactly because of the welfare system: [VIDEO] When you don’t have to struggle for a living, you’re more free to take risks and start your own business. Workers accept disruptive innovation and being let go, knowing that the state will help them reskill if necessary."},{"id":28393333,"author":"kirsebaer","month":"2021-09","story":"Mayor suggests Helsinki declare itself an English-language city","stance":"reference","why":"Norway has more millionaires per capita than the US.","quote":"Norway has more $-millionaires per capita than the US","text":"I'm in Oslo now. There is an active start-up scene here. I have lots of Norwegian friends who have started businesses or non-profits. I know of people who started a tech business in SV and then moved back home to Norway for the quality of life. The safety-net gives you the ability to take risks, without risking your healthcare or your children's future. There is very little poverty and crime here, compared to SF. Public services work. If you go to a shop or restaurant, it feels good knowing that the people working there are making a living wage with 5 weeks vacation, etc. Norway has more $-millionaires per capita than the US: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26366959,"author":"5cents","month":"2021-03","story":"Student passes 3 classes in 4 years, ranks near top half of class with 0.13 GPA","stance":"reference","why":"Nordic countries have most billionaires and better social mobility.","quote":"Iceland, Norway and Sweden have the highest proportions of billionares in the world","text":"I agree with you. In the US, looking at the lowest 20% earning men; 42% of their sons are still in the lowest 20% compared to below 30% in Nordic countries. Looking at those going from bottom 20% to top 20%, the US is at about 8% compared to 14% in Denmark Where in the world is it easiest to become rich? The Nordic countries, of course! Iceland, Norway and Sweden have the highest proportions of billionares in the world [VIDEO]"},{"id":17530185,"author":"mockingbirdy","month":"2018-07","story":"Unlike most millennials, Norway's are rich","stance":"reference","why":"Supports claim that safety nets enable risk taking.","quote":"","text":"> what about sharing it among the people who didn't have the same luck than you? That's one big thing what taxes are for: To redistribute wealth. And I have employees which earn good money. I don't know what you expect from me. > Some people can afford to take risks and earn even more while some simply cannot. That's why a solid social system is so important. It gives us the room to take risks because we can be sure that we can get food, shelter and healthcare. See [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14827625,"author":"fergie","month":"2017-07","story":"Norway, where no salaries are secret","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Norway is among the easiest countries to become rich.","quote":"Norway is one of the easiest countries to become rich","text":"No. Norway has extremely high salaries, and income tax that is pretty much equivalent to California (about 1/3 of your gross salary after deductions), in fact Norway is one of the easiest countries to become rich: [VIDEO] You could argue that radical financial transparency is what causes this."}],"r":[["vsMydMDi3rI",1],["HxaD_trXwRE",1],["sfXn_ecH5Rw",1],["gEKSpZPByD0",1],["SrU9YDoXE88",1],["U6qZPx4uD0g",1],["lyqt6u5_sHA",1],["wtV5ev6813I",1],["0xS68sl2D70",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["u6XAPnuFjJc",1],["-4EDhdAHrOg",1]]},"UEAMfLPZZhE":{"wy":"HN regularly recommends the series as a free, accessible, theory-oriented course on distributed computing that complements his book Designing Data-Intensive Applications.","q":{"x":"the distributed system audio course on youtube is extremely accessible","i":29696717,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30561084,"author":"posharma","month":"2022-03","story":"Making CRDTs Byzantine Fault Tolerant [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear, enjoyable explanations of distributed systems by Kleppmann","quote":"Martin's writing and explanation style is truly awesome!","text":"Martin's writing and explanation style is truly awesome! I read his book DDIA (Designing data intensive applications) and listened to his distributed systems class lectures [1]. It was a joy learning. I wish I had him or someone like him in graduate school. I would've probably taken all his courses :-). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29696717,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: What are the best tech books you read in 2021?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Theory-oriented lecture series complementing the DDIA book","quote":"the distributed system audio course on youtube is extremely accessible","text":"i see quite a bit of overlap between the book and his lecture series on distributed systems [VIDEO] These lectures give a more theory based approach, while the book is more based on discussing practical systems; now both are covering distributed systems as such. Now the distributed system audio course on youtube is extremely accessible (i listened to them while taking walks with our dog) The book is also great, Kleppmann is writing in a very accessible style."},{"id":43197284,"author":"bigmutant","month":"2025-02","story":"Distributed systems programming has stalled","stance":"rec","why":"Kleppmann's course builds understanding of consensus and causality","quote":"Martin Kleppmann (author of DDIA)","text":"Good resources for understanding Distributed Systems: - MIT course with Robert Morris (of Morris Worm fame): [LINK] - Martin Kleppmann (author of DDIA): [VIDEO] If you can work through the above (and DDIA), you'll have a solid understanding of the issues in Distributed System, like Consensus, Causality, Split Brain, etc. You'll also gain a critical eye of Cloud Services and be able to articulate their drawbacks (ex: did you know that replication to DynamoDB Secondary Indexes is eventually consistent? What effects can that have on your applications?)"},{"id":29710914,"author":"MichaelMoser123","month":"2021-12","story":"Systems Software Research is Irrelevant (2000) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Lecture series for understanding distributed systems","quote":"This lecture series by Martin Kleppman has helped me quite a bit here","text":"I think that i understand the argument, but things changed with the advent of microservices. Suddenly everything turned into a distributed system, and it is hard to cope without some understanding of the subject matter. This lecture series by Martin Kleppman has helped me quite a bit here: [VIDEO] Also see his book 'Designing Data-Intensive Applications', which is more focused on discussing practical systems."},{"id":27841095,"author":"Wonnk13","month":"2021-07","story":"Thinking in Events: From Databases to Distributed Collaboration Software [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Kleppmann's lectures on distributed systems","quote":"His lectures are also on youtube","text":"His lectures are also on youtube: [VIDEO] I've been trying to watch snippets while also watching the MIT 6.824 lectures as well."},{"id":27393612,"author":"avinassh","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Suggest me a free/open course on distributed computing","stance":"rec","why":"Kleppmann's distributed systems lecture series","quote":"The Book Designing Data-Intensive Applications by Martin Kleppmann and the Youtube videos -","text":"1. MIT's 6.824: Distributed Systems - [LINK] Lectures are available on Youtube. Assignments code and test runner are also available on the course page. 2. The Book Designing Data-Intensive Applications by Martin Kleppmann and the Youtube videos - [VIDEO] 3. Distributed Systems course by Lindsey Kuper - [LINK] 4. CMU's Database Systems and Advanced Database Systems by Andy Pavlo"},{"id":34284784,"author":"britannio","month":"2023-01","story":"Show HN: I spent 2 years building Tablane as a 17-year-old","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Sometimes YouTube is great.","text":"I created a smart planner app at a similar age [1] and I'm now in my third year of university studying CS. I've also had a taste of industry from building a journal app as a freelancer [2], interning at a department of my uni [3] and interning at Wise [4]. - The whole js, css, html, react stuff sounds more like a beginner web dev bootcamp that should last a few months rather than a 3-4 year CS degree. Avoid that. - Sometimes YouTube is great. E.g., a playlist from a Cambridge lecturer [5] was immensely more valuable than the live lectures I was being delivered alongside 200+ other students. I don't think lectures are efficient but I typically have a mix of lectures + labs + coursework and now, a substantial individual project supervised by a researcher. I'm excited for the future of edtech and programs like OSSU [6] are great if the focus is purely on absorbing CS content but its limited. Something else I found is that the modules I dreaded the most and certainly wouldn't have opted into had they been optional were the ones I learned the most from. Who knew that an Interaction Design class would be so relevant for doing customer discovery in a startup. - No doubt that you can also have an income while studying. - Pick up an internship to learn from experienced software engineers. - The life experience is something I'll miss dearly, be it sports, socialising, dipping into the startup culture (We have an on campus accelerator). - I stress more about turning down jobs than worry"}],"r":[["cQP8WApzIQQ",2],["oeYBdghaIjc",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1],["n8m7lFQ3njk",1],["rHdS_4GsKmg",1]]},"jo_B4LTHi3I":{"wy":"Recommended as a short, fun talk on Node's philosophy and the reasons it was built that remains relevant.","q":{"x":"it's not really outdated. It's a short talk, but it talks a little about Node's philosophy and I highly recommend watching it.","i":4157286,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":17248345,"author":"ahmetkun","month":"2018-06","story":"Things I Regret About Node.js [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sarcastic wit and a fun watch","quote":"It's still my favorite tech talk, very fun to watch.","text":"You can see the same sarcastic wit in this talk too: [VIDEO] It's still my favorite tech talk, very fun to watch."},{"id":4157286,"author":"pooriaazimi","month":"2012-06","story":"Node.js v0.8.0 [stable] is out","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Short talk on Node's philosophy that is not outdated","quote":"it's not really outdated. It's a short talk, but it talks a little about Node's philosophy and I highly recommend watching it.","text":"It's been a long since I watched \"Introduction to Node.js with Ryan Dahl\"[1], but although I think it was circa v0.4.0, it's not really outdated. It's a short talk, but it talks a little about Node's philosophy and I highly recommend watching it. I haven't watched Pedro Teixeira's tutorials[2], but many recommend it. But I personally think Manuel Kiessling's online book[3] is the best. It really teaches you to think asynchronously. I took a quick look and I don't think any of the modules he's used have had a significant change. Be sure to check it out. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":7067880,"author":"ricardobeat","month":"2014-01","story":"The Next Phase of Node.js","stance":"rec","why":"Explains where Node comes from and why it was built","quote":"You should read this interview [1] and watch the talk [2] by node's creator Ryan Dahl","text":"You should read this interview [1] and watch the talk [2] by node's creator Ryan Dahl to get a better understanding of where it comes from. There was much more to it than \"we really like javascript\" (tldr: single-threaded, evented, dynamic, freedom to write new I/O apis, backed by huge companies, well known). [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3687596,"author":"drostie","month":"2012-03","story":"Create a blog in 30 mins with Django 1.3 (Screencast)","stance":"rec","why":"A model for how-to-start talks on a language","quote":"One of my favorites was Ryan Dahl's introduction to Node.js","text":"I always like presentations like this. I think most languages really just need screencasts where people show you, \"here is how you start using this language.\" One of my favorites was Ryan Dahl's introduction to Node.js, which had the same general form: [VIDEO] So thanks for that. I still don't like Django so much, precisely because of the fact that you're sitting here navigating through these massive config files -- and you even need Django's bundled applications to make those files in the first place. Libraries seem much cleaner to me. I guess what I'm dreaming of is a version of django where this whole talk becomes a little simpler, a sort of cookie-cutter formalism where you just start writing: #!/usr/bin/python import django serve = django.server(port=8080, db=('sqlite3', 'mysite.db')) serve.url('/admin/.*', django.admin_site()) blog = serve.app(model={ \"some json model\": \"I don't know exactly how it works\" }) serve.url('/blog/.*', blog) ...to accomplish the same thing that you're doing in this talk. I mean, it's okay that applications often get their own folder, but shouldn't they grow into that with import statements? An open question to HN: are there libraries which do this sort of thing which I should know about?"},{"id":2513736,"author":"thirtysixred","month":"2011-05","story":"Ask HN: what makes Node.js hip?","stance":"rec","why":"Introductory talk on Node.js by its creator","quote":"Also I recommend watching Ryan's Introduction to node.js talk","text":"The only thing that makes node.js hip is people who call it hip. Using node.js isn't about being hip or cool, or whatever else you want to call it. It's about solving a problem you have. Personally node.js has solved a problem I was having with a recent project that needed a Comet server, and since node is all async I got this server up and running in a few minutes. I was never successful with Apache or Nginx. I recommend taking a look at this Stackoverflow answer as it outlines the benefits of node.js: [LINK] Also I recommend watching Ryan's Introduction to node.js talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2520928,"author":"mwill","month":"2011-05","story":"Ryan Dahl's NodeConf Slides ","stance":"reference","why":"Dahl briefly addresses Windows support around the one-minute mark","quote":"Ryan briefly mentions node on Windows in this talk at about the 1:00 mark","text":"I don't use node on Windows personally, but a review of the mailing list shows there's certainly a demand, a lot of chatter, especially directly after releases. Ryan seems to recognize Windows support is something a non-trivial portion of the community want, and is responding appropriately. btw, Ryan briefly mentions node on Windows in this talk at about the 1:00 mark, and replies \"Windows is very important\" to an audience member: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xb8idEf-Iak",1],["k2h2lvhzMDc",1]]},"ceFyF9px20Y":{"wy":"Recommended as a follow-up to Lustig's original sugar talk for the metabolic details of fructose.","q":{"x":"I'd highly recommend these two talks by Robert Lustig on the subject.","i":26239385,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26239385,"author":"akurzon","month":"2021-02","story":"Fructose reprogrammes glutamine-dependent oxidative metabolism to support LPS","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Detailed fructose metabolism and its role in obesity","quote":"I'd highly recommend these two talks by Robert Lustig on the subject.","text":"For anyone interested in a detailed explanation of fructose metabolism (and why it's bad for you), I'd highly recommend these two talks by Robert Lustig on the subject. The first one is about how the body reacts to fructose, and the second video is a follow-up that emphasizes how fructose has contributed to the obesity epidemic in the US and elsewhere. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40403355,"author":"aficiomaquinas","month":"2024-05","story":"Japan: The land that doesn't need Ozempic","stance":"rec","why":"Lustig presentation on fructose and behavior","quote":"there is a great presentation by Dr. Robert Lustig called","text":"If by lifestyle you mean behavioral disorder, It seems to me that the science from the last 10 years is starting to point in the opposite direction. If I may recommend, there is a great presentation by Dr. Robert Lustig called \"Fat Chance: Fructose 2.0\". [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17156621,"author":"mirko22","month":"2018-05","story":"Flipping the Metabolic Switch: Applying the Health Benefits of Fasting","stance":"rec","why":"Lustig on fructose in diet","quote":"Robert Lusting has cery intersting book on this, and more specifically the influence of fructose sugar in diet.","text":"Robert Lusting has cery intersting book on this, and more specifically the influence of fructose sugar in diet. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16449094,"author":"halotrope","month":"2018-02","story":"Obesity and Overweight Prevalence for the U.S","stance":"rec","why":"Lustig explains sugar and hormones driving obesity","quote":"Dr. Robert Lustig has put a lot of research into the causes of the obesity crisis.","text":"Sugar seems to be a major driver of this development. The main problem is likely excessive sugar and especially fructose consumption starting with the emerge of cheap HFCS in most products. Dr. Robert Lustig has put a lot of research into the causes of the obesity crisis. He goes into great detail in his lectures on YouTube [VIDEO] He also argues against the \"gluttony and sloth\" prejudice by saying that you eat too much and move to little as a consequence of messed up hormones due to excessive fat. Not the other way around. The problem is probably more dimensional but the correlation between increasing Sugar/HFCS amounts in foods and the onset of obesity seems to be pretty strong."},{"id":7763249,"author":"pkulak","month":"2014-05","story":"Always Hungry? Here’s Why","stance":"rec","why":"Lustig on fructose","quote":"If carbs are bad, sugar is horrible. Just in case you haven't seen these","text":"If carbs are bad, sugar is horrible. Just in case you haven't seen these: Sugar: The Bitter Truth: [LINK] Fat Chance: Fructose 2.0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25166301,"author":"mrmuagi","month":"2020-11","story":"Why Americans Have Been Deceived About Canada's Health Care System","stance":"reference","why":"Metabolic syndrome driving healthcare spending","quote":"metabolic syndrome treatment accounts for a large chunk of health care spending (75% [0])","text":"I don't think having everybody insured would be the criteria for it being working either. I came across a lecture about how metabolic syndrome treatment accounts for a large chunk of health care spending (75% [0]), and there's a lot of blame put on added sugars and processed foods. A large push for preventative care would be ideal. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["dBnniua6-oM",2],["b1HWNcLDK88",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1]]},"2PdJFbjWHEU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the theory behind Body by Science, explaining metabolism and why brief intense training works.","q":{"x":"Doug McGuff covers med school biochemistry and metabolism in the last part of this presentation","i":32491460,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29405286,"author":"tacon","month":"2021-12","story":"Darebee – A database of workouts","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Gateway to high-intensity training theory covering fitness, health, and fat metabolism.","quote":"you should be familiar with the theory covered in Body by Science","text":"Julian Shapiro, YCombinator alum, has a very nice guide to building muscle.[0] The gateway drug to the HIT tradition, which began with genius Arthur Jones and Nautilus in the early 1970s, is this video presentation by Doug McGuff[1], author of Body by Science[2]. Whether you follow a HIT protocol or not, you should be familiar with the theory covered in Body by Science. Doug covers the definition of fitness and health (which is unusual right there for a fitness book!), the confounding effects of genetics, fat metabolism, and much, much more. The High Intensity Business podcast has all the leading figures in exercise research as guests, with a rich back catalog and very detailed links in the show notes.[3] And if you plan to do this for the rest of your life, give your future joints a break by not destroying them in your youth.[4][5] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK]"},{"id":8506961,"author":"kolev","month":"2014-10","story":"The Scientific 7-Minute Workout","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Doug McGuff lecture on high-intensity strength training science.","quote":"If you have 100 minutes, invest watching this [2] from God himself (Doug McGuff M.D.)","text":"The true scientific one is called Body by Science [1]. If you have 100 minutes, invest watching this [2] from God himself (Doug McGuff M.D.). P.S. The next The 21 Convention will be in January 2015 [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":25796041,"author":"tacon","month":"2021-01","story":"The Paradox of Abundance","stance":"rec","why":"Mini-lecture on fat biochemistry and internal starvation.","quote":"The mini-lecture on fat biochemistry is here[0].","text":">I'd love to see a star[v]ing obese person. Most obese people are suffering from something called \"internal starvation\", which is a byproduct of insulin resistance. The mini-lecture on fat biochemistry is here[0]. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":12453587,"author":"xutopia","month":"2016-09","story":"Fasting triggers stem cell regeneration of damaged, old immune system (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how the body decides to store or burn fat.","quote":"Though the video is to understand how energy is used and stored for workout reasons it explains a bit the mechanism whereby the body decides to store fat","text":"Actually from what I understood about intermittent fasting it is way better than simply eating less. I'm not a doctor but the mechanisms by which the body burns fat is that it has to send the proper signaling at a cellular level. If you listen to the video here starting at 1h03: [VIDEO] Though the video is to understand how energy is used and stored for workout reasons it explains a bit the mechanism whereby the body decides to store fat or convert it to phosphates for semi-immediate use. I'm convinced that intermittent fasting turns off the body fat storage mechanism and turns on the burn fat mechanism. Unfortunately if you only eat less you never send the signals to your body that it needs to stop storing fat. An intermittent fast is therefore way better than just eating less. For me intermittent fasting means not eating breakfast and eating around 12h30. I've lost 14 pounds this way and I'm finding it really easy to skip a meal now where I couldn't do it without having a headache before."},{"id":48738622,"author":"tacon","month":"2026-06","story":"Exercise intensity influences body composition in healthy older adults","stance":"reference","why":"Related to the original high-intensity strength training lineage.","quote":"","text":"If you want to do Super Slow, great, but that is the original HIT, High Intensity (Strength) Training from Arthur Jones of Nautilus fame. I just went to YouTube and searched for HIIT. I don't see any videos of \"moving slowly with form\" (which is exactly what Super Slow is built around - 10 seconds concentric, 10 seconds eccentric, one set to failure). [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32491460,"author":"tacon","month":"2022-08","story":"A little exercise each day improves muscles more than one big weekly workout","stance":"reference","why":"McGuff covers biochemistry and internal starvation in obesity.","quote":"Doug McGuff covers med school biochemistry and metabolism in the last part of this presentation","text":">Sorry but in terms of obesity and weight loss, it's really not more complex. No, it is extremely complex. \"Calories in < Calories out\" isn't even stating the principle you think it is. You want to make a statement about fat balance depending on calorie deficit, independent of what type of food you eat, and even that is completely wrong. Different foods take completely different paths through the human digestive system (e.g., fructose vs. glucose) and thus have completely different effects independent of their \"calorie\" count. Most people on a calorie deficit are not also exercising, and their weight loss is 25% muscle loss, which is a disaster for their future health. Peter Attia makes a careful statement about \"calories in/calories out\" in the first few minutes of this lecture: [LINK] Doug McGuff covers med school biochemistry and metabolism in the last part of this presentation, and mentions \"internal starvation\" where obese people crave food at regular intervals, but the food goes directly into fat tissue. [VIDEO] Hey, if it is just calories in < calories out, why is Bill Gates so much richer than me? All he did was spend less than he earned, right?"},{"id":23812404,"author":"tacon","month":"2020-07","story":"Dealing with Insomnia","stance":"reference","why":"Background on superslow training to failure.","quote":"","text":"It doesn't take much time to exhaust yourself physically, at least to exhaust specific muscle groups. Systemic exhaustion (steady state exercise, like running) is another matter. It doesn't even cause sweating, as it is over too quickly. I have been experimenting lately with pushups to failure just before I turn out the lights and climb into bed. By failure, I mean MMF (momentary muscular failure), not being able to repeat the motion as hard as I try. An alternative is body weight squats by the bed, to failure. Pick the range of motion to be in the hard (low) part of the squat, move slowly (SuperSlow protocol is 10 seconds concentric, 10 seconds eccentric) to failure, and make it hard enough to fail in less than 90 seconds. Crawl into bed and fall asleep easily (after your heart stops pounding). Of course, you will also build muscle mass over time, which doesn't hurt. [0] [VIDEO] [1] \"Body by Science: A Research Based Program to Get the Results You Want in 12 Minutes a Week\" [LINK]"},{"id":21867346,"author":"tacon","month":"2019-12","story":"Evolutionary history and why physical activity is important for brain health","stance":"reference","why":"McGuff on the catabolic energy state of the modern population.","quote":"Except McGuff makes it clear that almost the entire population in the developed world lives in a catabolic energy state, eating way more than we need","text":"I like your reference to catabolic/anabolic processes. Alas, you then made some incorrect claims. >you can't lift weights and gain muscle (i.e. grow) without also gaining fat Of course you can. I'd be happy to share my Google sheet of daily weight, losing about 0.06lb/day, and my BodPod measurements showing going from 28% to 20% body fat while gaining 2.5lbs of muscle mass. Sarcopenia would have taken another 0.5lbs of muscle in that one year period. Drew Baye has several articles on losing fat while gaining muscle[0]. In the first few pages of Body by Science[1], Doug McGuff defines health as (1) the absence of disease and (2) a balance between anabolic and catabolic processes. Except McGuff makes it clear that almost the entire population in the developed world lives in a catabolic energy state, eating way more than we need, never flushing the stored glucose out of our muscles, the tank is always full[2]. But while that is happening, sarcopenia [catabolic] is removing muscle mass as we age. [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18266690,"author":"tacon","month":"2018-10","story":"Not exercising worse than smoking, diabetes and heart disease study finds","stance":"reference","why":"Companion resource to Body by Science definitions of health.","quote":"","text":"If you check, you will find it is incredibly rare for health and fitness books and articles to define \"health\", \"fitness\" or \"exercise\". Body by Science[0][1] gets right to it on pages 2 and 3 to define all three: Health: A physiological state in which there is an absence of disease or pathology and that maintains the necessary biologic balance between the catabolic and anabolic states. Fitness: The bodily state of being physiologically capable of handling challenges that exist above a resting threshold of activity. Exercise: A specific activity that stimulates a positive physiological adaptation that serves to enhance fitness and health and does not undermine the latter in the process of enhancing the former. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14539640,"author":"tacon","month":"2017-06","story":"Weight Training Is the Best Exercise for Bone Strength","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among resources on high-intensity strength training.","quote":"","text":"It's surprising that no one has mentioned Body by Science[1], SuperSlow from Ken Hutchins, Arthur Jones and Nautilus, and other research based programs from the last forty years. Free weights can be very dangerous as you approach muscle failure, and all sorts of momentum, breath holding, etc. are problematic. Unless hypertrophy and/or bad time management are your goals, I prefer the system of deep muscle inroading in 12 minutes a week, and then I work on sport skills, not building muscle mass, the rest of the week. I have a median genotype, not the genetic makeup of a muscle magazine. [1] [LINK] \"Body by Science: A Research Based Program for Strength Training, Body building, and Complete Fitness in 12 Minutes a Week\" [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"}]},"eOti3kKWX-c":{"wy":"HN calls it one of the best explanations of how atomic clocks actually work.","q":{"x":"I've yet to see a description better than in CuriousMarc's HP 5061A series","i":49210263,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":49210263,"author":"alnwlsn","month":"2026-08","story":"Atomic Clocks","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best description of how atomic clocks work","quote":"I've yet to see a description better than in CuriousMarc's HP 5061A series","text":"If you want to see how an actual application of this works, I've yet to see a description better than in CuriousMarc's HP 5061A series: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33351022,"author":"somat","month":"2022-10","story":"Time is an illusion, Unix time doubly so","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent atomic clock episode","quote":"CuriousMarc has an excellent episode on an atomic clock.","text":"CuriousMarc has an excellent episode on an atomic clock. [VIDEO] I like to pretend I can fix computers, this guy can actually fix computers, which is probably why I find his show so fascinating."},{"id":34125365,"author":"puzzledobserver","month":"2022-12","story":"The state of the art in amateur timekeeping (2003) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Startup of cesium clock compared to GPS reference","quote":"was really fascinating","text":"CuriousMarc uploaded a video to Youtube showing the startup procedure for an HP 5061A cesium clock [0]. They switch on the clock and compare it to a GPS reference. Seeing the abstract description realized in concrete terms through hot cesium tubes, phase locked loops, and Lissajous curves was really fascinating. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22666199,"author":"kens","month":"2020-03","story":"Living Without Atomic Clocks (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains a vintage 1960s HP cesium atomic clock","quote":"It's interesting to see that atomic clocks aren't just some giant laboratory thing","text":"CuriousMarc made a video last week where he uses a vintage HP 5061A atomic clock and explains how it works. It's interesting to see that atomic clocks aren't just some giant laboratory thing, but a product you could buy, even in the 1960s. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["meHYBhcpdvQ",1],["NY66qpMFOYo",1],["i9i47sgi-V4",1],["tMP5Pbx8I4s",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["uOSnQM1Zu4w",1],["oSCX78-8-q0",1],["z8VhNF_0I5c",1],["ND2TeNfcmKA",1],["QCAL_YgYiP0",1]]},"wpOxbesRNBc":{"wy":"Regarded as the seminal talk on Dasher, recommended in threads on accessible and alternative text input.","q":{"x":"Dasher is fantastic, because it's based on rock solid information theory, designed by the late David MacKay.","i":17105728,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":38400368,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-11","story":"AI is currently just glorified compression","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Seminal Google Tech Talk on Dasher.","quote":"Here is the seminal Google Tech Talk about it","text":"I love David MacKay's brilliant work on the Dasher text input system, which draws deeply from his work on information theory -- imagine Dasher integrated with an IDE and code search and Copilot and language model! \"Writing is navigating in the library of all possible books.\" -David MacKay We just allocate more shelf space to the more probably letters. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] DonHopkins on May 18, 2018 | parent | context | favorite | on: Pie Menus: A 30-Year Retrospective: Take a Look an... Dasher is fantastic, because it's based on rock solid information theory, designed by the late David MacKay. Here is the seminal Google Tech Talk about it: [VIDEO] Here is a demo of using Dasher by an engineer at Google, Ada Majorek, who has ALS and uses Dasher and a Headmouse to program: [LINK] Another one of her demonstrating Dasher: Ada Majorek Introduction - CSUN Dasher [LINK] Here’s a more recent presentation about it, that tells all about the latest open source release of Dasher 5: Dasher - CSUN 2016 - Ada Majorek and Raquel Romano [LINK] Here's the github repo: Dasher Version 4.11 [LINK] >Dasher is a zooming predictive text entry system, designed for situations where keyboard input is impractical (for instance, accessibility or PDAs). It is usable with highly limited amounts of physical input while still allowing high rates of text entry. Ada referred me to this mind bending prototype: D@sher Prototype - An adaptive, hierarchical radial menu. [LINK] >( [LINK] ) - a really neat way to \"d"},{"id":17105728,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2018-05","story":"Pie Menus: A 30-Year Retrospective: Take a Look and Feel Free","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Seminal talk on Dasher, built on information theory by David MacKay.","quote":"Dasher is fantastic, because it's based on rock solid information theory, designed by the late David MacKay.","text":"Dasher is fantastic, because it's based on rock solid information theory, designed by the late David MacKay. Here is the seminal Google Tech Talk about it: [VIDEO] Here is a demo of using Dasher by an engineer at Google, Ada Majorek, who has ALS and uses Dasher and a Headmouse to program: [LINK] Another one of her demonstrating Dasher: Ada Majorek Introduction - CSUN Dasher [LINK] Here’s a more recent presentation about it, that tells all about the latest open source release of Dasher 5: Dasher - CSUN 2016 - Ada Majorek and Raquel Romano [LINK] Here's the github repo: Dasher Version 4.11 [LINK] >Dasher is a zooming predictive text entry system, designed for situations where keyboard input is impractical (for instance, accessibility or PDAs). It is usable with highly limited amounts of physical input while still allowing high rates of text entry. Ada referred me to this mind bending prototype: D@sher Prototype - An adaptive, hierarchical radial menu. [LINK] >( [LINK] ) - a really neat way to \"dive\" through a menu hierarchy/, or through recursively nested options (to build words, letter by letter, swiftly). D@sher takes Dasher, and gives it a twist, making slightly better use of screen revenue. >It also \"learns\" your typical useage, making more frequently selected options larger than sibling options. This makes it faster to use, each time you use it. >More information here: [LINK] and here: [LINK] Dasher is even a viable way to input text in VR, just by pointing your head, with"},{"id":44173863,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2025-06","story":"Ask HN: Options for One-Handed Typing","stance":"rec","why":"Seminal Google Tech Talk on Dasher.","quote":"Here is the seminal Google Tech Talk about it","text":"[LINK] DonHopkins on Nov 24, 2023 | parent | context | favorite | on: AI is currently just glorified compression I love David MacKay's brilliant work on the Dasher text input system, which draws deeply from his work on information theory -- imagine Dasher integrated with an IDE and code search and Copilot and language model! \"Writing is navigating in the library of all possible books.\" -David MacKay We just allocate more shelf space to the more probable letters. Why isn't Dasher built into every operating system and mobile phone? [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] DonHopkins on May 18, 2018 | parent | context | favorite | on: Pie Menus: A 30-Year Retrospective: Take a Look an... Dasher is fantastic, because it's based on rock solid information theory, designed by the late David MacKay. Here is the seminal Google Tech Talk about it: [VIDEO] Here is a demo of using Dasher by an engineer at Google, Ada Majorek, who has ALS and uses Dasher and a Headmouse to program: [LINK] Another one of her demonstrating Dasher: Ada Majorek Introduction - CSUN Dasher [LINK] Here’s a more recent presentation about it, that tells all about the latest open source release of Dasher 5: Dasher - CSUN 2016 - Ada Majorek and Raquel Romano [LINK] Here's the github repo: Dasher Version 4.11 [LINK] >Dasher is a zooming predictive text entry system, designed for situations where keyboard input is impractical (for instance, accessibility or PDAs). It is usable with highly limited amounts of physical input while still all"},{"id":13262404,"author":"devilsenigma","month":"2016-12","story":"Dasher: Writing text really fast without typing or speaking","stance":"rec","why":"2007 talk explaining Dasher well.","quote":"Here's a talk from 2007 that explains it well","text":"This is really awesome. It looks hard, but it isn't. Here's a talk from 2007 that explains it well [VIDEO]"},{"id":28167590,"author":"tosh","month":"2021-08","story":"Dasher: Information-efficient text entry (2007) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Breathing as one-dimensional input method at minute 32.","quote":"minute 32: breathing as one-dimensional input method (!)","text":"minute 32: breathing as one-dimensional input method (!): [VIDEO] current version of Dasher in the browser: [LINK]"},{"id":27134909,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-05","story":"Neural implant lets paralyzed person type by imagining writing","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'd love a neural interface to Dasher, which adaptively learns as you use it which letter combinations are the most popular, and adjusts over time to make it easier and faster to input common text. It would be wonderful integrated with a context and language sensitive IDE. [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] >To make the interface efficient, we use the predictions of a language model to determine how much of the world is devoted to each piece of text. Probable pieces of text are given more space, so they are quick and easy to select. Improbable pieces of text (for example, text with spelling mistakes) are given less space, so they are harder to write. The language model learns all the time: if you use a novel word once, it is easier to write next time. [...] >Imagine a library containing all possible books, ordered alphabetically on a single shelf. Books in which the first letter is \"a\" are at the left hand side. Books in which the first letter is \"z\" are at the right. In picture (i) below, the shelf is shown vertically with \"left\" (a) at the top and \"right\" (z) at the bottom. The first book in the \"a\" section reads \"aaaaaaaaaaaa...\"; somewhere to its right are books that start \"all good things must come to an end...\"; a tiny bit further to the right are books that start \"all good things must come to an enema...\". [...] >.... This is exactly how Dasher works, except for one crucial point: we alter the SIZE of the shelf space devoted to each book in proportion to the probability of the corre"},{"id":13262409,"author":"devilsenigma","month":"2016-12","story":"Dasher: Writing text really fast without typing or speaking","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of Dasher.","quote":"the demo makes it seem much easier than it is","text":"People seem to pick up Dasher pick up pretty quickly [LINK] and the demo makes it seem much easier than it is [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["v4Wy7gRGgeA",1],["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["4nigRT2KmCE",1],["helScS3coAE",1],["KVN_0qvuhhw",1],["yMjPqr1s-cg",1],["z_dt7NG38V4",1],["whPWKecazgM",1],["pqQSwQLDIK8",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["8Gv0H-vPoDc",1],["7hwO8Q_TyCA",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["I4flQ8XdvJM",1],["8QiPFmIMxFc",1],["1iAJPoc23-M",1],["Jd3-eiid-Uw",1]]},"ftcIcn8AmSY":{"wy":"HN points to it as a clear lesson that algebraic properties like associativity unlock parallelism, applicable in any language.","q":{"x":"watch Guy Steele's talks [1][2][3] if you are interested in data parallelism!","i":24702053,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24702053,"author":"tkf","month":"2020-10","story":"A quick introduction to data parallelism in Julia","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Guy Steele's talks on data parallelism apply in any language","quote":"watch Guy Steele's talks [1][2][3] if you are interested in data parallelism!","text":"Hi, the author here. One of the hidden messages of the introduction is: watch Guy Steele's talks [1][2][3] if you are interested in data parallelism! These talks are not Julia-specific and the idea is applicable and very useful in any languages. My libraries are heavily inspired by these talks. Of course, if you haven't used Julia yet, it'd be great if (data) parallelism gives you an excuse to try it out! It has a fantastic foundation for composable multi-threaded parallelism [4]. [1] How to Think about Parallel Programming: Not! [LINK] [2] Four Solutions to a Trivial Problem [VIDEO] [3] Organizing Functional Code for Parallel Execution; or, foldl and foldr Considered Slightly Harmful [LINK] [4] Announcing composable multi-threaded parallelism in Julia [LINK]"},{"id":36784047,"author":"MathMonkeyMan","month":"2023-07","story":"Coroutines for Go","stance":"rec","why":"Guy Steele Jr. toy-problem talk among his good talks","quote":"There are a few good talks that he gives","text":"Guy Steele Jr. worked on a language called \"Fortress.\" There are a few good talks that he gives: - (toy problem) [VIDEO] - (parallelism) [LINK] - (languages) [LINK]"},{"id":33002972,"author":"heleninboodler","month":"2022-09","story":"Someone is pretending to be me","stance":"rec","why":"Guy Steele's talk on four ways to solve one problem","quote":"Guy Steele gave an incredibly interesting guest talk[1] at google about four different ways to solve this exact problem","text":"This type of interviewing style is bullshit. It means the interviewer knows a better solution that is \"clever\" and expects you to either have the same cleverness epiphany on the spot or to have studied this question. Neither is actually very useful as a hiring criterion. Guy Steele gave an incredibly interesting guest talk[1] at google about four different ways to solve this exact problem, and the fact that it's an interesting enough topic for an hour long google talk should probably be a clue that you shouldn't be expected to invent the best solution on the whiteboard in 40 minutes. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17733599,"author":"platz","month":"2018-08","story":"Organizing Functional Code for Parallel Execution (2009) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Avoid forcing sequential solutions; exploit commutative and associative steps for parallelism","quote":"I enjoyed this talk along the same lines by Guy Steele, regarding not necessarily forcing a sequential solution to a non-sequential problem","text":"I enjoyed this talk along the same lines by Guy Steele, regarding not necessarily forcing a sequential solution to a non-sequential problem: Four Solutions to a Trivial Problem - Guy Steele Jr.: [VIDEO] Look for steps that are commutative and/or associative in the problem to exploit parallelism."},{"id":30462835,"author":"raphlinus","month":"2022-02","story":"Mastering Dyalog APL (2009) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Source of a problem the commenter implemented in APL with scans","quote":"","text":"I think APL has a lot to teach us about parallel (specifically GPU) programming. I've rewatched a few talks with Aaron Hsu and listened to his arraycast podcast[1]. In researching an upcoming blog post on monoids, I tried implementing the problem in Guy Steele's \"Four Solutions to a Trivial Problem\" talk[2] in APL (actually I tried GNU APL for this, because I didn't need anything fancy). This is what I came up with: +/((⌽⌈\\⌽a)⌊⌈\\a)-a That in turn should be fully parallelizable and run fast on the GPU, as it's based on a couple scan (generalized prefix sum) operations. There's a considerable amount of DNA from APL in modern compiler-based machine learning frameworks, mostly by way of numpy. Dr. Hsu makes a good case that APL has quite a bit of power which is considerably diluted in later spins such as numpy. I'm especially fond of the power (⍣) operator[3], which represents parallel iteration and I think can often be translated to a per-thread while or for loop in a compute shader. I don't believe numpy has anything quite like that (but feel free to correct me!). [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":19504788,"author":"platz","month":"2019-03","story":"Why are monoidal categories interesting?","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates associativity as the key to parallelism using a monoid-cached tree","quote":"associativity is the key to parallelism","text":"associativity is the key to parallelism Four Solutions to a Trivial Problem - Guy Steele Jr. [VIDEO] (using a monoid-cached tree)"},{"id":13842987,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2017-03","story":"Abstract Algebra (2016) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Guy Steele on why algebraic properties of code matter for compilers","quote":"He gave an interesting Google Tech Talk called Four Solutions to a Trivial Problem [2]","text":"Guy Steele [1] sometimes mentions it. He gave an interesting Google Tech Talk called Four Solutions to a Trivial Problem [2], and at 1:59 [3] he said: Also, algebraic properties are important. I've got a background in applied algebra, and I think that has informed my programming. And I think that making programmers aware of algebraic properties of their code, and communicating some of those properties to the compiler, may be worthwhile. The language Fortress [4], which he was one of the language designers of, allowed one to explicitly provide the compiler with such information - you could say that a certain operation was distributive or associative for instance, and the compiler could then do some refactorings and optimizations taking this knowledge into account. [1] [LINK] . [2] [VIDEO] [3] [VIDEO] [4] [LINK]"}],"r":[["QZy4Yml3LTY",1],["fNpsgTIpODA",1],["_8bhtEgB8Mo",1],["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1],["Agu6jipKfYw",1],["MEUWxNifJJ8",1],["dCuZkaaou0Q",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["dPK6t7echuA",1]]},"lkgszkPnV8g":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a must-watch catalogue of C++ footguns, often cited in debates about Rust and C++ safety.","q":{"x":"is a great video that covers a lot of good stuff in particular this usability bug. It's worth watching in its entirety.","i":22183456,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22183456,"author":"wyldfire","month":"2020-01","story":"C++20 Reference Card","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Covers many good C++ bugs including a unique_lock usability bug.","quote":"is a great video that covers a lot of good stuff in particular this usability bug. It's worth watching in its entirety.","text":"No. Sorry for being unclear. [1] is a great video that covers a lot of good stuff in particular this usability bug. It's worth watching in its entirety. If you don't want to sit through the video, here's the broken code in question: void Obj::update() noexcept { unique_lock<mutex>(m_mutex); do_the_mutation(); } \"unique_lock<mutex>(m_mutex);\" has no effect, but you might mistakenly think that this will block on m_mutex. Labeling unique_lock's constructor as [[nodiscard]] would force you to think twice. See [2] for more details. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":43604887,"author":"ddulaney","month":"2025-04","story":"Pitfalls of Safe Rust","stance":"rec","why":"Covers vector and map operator[] bugs and other tricky bugs.","quote":"There’s a great talk by Louis Brandy called “Curiously Recurring C++ Bugs at Facebook” [0] that covers this really well","text":"Unfortunately, operator[] on std::vector is inherently unsafe. You can potentially try to ban it (using at() instead), but that has its own problems. There’s a great talk by Louis Brandy called “Curiously Recurring C++ Bugs at Facebook” [0] that covers this really well, along with std::map’s operator[] and some more tricky bugs. An interesting question to ask if you try to watch that talk is: How does Rust design around those bugs, and what trade offs does it make? [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34584557,"author":"oconnor663","month":"2023-01","story":"Swift Achieved Dynamic Linking Where Rust Couldn't","stance":"rec","why":"Bug #5 on shared_ptr data races is covered.","quote":"This is Bug #5 in this great talk","text":"There are many differences when it comes to safety: - Like you said, Rust doesn't let you data race on the contents of an Arc. - Rust also doesn't let you data race on the Arc itself (i.e. repointing a global Arc without synchronization). This is Bug #5 in this great talk: [VIDEO] - Rust also doesn't let you retain a reference to the contents of an Arc longer than you retain the Arc. This is the bug that C++ Core Guideline R.37 is about: [LINK] But I'm not sure I'd call these Arc vs shared_ptr differences per se, because almost any comparison between a Rust container and its C++ equivalent looks similar."},{"id":27602205,"author":"slavik81","month":"2021-06","story":"My favourite C++ footgun","stance":"rec","why":"Similar feel to Effective C++, covering a few recurring bugs.","quote":"Louis Brandy's Curiously Recurring Bugs at Facebook presentation from CppCon17 has somewhat of the same feeling","text":"I'm a big fan of Scott Meyer's Effective C++ . It's a little dated now, but it was the most valuable book I'd ever read on the language. Louis Brandy's Curiously Recurring Bugs at Facebook presentation from CppCon17 has somewhat of the same feeling, though it only covers a few bugs: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39324834,"author":"dureuill","month":"2024-02","story":"Too dangerous for C++","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for shared_ptr thread-safety footguns; claims all bugs impossible in safe Rust.","quote":"every single of the bugs from that talk is impossible in safe Rust.","text":"The point of the article isn't that single-threaded non-atomic shared pointers are unfeasible in C++, it is that their usage is too dangerous. The fact that it wasn't included in the standard library for this reason is an argument for this. The fact that even `shared_ptr` has thread-safety footguns, one of which made it to the famous C++ talk, “Curiously Recurring C++ Bugs at Facebook”[1], is another. By the way, every single of the bugs from that talk is impossible in safe Rust. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RxD6H3ri8RI",1],["lY8Ol2n4o4A",1],["4AfRAVcThyA",1],["sD0NjbwqlYw",1],["PorfLSr3DDI",1],["fHNmRkzxHWs",1],["TPpoJGYlW54",1],["f9EbD6iY9zI",1],["aKLntZcp27M",1],["ibqiet6Bg38",1],["gwvA49ZXnf8",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1]]},"uHQ4WCU1WQc":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the thorough, well-researched answer to the first video game question.","q":{"x":"Here's an hour-long, meticulously sourced, rather thoughtful video on the question","i":34671041,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":29113324,"author":"icelancer","month":"2021-11","story":"The Magnavox Odyssey was the first commercial home video game console","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great documentary on the first video game.","quote":"A great video documentary on The First Video Game, by one of the best YouTube producers in this field","text":"Related: A great video documentary on The First Video Game, by one of the best YouTube producers in this field: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47574729,"author":"teddyh","month":"2026-03","story":"The First Video Game Was Just a Box in the Corner of a Bar","stance":"rec","why":"Comprehensive video on the first video game.","quote":"Ahoy did a comprehensive video about it","text":"Ahoy did a comprehensive video about it: < [VIDEO] >, and a video is a more appropriate medium for discussing and demonstrating video games."},{"id":34671041,"author":"twic","month":"2023-02","story":"C Port of Ken Thompson's Space Travel","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long, well-sourced look at which was the first video game.","quote":"Here's an hour-long, meticulously sourced, rather thoughtful video on the question","text":"Video games are like compilers, or computers themselves - there isn't really a first one. Far enough back, there are things which are clearly not video games; recently enough, there are things which clearly are video games, and in between, there is a parade of things which are not clearly video games or not. Here's an hour-long, meticulously sourced, rather thoughtful video on the question: [VIDEO] (which naturally concludes that video games are a British invention!)"},{"id":24953988,"author":"banana_giraffe","month":"2020-10","story":"EXoDOS: Collecting every game developed for DOS from original media","stance":"rec","why":"Long video on what counts as the first video game.","quote":"It's a long one, but it tries to answer when the first video game was made.","text":"This indirectly reminds me of this youtube video: [VIDEO] It's a long one, but it tries to answer when the first video game was made. Most of it, though, is trying to figure out what it means to be a video game in the first place. Sometimes really the trouble with the question is the lack of precision in the definitions involved."},{"id":43943828,"author":"M4v3R","month":"2025-05","story":"Inventing the Adventure Game (1984)","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for pre-Spacewar games like Draughts.","quote":"","text":"> The first video game was called Spacewar Spacewar! was not the first video game ever. Before it in 1958 came Tennis for Two and even before that, in 1952 Christopher Strachey made Draughts. [1] What Spacewar! can be considered, as per wiki [2] is one of the first recognized video games that enjoyed wider distribution behind a single exhibition system. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["k_5M-z_RUKA",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["wfYbgdo8e-8",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["snAhsXyO3Ck",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["LkH2r-sNjQs",1],["jB0vBmiTr6o",1],["sWblpsLZ-O8",1],["x1bX3F7uTrg",1]]},"9-IWMbJXoLM":{"wy":"Recommended as an insightful and entertaining critique of UNIX design choices such as file descriptors and 'everything is a file'.","q":{"x":"Benno Rice is an insightful, energetic, witty, and just plain enjoyable tech speaker.","i":41175677,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":43327739,"author":"favorited","month":"2025-03","story":"Exploring Polymorphism in C: Lessons from Linux and FFmpeg's Code Design (2019)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows USB device code on macOS, Windows, Linux to critique files-as-everything","quote":"Benno Rice gave a fantastic talk a few years ago called","text":"> for instance, Linux handles network socket, special files (like /proc/cpuinfo) or even USB devices as files. This is a powerful idea that can make easy to write or use programs for linux since we can rely in a set of well known operations from this abstraction called file. Benno Rice gave a fantastic talk a few years ago called \"What UNIX Cost Us,\" which he starts off by showing how to write some USB device code in macOS, Windows, and Linux. It only takes a few minutes to demonstrate how pretending that everything is a file can be a pretty poor abstraction, and result in far more confusing code, which is why everyone ends up using libusb instead of sysfs. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41175677,"author":"pxc","month":"2024-08","story":"Systemd, 10 years later: a historical and technical retrospective (2020)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Insightful, witty talk on UNIX's historical costs by an engaging speaker","quote":"Benno Rice is an insightful, energetic, witty, and just plain enjoyable tech speaker.","text":"I'll second this recommendation! Benno Rice is an insightful, energetic, witty, and just plain enjoyable tech speaker. 'What UNIX Cost Us'[1] is another favorite of mine that the audience on this post might enjoy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38764526,"author":"sph","month":"2023-12","story":"Reforming Unix","stance":"rec","why":"Argues file descriptors and 'everything is a file' are idiosyncratic flaws","quote":"this video argues that file descriptors are not as good as the author claims in their opening paragraph","text":"Not the author but I recommend: \"What UNIX cost us\" by Benno Rice: [VIDEO] In fact, this video argues that file descriptors are not as good as the author claims in their opening paragraph. They are another runaway idiosyncrasy borne out of the \"everything is a file (but not really)\" philosophy that underpins Linux."},{"id":41009892,"author":"throwaway1194","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: Performance of Apple Sillicon vs. ThinkPads (Or Other Linux Laptops)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I get that in abstract improvements \"could\" trickle down from that but once again without any performance benchmarks that is the definition of speculation. Fair enough, I am not sure whether CPU schedulers are shared across architectures, I am also not aware of any recent benchmarks other than: [LINK] Where I was going with my original statement/post is that I wouldn't be surprised to see Linux doing better in some benchmarks due to improvements in the kernel, there has been many recent developments such as io_uring[0], MGLRU, etc. that could make a difference compared to other systems. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39127284,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2024-01","story":"Everything Is a File","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Even PowerShell's flawed and limited execution definitively proves how terribly misguided and morally bankrupt the pin-headed one-dimensional \"everything is a file\" Unix philosophy is, especially with its the ridiculously hard-to-parse ad-hoc non-standard mixed-bag syntax of /proc and /etc files, which would have been much better simply using xml or json, yet which don't even come close to achieving that low bar of machine readability and ease of scripting. If everything is a file, then why the fuck is the insufferable abomination of ioctl so crucial to haphazardly papier-mâché and duct tape over all the uncanny gaps and gaping cracks? Yet another strike against the mindless cargo cult nonsense and unquestioning sycophantic hero worship of TAOUP. [LINK] 13 The File System Sure It Corrupts Your Files, But Look How Fast It Is! Pretty daring of you to be storing important files on a Unix system. —Robert E. Seastrom The traditional Unix file system is a grotesque hack that, over the years, has been enshrined as a “standard” by virtue of its widespread use. Indeed, after years of indoctrination and brainwashing, people now accept Unix’s flaws as desired features. It’s like a cancer victim’s immune system enshrining the carcinoma cell as ideal because the body is so good at making them. Way back in the chapter “Welcome, New User” we started a list of what’s wrong with the Unix file systems. For users, we wrote, the the most obvious failing is that the file systems don’t have versio"},{"id":34019928,"author":"ddulaney","month":"2022-12","story":"A Linux Evening","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Eh, having worked with both systems, I'd say they're roughly equally impenetrable. For sure, the registry sucks. But at least it's uniform and scriptable. Once you know what needs to be changed, you can write a .reg file and be reasonably confident it will at least change the registry in a valid way. On the other hand, if the solution to a problem on Linux is to change some dotfile controlling some daemon, reliably scripting it can be difficult to impossible. Did the distro put the config file somewhere weird? Is awk good enough here? What if the key I'm looking to update is found in a comment, and what is the commenting structure for this file anyway? What if the daemon rewrites its config file on exit [0]? Or has a config file with an extremely complex format [1]? Or I need to poke several /sys files in a particular order [2]? Both systems are hard to reason about, especially if you don't know them. But Linux isn't easy to approach for the first time, and there are advantages to the way Windows does things. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xUezfuy8Qpc",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["ia8Q51ouA_s",1],["zBgCRJluWTc",1],["jpNU72dny2s",1],["mb3uK-_QkOo",1],["RTB5XhjbgZA",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["8k2AbqTBxao",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["SqayDnQ2wmw",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["w70Xc9CStoE",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["ctS8FzqcRug",1],["agzNANfNlTs",1],["dPHPNgIihR0",1]]},"REni8Oi1QJQ":{"wy":"HN users cite it as the standard rebuttal whenever someone says that America is too big for rail or good urban design.","q":{"x":"For a video describing how wrong it is, refer to Not Just Bikes","i":39865795,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":46811326,"author":"CalRobert","month":"2026-01","story":"London became the rest of the startup capital","stance":"rec","why":"Explores why country size isn't what limits rail","quote":"Here’s a video that explores the topic if you’re curious","text":"This is irrelevant, though, since the size of the country isn’t what determines where people go. It’s not like trains got less practical when Alaska got admitted to the union. Sprawling, low density, single use zoning, combined with parking minimums, have much more to do with it. Here’s a video that explores the topic if you’re curious [VIDEO]"},{"id":39865795,"author":"sofixa","month":"2024-03","story":"Tesla's $25,000 Car Means Tossing Out the 100-Year-Old Assembly Line","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the size-of-country argument is wrong","quote":"For a video describing how wrong it is, refer to Not Just Bikes","text":"This argument is just.... wrong. For a video describing how wrong it is, refer to Not Just Bikes: [VIDEO] For text and numbers: Shenzhen urban area is 1,748 km2. Atlanta is 353.04km2. Los Angeles City is 1,299 km2 (yes the metro area is quite larger, but so is Shenzhen's). The average length of a trip in the US is 19.63 km. This is simple basic stuff. There is absolutely nothing that makes Americans uniquely need tank-sized vehicles with 1000km range, even with the uniquely terrible urban design that most American cities have."},{"id":37274274,"author":"pierat","month":"2023-08","story":"Armed with traffic cones, protesters are immobilizing driverless cars","stance":"rec","why":"Directly refutes the country-size excuse","quote":"is also directly refuted","text":"HAH that is one of the most ridiculous excuses... and is also directly refuted: [VIDEO] ^^ \"Not Just Bikes\""},{"id":36423469,"author":"jtakkala","month":"2023-06","story":"Maps Distort How We See the World","stance":"rec","why":"Rebuts country size as excuse for poor transit","quote":"Notjustbikes posted a good video rebutting this argument a few days ago","text":"The size of Canada really isn’t a valid excuse for Canada’s poor public transit, nor is Canada’s population. Notjustbikes posted a good video rebutting this argument a few days ago: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45327434,"author":"rglullis","month":"2025-09","story":"Why, as a responsible adult, SimCity 2000 hits differently","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Most American cities are built on a scale designed around human driving. That's absolutely not true. It only became true in the post-war when there was a push for suburban sprawl, lobbied by GM and all the auto industry [0] > it's pretty safe to bet that she doesn't think that she can live where she does without a car. \"Where there is a will, there is a way\", right? The discussion is not even if she can go by without a car, but whether she would want it. > We can't even manage the political will to build enough housing to keep people off the streets. Ok, but then don't go around trying to rationalize your bad choices and poor capacity for civic organization. [1] Don't go around saying \"I lived in NYC and I thought I could live without a car, but after I had kids I realized they are not so bad\", and please don't go around saying \"it can't be done\". Amsterdam and Rotterdam were once also car-centric cities which managed to turn themselves around in less than a generation. There is no inherit limitation in the US that forbids this change to happen. There is no amount of American Exceptionalism that can prevent people from clamoring change. Maybe it won't be done in the US, because people are lazy and not willing to sacrifice their own convenience for some communal benefit, but it's super annoying to always get in these discussions when people try to hide their preferences on external circumstances. North American cities are they way they are by choice . [0]: How The Auto Indus"},{"id":38057294,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-10","story":"Cruise pauses driverless operations across all cities","stance":"reference","why":"Shows most trips are short, answering the distance objection","quote":"You cover 200 miles every day?","text":"> A bicycle is trash (fun to ride, not that useful). Entirely dependent on the infrastructure: [LINK] > What are you going to carry on your bike? Five bags? Seems like a lot of groceries to buy at a time, but if you really want to: [LINK] > I don’t want to go 5 miles away, I want to go 200. You cover 200 miles every day? [VIDEO] Regardless, you can own a car for those longer journeys just fine and still make most trips by bicycle. > With bikes and walking, your world is small. Yeah, that's why public transport is important too. Cars as well, although deprioritized. > Electric cars powered by renewable energy get rid of most of the negative externalities. They get rid of some of the noise and almost all of the harmful exhaust. That's about it. They're still 2 ton+ massive steel cages that are incredibly dangerous to everyone else, take up a ridiculous amount of space, and require extremely expensive infrastructure. As for the rest, sure, I'm not really criticising your particular choices, it sounds like you live in a rural area where a car is necessary anyway. However, there's no way for everybody to be able to live like you do. There are just too many people. We need to live with long-term sustainability in mind."},{"id":37021908,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-08","story":"Driving is more expensive than you think (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Argues national density is irrelevant; city density matters","quote":"See \"The Dumbest Excuse for Bad Cities\"","text":"> NL is an easy case to make though: super densely populated so there is a large tax base for such projects. See \"The Dumbest Excuse for Bad Cities\": * [VIDEO] We don't care about the densities of entire countries, we care about the densities of where people live: cities. For the US, 40% of the population lives in counties on a coast: * [LINK] 200M people live with-in 100 mi (160 km) of a border: * [LINK] The US population is already highly concentrated: * [LINK] * [LINK] There are large swaths of the US where there's a whole lotta nothing (population-wise)."},{"id":37110317,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-08","story":"Texas could get a bullet train between Houston and Dallas","stance":"reference","why":"Addresses 'America is too big for rail' argument","quote":"Addressing the \"America is too big and spread out for rail\" arguments","text":"There are several talking points mentioned in this thread that are explored more on the NotJustBikes YouTube Channel. - When Canada built a relatively recent urban rail to Toronto, they built it so you have to drive to the train station , and used the high value land right around the station for ... surface car parking. This is absurdly wasteful of valuable real estate and makes the expensive train add much less value to the people. Also the train only runs around rush hours, making it useless for almost anyone who isn't driving to the train for a commute to work. \"The Trains that Subsidize Suburbia - GO Transit Commuter Rail\" - [LINK] - \"Why city design is import (and why I hate Houston)\" [it's not walkable] - [LINK] - How American urban transit is built misunderstanding the way transit needs to work to be successful, notably trying to build transit as a bandaid over car problems, but not making it regular, frequent, reliable, fast, convenient, instead plonking an isolated transit connection from nowhere to nowhere which runs once every couple of hours and then pointing to it as an example of how transit doesn't work in America - \"America Always Gets This Wrong (when building transit)\" - [LINK] - Addressing the \"America is too big and spread out for rail\" arguments, notably that USA and Canada were built by trains, and streetcar suburbs, and that most journes Americans take every day are less than 5 miles. - \"The Dumbest Excuse for Bad Cities\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":37112386,"author":"cryptonector","month":"2023-08","story":"Texas could get a bullet train between Houston and Dallas","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> - Addressing the \"America is too big and spread out for rail\" arguments, notably that USA and Canada were built by trains, and streetcar suburbs, and that most journes Americans take every day are less than 5 miles. - \"The Dumbest Excuse for Bad Cities\" - [VIDEO] The Eastern seaboard of the U.S. absolutely has a) a very linear quality to it (from DC to Boston), b) density, and it's been so since the the early 19th century. Canada is huge, but its population lives along a very narrow latitude band, so it's linear (and low-density). Outside the Eastern seaboard the U.S. really is huge, with nothing like a linear arrangement of cities."}],"r":[["Y5K9HBiJpuk",1],["AW3gaelBypY",1],["7OpM_zKGE4o",1],["040ejWnFkj0",1],["Ot4qdCs54ZE",1],["YHLU0Uej1WA",1],["bglWCuCMSWc",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["oHlpmxLTxpw",1],["r-TuGAHR78w",1],["0lvUByM-fZk",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1]]},"fwjwePe-HmA":{"wy":"HN links it in rail-versus-air threads as a clear cost breakdown of American passenger rail.","q":{"x":"There's a good video that breaks down the costs that make up your train ticket","i":16173361,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":22335085,"author":"MaulingMonkey","month":"2020-02","story":"More emphasis on trains in Europe to replace flights","stance":"rec","why":"Cost breakdowns, especially for American passenger rail","quote":"I recently watched which covered much of the cost breakdowns - especially for American passenger rail.","text":"> I really don't get why trains can be so expensive and what we need to do to fix that. I recently watched [VIDEO] which covered much of the cost breakdowns - especially for American passenger rail. TL;DR: * Labor is expensive. That 10 hour train ride is 10 hours you're paying every employee on the train. The faster speed of an equivalent plane ride means you're not paying pilots for as long, or allows them to make more trips. * Rail is expensive. You have to buy the land between point A and point B. You have to build the rails, bridges, crossing guards, with lots of labor to build and maintain it all, with plenty of regulatory burdens to work through (ecological, safety, eminent domain, ...). The sky between airports, on the other hand, is free. I've seen some suggestions that the sweet spot for trains is in medium length routes (\"too far to drive, too near to fly\"), with short haul routes being dominated by road vehicles (cars, taxis, buses) and long haul routes being dominated by airplanes (fast, don't need to buy/lease/maintain rails across thousands of miles)"},{"id":16888221,"author":"pocketstar","month":"2018-04","story":"Want airline food? Take Amtrak","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Amtrak staffing and cost","quote":"This video does a pretty good explonation i think","text":"I understand the root issues is Amtrack unions. Amtrack staffing is one employee to four passengers. I dont know what the ratio is for airlines but it must be lower. This video does a pretty good explonation i think: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16888689,"author":"rdl","month":"2018-04","story":"Want airline food? Take Amtrak","stance":"rec","why":"Analysis of train versus air costs and personnel","quote":"This video is a good analysis.","text":"Also because it's so slow. You're tying up a lot of personnel on the train, as well as capital equipment, for multiple days, rather than hours for a plane. With the same personnel (which cost about as much as pilots/cabin crew), you could run ~10 equivalent flights. Capital equipment is cheaper for rail than in the air, but the portion of rail capex/opex (either directly, or usually via fees to freight lines) is also high. This video is a good analysis. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16173361,"author":"MrFoof","month":"2018-01","story":"California bullet train cost surges by $2.8B","stance":"rec","why":"Breaks down the costs that make up a train ticket","quote":"There's a good video that breaks down the costs that make up your train ticket","text":"There's a lot of reasons as to why this is the case, but the bulk of it is labor. * Trains ultimately require close to 1 staff member per 4 passengers, much more than a budget airline. * Unions and legacy salaries further add to those personnel costs. The average Amtrak employee ($75K/yr) nearly makes as much as the average commercial pilot ($77K/yr). * Since train journeys take longer, the labor cost per trip is considerably higher, especially for long haul routes. When you're doing a long trip like Chicago to Los Angeles, a train takes a lot longer than a plane, so you're paying for yet more labor for that trip. 44 hours of labor for the train versus 4 hours for the plane. There's a good video that breaks down the costs that make up your train ticket: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14132216,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-04","story":"How the Airlines Became Cartels","stance":"rec","why":"Wendover on Amtrak compared to flying","quote":"And another great one about AmTrak in relation to flight","text":"There's a good Wendover video on airline classes: [LINK] And another great one about AmTrak in relation to flight: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30995305,"author":"chrisbolt","month":"2022-04","story":"Europe is investing heavily in trains","stance":"reference","why":"Detail on why Amtrak is expensive due to slowness","quote":"Wendover Productions goes into detail","text":"Part of the reason that Amtrak is expensive is that it's so slow. For a flight from Chicago to Seattle you only need to pay pilots/flight attendants/etc for around 5 hours of work, whereas for Amtrak it's 46 hours or more. Wendover Productions goes into detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26776467,"author":"matejn","month":"2021-04","story":"French lawmakers approve a ban on short domestic flights","stance":"reference","why":"Wendover video on why trains are expensive","quote":"Your question reminds me of this video by Wendover Productions: Why Trains are so Expensive","text":"Your question reminds me of this video by Wendover Productions: Why Trains are so Expensive: [VIDEO] (12m38s)"}],"r":[["KNWh6Kw3ejQ",1],["uI1eRy0uBI8",1],["pnv5iKB2hl4",1],["LrObZ_HZZUc",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["-HFwok9SlQQ",1],["niKblgZupOc",1],["35Idb_lCU4o",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["Eco2s3-0zsQ",1],["g3Ud6mHdhlQ",1],["7tsXtk7psUc",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["ZWQ0591PAxM",1]]},"tke6X2eME3c":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it in discussions of shorter workweeks and productivity, arguing that rest and energy matter more than hours.","q":{"x":"It's worth watching this talk from the Leading @ Google series -","i":9459157,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10842336,"author":"harryf","month":"2016-01","story":"Working fewer hours would make us more productive","stance":"rec","why":"Four energy sources fuel sustainable high performance.","quote":"Interesting somewhat related talk from Tony Schwarz in the Leading @ Google series -","text":"Interesting somewhat related talk from Tony Schwarz in the Leading @ Google series - [VIDEO] \"Demand is relentlessly rising. Our capacity is not keeping pace. The traditional solution to higher demand has been to invest more time. Unfortunately, time is finite, and most of us have no hours left to invest. Energy, however, can be systematically expanded --- and it can also be regularly renewed. To operate at our best, we need four energy sources: physical (quantity), emotional (quality), mental (focus), and the energy of the human spirit (purpose). This talk will focus on the role of energy in fueling sustainable high performance, and in motivating others.\" ...which relates to the work he does at the Energy Project - [LINK]"},{"id":9459157,"author":"harryf","month":"2015-04","story":"Why Some Men Pretend to Work 80-Hour Weeks","stance":"rec","why":"Productivity is not proportional to time spent.","quote":"It's worth watching this talk from the Leading @ Google series -","text":"In my experience people who work long hours are often too tired to be objective about the quality of the work they are delivering or their own efficiency . It's worth watching this talk from the Leading @ Google series - [VIDEO] The short version is, despite the information-age, we still think about this stuff in an industrial-age terms; that productivity is directly proportional to time spent. In fact it's highly non-linear for most forms of information-age value creating activitie."},{"id":7838584,"author":"nobbyclark","month":"2014-06","story":"Sweden’s proposed six-hour workday","stance":"rec","why":"Managing energy rather than hours.","quote":"talk on this topic is excellent","text":"As we move from the Industrial Age to the Information Age, it becomes more about managing our energy as individuals, not how many hours we're putting on the clock Tony Schwarz's \"Leading at Google\" talk on this topic is excellent - [VIDEO]"},{"id":1430605,"author":"forgottenpaswrd","month":"2010-06","story":"Ask HN: Why do I always waste time on the weekends?","stance":"rec","why":"Rest and energy matter for productive work.","quote":"Read Tony Schwartz and see this video","text":"Oh man, don't you know that you need to rest and relax for making something productive? Read Tony Schwartz and see this video: [VIDEO] The human body is not a machine, you know... About the thing: \"you want to be rich\" Man, this is ethereal, an entelechy by itself, you can't motivate yourself with an entelechy. Your subconscious mind needs real, fixed things or goals, you know \"not to be rich\" but \"4000\", 5000, 6000, 1000eur/month rich, or getting a million, or two, whatever. You don't want to be rich, you want certain things money can give you, write it down in a paper what they are(as fixed specific goals). You will start realizing that you could get them without \"being rich\". You want so much money only for yourself?,IMHO that would be sad, the richest kid on the block. Maybe you want to really help other people in the process. The best money making people I know, and I know dozens of them, some are famous, are very good at filling OTHER PEOPLE necessities first. People can't stop giving them money because they did something for them. Money is only an exchange of value. Studying marketing is a way of getting what people necessities are. If you are sick of working for others, work for yourself, every day. At first it will be very hard, but once you get it you will love it: [LINK]"}],"r":[["XEVlyP4_11M",1]]},"zxRgfBXn6Mg":{"wy":"Recommended as a deeply researched, visual series that shows how overwhelming the coalition air campaign was.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend watching this series about it, no one has dug into history like the Operations Room channel","i":29076717,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29076717,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2021-11","story":"Could the U.S. Lose a War with China over Taiwan?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deeply researched series on how overwhelming Desert Storm was.","quote":"I highly recommend watching this series about it, no one has dug into history like the Operations Room channel","text":"For those who have forgotten, the US won Operation Desert Storm in the most overkill way. It was so insane and completely overwhelming. I highly recommend watching this series about it, no one has dug into history like the Operations Room channel: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27454956,"author":"remarkEon","month":"2021-06","story":"Midair plane refuels done by a drone for the first time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animated series on the Desert Storm air war.","quote":"That whole channel is amazing.","text":"That whole channel is amazing. Check out the Desert Storm series if you need to burn an hour or two on the weekend refreshing yourself on the history of modern warfare. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29676803,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2021-12","story":"The leader who's standing up to China","stance":"rec","why":"Extremely detailed account of Desert Storm.","quote":"Here is an extremely detailed account of it","text":"> US couldn't defeat couple of goat herders and they're going to level up to S-tier opponents? I wouldn't even bet on US against Venezuela or Iran at this point. Operation Desert Storm was a success and completely overwhelming. The amount of power projected was incalculable. Here is an extremely detailed account of it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48519937,"author":"mmh0000","month":"2026-06","story":"US Government directive to suspend access to Fable 5 and Mythos 5","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"That video makes a lot of claims that sound reasonable, but doesn't provide data to back it up. For example, in the first 30 seconds, he says that at the beginning of the Iran War, AI was used to strike 900 targets in 12 hours. Which he calls \"unprecedented\" but then never backs up. For context, in the 1991 Gulf War \"Operation Desert Storm\" the U.S. struck about 1500 targets[1][2][3] in 12 hours. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] -"},{"id":33060109,"author":"trevorishere","month":"2022-10","story":"Cobra Maneuver","stance":"reference","why":"Visual illustration of the 76-aircraft strike package.","quote":"here's a visual illustration of the entire strike package that went out.","text":"It's an airshow maneuver. Speed is life. USAF (and presumably other AF's) send out 2 to 4 ship (or if we look at Desert Storm, over 70 ships). If you \"stall\" or perform this maneuver, whomever is behind you might overshoot, but his or her buddy will nab you. Check out Stroke 3. This is an F-16 strike on an oil facility in Iraq. Stroke 3 avoided six Iraq (Russian-made) SAMs /without/ deploying any counter measures. Simply amazing to listen to. [LINK] And here's a visual illustration of the entire strike package that went out. 76 aircraft as part of Package Q. [VIDEO] Fun (?) fact -- the first strike in Desert Storm was performed by eight Apache attack helicopters with two Pave Low helicopters leading them at NAP of the earth level at nighttime."}],"r":[["nM9f0W2KD5s",1],["rhgwIhB58PA",1],["oIG9ztQw2Gc",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["C4VHfmiwuv4",1]]},"Ep2-ohgFOys":{"wy":"HN points to it as a substantive debate on intellectual property from two libertarian angles, with Kinsella as the abolitionist voice.","q":{"x":"Here is a debate on IP with two different libertarian perspective","i":33160613,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":35702868,"author":"breck","month":"2023-04","story":"'Delete Act' seeks to give Californians more power to block data tracking","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Kinsella argues for abolishing IP","quote":"Anything by Stephan Kinsella is worth watching","text":"Anything by Stephan Kinsella is worth watching: [VIDEO] > ignore the current economic system and wellbeing of the authors and artists who produce work. I'm not ignoring it, I will freely admit that I do not care about preserving the wealth of Atherton and Beverly Hills at the expense of poor children having second rate information. If they can figure out a way to make money in a world with actual property rights, great for them. I also don't think Oxcontin or mRNA vaccines or Fox News or CNN, etc, make a good case for having patents and copyrights."},{"id":35992344,"author":"interroboink","month":"2023-05","story":"Supreme Court Rules Andy Warhol’s Prince Art Is Copyright Infringement","stance":"rec","why":"Both sides agree current copyright and patent system is in poor shape","quote":"I thought this was a decent debate on the subject","text":"I thought this was a decent debate on the subject: \"Abolish Copyrights and Patents? A Soho Forum Debate\" ( [VIDEO] ) One thing that struck me was that both sides in that debate agreed that the current copyright/patent system was not in very good shape. That's one nuance that I think can get lost in these discussions — somebody says \"copyright can be good\" and is met with the retort \"look at how bad our system is right now\" without allowing for the possibility that we could have a better system, rather than outright abolishing it. Anyway, I think there are good points on both sides; you seem to be more on the absolutist side (fair enough), but you might enjoy watching it."},{"id":33160613,"author":"aaa_aaa","month":"2022-10","story":"Vectis IP Announces Call for Patents Essential to the Opus codec","stance":"rec","why":"Debate on IP from two libertarian perspectives with research against IP","quote":"Here is a debate on IP with two different libertarian perspective","text":"Here is a debate on IP with two different libertarian perspective: [VIDEO] `Abolish the IP laws` side provides some pointers to related research and empirical evidence against IP."}],"r":[["9h9wStdPkQY",1]]},"IaWdEtANi-0":{"wy":"HN resurfaces it during Boeing safety stories as early, firsthand evidence that production and management problems long predate the 737 Max.","q":{"x":"Here are gruesome, firsthand details about production and management problems with the 737NG in a documentary from Al Jazeera.","i":26416985,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26416985,"author":"bolangi","month":"2021-03","story":"Emirates’ Tim Clark says Boeing ‘not getting it’ on 737 Max, 787","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Firsthand details on 737NG production and management problems and a government cover-up.","quote":"Here are gruesome, firsthand details about production and management problems with the 737NG in a documentary from Al Jazeera.","text":"Here are gruesome, firsthand details about production and management problems with the 737NG in a documentary from Al Jazeera. [VIDEO] The following summary is my own: The whole story is shown in the first seconds: three aircraft encountering more force than a usual landing (such as a runway overrun). All three fuselages breaking into three parts in exactly the same two places! All three aircraft exactly eight years old. Aluminum members called chords run the length of the fuselage and around the circumference to make up the fuselage structure. The chords are joined to each other and to reinforcing surrounds for large openings like doors. Poorly fitting chords and bear plates around openings were redrilled when mounting holes were misplaced, reshaped and pounded with hammers in order to mate them. This weakened of the structure to below design margins. In addition excessive metal fatigue due to hand processing led to accelerated corrosion, likely causing structural failure in three eight-year-old NG aircraft that broke up on landing. The aircraft were engineered to survive an emergency landing intact and were designed for 30-year airframe life. More to the story as the government colluded with Boeing to cover these deficiencies and frustrate the actions (eventually lawsuits) by a team of whistleblowers. Basically, the subcontractor agreed to use computer controlled manufacturing processes, then did 90% of the work by hand. The resulting substandard parts were beaten into shap"},{"id":38920817,"author":"mynameisnoone","month":"2024-01","story":"United finds loose bolts on plug doors during 737 Max 9 inspections","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on unaddressed 737 NG critical problems.","quote":"Here's a documentary about it","text":"Link? Also, the rot didn't start with the 737 MAX. The 737 NG has critical problems that went unaddressed that has contributed to multiple fatalities and equipment damage. Here's a documentary about it: People & Power - On a wing and a prayer (2010) Al Jazeera [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":38920962,"author":"mynameisnoone","month":"2024-01","story":"United finds loose bolts on plug doors during 737 Max 9 inspections","stance":"rec","why":"Whistleblowers describe workers hammering structural parts into place on the 737 NG line.","quote":"Watch this documentary about whistleblower complaints as far back as the 737 NG line","text":"You're talking out of your ass. Watch this documentary about whistleblower complaints as far back as the 737 NG line as they observed workers beating structural parts into place with hammers. Clearly, things haven't improved since then. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20552773,"author":"reimertz","month":"2019-07","story":"Work on production line of Boeing 737 Max ‘not adequately funded’","stance":"rec","why":"Al Jazeera documentary on faulty parts and cover-up on the 737NG.","quote":"Here is the video documentary by Al Jazeera for said scandal","text":"Here is the video documentary by Al Jazeera for said scandal: [VIDEO] I just watched it. This is bad. Boeing, with the help of the American government, is covering up serious issues with how the 737NG is being built with faulty parts."},{"id":21484936,"author":"krn","month":"2019-11","story":"Delays in Boeing Max Return Began with Near-Crash in Simulator","stance":"reference","why":"Shows metallurgical problems in Boeing planes can be as critical as software.","quote":"metallurgical issues in Boeing planes can be as critical as software issues","text":"> I'm saying this to highlight that what you're pointing out, while not desired, is kinda expected. If it was expected, it wouldn't be newsworthy. And metallurgical issues in Boeing planes can be as critical as software issues: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21139875,"author":"alexis_fr","month":"2019-10","story":"US set to impose tariffs on $7.5B of EU exports in Airbus row","stance":"reference","why":"Source on FAA failures and metal frame defects at Boeing.","quote":"","text":"Or: Boeing is in a spiral to death, involving bigger-than-themselves trends: - Airbus started later, so they started with newer technology, on top of it the fly-by-wire / all electronics commands. Boeing is weighted by its legacy. Dozens of thousands of pilots have been trained on mechanical systems, it’s hard to migrate them. Airbus benefited from watching Boeing’s experience and starting at the age of electronics. - Boeing bought McDonnell Douglas in the 2000’s which has extremely bad engineering records (Half of episodes of Air Crash Investigation are about the DC-9/DC-10/MD-11, involving FAA leniency on the flying doors, FAA leniency on extending the length without doing the proper engineering, and I don’t know if one sees a ressemblance with current events). So the MD acquisition may have entirely modified Boeing’s middle management approach to « handling FAA ». By negotiating FAA’s leniency, or by having people exchange jobs and offices... - Since the MD purchase 20 years ago, Boeing has chosen to collude with FAA to become the certifier instead of the FAA, in order to win competition against Airbus. Unfortunately it has eroded customer trust when we noticed the FAA isn’t doing its job at all, for example about the metal frames where workers had to drill extra holes next to misplaced ones, or bend the frames, compromising structural integrity entirely [1], the batteries or the MCAS. - Now with 200 aircrafts 737 Max grounded since 6 months, and perhaps in need of being t"},{"id":21102845,"author":"hansthehorse","month":"2019-09","story":"Unexpected cracking found on critical Boeing 737 Next Generation part","stance":"reference","why":"Hour-long documentary with two Boeing whistleblowers on 737NG cracking.","quote":"There is a 9 year old documentary on two Boing whistleblowers describing cracking probability on the 737ng.","text":"There is a 9 year old documentary on two Boing whistleblowers describing cracking probability on the 737ng. It's an hour long: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21105106,"author":"anon91831837","month":"2019-09","story":"Unexpected cracking found on critical Boeing 737 Next Generation part","stance":"reference","why":"Reveals Boeing-Ducommun cover-up of hand-made structural parts on 737NG.","quote":"","text":"Unsurprising given the 2010 Al Jazeera revelations of the Boeing-Ducommun conspiracy and cover-up: critical structural elements that were supposed to be CNC machined by Ducommun were crudely made by hand and Boeing managers ordered them installed into 737 NG (-600, -700, -800, -900) anyhow. And then when a blue-ribbon panel of Boeing employees went to find out what happened, their findings were buried and no corrective action was taken. 737 NG's are flying around with substandard critical structural components like spars and the areas around doors, leading to several fuselage breakups on hard landings and runway overruns, which has killed at least several passengers to date. In past similar incidents, aircraft fuselages survived intact. There's a very real possibility that one or more 737 NG's may breakup in heavy turbulence, hard landings and runway overruns at any time, and Boeing knows about it, but has done nothing to remedy it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19917576,"author":"bwilli123","month":"2019-05","story":"Boeing Resisted Pilots’ Calls for Aggressive Steps on 737 Max","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on long-standing Boeing production problems.","quote":"the problems have been there since at least 2005","text":"Some Boeing history from 2010. On the Dreamliner. [VIDEO] Short version: the problems have been there since at least 2005"}]},"JiNKlhspdKg":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it for the mind-blowing connection between rhythm and pitch and as an approachable intro to music theory.","q":{"x":"Highly recommend Adam's channel if you're interested in music/music theory.","i":37619007,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37619007,"author":"patmorgan23","month":"2023-09","story":"Show HN: Learn piano without sheet music","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Approachable explanation that polyrhythms are really chords.","quote":"Highly recommend Adam's channel if you're interested in music/music theory.","text":"Yeah, it even goes down to the physics of harmonics. There's this absolutely wild video by Adam nealy about how polyrhythms are actually cords. (It's very approachable if you know just a smidgeon of music theory). Highly recommend Adam's channel if you're interested in music/music theory. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21384181,"author":"graup","month":"2019-10","story":"The Color of Noise: What Do Hues Have to Do with Sound?","stance":"rec","why":"Adam Neely talk on relations between sound and color.","quote":"There's a great talk by Adam Neely going into some interesting relations between sound and color","text":"There's a great talk by Adam Neely going into some interesting relations between sound and color: [VIDEO] (timestamp where he starts talking about color, but the whole talk is interesting.)"},{"id":17928255,"author":"exabrial","month":"2018-09","story":"We know music is pleasurable, the question is why?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how polyrhythms sped up become chords, linking tempo and pitch.","quote":"What's even more mind blowing is Adam Neely's explanation of pitch ratios and how they're related to tempo.","text":"We're able to recognize simple ratios instantly. The fact is without number the actual digits, we know they instantly simplify into a handful of fractions. That is mind blowing. What's even more mind blowing is Adam Neely's explanation of pitch ratios and how they're related to tempo. The short incorrect explanation is if you layer beats 4:4, 5:4, 6:4 ratios (which is the ratio of pitches against the root in a major chord), then speed it up to a ridiculous frequency, our ears hear a major chord. It would seem that Tempo and Pitch are very much intertwined and our brains decipher that very quickly without evening consciously dividing the frequencies. Major chord demo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16593256,"author":"badosu","month":"2018-03","story":"Making music using new sounds generated with machine learning","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"With regard to tonality: you might enjoy some microtonal works, traditional turkish music [0], electronic music [1] and many other non-european musical traditions (indian, arab). For rhythm there's a wide range for experimentation: from really slow tempos [2] to really fast and (seemingly) chaotic [3]. I like this study from Conlon Nancarrow [4], where the tempo between two voices evolve in time in coordination with each other. So this 'different' music exists, and has existed for a long time in human history, it's just not very popular. E.G. In [1], apart from Wendy Carlos's invented microtonal scales there are some songs that take from other cultures [5] (See how the 7th track is similar to [3] interestingly enough). And it makes sense for not being popular actually, given how human ears perceive chords as really fast polyrhythms. The simpler the more harmonic they sound. [6] 0: [LINK] 1: [LINK] 2: [LINK] 3: [LINK] (1:50 onwards) 4: [LINK] 5: [LINK] 6: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hvk_XylEmLo",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["G1m0aX9Lpts",1],["LPjzfGChGlE",1],["xixM_cwSLcQ",1]]},"bNfAAQUQ_54":{"wy":"HN recommends Cantrill's integrity rant when discussing corporate ethics and companies that act on principles.","q":{"x":"the whole thing is worth watching","i":15290909,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15290909,"author":"musage","month":"2017-09","story":"Facebook’s war on free will","stance":"strong_rec","why":"A rant that restores faith in integrity-first values.","quote":"the whole thing is worth watching","text":"A few days ago someone posted this in a comment.. I can't find the comment, and the whole thing is worth watching, but since apparently a lot of people share this sentiment, maybe they too can have some of their faith restored a bit by this rant; and it's why the idea of \"integrity first\" was floating in my head: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37126516,"author":"josephg","month":"2023-08","story":"Following Pushback, Zoom Says It Won't Use Customer Data to Train AI Models","stance":"rec","why":"Cantrill argues companies can act on principles; money is fuel, not destination.","quote":"Bryan Cantrill has had some excellent rants about this over the years.","text":"> That's how a company works: try to do everything they legally can to make as much money as they can. No, companies don’t need to be like that. This is a meme that needs to die. Companies can have a set of values (principles) and act according to those principles. Any investors can be told ahead of time the principles by which the company operates, and if they don’t want to buy stock on that basis, they’re welcome to stay out. Bryan Cantrill has had some excellent rants about this over the years. Eg: [VIDEO] . His take is that money for a company is like fuel in a car. You don’t go for a road trip (start a company) because you want to get more fuel. You go because there’s some place you want to get to. And fuel (money) is something you need along the way to make your journey possible. Don’t let sociopathic assholes off the hook. They aren’t forced to be like that. They’re choosing to abandon their ethics and common decency. Everyone would be better off if this sort of behaviour wasn’t tolerated."},{"id":15895435,"author":"musage","month":"2017-12","story":"Amazon delivery drivers complain about bad work conditions","stance":"rec","why":"Passionate rant about important things for the right reasons.","quote":"You might enjoy this, I know I agree with it","text":"You might enjoy this, I know I agree with it: [VIDEO] (turn the sound down some before clicking the link though, it features screaming about important things for the right reasons)"},{"id":15278248,"author":"bproven","month":"2017-09","story":"Silicon Valley CEO Called Employees the N-Word and Hit Three Women, Suit Claims","stance":"rec","why":"Bryan Cantrill's rant on integrity in companies.","quote":"Summed up here in the integrity rant by Bryan Cantrill","text":"Summed up here in the integrity rant by Bryan Cantrill - many companies don't have it (or care): [LINK] ( [VIDEO] ) >In the short run, they win. In the current world of high (fake?) valuations, make money fast and now - this strategy wins so it is considered \"OK\", overlooked or the norm. The fact that it is revealed to be happening in SV is only IMO b/c the industry is so hot now. All eyes on on it. Its also the only real investment/booming industry left in America. I'm sure we saw a lot of this in the past too (80s wall street etc) :("},{"id":21975688,"author":"floatboth","month":"2020-01","story":"Products I Wish Existed","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Defense contractors and longevity are also disturbing. Rich pervert vibes. The latter always reminds me of Jeffrey Epstein wanting to cryo freeze his head and penis (LOL) and Larry Ellison's foundation dedicated to longevity — \"namely, his\": [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["aMcjxSThD54",1],["cVaTc15plVs",1],["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["JcJSW7Rprio",1],["-6BsiVyC1kM",1],["Y65FRxE7uMc",1],["v3XcQtoja_Y",1],["XZ3w_jec1v8",1],["OjMPJVmXxV8",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1]]},"iw17c70uJes":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the landmark demo of the demoscene's golden era, and this capture lets people watch it without running it under DOS.","q":{"x":"Future Crew's 2nd Reality demo, for those that haven't seen it","i":26314725,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":37927740,"author":"ChrisArchitect","month":"2023-10","story":"What is the Demoscene? An obscure but influential art form","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Classic demo of the golden era","quote":"Future Crew \"Second Reality\" forever. ;)","text":"Nice little interview that touches on a number of the magical parts of the scene and the many elements and major sites. This guy only started in 1997? All good, but feel like that's just a bit 'after' the golden, significant era. There was definitely a change as PC/windows development happened ...right around then I'd say. But maybe that's just my take on it. Feel like references to some classic demos should be late 80s/early 90s for the sheer impact and that it was a much much more active scene then. Future Crew \"Second Reality\" forever. ;) [VIDEO]"},{"id":38182464,"author":"Flow","month":"2023-11","story":"Northlight makes Alan Wake 2 shine","stance":"rec","why":"Demo shown at its intended 60fps","quote":"Here's a 60fps video of the same demo.","text":"Here's a 60fps video of the same demo. The demo-sceners work really hard to make everything 60fps(1 vbl), so watching it in any other frame-rate feels wrong. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26314725,"author":"herodoturtle","month":"2021-03","story":"Demoscene the Amiga Years Volume 1","stance":"rec","why":"Landmark early 90s demo","quote":"Future Crew's 2nd Reality demo, for those that haven't seen it","text":"Loved reading this comment, thanks for sharing. The demo scene is what got me into programming in my youth - in particular Denthor's BBS tuts, and many late night adventures in mode13h. Good times! > That was when Future Crew showed up with 2nd Reality and the double bouncing, transparent balls. I still remember the feeling of amazement and utter loss at seeing that :) Future Crew's 2nd Reality demo, for those that haven't seen it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37937773,"author":"stevefan1999","month":"2023-10","story":"What is the Demoscene? An obscure but influential art form","stance":"reference","why":"Influential demo that impressed Tim Sweeney","quote":"One of the most influential demo, Second Reality [1]","text":"The gaming industry should always look on the demoscene. One of the most influential demo, Second Reality [1] (I think it is internally called Unreal 2), eventually impressed Tim Sweeney (yes, that Tim Sweeney from Epic Games today) and wanted to hire Future Crew to make Epic Pinball but was unsuccessful. Coincidentally, the first FC Assembly demo is called Unreal, and I'm not sure if that has anything to do with Unreal the game, and the etymology of Unreal Engine itself. I always liked demoscene because they push the tech to its limit, for example kkrieger which is a DirectX game in 96K [3], which is rare to this day due to Wirth's law [2], considering most of the games today are bloated as hell. Too bad there aren't demo groups in Hong Kong, or I may join when I was still a carefree teen back in 10 years ago when I was 14. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":25043690,"author":"elvis70","month":"2020-11","story":"Demo or Die (1995)","stance":"reference","why":"Video of demo whose source code is open","quote":"Here is a video of the demo","text":"The source code (+data) of Second Reality by Future Crew from 1993 is available under an open source license: [LINK] It consists of 32K lines of assembler, 24K lines of C and 2K lines of Pascal. Here is a video of the demo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22880818,"author":"ChrisArchitect","month":"2020-04","story":"Finland adds the demoscene as a UNESCO intangible world cultural heritage","stance":"reference","why":"Classic demo marking the demoscene's cultural significance","quote":"All I can think of to commend this occasion is Future Crew 'Second Reality'","text":"this recognition gives me lots of warm feels. The number of aspects that the demoscene brought together: programming, art, music, an online world of connection, and community - was incredible and had a huge impact on me when I discovered and learned more about it as a young kid in the early 90s. All I can think of to commend this occasion is Future Crew 'Second Reality' :) [VIDEO]"},{"id":22097775,"author":"joking","month":"2020-01","story":"Demoscene, the secret behind Finnish game industry","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"now you can see on youtube instead of having to download an executable and run on msdos","text":"now you can see on youtube instead of having to download an executable and run on msdos ( [VIDEO] ) those old good times when internet access was limited and going to parties where the way to play online / share / meet."},{"id":22098063,"author":"hrgiger","month":"2020-01","story":"Demoscene, the secret behind Finnish game industry","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"lol : [VIDEO] without even knowingly this game was exist, when i was messing with photohop years ago i made the almost same guy:) [LINK]"}],"r":[["dK3zRXhrFZY",1],["gsffg5xxFQI",1]]},"46ehrFk-gLk":{"wy":"HN commenters link it as a short, friendly explanation of singular they and grammatical gender in discussions of pronoun usage.","q":{"x":"Tom Scott has a great video on grammatical gender that is worth a watch","i":6825373,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":10104554,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2015-08","story":"How to Fold a Julia Fractal","stance":"rec","why":"Problems with gendered pronouns and singular they.","quote":"I just thought these two Tom Scott clips were fun and relevant to these grammar issues.","text":"> the word cromulent ... is not even a real word Only to a prescriptivist[1] trying to keep the language static. \"Cromulent\" is slowly making its way into descriptivist[1] dictionaries and is recognized about as often as any other new word, so it just as much a \"real\" word as other new words (\"email\", \"google\" (transitive verb), \"truthiness\"). > the correctness of using a singular they The alternative is gendered pronouns which have several problems[2]. /* I'm not trying to nitpick your post; I just thought these two Tom Scott clips were fun and relevant to these grammar issues. */ [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6825373,"author":"mdisraeli","month":"2013-12","story":"The Power of a Pronoun","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott on grammatical gender.","quote":"Tom Scott has a great video on grammatical gender that is worth a watch","text":"Because no-one's posted it yet, Tom Scott has a great video on grammatical gender that is worth a watch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6058679,"author":"tehwalrus","month":"2013-07","story":"Reminding Starbucks that Beautiful Code Should Make Beautiful Language","stance":"rec","why":"Singular they is established gender-neutral usage.","quote":"Gender neutral pronouns: they're here to stay[1].","text":"They isn't just plural, it's also gender-neutral. Gender neutral pronouns: they're here to stay[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["7tScAyNaRdQ",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"5AVO1IyfA9M":{"wy":"HN points to it as real-world, side-by-side data on vertical versus angled panel orientation.","q":{"x":"this person did a side by side experiment on their property with both angled and vertical bifacial with some interesting results.","i":39970645,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":42149513,"author":"xnx","month":"2024-11","story":"100-MW solar farm just broke ground in Wisconsin","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed look at tradeoffs of vertical bifacial panels","quote":"This guy goes into a lot of detail","text":"There seems to be a real interesting mix of pros and cons (total watts generated through the year, by time of day, susceptibility to dirt and hail damage, reflections, etc.), especially with regards to mounting. This guy goes into a lot of detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39970645,"author":"mey","month":"2024-04","story":"Swiss satellite antennas make a comeback as solar powerhouses","stance":"rec","why":"Side-by-side test of angled vs vertical bifacial panels","quote":"this person did a side by side experiment on their property with both angled and vertical bifacial with some interesting results.","text":"[VIDEO] this person did a side by side experiment on their property with both angled and vertical bifacial with some interesting results. Their advantage didn't seem to be efficiency but consistency."},{"id":39663945,"author":"stephen_g","month":"2024-03","story":"Controlling 3.6kW of Solar EV Charging with an Arduino GIGA R1 WiFi","stance":"rec","why":"Real-world testing of panel orientations in varied circumstances","quote":"This guy did some interesting real-world testing in a bunch of different circumstances","text":"You'll still get more output for the same amount of panels when they're tilted in most places. This guy did some interesting real-world testing in a bunch of different circumstances - [VIDEO]"},{"id":43773921,"author":"thijson","month":"2025-04","story":"Offgrid Electric Car","stance":"reference","why":"Compares panel orientations; vertical yields about 70% of optimal","quote":"This guy compared different orientations","text":"This guy compared different orientations: [VIDEO] His conclusion is the vertical generate about 70% of optimal incline cells. The charge profile has two peaks, one in the morning, one in the evening. I can imagine using the vertical orientation for a fence. It would definitely keep them cleaner too."},{"id":41393760,"author":"pests","month":"2024-08","story":"Can solar costs keep shrinking?","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that vertical panels can be more efficient","quote":"There is research coming out that vertical solar panels are more efficient.","text":"There is research coming out that vertical solar panels are more efficient. I think part of it comes from lower temps allowing for more efficient operating. No tracking or pointing required. Less cleaning as its not a flat surface for dust or fall impacts. [VIDEO] edit: I see others have linked similar videos, all good sources on this topic."}],"r":[["C_r5UJrxcck",1],["hJP5GqnTrNo",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1]]},"FxKtZmQgxrI":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as sharp film criticism and a lesson in filmmaking, and cite it when arguing the prequels failed on basics.","q":{"x":"thanks to them I have learned a lot about movies, actors, directors and movie making in general.","i":32863756,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32863756,"author":"raspo","month":"2022-09","story":"Dick the Birthday Boy: The Legacy Continues [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Their famous Phantom Menace review from a channel the commenter loves","quote":"thanks to them I have learned a lot about movies, actors, directors and movie making in general.","text":"For people who are confused by this, Red Letter Media is a youtube channel that does movie reviews. I have been following them for over ten years and thanks to them I have learned a lot about movies, actors, directors and movie making in general. They have a very particular sense of humor, very dark and full of sarcasm; they like to troll their own audience and don’t care about what youtube wants. They respect my intelligence and never asked me to like and subscribe (unless it’s a joke) and for that, I like them even more. Some video recommendations: - Phantom Menace review - what they are most famous for ( [VIDEO] ) - Jack and Jill - my personal favorite ( [LINK] ) - Bruce Willis fake movie factory - more recent ( [LINK] ) - Scientist Man Explains Terminator: Genisys ( [LINK] ) - Prometheus ( [LINK] )"},{"id":11736626,"author":"smnscu","month":"2016-05","story":"How George Lucas Lost His Integrity…and Why It’s So Important","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best-in-class critical reviews of Lucas's Star Wars films","quote":"they're the best in the business","text":"In case you haven't watched Mr Plinkett's Star Wars reviews, they're the best in the business, and go down hard on George Lucas. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10768109,"author":"everyone","month":"2015-12","story":"How Being Named the 'Best New Restaurant in America' Hurt My Business","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The Phantom menace failed to achieve many basic elements of film.","text":"The Phantom menace failed to achieve many basic elements of film. This 7 part review will explain it [VIDEO] or just watch this bit here. Which highlights the soap opera style blocking.. goto 1:20:40 [LINK]"},{"id":5496358,"author":"KevinMS","month":"2013-04","story":"Roger Ebert dies at 70 after battle with cancer","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"No, its widely regarded as a disaster of a movie.","text":"> That movie did almost everything its makers were trying to accomplish. No, its widely regarded as a disaster of a movie. See this review, which I believe Ebert said that he enjoyed watching. [VIDEO]"},{"id":1371838,"author":"ericb","month":"2010-05","story":"Saving Star Wars: The Special Edition Restoration Process","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I feel like the criticisms here are pretty valid","text":"I am utterly confused by the turn of events. The first trilogy convinced me Lucas was amazingly talented. The dialog was good, but funny. The characters were characters . I feel like the criticisms here are pretty valid: [VIDEO] I'm at a loss as to why what he produced in his 50's would be so much worse. Shouldn't Lucas be the old wizened sage--the Obi Wan of films by now? What happened?"}],"r":[["TnzcwTyr6cE",1]]},"GC-0tCy4P1U":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as the canonical concrete demonstration that modern developer tools got slower despite vastly faster hardware.","q":{"x":"Casey Muratori has some great YouTube videos (a.k.a.: rants)","i":40461320,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40461320,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2024-05","story":"Ask HN: What makes Windows 11 perform much worse than Windows XP?","stance":"rec","why":"Casey Muratori on performance degradation despite far faster hardware.","quote":"Casey Muratori has some great YouTube videos (a.k.a.: rants) about the end-user visible performance degradation over the last two decades","text":"Casey Muratori has some great YouTube videos (a.k.a.: rants) about the end-user visible performance degradation over the last two decades despite computers becoming 10-100x faster by various metrics. E.g.: Visual Studio Rant [VIDEO] How fast should an unoptimized terminal run? [LINK] The root cause is that Microsoft simply doesn't have performance as a priority. It's not a KPI for (almost) anyone, certainly nobody in the desktop teams. Upper management doesn't prioritize input latency over telemetry or advertising \"features\". It's that simple. There's no pressure, no rejection of slow code, no metrics, no pride in good engineering. If it barely functions, it's good enough to ship.[1] Microsoft employees will occasionally comment to say otherwise, but their statements are contrary to easily verifiable facts, such as massive performance degradations in trivial tools such as the new Notepad, the new Terminal, and the Calculator app. The actual specific technical reasons include: - Executables used to be loaded from disk one 4KB page at a time. Execution could start as soon as the first 4KB block was loaded. For a long time now, executables are digitally signed using a hash like SHA1 or SHA256, which means that large executables can't actually start running any code until the entire file is loaded. The obvious fix -- that didn't occur to anyone at Microsoft -- is to use a Merkle tree hash instead of a linear, single-threaded, whole-file hash like SHA256. - Similarly, anti-malware"},{"id":28261178,"author":"yakubin","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: Do You Use a Debugger?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows smooth RemedyBG debugging versus a degraded Visual Studio debugger.","quote":"The whole video may be also worth watching if you like rants","text":"I try to use a debugger whenever I can. Unfortunately, I develop on Linux, so the debugger situation here is awful. Basically almost all debuggers are based on gdb or lldb in true Unix fashion parsing text to generate a GUI... They tend to break really fast IME, since text doesn't provide good stability/reliability guarantees (no type checking, no nothing) and even when they do work, there is a lot of visual delay in the feedback loop, which makes them a pain to use. Another problem is that often the debug builds of things at work run too slow to be practical, or they're too big to fit on the target device. My use of debugger at work usually boils down to getting and translating stack traces from core dumps we receive (on command line). There is a very rare occasion that I can do something on my local machine and then I use a debugger. Life is too short for 15-45 minute add logs + compile cycles. I also use bpftrace whenever I can. At home I write Rust, but IME templates don't play well with the debugger, so I usually fall back on prints and bpftrace again. But generally I'm convinced that using debuggers could be a big productivity boost. Here[1] you can see a demonstration of how smooth working with a debugger can be, in this case it's RemedyBG. But forget about this level of comfort on Linux. The whole video may be also worth watching if you like rants and want to see how Visual Studio debugger quality dipped over the years (first the current VS debugger is shown, and then"},{"id":23218382,"author":"nv-vn","month":"2020-05","story":"Ask HN: Is there still a place for native desktop apps?","stance":"rec","why":"Compares VS load and debugger update times with a Pentium 4.","quote":"Casey Muratori (game developer for the Witness) has a really good rant [1] about bloat in Visual Studio specifically","text":"It may be true that people are partially looking back in rose-tinted glasses, but there's more than just an inkling of truth to their side. Casey Muratori (game developer for the Witness) has a really good rant [1] about bloat in Visual Studio specifically, where he demonstrates load times & the debugger UI updating today vs on an Pentium 4 running XP. Whether or not you attribute the performance difference to new features in Win10/VS, it's worth considering the fact that workflows are still being impacted so significantly on modern hardware. We were able to extract 100s of times more out of hardware and gave it up for ??? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46937583,"author":"direwolf20","month":"2026-02","story":"(AI) Slop Terrifies Me","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Casey Muratori showing off the speed of visual studio 6 on a Pentium something","text":"We've gotten more pixels and bytes and flops, that's it. We haven't got more battery life, or faster computers, which is strange because they have orders of magnitude more flops in them. Casey Muratori showing off the speed of visual studio 6 on a Pentium something after ranting about it: Jump to 36:08 in [VIDEO]"},{"id":46155832,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2025-12","story":"Programming peaked","stance":"reference","why":"Visual Studio opens and debugs faster on a 20-year-old Pentium 4.","quote":"Casey Muratori ranting about Visual Studio Debugger slowness","text":"> \"I remember seeing a video on here where someone actually booted up a computer from the 2000's and showed how snappy everything was, including Visual Studio\" Was it this one? Casey Muratori ranting about Visual Studio Debugger slowness, and he shows a video of Visual Studio opening and debugging faster on a single core Pentium 4 from 20 years earlier - [VIDEO] Or this one? Roger Clark developing Notepad in C++ on Windows 2000 and commenting how fast Visual Studio opens: [LINK]"},{"id":43982197,"author":"dijit","month":"2025-05","story":"The world could run on older hardware if software optimization was a priority","stance":"reference","why":"Watch window delay makes debugging feel bad.","quote":"I think Casey Muratori's point about the watch window in visual studio is the right one.","text":"I think you're right, maybe the disconnect is UI slowness? I am annoyed at the startup time of programs that I keep closed and only open infrequently (Discord is one of those, the update loop takes a buttload of time because I don't use it daily), but I'm not annoyed when something I keep open takes 1-10s to open. But when I think of getting annoyed it's almost always because an action I'm doing takes too long. I grew up in an era with worse computers than we have today, but clicking a new list was perceptibly instant- it was like the computer was waiting for the screen to catch up. Today, it feels like the computer chugs to show you what you've clicked on. This is especially true with universal software, like chat programs, that everyone in an org is using. I think Casey Muratori's point about the watch window in visual studio is the right one. The watch window used to be instant, but someone added an artificial delay to start processing so that the CPU wouldn't work when stepping fast through the code. The result is that, well, you gotta wait for the watch window to update... Which \"feels bad\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":43731373,"author":"troupo","month":"2025-04","story":"Four Years of Jai (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Visual Studio debugger no longer updates values smoothly while stepping.","quote":"There's a great rant about Visual Studio debugger which in recent versions cannot even update debugged values","text":"> He has led a generation of impressionable programmers to believe that we in some dark age of software, when that statement couldn't be further from the truth. Have you actually used modern software? There's a great rant about Visual Studio debugger which in recent versions cannot even update debugged values as you step through the program unlike its predecessors: [VIDEO] And this is professional software. There's state of personal software is worse. Most programs cannot show a page of text with a few images without consuming gigabytes of RAM and not-insignificant percentages of CPU."},{"id":42430209,"author":"gary_0","month":"2024-12","story":"Software Is Way Less Performant Today [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows a 20-year-old computer where Visual Studio is instantaneous.","quote":"Here's the video he's referencing of the 20-year-old computer where Visual Studio is instantaneous","text":"Here's the video he's referencing of the 20-year-old computer where Visual Studio is instantaneous: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42435536,"author":"agilob","month":"2024-12","story":"Ask HN: SWEs how do you future-proof your career in light of LLMs?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No, math is major part of writing good code, but when was the last time you've seen somebody put effort into writing O(n) algorithm? 99% of programming is \"import sort from sort; sort.sortThisReallyQuick\". Programming is mostly writing code that just compiles and eventually gives correct results (and has bugs). You can do a lot of programming just buy copy-pasting results from stackoverflow. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40107183,"author":"vrnvu","month":"2024-04","story":"I can't stand using VSCode so I wrote my own","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the famous Visual Studio rant by Casey Muratori","text":"This reminds me of the famous Visual Studio rant by Casey Muratori [VIDEO] And the hundreds (if not thousands :D) of rants by Jon Blow saying how contemporary software performance sucks"},{"id":35080303,"author":"Hikikomori","month":"2023-03","story":"Leveraging Rust and the GPU to render user interfaces at 120 FPS","stance":"reference","why":"Compares slow Visual Studio with RemedyBG built on Dear ImGui.","quote":"Casey ranting about how slow visual studio is and compares it to RemedyBG","text":"Casey ranting about how slow visual studio is and compares it to RemedyBG that uses Dear Imgui (an implementation of Immediate mode GUI). [VIDEO]"},{"id":35106369,"author":"fwlr","month":"2023-03","story":"“Clean Code, Horrible Performance” Discussion","stance":"reference","why":"Visual Studio watch window slowness as example of performance neglect.","quote":"the Visual Studio watch window (about 25 minutes into )","text":"The “not all parts have to be performant, so you can make those parts clean” objection never sits well with me. And it’s not just the fact that clean code is presented as universal and this claim is only introduced after you complain that clean code has made your code less performant (Casey was remarkably polite in pointing this out and Bob was very gracious in concurring, both of which were a delight to read). The reason it doesn’t sit well is that, in practice, it seems like most products don’t know what needs to be fast and so developers end up making almost everything slow. Casey gives many examples but the one that sticks out most in my mind is the Visual Studio watch window (about 25 minutes into [VIDEO] ). The watch window lets you pin variables you’re interested in and it will show you their current values as you step through the program’s execution. Invaluable for debugging. Visual Studio’s watch window is incredibly slow, to the point that it actually has a debounce so it will stop updating when you’re stepping quickly and only update once you’ve stopped quick-stepping. This seriously impacts its usefulness! To me, the watch window seems like it’s obviously one of those modules that needs to be running in milliseconds or less - but the company and/or the developers don’t see that, and shipped it slow (and presumably clean) instead. So in my mind, “write clean except where performance matters” comes with an almost fatal caveat, something like “you won’t know where pe"},{"id":34663545,"author":"norman784","month":"2023-02","story":"De-Bloated Windows 11 Build Runs on 2GB of RAM","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This remind me a some rants from Casey Muratori about VS[0]","text":"You can achieve good memory footprints with Linux, just 2 or 3 years ago I was daily driving Arch linux with bspwm as a window manager, it used only 300 mb, for me is pretty darn good, but as soon as I opened my vscode with a JS project my ram usage was at 12gb. We have a lot of bloatware everywhere, that’s pretty sad. edit: This remind me a some rants from Casey Muratori about VS[0] and windows terminal[1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":33696358,"author":"AnIdiotOnTheNet","month":"2022-11","story":"Criticizing Computers","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"None of which can explain why launching an Explorer window takes a perceptible amount of time, or this: [VIDEO] , or any of thousands of similar atrocities of wasting everyone's computing resources and time to accomplish nothing of note."},{"id":33333013,"author":"Yoofie","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: What is your fav “I can't believe in 2022 this still doesn't work right”","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of these videos from Casey Muratori","text":"Reminds me of these videos from Casey Muratori: 30 million line problem - [LINK] Twitter and Visual Studio Rant - [VIDEO]"},{"id":31075728,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2022-04","story":"Unreal vs. Unity Opinion","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Yep, a gross and incorrect one, invented only to advance an argument that doesn't match reality. Performance and ms-per-frame is still highly critical in AAA-land, I beg to differ it's not an incorrect one. AAA Titles (+ if I did play them): Destiny - Review say perf OK. Elden Ring(+) - Performance Ok on consoles on release it was a slideshow. It ran like garbage. Personally I think it's a clusterfuck GTA V(+) - Had a huge performance blunder. Released for n-thienth time. Good performance today for game that was released in 2013. FIFA/NFL/Sports game - Runs ok. It's the same game as year X-1 except with some tweaks (like microtransactions and ads). Call of Duty - Review while perf is Ok, it's not something Battlefield - They were Ok, they went too loose with optimization resulting in the shitfest that was 2042. They got punished for it. Fortnite - Sure stellar work from the guys making Unreal engine no complaints. According to revenue PUBG belongs on this list. PlayerUnknown’s Battlegrounds - Barely holding itself at the seams this game made mad money while being a buggy and lice infested mess. It Pokemon - It's Ok, but nothing to write home about. Resident Evil: Village(+) - Played it. It ran like shite. Had to crack it to get decent performance. Far Cry 6 - Ok performance, but essentially Farcry X-1. With more content. --------- From examples given, while performance is usually critical for online multiplayer shooters, it's not so much being faster as being less slow than"},{"id":29762679,"author":"garaetjjte","month":"2022-01","story":"Does Visual Studio rot the mind? (2005)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"(if you enjoy watching rants there's one","text":"It would be fine but it is slow. Watch window refreshes and responds to expanding trees like it is traversing dozens of gigabytes on every single action. (if you enjoy watching rants there's one: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":29228419,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2021-11","story":"Making life easier for newcomers to the .NET ecosystem","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Obligatory rant by Casey Muratori on Visual Studio","text":"Tried Visual Studio 22 Preview. It couldn't run a debugger because \"property program isn't defined\". Apparently you need to specify some launch.json file god knows where, and god knows why. VSCode kinda works (but also creates a launch.json file somewhere). Rider just works. If it creates something somewhere, I couldn't care less (as I couldn't care less for any other IntelliJ product which tend to just work). Also. Obligatory rant by Casey Muratori on Visual Studio: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27592877,"author":"miltondts","month":"2021-06","story":"Your CPU may have slowed down on Wednesday","stance":"reference","why":"Concrete example of Visual Studio startup and debugging slowness.","quote":"Here is one concrete example: visual studio startup and debugging.","text":"> Which software exactly is functionally the same yet consumes more resources? Can you give some specific examples? Here is one concrete example: visual studio startup and debugging. Source: [VIDEO] Maybe I could start collecting examples. I never did because it is so obvious. I mean, have you never updated android/iOS/windows on the same hardware? Regarding the \"exactly\" part in you sentence. Why would more features equal less performance? The only possible reason I can see this happening, is if you have features that run in parallel. Otherwise it's just another callback/if etc. That is, something to be triggered by the user that should have imperceptible impact (slightly more pressure on the instruction cache) on the performance of other features."},{"id":22933807,"author":"jstimpfle","month":"2020-04","story":"Playstation Architecture: A Practical Analysis","stance":"reference","why":"Casey Muratori demonstrates Visual Studio slowness.","quote":"Did you see how fast VS6 started on a machine from almost 20 years ago.","text":"> Which is why I find so ironic that many still think only languages like Assembly, C or C++ have a place in IoT on devices like ESP32. Short answer, Parkinson's law. When I browse the Web, open my Windows File Explorer, open Photoshop, open Visual Studio, open just a graphical application that needs GPU acceleration, or do whatever thing that should not be an issue, I find that Parkinson's law very much applies. [VIDEO] [VIDEO] [LINK] Wtf. Did you see how fast VS6 started on a machine from almost 20 years ago. Today I get depressed whenever I need to open Visual Studio, and I don't even know that I use any more functionality that wasn't available 20 years ago. Many many programmers (in my perception at least) are extremely dissatisfied with today's state of computing, and the reason why we got here is the popular opinion that we don't need to worry about performance. Nobody should optimize representations of Pi or count CPU cycles by default. That's not the point. But if you claim that Java is always fast enough for example, then I think we're having a strong disagreement. It's partly confirmation bias and being unaware of vast parts of the landscape, but it's also a fact, that I couldn't name you a complex GUI written in Java with satisfying ergonomics."}],"r":[["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1],["6wBrOV2FJM8",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1]]},"ILNs1GChGDk":{"wy":"Commenters recommend it as the best introduction to the history of the ANSI and ASCII art scene.","q":{"x":"If you want to watch a cool talk about the evolution of NFO and ascii (color) art scene and processes, I recommend watching","i":33700760,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33700760,"author":"rx_tx","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: What weird technical scene are you fond/part of?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on the evolution of the NFO and ASCII art scene.","quote":"If you want to watch a cool talk about the evolution of NFO and ascii (color) art scene and processes, I recommend watching","text":"If you want to watch a cool talk about the evolution of NFO and ascii (color) art scene and processes, I recommend watching [VIDEO]"},{"id":9401689,"author":"empire29","month":"2015-04","story":"ANSI Art Collection from Old BBSs","stance":"rec","why":"History of the ANSI art scene moving from BBSs to IRC.","quote":"are a good watches if youre interested.","text":"[LINK] is another great archive for ANSi art. The art scene in the mid 90s was an interesting, vibrant, microcosm, moving from BBS's to IRC over the course of a few years. [VIDEO] and the longer [LINK] are a good watches if youre interested."},{"id":8962947,"author":"tdicola","month":"2015-01","story":"Show HN: Artpacks.org – Archive of the ANSI and ASCII art scene (1990 to present)","stance":"rec","why":"BSides talk on archiving the ANSI and ASCII art scene.","quote":"There's a great presentation from BSides Las Vegas a few years ago about the ANSI art scene","text":"Nice work! There's a great presentation from BSides Las Vegas a few years ago about the ANSI art scene, definitely worth checking out if anyone is interested: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["XRXwWbo_mX0",1],["ON0A1dsQOV0",1],["E8kUJL04ELA",1]]},"KINIAgRpkDA":{"wy":"HN points to it for background on how the Go toolchain targets many architectures and why its assembler is designed that way.","q":{"x":"Its Rob Pike talking about the go assembler and he answers a few of your questions.","i":15177402,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15177402,"author":"fourseventy","month":"2017-09","story":"Go Assembly by Example","stance":"rec","why":"Rob Pike on the Go assembler answers related questions.","quote":"Its Rob Pike talking about the go assembler and he answers a few of your questions.","text":"This is relevant [VIDEO] Its Rob Pike talking about the go assembler and he answers a few of your questions."},{"id":14180995,"author":"bkeroack","month":"2017-04","story":"A Foray into Go Assembly Programming","stance":"rec","why":"Go assembler backend, autogenerating backends from ISA documentation PDFs.","quote":"Here's a good talk about the Go assembler backend","text":"Here's a good talk about the Go assembler backend: [VIDEO] Spoiler: they're nearly to the point (maybe now they've achieved it fully) of autogenerating a new language backend from ISA documentation PDFs! Bring that up next time someone complains about Go not using LLVM."},{"id":13610915,"author":"chubot","month":"2017-02","story":"Show HN: How to write a tiny compiler","stance":"rec","why":"Rob Pike on Go's single assembly language across architectures.","quote":"I watched this talk and it was pretty interesting","text":"Have you looked at the Go toolchain at all? I don't know much about it, but they implemented their back ends in a manner that is much smaller than LLVM and GCC. And it sounds like the architecture of the compiler is significantly different. I watched this talk and it was pretty interesting: [VIDEO] [LINK] If I am understanding right, the claim is that they have a single assembly language for ALL GO architectures, based on something Ken Thompson wrote in the 90's for some National Instruments (?) chip. He actually says that all assembly language looks the same now. I was sort of surprised by that claim. I would like to hear a critique of this from other compiler writers. Does the fact that Go uses a single assembly language make the code slower? I imagine taking advantage of target-specific knowledge is useful, but I don't know how much. LLVM has this pretty elaborate TableGen system to express target-specific knowledge. He also makes the claim that they will be able auto-generate a new backend from the PDF description of the architecture. And he says somewhere that they reduce a lot of the work to simple \"text processing\" (symbol manipulation) and didn't require opening up any processor manuals. I can see how arithmetic and bitshifting is the same among all architectures. But I would think that even loads and stores have differences, at least if you want to use the processor efficiently. But he says everything kinda looks the same. Also, another thought is that compiling to W"},{"id":43703136,"author":"MathMonkeyMan","month":"2025-04","story":"Show HN: Torque – A lightweight meta-assembler for any processor","stance":"reference","why":"Pike describes Go's common-denominator assembly translated via table lookups.","quote":"Rob Pike describes the common-denominator assembly language that the Go compiler generates.","text":"I'm reminded of a 2016 [talk][1] where Rob Pike describes the common-denominator assembly language that the Go compiler generates. Then that assembly is translated into machine-specific code via table lookups. See the 11 minute mark. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41880371,"author":"saclark11","month":"2024-10","story":"Show HN: Go Plan9 Memo","stance":"reference","why":"Why Go uses a common assembly language.","quote":"I think it basically came down to the observation that most assembly language is roughly the same","text":"> Overall, pretty weird stuff. I am not sure why the Go team went down this route. Maybe it simplifies the compiler by having this bespoke assembly format? Rob Pike spoke on the design of Go's assembler at a talk in 2016 [1][2]. I think it basically came down to the observation that most assembly language is roughly the same, so why not build a common assembly language that \"lets you talk to the machine at the lowest level and yet not have to learn a new syntax.\" It also enables them to automatically generate a working assembler given an instruction manual PDF for a new architecture as input. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":40518217,"author":"yencabulator","month":"2024-05","story":"Runtime code generation and execution in Go","stance":"reference","why":"Background on the Go assembler.","quote":"","text":"Generally the inline assembly is hand-written and must follow the platform rules, see \"Architecture-specific details\" in [LINK] The few tools that generate that asm, such as [LINK] , would also have follow the rules. You're not meant to take C source, crank it through GCC, and slurp that in as inline asm in Go. Background: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35274494,"author":"cxr","month":"2023-03","story":"The Simplicity of Single-File Golang Deployments","stance":"reference","why":"Pike's idea of reading machine-readable descriptions with a machine.","quote":"you have a machine readable description[...] why not read it with a machine?","text":"> I kind of miss cgi-bin. If we're ever to get back to a place where random \"power users\" can knock up a quick server to meet some computing need they have, easy deployment has to be a big part of that. Can we make it as easy to deploy as to post on Instagram? I'd be happy with a future that takes some cues from literate programming, where if you want to deploy some application, the way you do it is to upload a copy of the software manual/specification. This should be sufficient to \"teach\" the server how the application should behave. It's tempting to say \"Ah, sounds like super advanced ChatGPT-ops or something approaching real AGI\", but what I have in mind is something decidedly less magical. It's more akin to the sort of thing that Rob Pike brought up in his \"The Design of the Go Assembler\" talk (\"you have a machine readable description[...] why not read it with a machine?\"). < [VIDEO] >"},{"id":18735698,"author":"jhawk28","month":"2018-12","story":"Porting Alpine Linux to RISC-V","stance":"reference","why":"Common assembly makes porting the Go toolchain easy.","quote":"Go uses a common assembly with ways to covert into the specific architecture which makes porting the toolchain to new targets somewhat trivial.","text":"Go uses a common assembly with ways to covert into the specific architecture which makes porting the toolchain to new targets somewhat trivial. ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":15055546,"author":"znpy","month":"2017-08","story":"Orbit: An Optimizing Compiler for Scheme (1986) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Go compiler emits intermediate assembler translated to native targets.","quote":"I find this interesting because in a certain way, this reminds me of how the go assembler works","text":"Interestingly, one of the pages you link ( [LINK] ) links a paper (grep \"1986: Kelsey dissertation on rewrite-based compilation \") that shows the implementation of a compiler with multiple frontends (BASIC, Scheme, Pascal) using only source-to-source) transformation. I find this interesting because in a certain way, this reminds me of how the go assembler works ( [VIDEO] ). For those who haven't seen the talk: basically the go compiler works on the assumption that pretty much all computer, in a way, move memory from one address to another, generate and handle interrupts, have call/return instructions and so on. Thus, the go compiler emits (IIRC) some sort of \"intermediate assembler\" (somehow similar to llvm's intermediate representation, but simpler) and then proceeds to \"translate\" such assembler to various platform native assembler. I clearly remember Rob Pike (in the talk) saying \"so the task of supporting a new platform becomes a task in text processing\" (okay, maybe it was worded a bit better). This might be a bit tangential, but it is interesting how some \"new\" ideas aren't that new after all. But I guess this is due to Rob Pike's deep experience and history with computers."}],"r":[["xguam0TKMw8",1],["lHLpKzUxjGk",1],["R3xX37RGJKE",1],["YR5WdGrpoug",1],["DJtef410XaM",1],["CuD7SCqHB7k",1],["TgmA48fILq8",1],["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["7HKbjYqqPPQ",1],["gFEE3w7F0ww",1],["IeSu_odkI5I",1],["hFSPHfZQpIQ",1],["ds77e3aO9nA",1]]},"MShbP3OpASA":{"wy":"HN links it for individual clips: the Nvidia moment, his views on C and his blunt communication style. Some commenters say the whole talk is worth watching.","q":{"x":"the whole talk by itself is very recommendable","i":4450700,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":10598205,"author":"mreiland","month":"2015-11","story":"Software development: do good manners matter?","stance":"rec","why":"Conveys Linus's reasons for his abrasive style.","quote":"Here's another video that does a better job of conveying his reasons for being the way he is.","text":"That's a caricature of his actual position, please be more honest than that. edit: Here's another video that does a better job of conveying his reasons for being the way he is. [VIDEO] He's also been on record as saying multiple times that a lot of it is because he comes from a culture that's much more abrasive than US."},{"id":5408643,"author":"dmiladinov","month":"2013-03","story":"An Open Source Author's Lament","stance":"rec","why":"Linus on natural hierarchy in kernel development.","quote":"The whole video, IMHO is worth a watch","text":"I'd like to piggy-back on your mention of Linus. It reminds me of a recent video I saw wherein he talked about how the work on the kernel works within a natural hierarchy. The whole video, IMHO is worth a watch, but the relevant snippet is here, you can watch from about 15:57 to about 18:20 to get the idea: [VIDEO] I especially liked his comment at the end of this section: \"The way people work ... is inherent in our brains ... the whole development process ... works really well ... we used the hierarchy that just worked on its own, and that turns out to be the right hierarchy.\""},{"id":4450700,"author":"jcfrei","month":"2012-08","story":"What killed the Linux desktop","stance":"rec","why":"Linus explains Linux desktop lacks preinstalled OS presence; whole talk worth watching.","quote":"the whole talk by itself is very recommendable","text":"in a q&a round at aalto university in finland linus adressed the question why linux never took off on the desktop: the lack of being a pre-installed os. he mentions that without preinstalled operating systems there's now way to gain a significant market share in the desktop segment. the whole talk by itself is very recommendable: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42980056,"author":"zinekeller","month":"2025-02","story":"Asahi Linux lead developer Hector Martin resigns from Linux kernel","stance":"reference","why":"Linus's famous middle finger to Nvidia.","quote":"Linus brandishing his middle finger to Nvidia","text":"DrillShopper has already posted ( [LINK] ) the technical reason on why Linux does not do it this way: [LINK] Additionally, there is politics in play here. Not the politics that is normally discussed outside of HN, but the politics of having companies (at least) release the detailed specifications of their hardware. I cannot really state authoritatively what the Linux developers think on this side, but Linus brandishing his middle finger to Nvidia ( [VIDEO] ) says a lot of things."},{"id":41208091,"author":"throwaway1194","month":"2024-08","story":"Fedora Asahi Remix","stance":"reference","why":"Linus on benefits of a shared kernel.","quote":"Linus himself mentions this advantage here","text":"> I think that makes some sense for performance, too: people run databases where IO efficiency eventually translates to money saved every month, and then we desktop users benefit from many of those as well. Indeed, that's a huge advantage of Linux, the fact that we all run the same Linux kernel and benefit from improvements coming from all directions. Linus himself mentions this advantage here: [VIDEO] There's a nice recent benchmark here (Bcachefs vs. Btrfs vs. EXT4 vs. F2FS vs. XFS): [LINK] Bcachefs is starting to look really good against Btrfs, no APFS or ZFS though, but still interesting nonetheless."},{"id":32924662,"author":"dijit","month":"2022-09","story":"Next steps for Rust in the kernel","stance":"reference","why":"Linus on C mapping closely to assembly.","quote":"","text":"Steve Klabnik has done a lot for systems programming and that post is a lot more even-handed than you're suggesting. If, however, we're talking about the kernel then perhaps we should listen to what Torvalds says about this topic. \"People who designed C, designed it at a time where the language had to be geared towards the output, so when I read C I can think about the Assembly that it will create\": [VIDEO] Consider that he said this in a time with C++, even if Rust was in its infancy: it is not as tied to the assembly it produces. Not by a long shot."},{"id":28455062,"author":"PartiallyTyped","month":"2021-09","story":"Libwebsockets a powerful and lightweight pure C library","stance":"reference","why":"Linus on reasoning about C's compiled output.","quote":"","text":"Linus seems to disagree with you. Albeit old [VIDEO] It remains true. Rust uses references all over the place and reuses the same memory addresses within stack that once belonged to another object because the compiler can guarantee it, but that makes it much less reasonable to write. If you can reason with -O0 you can take it that your code will remain correct in later levels."},{"id":17706461,"author":"buth_lika","month":"2018-08","story":"Linus Torvalds on regressions","stance":"reference","why":"Linus on being straightforward and avoiding ambiguity.","quote":"Here's Linux talking about being straightforward, and why he cannot afford ambiguity","text":"I agree.. people can be dishonest and hurtful while being perfectly polite, just like they can be honest, kind and caring while swearing like sailors. That is the difference that matters. When you ignore that and just look at the form, you get [LINK] Here's Linux talking about being straightforward, and why he cannot afford ambiguity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15708130,"author":"icc97","month":"2017-11","story":"All 500 of the world's top 500 supercomputers are running Linux","stance":"reference","why":"Linus on supercomputer tuning benefiting mobile phones.","quote":"","text":"I remember Linus mentioning [0] about the major benefits of tuning Linux for super computers that came to be really useful for when trying to get performance out of a mobile phones decades later. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14103866,"author":"pmilot","month":"2017-04","story":"The Safe C Library (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Linus as experienced C programmer on C's merits.","quote":"listen to an actual experienced C programmer","text":"That's an unfair judgement toward the C development community as a whole. There are legitimate use cases for C. If you're writing low-level, bare-metal programs then there is no other portable programming language that offers better semantics. C programmers often laud that language because they can easily reason what the output of the compiler will be. That is an extremely important and rare property for a portable programming language. But hey, don't listen to me, listen to an actual experienced C programmer: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10545772,"author":"oDot","month":"2015-11","story":"Which language has the brightest future for replacing C: D, Go, Rust?","stance":"reference","why":"Linus on replacing C.","quote":"Here's Linus on replacing C","text":"While the post is fantastic in showing the pros/cons of the other languages, it doesn't talk about the advantages of C, advantages to some people at least. Here's Linus on replacing C: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8252255,"author":"conistonwater","month":"2014-09","story":"DebConf 2014's QA with Linus Torvalds [video]","stance":"reference","why":"The relevant part of the talk, with convincing reasoning.","quote":"I actually find his reasons quite convincing, I think he's got a point.","text":"This is the part of that talk, I believe: [VIDEO] I actually find his reasons quite convincing, I think he's got a point."},{"id":7467470,"author":"0xdeadbeefbabe","month":"2014-03","story":"Google propels Linux to the top","stance":"reference","why":"Linus counts Android as a victory.","quote":"Linus counted android as a victory in this video where he swears at nvidia","text":"\"obviously\" is an understatement :) Linus counted android as a victory in this video where he swears at nvidia [VIDEO]"},{"id":4121769,"author":"SkyMarshal","month":"2012-06","story":"Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds ","stance":"reference","why":"Linus answers a Q&A question on Nvidia Optimus support.","quote":"Linus responds that Nvidia is being really difficult about that without good reason","text":"TLDR: Linus at a Q&A, a woman asks why Nvidia still isn't providing any support for Optimus on Linux, Linus responds that Nvidia is being really difficult about that without good reason and \"Fuck You Nvidia!\" (flicks off the camera). Her question actually starts a minute earlier than the link: [VIDEO] PS - Bravo Linus. This is issue is a real PITA, and a bit incongruent considering the historically awesome driver support Nvidia has provided for Linux."},{"id":4121827,"author":"bingbing","month":"2012-06","story":"Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds ","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamped link to the Nvidia Q&A moment.","quote":"","text":"Your link didn't quite work for me, starting in the 49th minute somewhere, Using a fragment identifier to demarc the time offset works better for me: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4121959,"author":"kennywinker","month":"2012-06","story":"Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds ","stance":"reference","why":"Nvidia employee in audience responds politely to Linus.","quote":"a guy in the audience who works at Nvidia responds during Q&A, ever so politely.","text":"if you skip ahead to 1:00:30 a guy in the audience who works at Nvidia responds during Q&A, ever so politely. Linus responds, quite politely as well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":4121965,"author":"jofer","month":"2012-06","story":"Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds ","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamped link past the one hour mark.","quote":"","text":"Linking to a specific time over 1 hour isn't working correctly for some reason, for whatever it's worth. (Or at least it hasn't been for me, anyway...) Nevermind, we were both just doing it wrong. This should work: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4121971,"author":"AlexDanger","month":"2012-06","story":"Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds ","stance":"reference","why":"Asks what an Nvidia employee said later in the talk.","quote":"","text":"Later in the talk someone says they are working for nVidia and doing something with Tegra. I couldnt quite make it out, what is he saying? [VIDEO]"},{"id":4122501,"author":"faragon","month":"2012-06","story":"Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds ","stance":"reference","why":"Identifies the source talk of a Linus quote.","quote":"Link to the source (Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds)","text":"Link to the source (Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds): [VIDEO] I've found it while watching the video after the \"fuck you nVidia\" discussion: [LINK] Edit: Actual quote: \"I like offending people, because I think people who get offended should be offended\" (without the \"the\" before \"people\")."},{"id":4122043,"author":"flabbergasted","month":"2012-06","story":"Aalto Talk with Linus Torvalds ","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I guess the moderators were offended by a four letter word.","text":"I guess the moderators were offended by a four letter word. \"People who get offended should be offended!\" - Linus [VIDEO]"}]},"j4H9LL7A-nQ":{"wy":"Recommended as detailed math on rent versus buy, showing the choice is often roughly a wash.","m":[{"id":48283630,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2026-05","story":"The Cost of Owning a Home","stance":"rec","why":"Argues rent versus buy is often a wash; includes 5% rule of thumb.","quote":"","text":"Ben Felix, a Canadian portfolio manager that does personal finance videos and podcast, has been arguing for years that rent versus buy is often a wash financially, and that you should make the decision for non-financial resasons. * recent 2026 video: [LINK] * 2025a: [VIDEO] * 2025b: [LINK] * 2021: [LINK] * 2019: [LINK] The 2019 video goes over a handy \"5% rule of thumb\": start with the purchase price of home, take 5% of that: if your rent is less than that, better numbers-wise to continue renting (and investing the difference); if your rent is more, probably better numbers-wise to buy/own. The company he works for created a tool to examine the numbers: * [LINK]"},{"id":45244082,"author":"whymarrh","month":"2025-09","story":"My thoughts on renting versus buying","stance":"rec","why":"Popular finance YouTuber's detailed math on rent versus buy.","quote":"","text":"You’re right, the math is slightly more complicated than rent v. mortgage payment. Ben Felix, a popular financial YouTuber, made many a video about the math: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":45084446,"author":"zahlman","month":"2025-08","story":"Google: 'Your $1000 phone needs our permission to install apps now' [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Shows ownership dogma is overstated; the financial comparison is complex.","quote":"","text":"Sure, but that's demonstrably more to do with the actual financial position than the ownership status. If you own the house and no longer pay a mortgage then you're ipso facto wealthier — although you presumably do still pay property tax. During the period of the mortgage, while rent wouldn't necessarily be cheap, it would be cheaper than the TCO of the house. The overall financial consideration is complex and pro-ownership dogma is just dogma: see e.g. [VIDEO] . More importantly in my opinion, though (and especially for single childless people), rental housing is available in smaller and finer-grained quantities."},{"id":46081698,"author":"apsurd","month":"2025-11","story":"How wealth dies","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for reasoning about asset allocation.","quote":"","text":"C. it's the only one that has you in the arena. There's no guaranteed outcome, but money itself is a team-sport. I'm a simple man myself, so I'm answering in order to verify my own reasoning. A. will have negative returns from inflation. D. defends against inflation but is too conservative. B. There's arguably negative value in holding more equity in terms of opportunity cost since you already have > 200% position in equity. source: I believe this guy [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["zT58pzY41wA",1],["KPUlgSRn6e0",1],["Uwl3-jBNEd4",1],["MejbOFk7H6c",1],["qWKz3psejb0",1],["cj02_UeUnGQ",1],["Iyp_X3mwE1w",1],["QvW61K2s0tA",1],["LWGJA9i18Co",1],["o1Fyn_h6LKU",1],["8wlld3Z9wRc",1],["BCmz-fgp24E",1],["-VsmF9m_Nt8",1],["jHP942Livy0",1],["o9pEzgHorH0",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["_rBO8neWw04",1],["jSo5v5t4OQM",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1]]},"umatbZ0n4mE":{"wy":"HN points to it as a demonstration of the OP-1, a well-designed instrument and an accessible way to start making music.","q":{"x":"The OP-1 in action","i":13684719,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":18549339,"author":"neom","month":"2018-11","story":"Dropping Acid","stance":"rec","why":"OP-1 live looping demo for the musically inclined.","quote":"the OP-1 is a great tool[1][2].","text":"Seems like you really know your house. Not sure if you're musically inclined but if so the OP-1 is a great tool[1][2]. If you ever make it to nyc hit me up, I know James well and I'm sure he'd enjoy your thoughts around E2-E4. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14304647,"author":"GuiA","month":"2017-05","story":"Get started making music","stance":"rec","why":"OP-1 suggested as an accessible way into making music.","quote":"as an intermediary step, like another commenter suggested, an OP1","text":"Sounds like you need to get a modular synthesizer :) [LINK] Or, as an intermediary step, like another commenter suggested, an OP1: [VIDEO] llllllll is a fantastic community of people building/playing with new & experimental musical instruments: [LINK]"},{"id":13684719,"author":"kevinqi","month":"2017-02","story":"Ask HN: What're the best-designed things you've ever used?","stance":"rec","why":"Live looping demo of the Teenage Engineering OP-1 synthesizer.","quote":"The OP-1 in action","text":"Teenage Engineering OP-1, a music synthesizer. Really well built, buttons and knobs feel fantastic, and the display is super fun. The OP-1 in action: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9XkHBmE-N34",1],["2JAOTJxYqh8",1],["RlXdsyctD50",1],["AZX6awZq5Z0",1],["YycAzdtUIko",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["mht-1c4wc0Q",1],["xRel1JKOEbI",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["G0Q4UAiKacw",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["KePNhUJ2reI",1],["msVuCEs8Ydo",1]]},"Ly6USRwTHe0":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a quick way to see how the Krita AI plugin works and how it matches Photoshop's generative features.","q":{"x":"the video is mindblowing","i":38343041,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38343041,"author":"2Gkashmiri","month":"2023-11","story":"Krita AI Diffusion","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Open source Krita plugin matches Photoshop generative features","quote":"the video is mindblowing","text":"[VIDEO] the video is mindblowing because on one hand, adobe photoshop announced this as \"their own next big thing\" and here we have an open source software replicating this same thing, so cool. edit: this also means photoshop doesnt have the \"moat\" they seem to have built around the generative ai thing and their software."},{"id":42878807,"author":"haunter","month":"2025-01","story":"Krita 5.2.9 Released","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstration videos taught the workflow","quote":"I actually just learned from the original demonstration videos.","text":"Yes exactly. There are some written guides now but I actually just learned from the original demonstration videos. Copied what happened there and started tinkering around Most of the times if something didn't work for me that was mostly because my lack of knowledge about Krita [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":46708046,"author":"cthalupa","month":"2026-01","story":"Comic-Con Bans AI Art After Artist Pushback","stance":"reference","why":"Using Krita AI to help finish a traditional artwork","quote":"","text":"> there's been zero progress on actually meaningfully integrating AI into artists' workflow. This simply isn't true. An easy way someone who does most of their art more \"traditionally\" can use Krita to very much just use AI to help finish a project. [VIDEO] ComfyUI was also designed from the ground up to follow the same sort of node-based workflow that many CG artists are used to with things like Houdini, Blender, Substance Designer, etc., and includes all sorts of ways you can arbitrarily control where and how much the AI is used for."}],"r":[["15_-hgsX2V0",1],["Mw9qiV7XlFs",1],["Z8ZhQPaw4rE",1],["hBmhDf8cl0k",1]]},"gn8m5d74fk8":{"wy":"HN recommends it for Carmack's detailed engineering insight into VR rendering and latency challenges.","q":{"x":"Carmack's 2014 Oculus Connect keynote is well worth watching","i":9703410,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9703410,"author":"lhl","month":"2015-06","story":"Oculus Rift","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Carmack explains mobile GPU optimizations and latency challenges in VR rendering.","quote":"Carmack's 2014 Oculus Connect keynote is well worth watching","text":"While this may be true for high fidelity gaming, for moving some textured triangles around, mobile GPUs are fine. Also, they can (and do) perform much better clock-for-clock vs a PC (and especially a Mac) thanks to the ability to optimize drivers. For a fascinating discussion of the optimizations possible, Carmack's 2014 Oculus Connect keynote is well worth watching: [VIDEO] This talk was a slightly long but very informative discussion of the various challenges in reducing latency and dealing w/ PC rendering: [LINK]"},{"id":27687730,"author":"nickjj","month":"2021-06","story":"Note that I wouldn’t pass the listed minimum requirements","stance":"rec","why":"Carmack keynote on Oculus development focus.","quote":"there's a bunch of keynotes from Carmack around Oculus Connect on YouTube. Here's one from 2014","text":"> What was Carmack's development focus during Oculus? I'm not sure exactly but there's a bunch of keynotes from Carmack around Oculus Connect on YouTube. Here's one from 2014: [VIDEO] , and another one from a year later where he's live coding in a VR environment [LINK] . You'll find a bunch more if you search too. I remember skimming through them years ago not because I'm into VR development but I've been a life long fan of Carmack's work and like listening to him talk about things."},{"id":12903848,"author":"pfranz","month":"2016-11","story":"Inside Magic Leap","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack discusses rendering approaches like interlacing and partial scanline rendering for VR.","quote":"In talking about interlacing, he was ideally talking about not having to create a whole image buffer before sending data to the hardware","text":"Found where Carmack talked about it[1]. For VR, the consensus I've seen seem to push for multi-sample AA > full-screen AA > temporal AA (I feel like I've even seen no AA is better than temporal AA). I'm not quite sure if it's performance, architecture (they really prefer forward renderers instead of deferred renderers for low latency), or aesthetics. In the little bit I've done, when just playing with knobs temporal AA looks better to me. Without AA specular highlights are way too distracting and pop too much. This reference[2] talks about the extra velocity buffer needed for temporal AA and the fact it tends to over-blur which can fuzz out fine details (low resolution is a touchy subject in VR). There's a lot of moving pieces when making decisions. In talking about interlacing, he was ideally talking about not having to create a whole image buffer before sending data to the hardware--just rendering parts (well, scanline) of the image that moved. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":8794519,"author":"barium","month":"2014-12","story":"Why movies look weird at 48fps, and games are better at 60fps","stance":"reference","why":"Carmack on programmable interlacing for HMD displays","quote":"he talks about trying to persuade Samsung to integrate programmable interlacing into their displays","text":"Speaking of \"frameless\" rendering, I noticed during Carmack's Oculus keynote ( [VIDEO] ), he talks about trying to persuade Samsung to integrate programmable interlacing into their displays in order to give dynamic per-frame control over which lines are being scanned. This would give you the same \"adaptive per-pixel updating\" seen in your link, though primarily to tackle the problems with HMDs (low-persistence at high frame-rates)."},{"id":9667303,"author":"DanAndersen","month":"2015-06","story":"Presentation anti-patterns","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"legs planted firmly on the stage like a colossus","text":"Excellent list of things to keep in mind. Of course, being The Statue is allowed, if you happen to be John Carmack, legs planted firmly on the stage like a colossus: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["qpoRO378qRY",1],["480xteW2wq4",1],["V8DfjXnIk6c",1],["VBwWbFpkltg",1]]},"sgHbC6udIqc":{"wy":"Commenters point to it as the clearest explanation of Python's bytes versus Unicode model and the Unicode sandwich, which finally makes the confusion click.","q":{"x":"After A TON OF PAIN I decided to put the time in to watch the talk","i":19949547,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19949547,"author":"jteppinette","month":"2019-05","story":"“Python's batteries are leaking”","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made Python strings and the unicode sandwich click after much pain.","quote":"After A TON OF PAIN I decided to put the time in to watch the talk","text":"You have to invest some time in understanding how strings (byte strings and unicode) work in Python. I put this off for way too long. After A TON OF PAIN I decided to put the time in to watch the talk \"Pragmatic Unicode, or, How do I stop the pain?\"[0] by Ned Batchelder and it all just clicked. Now, I make unicode sandwiches like a boss. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8909383,"author":"ptx","month":"2015-01","story":"Why Perl Didn't Win","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how Python 3 handles Unicode by decoding explicitly at input.","quote":"Ned Batchelder gave an excellent talk[1] that explains how the Python 3 approach to Unicode works.","text":"From a quick skimming through the docs[0], Perl 5.8's Unicode support sounds a lot like Python 2's except that the default encoding is latin1 instead of ASCII - i.e. unlike the Python 3 way of explicitly decoding binary data into Unicode text at the point where it's read (where presumably the encoding of the data is known), it will defer the decoding until the data is used (at some completely unrelated point in the program) and decode it with some assumed globally agreed upon encoding. Since there is no globally agreed encoding (Windows even has different legacy encodings between Win32 and the command prompt!) this will appear to work as long as the data is ASCII but later, when you least expect it (and someone inputs a non-ASCII string), give UnicodeDecodeError in Python 2 or garbage data in Perl 5.8. Ned Batchelder gave an excellent talk[1] that explains how the Python 3 approach to Unicode works. I think it makes a lot more sense once you understand it; the Python 2 way was clearly broken, and it looks like Perl 5 has the same problem but hides it better. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15711739,"author":"thanksgiving","month":"2017-11","story":"Numpy: Plan for dropping Python 2.7 support","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as illustration of Python bytes versus Unicode confusion.","quote":"","text":"I don't know the details (because I don't understand unicode) but I am not sure what you said is possible. But to me ASCII seems like a hindrance. ``` fmt.Println(\"Hello, 世界\") ``` This line of go lang looks beautiful. I bet they never have to worry about bytes vs Unicode. Here is python... [VIDEO] I'll add one more assertion to the list. People are born and people die. It is not fair for us to impose our mistakes and sins to the next generation. With that being said, my thought was simply all the scripts that are in python (even the simple build for Firefox was python 2 only last time I checked). The way forward for anyone new to python is for us to tell them: Python 2 doesn't matter. Don't look at it. Learn python by which I mean 3.0+ I still don't get why Google of all places can't support python 3 in its standard app engine. To me it sounds like standard is deprecated for the flexible (which doesn't have a free tier)."},{"id":9275710,"author":"ptx","month":"2015-03","story":"Learn Python the Hard Way","stance":"reference","why":"Source for the Unicode sandwich approach.","quote":"","text":"Python 3 is useful for a beginner to start with because they don't have to learn the deprecated cruft that Python 2 preserves for compatibility. If they just learn the Python 3 way of doing things, it generally works in both 2 and 3. In his explanation of classes he has several paragraphs about old-style vs. new-style classes, ending it with: \"Just completely ignore the idea of old style versus new style classes and assume that Python always requires (object) when you make a class. Save your brain power for something important.\" Python 3 only has new-style classes, so the entire explanation could have been left out, allowing the beginner to, as he recommends, focus on more interesting things. Another example is his advice, in exercise 11, to avoid the input() function because of its security problems. Python 3 doesn't have that function, so the beginner doesn't have to remember to avoid it. And of course, there's Unicode, which this book seems to completely ignore. A beginner starting with Python 3 has to learn the \"Unicode sandwich\" approach[1], which applies very well to Python 2 code as well. But someone starting with Python 2 can easily be confused about the concept (because the language is confused) and will have a hard time getting things to work correctly. For example, the book recommends that people \"from another country\" set their source encoding to UTF-8 – good luck printing things on Windows. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"7cC3_jGwl_U":{"wy":"HN points to it as the foundational argument for sans-I/O protocol library design.","q":{"x":"give some really great brainfood about the way currently all kinds of network protocols are implemented","i":15833317,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15833317,"author":"mindcrash","month":"2017-12","story":"Ask HN: Separate Logic from IO","stance":"rec","why":"Great brainfood on separating protocol logic from IO.","quote":"give some really great brainfood about the way currently all kinds of network protocols are implemented","text":"In some cases it is very practical, even in other (non functional) programming languages. For example, [LINK] (and its accompanying talk [VIDEO] ) give some really great brainfood about the way currently all kinds of network protocols are implemented, and the benefits of separating the protocol (Logic) from the actual implementation (IO). You might also want to query Google or DDG for \"Free monads\" if you want to implement a similar layered approach in a functional language such as Scala or Haskell."},{"id":11847831,"author":"geofft","month":"2016-06","story":"Accidentally nonblocking","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for separating I/O from protocol parsing across frameworks.","quote":"makes the argument that a large number of problems can be traced to not cleanly separating responsibilities","text":"Cory Benfield's PyCon talk last week, \"Building Protocol Libraries the Right Way\" ( [VIDEO] ), makes the argument that a large number of problems can be traced to not cleanly separating responsibilities of actually physically doing I/O and making semantic sense of the bytes. His primary worry was about reimplementing things like HTTP many times, once for each I/O framework (why do Twisted, Tornado, and asyncio all have their own HTTP implementation?). But it seems the same problem can be seen here: every single part of the code thinks it knows how to actually retrieve data from the network, so it interacts with the network on its own, causing nested polling and similar awkwardness. If every part of the event-processing code thinks it knows how to do network I/O, you have many more opportunities for getting network I/O wrong. If xterm were designed so that e.g. xevents() had only the responsibility of fetching bytes from the X socket and do_xevents() and everything else had only the responsibility of handling bytes from an buffer, there would be no temptation to poll in two different functions. Only one function would even know that the byte source is a socket; the rest just know about the buffer."},{"id":39958452,"author":"dloss","month":"2024-04","story":"WinBtrfs – an open-source btrfs driver for Windows","stance":"reference","why":"Talk linked from the sans-io page.","quote":"The above sans-io page links to this PyCon 2016 talk","text":"The above sans-io page links to this PyCon 2016 talk: Cory Benfield - Building Protocol Libraries The Right Way [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["pVI_smLgTY0",1],["cidchWg74Y4",1]]},"SBvclVcesEE":{"wy":"Linked in Panama Canal threads as a clear diagrammatic explanation of how locks save water.","q":{"x":"this old Practical Engineering video explains with diagrams","i":37056389,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38255059,"author":"FriedPickles","month":"2023-11","story":"Panama Canal is so congested that one ship owner just paid $4M to skip the line","stance":"rec","why":"Side ponds trade water consumption for land.","quote":"explaining how reservoirs can be used to trade-off water consumption for land requirements using side ponds","text":"I enjoyed this well made video illustrating how it works: [LINK] And this one explaining how reservoirs can be used to trade-off water consumption for land requirements using side ponds: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37056389,"author":"stickfigure","month":"2023-08","story":"Panama Canal Draught Restrictions Spark Liner Surcharges","stance":"rec","why":"Diagrams explaining how new locks reuse water without pumps.","quote":"this old Practical Engineering video explains with diagrams","text":"For anyone interested in how the new (bigger) locks use less water than the old locks (reclaiming 60% of the water - without pumps!), this old Practical Engineering video explains with diagrams: [VIDEO] They store the water in a series of basins at different levels, and use those basins to fill the portion of the lock below. It's more obvious with diagrams."},{"id":38988714,"author":"krisoft","month":"2024-01","story":"Panama Canal drought forces Maersk to start using land bridge for Oceania cargo","stance":"reference","why":"How to construct a water-saving lock.","quote":"here is a video on how one can construct a “water saving lock” from Practical Engineering","text":"> without saying anything about which one is used how and where I agree that it is strangely edited. But to help you out with the info: both are used on the Panama canal. There are two paralel sets of locks, the older ones and a more modern ones. The more modern ones can fit a bit bigger ships and they are more efficient with water. This is a wikipedia page about the expansion project (the one which also constructed these more modern locks): [LINK] And here is a video on how one can construct a “water saving lock” from Practical Engineering: [VIDEO]"},{"id":39042858,"author":"nostrademons","month":"2024-01","story":"A drought has forced authorities to further slash traffic in Panama Canal","stance":"reference","why":"Supports the point about storage basins in locks.","quote":"","text":"They do pump water out and back into the locks from neighboring storage. There is a limit to the minimum amount of water that you can use, though, since the potential energy to raise the ship comes from the loss of water. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["lqzJewjZUkk",1],["YpuX-5E7xoU",1],["31CcclqEiZw",1]]},"c0O6UXrOZJo":{"wy":"The standard reference whenever HN discusses the Xerox scanner bug or AI and compression that fabricates content.","q":{"x":"There a few videos of David Kriesel explaining the bug for the curious","i":24132919,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":36305410,"author":"lynx23","month":"2023-06","story":"","stance":"rec","why":"A neat CCC presentation about the Xerox document-altering scanner bug.","quote":"the CCC had a pretty neat presentation at that time as well","text":"Even though this happened a long time ago, whenever I hear or think about it, I am a mazed that it didn't put Xerox out of business, or at least hurt a little more. After all, some big players were already doing digital archiving at the time. :-/ BTW, the CCC had a pretty neat presentation at that time as well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24132919,"author":"bXVsbGVy","month":"2020-08","story":"PDF: Still unfit for human consumption, 20 years later","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the Xerox scanner compression bug that altered digits in scans.","quote":"There a few videos of David Kriesel explaining the bug for the curious","text":"> You may remember that this played a strange role in Obama's birth certificate some years back Just to clarify, the issue with the compression algorithm used by the scanner, not with the PDF format itself. There a few videos of David Kriesel explaining the bug for the curious: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47577284,"author":"Terr_","month":"2026-03","story":"OCR For construction documents does not work, we fixed it","stance":"reference","why":"Covers the JBIG2 scanner bug that silently replaced numbers in documents.","quote":"I'm reminded of the Xerox JBIG2 bug back in ~2013","text":"> OCR for construction documents does not work I'm reminded of the Xerox JBIG2 bug back in ~2013, where certain scan settings could silently replace numbers inside documents, and bad construction-plans were one of the cases that led to it being discovered. [0] It wasn't overt OCR per se , users weren't trying to convert pixels to characters. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29209075,"author":"maxnoe","month":"2021-11","story":"Confused Xerox copiers rewrite scanned documents, expert finds (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"English-language version of the talk on the Xerox scanning bug.","quote":"And the talk he gave in English himself here","text":"There is a version with an English voice over of the original talk here: [LINK] And the talk he gave in English himself here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26458484,"author":"dorgo","month":"2021-03","story":"Adobe Photoshop's 'super resolution' made my jaw hit the floor","stance":"reference","why":"Describes Xerox scanners silently replacing digits in scanned documents.","quote":"Xerox scancopiers literally are making stuff up","text":"Sounds like the next iteration of \"Lies, damned lies and scans\": [VIDEO] >On the scale of things too horrible to contemplate, \"document-altering scanner\" is right up there with \"flesh-eating bacteria\". Since 2006, Xerox scancopiers literally are making stuff up. They, for example, replace digits with others in scans."}],"r":[["EtsXgODHMWk",1]]},"lD9FAOPBiDk":{"wy":"Cited in threads about IE's reputation as an example of Microsoft's self-aware, funny advertising.","q":{"x":"Interesting and funny video, regardless of if you think anything changed.","i":5980027,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5980027,"author":"nwilliams","month":"2013-07","story":"Internet Explorer 11: “Don’t call me IE”","stance":"rec","why":"Funny Microsoft ad aimed at web developers.","quote":"Interesting and funny video, regardless of if you think anything changed.","text":"That comment reminded me of the Microsoft ad video targeted at web devs. [VIDEO] Interesting and funny video, regardless of if you think anything changed."},{"id":4887641,"author":"sharkweek","month":"2012-12","story":"Microsoft's DroidRage Twitter campaign goes painfully wrong","stance":"rec","why":"Microsoft IE ad seen as clever long-term image building.","quote":"pretty clever for a long term hearts-and-minds strategy","text":"I thought this was \"hip\" or whatever you want to call it, and pretty clever for a long term hearts-and-minds strategy -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":4891128,"author":"mynameisvlad","month":"2012-12","story":"Microsoft's DroidRage Twitter campaign goes painfully wrong","stance":"rec","why":"Humorous IE10 ad as part of Microsoft's improved campaigns.","quote":"Their newest ads are pretty awesome.","text":"Their newest ads are pretty awesome. Surface's dancing ad [LINK] , the IE9 dubstep [LINK] , the IE10 troll [VIDEO] , and the various Windows 8 international musical ads (the wave after the countdown ad) [LINK] [LINK] and [LINK] are definitely a step way up from previous ad campaigns that Microsoft launched. All in all, the Windows 8 ads are IMO fresh, and definitely leaning more towards the younger adult hip side. And they're pulling it off pretty nicely."},{"id":5086320,"author":"devcpp","month":"2013-01","story":"Microsoft hires designer behind \"The Next Microsoft\", Andrew Kim","stance":"reference","why":"Microsoft ad acknowledging how bad IE used to be.","quote":"We even do funny ads acknowledging how bad we were","text":"A few days ago, a Microsoft employee who has worked on Windows 7 and 8 said on a Reddit AMA: \"There is no doubt that we have catching up to do. We even do funny ads acknowledging how bad we were: [VIDEO] \" They still know they're not perfect but they seem to think they're good enough already. I hope one day they learn to do things right."},{"id":4891083,"author":"rospaya","month":"2012-12","story":"Microsoft's DroidRage Twitter campaign goes painfully wrong","stance":"reference","why":"IE karaoke ad as an example of decent Microsoft ads.","quote":"Internet Explorer karaoke wasn't bad","text":"Some WP ads were great. \"Really?\" was simple and to the point: [LINK] Internet Explorer karaoke wasn't bad: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4848898,"author":"runjake","month":"2012-11","story":"Ask HN: What would it take for IE not to be hated?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Direct link to video for others who don't read TechCrunch: [VIDEO] I don't hate IE, but for me to consider start using it, it would need to run on all major platforms and be open ala Chrome and Firefox. Otherwise, I don't see the point."}],"r":[["LtFyP0qy9XU",1],["SR1jwwagtaQ",1],["a0atL1HSwi8",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1],["_fZOl7C_vDI",1],["BzB5xtGGsTc",1],["GFE4rkSaKOY",1],["ojm74VGsZBU",1]]},"p_iZr2-Coqc":{"wy":"Linked in salt mine and industrial accident threads as a fascinating, quick account with striking footage.","q":{"x":"Quick 8-minute documentary on it that I found interesting","i":38708667,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38708667,"author":"ljoshua","month":"2023-12","story":"Was an ancient bacterium awakened by an industrial accident?","stance":"rec","why":"Short documentary with footage of the accident","quote":"Quick 8-minute documentary on it that I found interesting","text":"Also fascinating because we have video footage of it, and it’s as crazy as it sounds. Quick 8-minute documentary on it that I found interesting: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22781105,"author":"kyuudou","month":"2020-04","story":"What is the Strategic Petroleum Reserve?","stance":"rec","why":"Footage of a barge pulled into the whirlpool","quote":"still blows my mind","text":"I bet you know about that salt cavern that collapsed at Lake Peigneur[1]. There should be a documentary on youtube where you can see the footage, including a really big boat going \"phoomph\" into the whirlpool created by the bayou water filling in the cavern. Edit: here's one[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] still blows my mind"},{"id":47584316,"author":"wewewedxfgdf","month":"2026-03","story":"One of the largest salt mines in the world exists under Lake Erie","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Reminds me of the Lake Peigneur disaster in 1980 in Louisiana when an oil drilling rig entered a salt mine located under the lake. [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22158431,"author":"tunap","month":"2020-01","story":"Detroit's salt mine: City beneath the city","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary of the Lake Peigneur drilling accident","quote":"Fascinating story","text":"Fascinating story, even more amazing nobody died when you realize barges were sucked down into it and the ocean water ran miles(?) inland to fill the void. Saw this documentary on TV a decade or so ago. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["hSrGfElyfVE",1],["OINAk2xl8Bc",1],["24OXzIRIiMQ",1],["iY57ErBkFFE",1],["hs3piaN4b5I",1]]},"vt-zXEsJ61U":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clear explanation of self-custody and of why to move coins off exchanges.","q":{"x":"Here's a talk by the venerable Andreas Antonopoulos on this subject","i":15862289,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":16250642,"author":"bastijn","month":"2018-01","story":"Bitfinex fails to perform promised audits","stance":"rec","why":"Full seminar on securing bitcoin","quote":"The full seminar is worth watching as well","text":"Obligatory matching advice: [LINK] The full seminar is worth watching as well: “How do I secure my bitcoin?” — Andreas Antonopoulos [VIDEO]"},{"id":15862289,"author":"brndnmtthws","month":"2017-12","story":"How I recovered cryptocurrency from a broken laptop","stance":"rec","why":"Antonopoulos on moving coins off exchanges","quote":"Here's a talk by the venerable Andreas Antonopoulos on this subject","text":"Please make sure you take your coins off of Coinbase (or any other exchange) as soon as you can. Here's a talk by the venerable Andreas Antonopoulos on this subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15308691,"author":"gst","month":"2017-09","story":"Announcing Ethereum and Litecoin vaults","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why to hold your own keys","quote":"Not holding the keys for my own coins is not an option","text":"Didn't have a multisig BTC wallet before (created one in the past, but deleted it months ago) and I don't seem to be able to add another multisig wallet - only those multi-email-confirmation-required wallets. Not holding the keys for my own coins is not an option (see [VIDEO] for why), but at least there are alternatives such as Ledger hardware wallets."}]},"yvBR71D0nAQ":{"wy":"HN points to it as an authoritative look at DynamoDB internals and as evidence it is a complex distributed system.","q":{"x":"here's a talk you might find interesting from one of the folks who built and ran DDB for a long time","i":30009415,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30009415,"author":"mjb","month":"2022-01","story":"DynamoDB 10 years later","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by a builder of DynamoDB on its internals.","quote":"here's a talk you might find interesting from one of the folks who built and ran DDB for a long time","text":"We don't have a paper on DynamoDB's internals (yet?), but here's a talk you might find interesting from one of the folks who built and ran DDB for a long time: [VIDEO] And Doug Terry talking through the details of how DynamoDB's transaction protocol works: [LINK] If we did publish more about the internals of DDB, what would you be looking to learn? Architecture? Operational experience? Developer experience? There's a lot of material we could share, and it's useful to hear where people would like us to focus."},{"id":20253237,"author":"appwiz","month":"2019-06","story":"Learning to Build Distributed Systems","stance":"rec","why":"DynamoDB internals talk from re:Invent 2018.","quote":"Jaso talking about DynamoDB internals","text":"Here are a couple videos from reInvent 2018: Jaso talking about DynamoDB internals [VIDEO] Marc talking about Lambda internals [LINK]"},{"id":18871753,"author":"talawahtech","month":"2019-01","story":"Amazon DocumentDB, with MongoDB compatibility","stance":"reference","why":"Shows DynamoDB is a complex distributed system.","quote":"Saying DynamoDB is built on top of InnoDB is a pretty big oversimplification of a much more complex distributed system","text":"Saying DynamoDB is built on top of InnoDB is a pretty big oversimplification of a much more complex distributed system[1] and for all we know they could have switched out the low level the storage engine on the backend to something like RocksDB or WiredTiger. The Aurora storage subsystem is much more limited in terms of horizontal scalability and performance, they probably chose it because it was a better/quicker fit. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18586339,"author":"manigandham","month":"2018-12","story":"Amazon's cloud business is competing with its own customers","stance":"reference","why":"Deep dive on DynamoDB internals, consistent with MySQL storage nodes.","quote":"The latest 2018 ReInvent deep dive on DynamoDB doesn't reveal anything","text":"DynamoDB is different from their published paper, which is mostly about designing a highly available key/value system that can be run on top of any other datastore. The paper does mention MySQL as a storage option. The recent MySQL info comes from this 2016 thread on DynamoDB storing empty strings: [LINK] Confirmed by this comment specifically: [LINK] The latest 2018 ReInvent deep dive on DynamoDB doesn't reveal anything but still fits if mysql is powering the storage nodes: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QdzV04T_kec",1]]},"I9LZ6TnSP40":{"wy":"HN cites it as early evidence that visual and interactive programming can go well beyond wire diagrams, in a Smalltalk and Alan Kay tradition.","q":{"x":"here's a demo of something called \"Alternate Reality Kit\"[2] back in 1986. Brace yourselves.","i":18334780,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18334780,"author":"rgrau","month":"2018-10","story":"Soup – Alan Kay on Objects","stance":"strong_rec","why":"1986 demo of Alternate Reality Kit from PARC.","quote":"here's a demo of something called \"Alternate Reality Kit\"[2] back in 1986. Brace yourselves.","text":"Yoshiki Ohshima regularly posts interesting \"new\" videos on his youtube channel[1]. Not only Alan Kay's talks but content produced in PARC back in the day, or relevant people around that environment. If you need a less abstract teaser before getting into the talks and \"philosophy\" part of the game, here's a demo of something called \"Alternate Reality Kit\"[2] back in 1986. Brace yourselves. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26158765,"author":"romaniv","month":"2021-02","story":"Functorio","stance":"reference","why":"1986 example of making programming feel like a computer game.","quote":"In 1986 people wanted to make real programming more like a computer game","text":"In 1986 people wanted to make real programming more like a computer game: [VIDEO] Today, when we have much more computational resources, I'm reading this article about representing Factorio as static text with compiler checks. Something somewhere is off."},{"id":20395865,"author":"gambler","month":"2019-07","story":"The challenges of teaching software engineering","stance":"reference","why":"Example of Smalltalk-style programmable interfaces.","quote":"","text":"Sounds more like \"the challenge of using a mish-mash of non-integrated tools with no interface\". Developers could definitively solve this by moving away from 70+ year old abstractions (files, terminal) towards things more in line with Smalltalk ideas. But I guess it's better to make thousands of beginners suffer each year, instead of admitting that the current way of doing things is conceptually flawed and outdated. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] (And no, there is nothing special about copy-pasting commands and Unix pipelines that cannot be easily recreated in UI if the UI is designed to be programmable. )"},{"id":19649043,"author":"gambler","month":"2019-04","story":"Apply Unix Principles to Write Testable Microservices","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"The reason people still care about Unix pipes is because you can take a bunch of arbitrary commands and string them together in a way that produces some useful results. Moreover, you can configure each pipe to do different things depending on what you need at the moment. What stops you from stringing together a bunch of arbitrary objects to achieve the same effect? Well, a bunch of things: 1. Verbose syntax of the language. 2. The need to compile new code. 3. Early binding that stops you from calling things you don't know about before compiling code. 4. The fact that objects can't just take arbitrary method arguments. #1 is self-imposed. #2 is solved by JIT compilation and some other technique. #3 is not a problem in dynamically typed languages. Most importantly, #4 is explicitly what polymorphism in OOP was supposed to solve. Poly = many. Morphos = shapes. The idea that the object adapts to the messages you send it. With this in mind, ls -a | grep “gear” can be interpreted as code that does the following: 1. Send the object representing 'ls' a message with -a argument. This will supposedly construct a list. 2. Send 'grep' object a message with \"gear\" argument. This will construct a configured grep instance. 3. Send response from #1 as a message (or stream of messages) to #2. This will generate a filtered list. 4. Send the object resulting from #3 a message that would request a console-friendly representation (e.g. a string with codes for color in console). 5. Display #5. Thi"},{"id":18495715,"author":"gambler","month":"2018-11","story":"Visual Programming – Why It’s a Bad Idea","stance":"reference","why":"Prior work showing visual programming beyond wire diagrams.","quote":"There is more to visual programming than simple wire diagrams and \"code-blocks-as-visual-blocks\"","text":"There is more to visual programming than simple wire diagrams and \"code-blocks-as-visual-blocks\": [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] And yes, this: [LINK] This is not 100% real programming, because it does not include sensors, but you do create constructs that interact with the environment and accomplish goals. It's close enough to show what's possible with certain interfaces. Long-term: [LINK] There have been much fewer resources allocated to the development of these concepts. Mostly for historic reasons. The sad reality is that both critics and proponents of visual programming are often uninformed about prior work, user studies and related research rooted in cognitive and developmental psychology."}]},"NM-zWTU7X-k":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference in threads on physics theories, models and what real understanding means.","q":{"x":"I reference it because I already felt the same in my work on A.I","i":10274074,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10274074,"author":"dframe","month":"2015-09","story":"Visions of Future Physics","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman on knowing versus understanding and keeping multiple ideas","quote":"Watch the Feynman video and truly grasp what he is saying.","text":"You and the person's comment above you are missing the point. First watch : [LINK] (A.I and physics) are quite related and workable ^I reference the above video not because I watched it and thought (hey that makes sense). I reference it because I already felt the same in my work on A.I and felt (wow someone else gets it) Also : [VIDEO] It's good to keep all sorts of working ideas/knowledge in your head when you're working towards solving a problem. All the physics experiments in the world aren't going to give you insight into problems unless you have workable ideas for how to integrate the findings into understanding/knowledge. What people often error in is that you can keep hacking away at and brute force your way to knowledge/understanding ... “A theory can be proved by experiment; but no path leads from experiment to the birth of a theory.” — Albert Einstein Ancient philosophers solved a lot of problems. More than you seem to be giving credit to. It's from their ideas that people knew where to look to 'prove' things. Watch the Feynman video and truly grasp what he is saying."},{"id":43509964,"author":"gloomyday","month":"2025-03","story":"Learning Theory from First Principles [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman lecture on holding multiple equivalent theories for guessing","quote":"","text":"The pursuit of knowledge is not a linear path. The reason you benefit from deep learning now is because a few people in the past believed neural networks had a future despite not working as well as other techniques such as SVMs. Discovering knowledge and using the knowledge that works best are very different. Your argument remind me from this lecture from Feynman. Quoting him: \"...and every theoretical physicist that is any good knows 6 or 7 different theoretical representations for exactly the same physics and knows that they are equivalent... but he keeps them in his head hoping that they'll give him different ideas for guessing.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":32417891,"author":"RichardCA","month":"2022-08","story":"Dark Matter Doesn't Exist","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman explaining the role of ideas versus proof in physics","quote":"Feynman explained it better than I ever will.","text":"There is always a tension in Physics between physical proof and observability on the one hand, and having the right idea, the right intuition on the other. Going back to my Einstein example, there were other Physicists who contributed to Relativity (Lorentz, Minkowski, Poincaré, Fitzgerald) but we give Einstein the most credit because he had the right idea, that it's not enough to treat the speed of light as a constant in your math, you actually have to believe the Universe works that way. One of the stories that gets told is the reason Einstein never got a Nobel prize for Relativity is because the Nobel committee just wasn't ready to give it up and credit Einstein with discovering a new, fundamental truth about the Universe. Another story that gets told is that when Einstein was an undergrad at the ETH in Zurich, Minkowski was one of his professors and he dismissed Einstein for being a \"Lazy Dog\" because the math never came easy to him. But he looked at how Physics was taught at the time and knew something had to change. And he was right about that. I'll just leave this here, Feynman explained it better than I ever will. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22873989,"author":"comment_ran","month":"2020-04","story":"The Wolfram Physics Project","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on testing theories and finding new ideas","quote":"This reminds me what Feynman talk to us","text":"This reminds me what Feynman talk to us: [VIDEO] > If you can find any other view of the world which agrees over the entire range where things have already been observed, but disagrees somewhere else, you have made a great discovery. It is very nearly impossible, but not quite, to find any theory which agrees with experiments over the entire range in which all theories have been checked, and yet gives different consequences in some other range, even a theory whose different consequences do not turn out to agree with nature. A new idea is extremely difficult to think of. It takes a fantastic imagination."},{"id":11733786,"author":"pvillano","month":"2016-05","story":"The brain is not a computer","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on why good models matter","quote":"Feynman explains this better","text":"I think you're missing some key points - Good models are important While the traditional computer model is good at explaining behavior, it doesn't help us ask new questions. And no matter how well what we currently know brain fits the traditional computer model, if it can't be used to inspire new questions then we need to explore new models. Feynman explains this better [ [VIDEO] ]. Even though our finite non-magic brains could fit in a computer, a computer isn't the best metaphor. - Computers don't act like brains by themselves. In any FUZZY LOGIC compiled and ran on a computer, all your code is translated into functions and data. LOSSY COMPRESSION is actually the application of experiments on perception, and not an inherent property of computers. A jpg is designed to look the same while using less space, but the information from it is not integrated in the same way as a human. Our MEMORY is not like a computer's. It is shockingly constructive, something that isn't expected with the computer model. - New programming paradigms might be better models. My beef with the metaphor is that is has very separate memory and computation. I don't think the brain \"reads and writes from memory\". I think it creates and updates a mesh of functions. Something like FUNCTIONAL PROGRAMMING could be used to model this. What if instead of looking for a neural hard drive, we treat each neuron as a function? What if generation of a dollar bill is actually OPTIMIZATION of our recognition function? W"}],"r":[["rbsqaJwpu6A",1]]},"ZHqFrNyLlpA":{"wy":"HN points to it as the practical demonstration of data-oriented design, including structure packing and switching between AOS and SOA.","q":{"x":"He gives a fantastic walkthrough on his approach to structure packing in the data-oriented demo[1].","i":9071008,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9071008,"author":"tomyws","month":"2015-02","story":"The Lost Art of C Structure Packing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Walkthrough of structure packing approach in data-oriented design","quote":"He gives a fantastic walkthrough on his approach to structure packing in the data-oriented demo[1].","text":"He gives a fantastic walkthrough on his approach to structure packing in the data-oriented demo[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24146300,"author":"clucas","month":"2020-08","story":"Handles Are the Better Pointers (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of the techniques under discussion","quote":"Here is the demo video of the stuff zero_iq is talking about","text":"Here is the demo video of the stuff zero_iq is talking about: [VIDEO] Basically right on point with what the GP is saying."},{"id":9397994,"author":"taliesinb","month":"2015-04","story":"The death of optimizing compilers [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Jai switching between AOS and SOA","quote":"Jonathan Blow's Jai language offers transparent switching between AOS and SOA","text":"> I'm not aware of any general-purpose language where this is an option to any significant degree (silent AOS-to-SOA, hot-vs-cold data segregation, tree clustering, etc...) Not yet, of course, because it hasn't been released, but Jonathan Blow's Jai language offers transparent switching between AOS and SOA, as well as many other features that make it easy to explore the 'optimization space' of a program manually. [VIDEO] (for those who don't know, Jonathan Blow was the creator of Braid)"},{"id":8994864,"author":"agentultra","month":"2015-02","story":"An introduction to NoFlo and flow-based programming","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates Jai, a compiler designed around data-oriented design","quote":"A video demonstration of Jai; a new compiler from Jonathan Blow designed around this paradigm.","text":"I think you're referring to confirmation bias which is related but not quite the same. Never the less I understand what you're getting at. I took it to mean that the kinds of problems a programmer encounters is not sufficiently random to properly determine the usefulness of a given language or \"paradigm.\" Which may keep people from switching to new, better tools (or from trying them out at all). The data-oriented design thing is interesting and something I've heard come and go over the years. Check out: [LINK] and [VIDEO] A video demonstration of Jai; a new compiler from Jonathan Blow designed around this paradigm. Cheers!"}]},"BemHqUqcpI8":{"wy":"HN commenters use it to show that behavior in interrogations is not a reliable sign of guilt and that police lie to innocent people.","q":{"x":"It shows two example interrogations of innocent suspects, each displaying completely different behaviour.","i":26609176,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26609176,"author":"oftenwrong","month":"2021-03","story":"How can you tell if someone is lying?","stance":"rec","why":"Innocent suspects behave differently in interrogations, showing guilt cannot be inferred.","quote":"It shows two example interrogations of innocent suspects, each displaying completely different behaviour.","text":"This video is relevant: [VIDEO] It's about interrogation, and the common notion that a guilt can be inferred by the suspect's behaviour. It shows two example interrogations of innocent suspects, each displaying completely different behaviour."},{"id":27857805,"author":"lm28469","month":"2021-07","story":"Illinois first state to tell police they can't lie to minors in interrogations","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a cop lying to innocent people.","quote":"This timestamped video is an example of a cop lying (to innocent people)","text":"If you're interested there are plenty of interrogation videos on yt, some by the FBI are particularly interesting. Sometimes they have a pretty good case but getting a confession tremendously speed up the process so they lie to get confessions. They also can lie to trigger responses and see how the suspect react, guilty people seem to react in way different ways than innocent ones. It's obviously not an exact science, even though it's heavily studied and has some psychology compounds, but it worked and put countless number of guilty people behind the bars A source I like is: [LINK] This timestamped video is an example of a cop lying (to innocent people): [VIDEO]"},{"id":27587198,"author":"meowface","month":"2021-06","story":"The mind of a murderer: the power and limits of forensic psychiatry","stance":"reference","why":"Innocent people's truthful statements treated as evidence of guilt.","quote":"This JCS video shows 100% non-guilty people being 100% truthful","text":">It's not really about leverage or power. It's more of a strategic setting where the winning move is either to be 100% truthful (if you're innocent - and even JCS acknowledges this!) or not to play. It seems to be the opposite. This JCS video shows 100% non-guilty people being 100% truthful, and their truthful statements are regularly seen as further evidence of their criminality: [VIDEO] Whether you're completely guilty, partly guilty, or completely non-guilty, the safest thing to do is to say absolutely nothing. >It can however be strategically beneficial to the innocent if done lightly and with proper foreknowledge, because if you can precisely and vigorously call the interrogator's BS about what evidence they do or do not have, this sets you apart from someone who's just making stuff up as they go along and cannot possibly be able to do this. I really don't think this is true at all. Again, I think it's the complete opposite. I think it's total BS that trying to call BS about evidence would somehow help you in any way. Someone making stuff up as they go along can definitely very possibly do what you say, especially if they're relatively intelligent. And someone being 100% truthful may not be able to do this, if they think the police can't lie, and/or if they know nothing about forensics. In any of the four scenarios (guilty and try to call them out; not guilty and try to call them out; guilty and don't try to call them out; not guilty and don't try to call them out), you'"}],"r":[["m64SWl9bfvk",1],["TQB0KK2rxcw",1]]},"DGscoaUWW2M":{"wy":"Posted as a joke and a reference whenever threads discuss newspaper bias and media pandering to readers.","q":{"x":"news companies are openly biased towards one political party or bias their articles towards their main customer group","i":13626345,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":45522050,"author":"Zenst","month":"2025-10","story":"One-man campaign ravages EU 'Chat Control' bill","stance":"rec","why":"Comedy that captures press pandering to readers' prejudices","quote":"So much wisdom on comedy, even 80s comedy","text":"The quote from Yes, Prime Minster - \"The only way to understand the press, is to remember that they pander to their readers prejudices\" which makes those news aggregations sites more appealing, though cheaper to stick with other avenues for balance. So much wisdom on comedy, even 80s comedy Who reads the papers? - Yes, Prime Minister - BBC comedy: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19565979,"author":"CM30","month":"2019-04","story":"Zuckerberg’s Rules Would Hurt Everyone but Facebook","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates partisan UK media aimed at readers","quote":"And the media here have been aimed at partisans for a long time.","text":"Is it? We've had tabloids here in the UK for years, and it seems the more extreme ones have generally had higher circulation numbers than their more moderate rivals. And the media here have been aimed at partisans for a long time. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18006744,"author":"gowld","month":"2018-09","story":"Gell-Mann amnesia effect","stance":"reference","why":"Hacker explains who reads which British newspaper","quote":"In this timeless clip, Prime Minister, Jim Hacker explains to Sir Humphrey and Bernard the importance of the papers and who reads which one.","text":"[VIDEO] \"In this timeless clip, Prime Minister, Jim Hacker explains to Sir Humphrey and Bernard the importance of the papers and who reads which one.\" [LINK] Sir Humphrey: The only way to understand the Press is to remember that they pander to their readers' prejudices. Jim Hacker: Don't tell me about the Press. I know exactly who reads the papers. * The Daily Mirror is read by the people who think they run the country. * The Guardian is read by people who think they ought to run the country. * The Times is read by the people who actually do run the country. * The Daily Mail is read by the wives of the people who run the country. * The Financial Times is read by people who own the country. * The Morning Star is read by people who think the country ought to be run by another country. * The Daily Telegraph is read by the people who think it is. Sir Humphrey: Prime Minister, what about the people who read The Sun? Bernard Woolley: Sun readers don't care who runs the country as long as she's got big"},{"id":17111759,"author":"belorn","month":"2018-05","story":"A Wikipedia editor's long-running campaign","stance":"reference","why":"Classic sketch showing papers cater to readership","quote":"Its a joke of course but one that rings quite true for a lot of people.","text":"> non-partisan news organizations. Generally, their news is relatively unbiased; One has to be very careful about who their readership are and whom the journalist cater to in their writing. For example we had this study done for reporting on the 2016 election and its only a small selection in green that indicate nonpartisan target audience: [LINK] It should be noted that the map do not show official endorsements which some of the green marked organization (or owners) made. We also have the classic \"Who reads the papers?\" from Yes, Prime Minister. ( [VIDEO] ) Its a joke of course but one that rings quite true for a lot of people. The audience influence the paper which in turn incentives the writers to cater to the existing audience. Online advertisement is exceptional strong in this where the effect can be felt continuously with instant feedback. Encyclopedias are fun. One on side of the family I have academic ancestors who had two really old sets from around world war 1 and before. Amazing read, especially if you look up political topics. Occasional I read it just for the culture difference. Domain experts can be rather \"colored\" by their time, and its difficult argument to make that today Encyclopedia Britannica is enlighten and correct but 100 years ago they paid biased and blind experts. Which leaves doing the work oneself and make original meta research by seeking out studies, academic journals and papers. That is the proper way to do it if one has the time and energy."},{"id":13626345,"author":"belorn","month":"2017-02","story":"Handful of “highly toxic” Wikipedia editors cause 9% of abuse","stance":"reference","why":"Yes Minister clip on newspapers pandering to their readers","quote":"news companies are openly biased towards one political party or bias their articles towards their main customer group","text":"Honest question, what alternative do you use instead? HN mostly links to news article, and I would question if current politics and ongoing events are more accurately written by news media and with less bias. I don't know about polish news companies, but its not uncommon elsewhere that news companies are openly biased towards one political party or bias their articles towards their main customer group (UK example from Yes, Minister: [VIDEO] ). We have books or research papers, but meta research generally acknowledge that researchers include bias into their work, often linked to the opinion of those that funds the research. Meta research is thus generally a bit better, especially the meta-meta-research papers, through it generally takes a quite political contested topic for that to happen which then further increase the risk of bias by the meta researchers selecting results that favors their side."},{"id":11079948,"author":"arethuza","month":"2016-02","story":"Gravitational waves detected by LIGO","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Morning Star is read by people who think the country ought to be run by another country.","text":"From Yes, Prime Minister in '87: The Morning Star is read by people who think the country ought to be run by another country. The Daily Telegraph is read by the people who think it is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6625969,"author":"arethuza","month":"2013-10","story":"Is Glenn Greenwald the Future of News?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Yes Prime Minister explained the newspaper market in the UK pretty well","text":"\"it's simply engrained in their editorial outlook\" As far as I can see, in the UK we have a wide spectrum of \"editorial outlook\" in newspapers while the TV news channels (much to the disgust of Murdoch) are legally required to provide neutrality and impartial, unbiased coverage (and I know some people think the BBC is the last stronghold of communism). Yes Prime Minister explained the newspaper market in the UK pretty well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6638945,"author":"fnordfnordfnord","month":"2013-10","story":"Is The Economist left- or right-wing?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Bypass this if you dislike jokes","text":"Bypass this if you dislike jokes: [VIDEO] I have to post this clip from \"Yes Minister\" every time there is a discussion about political bias in the media."},{"id":6143630,"author":"fnordfnordfnord","month":"2013-08","story":"NSA pays £100m in secret funding for GCHQ","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Here amuse yourself and learn about the nature of various newspapers","text":"> Nudity is more accepted in Germany apparently, and it isn't in the US, That's the impression I get too. > that's just something we have to live with. But I differ with you there. > The Guardian presents itself as a news organization. It is. Here amuse yourself and learn about the nature of various newspapers: [VIDEO] >The person that picked out that picture should have considered posting it Oh, I bet that photo was chosen carefully to draw attention. > in light of the fact that they are now, with the Snowden leak, a global news organization, with visitors from Germany AND the US (in this example). They have always been so, and I hope they don't begin to pander to social conservatives. > What they should consider is \"Who is going to see this article?\" and then take the option that attracts the most people to their site. I think that's exactly what they've done. For better or worse. >Understand that I'm not trying to say that Guardian should change their site up at all, rather I'm saying that it might benefit them to think twice about what they post, because I'm no longer visiting their site at work. Not that I necessarily want more breastfeeding in my news but I certainly hope you're in the minority. How bad will it be when they have to pander to even more conservative Islamic sensibilities?"},{"id":6128811,"author":"rbehrends","month":"2013-07","story":"The Guardian Moves to .com Domain","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The relative popularity of the Sun and the Daily Mail is best explained by Jim Hacker and friends","text":"Socialist only by American standards (and, possibly, by the standards of certain Tories who still think of the US as a former British colony). By European standards, the Guardian is a fairly typical center-left newspaper. If you want to look at an actual socialist newspaper in the UK, try the Morning Star [1]. The relative popularity of the Sun and the Daily Mail is best explained by Jim Hacker and friends: [VIDEO] In other words, a classical broadsheet vs. tabloid situation. Politics doesn't sell as well as gossip and T&A. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":2632592,"author":"jdietrich","month":"2011-06","story":"Personalization Isn’t the Future of News (I Hope)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"As explained in 1987","text":"I don't know about the US, but here in the UK the easiest way to find out someone's political perspective is to ask them what newspaper they read. We just don't have a national newspaper that doesn't have a political agenda. You can choose a paper, safe in the knowledge that nothing you read will challenge your world view or upset your prejudices. There's nothing stopping you from reading all the other papers, but the only people who do are Media Studies students and PR men. As explained in 1987: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1863076,"author":"mdemare","month":"2010-11","story":"The Times UK Lost 4 Million Readers To Its Paywall Experiment","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"From Yes Prime Minister: \"I know exactly who reads the papers: the Daily Mirror is read by people who think they run the country; The Guardian is read by people who think they ought to run the country; The Times is read by people who actually do run the country; the Daily Mail is read by the wives of the people who run the country; the Financial Times is read by people who own the country; The Morning Star is read by people who think the country ought to be run by another country; and The Daily Telegraph is read by people who think it is.\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["GCSXNGc7pfs",1]]},"TM8L7bdwVaA":{"wy":"Used as the go-to illustration of corporate-owned local newscasts reading identical scripts.","q":{"x":"100s of local newscasts are all using the exact same script, even on trivial human interest stories","i":8348950,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":22533283,"author":"narrator","month":"2020-03","story":"Italy is extending its coronavirus quarantine measures to the entire country","stance":"rec","why":"Conan segments showing local news outlets repeating scripts verbatim","quote":"Conan O'Brien has some great segments on this","text":"The MSM means the stuff every local tv news outlet repeats word for word verbatim: Conan O'Brien has some great segments on this: [VIDEO] They also do this for serious political stuff, repeating the same phrases over and over again, but posting those video compilations would just invite downvotes."},{"id":16727313,"author":"hidenotslide","month":"2018-04","story":"Sinclair's script for stations [video]","stance":"reference","why":"One clip from Conan's segments on scripted local newscasts","quote":"Reminds me of the old Conan segment that highlighted this behavior.","text":"Reminds me of the old Conan segment that highlighted this behavior. Here are some clips from 2012-2014. Twinkie Trouble [LINK] Could this be the end of email overload? [LINK] I scream, you scream, you know the rest. [LINK] Enjoyed from a desk or the couch. [LINK] You don't need us to tell you that gas prices are back on the rise. [LINK] Economic factors may take some spring out of the Easter Bunny's step this year. [LINK] A child's happiness is priceless, especially on a birthday. [LINK] Mike Myers says \"Yeah, Baby.\" [LINK] It's okay, you can admit it if you've bought an item, or two, or ten for yourself. [VIDEO] Don't worry, be happy. [LINK] Those with a special someone may look to their mobile device to help them say \"I love you.\" [LINK] Frank Ocean tells a major fast food chain to buzz off, and which celeb peed in a glass jar? [LINK] Is it time for dogs to have a social network of their own? [LINK] The final days of the campaign can get a little salty. [LINK]"},{"id":15552709,"author":"Chardok","month":"2017-10","story":"FCC eliminates rule requiring a local presence for AM/FM/TV broadcasters","stance":"reference","why":"Conan montage showing homogenized corporate local news","quote":"I immediately think of this video","text":"Anyone who has been actively listening to the radio or watching local TV broadcasts for the past 10-20 years can probably already attest to the bland homogenization and conspicuous corporate take overs (I immediately think of this video [VIDEO] it seems like this is the next logical direction for them. Personally I detest this and miss the flavor each city brought to its local media, instead of the same stations playing the same 30 songs repeatedly, but with phone music streaming becoming so popular I cannot see a lot of people being outraged by this."},{"id":15555769,"author":"Digory","month":"2017-10","story":"FCC Moves to End TV-Newspaper Ownership Ban","stance":"reference","why":"Conan bit of anchors reading prepackaged scripts","quote":"One of Conan's recurring bits is anchors reading prepackaged scripts.","text":"CNN is in the business of orchestrating local news, though the local station is arguably 'subscribing' to those stories/policies. One of Conan's recurring bits is anchors reading prepackaged scripts.[0]. They're all packages by CNN for local affiliates,[1] one of several similar services (including Fox). [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":8348950,"author":"narrator","month":"2014-09","story":"The invasion of corporate news","stance":"reference","why":"Conan montage of local newscasters reading identical scripts","quote":"100s of local newscasts are all using the exact same script, even on trivial human interest stories","text":"Reminds me of this Conan O'Brein piece. [VIDEO] Basically, 100s of local newscasts are all using the exact same script, even on trivial human interest stories where they could improvise."}],"r":[["yErKTVdETpw",1]]},"TS1lpKBMkgg":{"wy":"Cited as an insider's critique of Scala and of non-incremental compilers, often by people warning others off Scala.","q":{"x":"Anyone seriously considering Scala should listen to his talk","i":9492414,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9492414,"author":"mietek","month":"2015-05","story":"What every computer science major should know (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Former Scala core contributor's critique, worth hearing before adopting Scala","quote":"Anyone seriously considering Scala should listen to his talk","text":"5 years ago, Paul Phillips was still a core contributor to Scala. Anyone seriously considering Scala should listen to his talk, titled “We’re doing it all wrong”, and take a look at Policy, his fork of the Scala compiler. [VIDEO] [LINK] Previous discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":9633150,"author":"arkadiyt","month":"2015-05","story":"JetBrains' MPS Projectional Editor [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Rant against Scala that also covers editing syntax trees directly","quote":"I would highly recommend the following rants against Scala by Paul Phillips","text":"If you enjoy these types of compiler videos then I would highly recommend the following rants against Scala by Paul Phillips (from TypeSafe and one of the core language contributors). He also talks about wanting to edit abstract syntax trees directly: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":24085252,"author":"pas","month":"2020-08","story":"What Is the Minimal Set of Optimizations Needed for Zero-Cost Abstraction?","stance":"reference","why":"Ex-Scala core contributor on why compilers must be incremental","quote":"an ex-Scala core contributor's lament about why compilers have to be incremental","text":"A bit tangential, but - an ex-Scala core contributor's lament about why compilers have to be incremental: [VIDEO] - a Rust core team member talking about responsive compilers: [LINK] (basically the \"low-level\" implementation of the talk at the previous link)"},{"id":13021636,"author":"pas","month":"2016-11","story":"How to speed up the Rust compiler some more","stance":"reference","why":"Scalac performance and incremental compilation talk","quote":"","text":"Scala's new compiler (dotty) also tries to go that way (first of all using streaming AST-s for better cache locality). The problem is, that usually this \"keep state and just percolate changes\" is easier said than done. But we're getting there. See also: [VIDEO] (scalac performance) [VIDEO] (what do we really need from \"computing science\" to do programming) [LINK]"},{"id":10046867,"author":"bobm_kite9","month":"2015-08","story":"Compilers Are Databases [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Talk that made commenter stop investigating Scala","quote":"This video is 2 years old now, but I think it marked the point when I stopped investigating scala.","text":"Really good points. I expect you're right about the expressiveness, although I don't really know exactly what you mean by this. The \"buffet of abstractions\" available in Scala actually put me off it. For me, there are too many options available to expect to have readable, maintainable code at the end of it all, especially if you work in a team which regularly creates almost wilfully hard-to-comprehend java. This video is 2 years old now, but I think it marked the point when I stopped investigating scala. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9258726,"author":"Dewie","month":"2015-03","story":"The underground world of neuroenhancing drugs (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Paul Phillips on what Scala does wrong","quote":"All I knew about him beforehand is from watching this video[1]","text":"Oh damn, I didn't know they were the same person. All I knew about him beforehand is from watching this video[1], but it's weird to read about a person and then find out that they also were the same person that you knew from somewhere else. [1] (How Scala [Typesafe] is doing \"everything wrong\") [VIDEO]"},{"id":8540071,"author":"bkeroack","month":"2014-10","story":"Scala Best Practices","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for Scala compiler internals being messy","quote":"The compiler internals are reportedly a mess","text":"a) Martin Odersky has admitted as much[1]. b) The compiler internals are reportedly a mess[2]--likely due at least in part to the extreme number of different syntax variants. c) I find rule 4.1 in OP interesting (\"SHOULD avoid concurrency like the plague it is\"). According to Odersky's Coursera course[3], one of the main advantages of functional programming as enabled by Scala is easy, safer concurrency (reduced/absent mutable shared state). Yet here we see a presumably seasoned Scala veteran basically saying that this is a failure and we should avoid concurrency whenever possible because it results in too many bugs. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO] 3. [LINK]"},{"id":8279294,"author":"jongraehl","month":"2014-09","story":"Policy – A fork of the Scala compiler","stance":"reference","why":"Slide on implicit numeric conversions and Float precision","quote":"","text":"Paul, why do you say it's wrong that Float(Long.max - Int.max) is equal to Float(Long.max)? Float has less than 32 significant binary digits, and Long.max - Int.max has the same MSBs as Long.max. You would need to use Double to see the difference. I'm not sure what implicit conversions/promotions have to do with that - you want a warning or error when going from Long to Float? That would be reasonable. You can even lose significant digits between Int->Float and Long->Double even though they have the same total number of bits, so you could require those to be explicit. (from a slide in [VIDEO] )"},{"id":7980613,"author":"babs474","month":"2014-07","story":"Gradle 2.0","stance":"reference","why":"Scala core contributor criticizes Scala for 50 minutes","quote":"presentation where Paul Phillips lays in to scala for 50 minutes.","text":"Very True. I'm pretty sure that quote wasn't meant to be a critique of the Groovy language, just an expression of interest in Scala. Yet its brought out every time somebody wants to dismiss Groovy. Compare that quote to this[1] presentation where Paul Phillips lays in to scala for 50 minutes. Even when somebody does criticize their own creation I doubt they mean \"Hey everybody, that thing I spent a bunch of time on? Throw it all in the garbage, its pointless\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8099798,"author":"juliangamble","month":"2014-07","story":"Scala: Next Steps","stance":"reference","why":"Scala compiler lead's talk explaining his departure and critique","quote":"the lead developer on the Scala Compiler, Paul Phillips, went slightly AWOL last year","text":"Some context - the lead developer on the Scala Compiler, Paul Phillips, went slightly AWOL last year, and presented the talk \"We're doing it all wrong\" [VIDEO] [LINK] He still seems slightly burnt by the whole thing: [LINK] Other critiques of Scala collections have been made with the Typelevel library [LINK] which aims to fix these issues. This post, 'Next Steps', seems to have come out of Martin Odersky's response to the situation. I commend him for taking practical and thought out action."},{"id":7145568,"author":"asdasf","month":"2014-01","story":"In Defense of Scala. Response to “I Don’t Like Scala”","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence Scala has inconsistencies average programmers miss","quote":"","text":">Edward Kmett is a Haskell evangelist Really? Could you point me to some of his evangelizing? Or did you mean \"he uses haskell and is quite happy with it\"? Because that isn't the same thing. >inconsistencies that the average programmer will never even realize exist. Do you honestly believe that? [VIDEO] >Scala that only when you leave it's confines do you realize, shit, I miss But the very point Edward makes is that you don't give up those things when you leave scala. You only give up those things if you leave scala for something worse. You could leave for something better, keep all those things, and get tons of important things that you also won't realize you are missing until you get used to them and then try to live without them."},{"id":6627116,"author":"jackfoxy","month":"2013-10","story":"How Kentucky Built the Country's Best Obamacare Website","stance":"reference","why":"Paul Phillips talk on what Scala gets wrong","quote":"Correctness is the dog. Everything else is tail.","text":"Correctness is the dog. Everything else is tail. Don't know who said that first, might have been this guy [VIDEO]"},{"id":7388670,"author":"joehillen","month":"2014-03","story":"Consider the Nimrod Programming Language","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"you should need no more reason to avoid Scala than this","text":"What is Scala doing in the same list with Rust, Julia, Nimrod, and C++? You do realize there is a JVM preventing it from being a systems level language, right? Also, you should need no more reason to avoid Scala than this: [VIDEO] If you're interested in Scala, learn Haskell. It's faster, its design is re-enforced with proven mathematical concepts, and it doesn't have the worst syntax ever."},{"id":7259000,"author":"fiatmoney","month":"2014-02","story":"Why we love Scala at Coursera","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"it looks like gazing into the guts of the compiler puts one on a course towards a nervous breakdown.","text":"This is a thing about Scala which gives me pause: it looks like gazing into the guts of the compiler puts one on a course towards a nervous breakdown. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7127208,"author":"platz","month":"2014-01","story":"Scala turns 10","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"My sample size is small, but the sentiment mainly comes from this","text":"My sample size is small, but the sentiment mainly comes from this: \"Pacific Northwest Scala 2013 We're Doing It All Wrong by Paul Phillips - YouTube\" [1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6846143,"author":"coolsunglasses","month":"2013-12","story":"Why Scala is the best language available today","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The Scala community didn't lose one its best core developers for nothing.","text":"Right, and how well do subtyping and implicits get along? This is the problem, everything and the kitchen sink is tossed into Scala without any consideration for how well things work together because they've let it be a PLT grad students playground. [LINK] [VIDEO] The Scala community didn't lose one its best core developers for nothing. I mention Clojure and Haskell in the same breath because FP and immutability are what I care about and Scala fails to be a nice functional language."}],"r":[["BLGFriOKz6U",1],["IOiZatlZtGU",1],["Dk306ZkNOuc",1]]},"b6URa-PTqfA":{"wy":"HN finds it a striking historical look at early text editors and remote work that feels very modern.","q":{"x":"Perhaps it's better to see for a minute how people used this kind of editors when they first appeared","i":32015023,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32015023,"author":"aasasd","month":"2022-07","story":"Ed is the standard text editor (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how early editors were used in 1979.","quote":"Perhaps it's better to see for a minute how people used this kind of editors when they first appeared","text":"Perhaps it's better to see for a minute how people used this kind of editors when they first appeared, than to keep reading about them for ages: [VIDEO] (BBC from '79, mentioning how people start working from home with the aid of newfangled microcomputers and network connectivity). I'm reminded most of all of the fdisk util, where the workflow is pretty much the same: print, modify, print, save. But also I can see where Vi/Vim come from, as I regularly do the same thing as in the vid: %s/something/something else/g and then wq."},{"id":39598300,"author":"newmana","month":"2024-03","story":"A woman named \"Steve\" – IT pioneer, entrepreneur, philanthropist (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Video from 1979 about F International (Dame Shirley's company) - working from home","text":"Video from 1979 about F International (Dame Shirley's company) - working from home, as a computer a programmer, is the greatest revolution for working women since the pill. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31805075,"author":"daenz","month":"2022-06","story":"Will word processors start a home working revolution? (1979) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The example of a mother working from home[0] feels very modern.","text":"The example of a mother working from home[0] feels very modern. It's strange to know that we drifted away from that direction at some point. 0. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["x_q50tvbQm4",1],["8CAafjodkyE",1],["i9MXYZh1jcs",1],["atvtBE6t48M",1],["8PwwRR8deHk",1]]},"eyll-bBZzyk":{"wy":"Cited in threads on minority languages as a funny but sad look at how hard it is to live in Irish.","q":{"x":"No Béarla[1] is one of the saddest documentaries I've ever seen.","i":44433317,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":22892399,"author":"DiogenesKynikos","month":"2020-04","story":"The many languages missing from the internet","stance":"rec","why":"Funny series on how useful Irish is.","quote":"There's a funny TV series that explores how useful the Iris","text":"Hebrew is the only successfully revived language that I know of, and it was revived under circumstances that are really pretty unique. Irish has had \"national language\" status for 100 years, during which time it has continued to decline. Strong government support for the language - making it a requirement for government jobs, teaching it to schoolchildren, defining regions of the country where Irish should be the primary everyday language - weren't enough to prevent the continued decline of the language. I think the problem with the sort of language policy that Ireland pursued is that language is a practical tool for most people. A lot of people liked the idea of Irish coming back (especially back when speaking Irish was a political statement against English colonialism), but in everyday life in Ireland, English is vastly more useful. The last monolingual Irish speaker died in 1998.[1] You will never encounter anyone who only knows how to speak Irish. Back when there were monolingual Irish speakers, they lived in some of the poorest areas of Ireland and hoped that their children would learn English, so that they would have better opportunities. In some ways, the language policy backfired. Some people actually viewed it as oppressive, because they didn't see the point of learning a language that was of no practical use to them in everyday life, and they weren't able to get certain jobs unless they spoke that language. There's a funny TV series that explores how useful the Iris"},{"id":44433317,"author":"xdennis","month":"2025-07","story":"YouTube No Translation","stance":"reference","why":"Native speaker struggles to do mundane tasks in Irish.","quote":"No Béarla[1] is one of the saddest documentaries I've ever seen.","text":"> whose first language is Gaelic Is that really true though? I think it's a bit optimistic to say \"first language\". I know that many people do speak Irish, but the number of people who can speak Irish better than English is abysmal. No Béarla[1] is one of the saddest documentaries I've ever seen. It follows a native speaker who tries to do mundane tasks in Ireland using only Irish, but can barely get anything done. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23735511,"author":"frabbit","month":"2020-07","story":"Survival of Scottish Gaelic in doubt","stance":"reference","why":"Protagonist tries to get services in Irish.","quote":"The scenes in which he attempts to get state-run tourist services to communicate with him in one of the official lanaguages of Ireland are painful.","text":"By coincidence I was just watching an 8 year old Teilifís na Gaeilge (TG4) program in which the protagonist attempts to travel the length and breadth of Ireland speaking only 'as Gaeilge'. The scenes in which he attempts to get state-run tourist services to communicate with him in one of the official lanaguages of Ireland are painful. In particular his visit to the prison cell in which his grandmother (one of the 1916 revolutionaries) was incarcerated starts off badly: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["AGCmPLWZKd8",1]]},"gp0FI8Gw1iA":{"wy":"Linked as a firsthand account of the NixOS moderation and governance disputes, from a banned member's perspective.","q":{"x":"For anyone looking to get a summary of the whole NixOS drama, Jon Ringer (who got permabanned) has a YouTube video","i":45398851,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45398851,"author":"srid","month":"2025-09","story":"NixOS moderation team resigns in protest of Steering Committee interference","stance":"rec","why":"Summary of the whole NixOS drama from a banned member","quote":"For anyone looking to get a summary of the whole NixOS drama, Jon Ringer (who got permabanned) has a YouTube video","text":"For anyone looking to get a summary of the whole NixOS drama, Jon Ringer (who got permabanned) has a YouTube video: [VIDEO] My own permanban is detailed in this post: [LINK]"},{"id":49219055,"author":"jonringer117","month":"2026-08","story":"The Nixpkgs core team has disbanded","stance":"reference","why":"Long video on 2022-2024 drama from banned person's perspective","quote":"I have a long winded video on the 2022-2024 drama.","text":"If you're unemployed enough, I have a long winded video on the 2022-2024 drama. From the perspective of the guy who was labeled a nazi and banned. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45401203,"author":"srid","month":"2025-09","story":"NixOS moderation team resigns over NixOS Steering Committee's interference","stance":"reference","why":"Events around NixOS moderation told by a banned member","quote":"has a YouTube video talking about several events around NixOS moderation","text":"Jon Ringer (who got permabanned) has a YouTube video talking about several events around NixOS moderation: [VIDEO] My own permanban is detailed in this post: [LINK]"},{"id":43026543,"author":"_huayra_","month":"2025-02","story":"Improved evaluation times with pre-resolved Nix store paths","stance":"reference","why":"Former contributor's account of his departure from NixOS","quote":"Jon Ringer (a former contributor) posted a giant video [0] outlining his history with the project","text":"The unusually high amount of drama in NixOS is one of the reasons I have been hesitant to start using it. Jon Ringer (a former contributor) posted a giant video [0] outlining his history with the project and how he left / was forced out (amongst several other key folks). For the record, I don't know how to characterize his exit, only that there was an absolute ton of drama around it that wasn't really related to even any technical aspect. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"lBfVxBj61z0":{"wy":"HN points to it as an example of the founders' realistic, down-to-earth approach and their bootstrapped, lifestyle-business philosophy.","q":{"x":"he approaches things in a much more realistic down to earth manner than many other engineers out there.","i":9946891,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9946891,"author":"jmui","month":"2015-07","story":"Ask HN: I am 30+ and bored with life and the software industry, what do I do?","stance":"rec","why":"DHH's down-to-earth approach to technology.","quote":"he approaches things in a much more realistic down to earth manner than many other engineers out there.","text":"I definitely see your point of view regarding all the efforts in the community to make the next shiny thing. It's very prevalent on the front page of HN all the time. I can't tell if it's representative of the culture as a whole or just those who are frequent posters of HN. In any case, I think that you'd have a lot in common with David Heinemeier Hansson. You can see in this video [VIDEO] and a lot of his blogs that he approaches things in a much more realistic down to earth manner than many other engineers out there. I think that if that's what you want, then it's all a matter of finding the right company that thinks the same way about using technology to solve real problems."},{"id":12785005,"author":"fuqted","month":"2016-10","story":"Twitter Plans Hundreds More Job Cuts as Soon as This Week","stance":"reference","why":"Interview on bootstrapped lifestyle business.","quote":"Here's one interview out of plenty where they talk about this kind of thing.","text":"A stock price takes into account future growth. When growth begins to stagnate, then the ticker will fall. People lose money. In most cases it probably isn't a huge deal, but the fact that Twitter isn't profitable is likely a (large) factor. Without a given amount of users, it won't be profitable and decreased growth obviously means that profitability is further out. Hence them cutting costs. There's obviously nothing wrong with a bootstrapped / lifestyle business. The people at Basecamp are big proponents of it. [VIDEO] Here's one interview out of plenty where they talk about this kind of thing."},{"id":8705508,"author":"justplay","month":"2014-12","story":"Node.js, a popular tool for building modern internet services, has split in two","stance":"reference","why":"Basecamp's stance on Rails direction.","quote":"","text":">Node was created by software developer Ryan Dahl as a way of building and running entire online applications with JavaScript—the standard programming language for writing code that runs in your browser. Really? I thought that they intentionally choose javascript because it doesn't have any features like threading. > The massively popular programming framework Ruby on Rails, for instance, is still sponsored by its creator, a company called Basecamp. Basecamp isn't happy to the direction that ruby on rails is going, but they accept it. [1] [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"orQKfIXMiA8":{"wy":"Commenters cite it when arguing for less phone time and more idle, undistracted thinking.","q":{"x":"Curiosity kicks in. Curiosity is a precursor to learning.","i":48047416,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":45179591,"author":"mixermachine","month":"2025-09","story":"iPhone dumbphone","stance":"rec","why":"Why boredom matters and how doing nothing brings clarity.","quote":"I get quite a lot of clarity with this exercise.","text":"Also great video regarding this topic: \"You Need to Be Bored. Here's Why.\" [VIDEO] I now regularly force myself to \"actively\" do nothing for 15 minutes and just think. All the things I put into my brain as \"todo, please remember\" at some point in time are coming back during these 15 minutes. I get quite a lot of clarity with this exercise. As soon I pick up my phone afterwards and start browsing the clarity evaporates which feels bad. So wasting time on my phone becomes less and less appealing to me. Lets see where this leads me. I so far wasted quite a bit of time with my phone."},{"id":45430051,"author":"davelacy","month":"2025-09","story":"Kagi News","stance":"rec","why":"Case for embracing boredom and less app time.","quote":"less app time and more boredom","text":"Nice design. I like the \"today in history\" feature Lately I've been working toward less app time and more boredom [VIDEO] ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯ works wonders"},{"id":49222655,"author":"FunnyLookinHat","month":"2026-08","story":"Don't use your phone while you poop","stance":"reference","why":"Argues boredom is needed, and harder than ever to find.","quote":"just staring ahead and thinking about nothing and everything","text":"\"You need to be bored\" - [VIDEO] This is harder than ever - I've always got the urge (no pun intended to the original post) to start a new worktree, keep the agents unblocked, or write down a new idea. I've started going on silent drives to help - 2 lane roads for an hour or so, just staring ahead and thinking about nothing and everything. I'll reward myself with a podcast on the way back from wherever my \"could be anywhere\" destination is."},{"id":48047416,"author":"latexr","month":"2026-05","story":"Vibe coding and agentic engineering are getting closer than I'd like","stance":"reference","why":"Boredom engages the default mode network and sparks curiosity.","quote":"Curiosity kicks in. Curiosity is a precursor to learning.","text":"> Also, when did we stop liking to learn? I suspect it happened when we achieved a level of such constant stimulation (there is a pocket computer always on us with infinite effortless distraction) that we’re never bored and never engage the default mode network. [LINK] [VIDEO] When you’re bored, your mind goes to places it wouldn’t otherwise go. Curiosity kicks in. Curiosity is a precursor to learning. Learning engages the brain and is fun. But when you have the digital equivalent to infinite candy and the brain equivalent to a sweet tooth, it’s hard to resist. The consequence is the brain equivalent to a stomachache—depression and loss of meaning—but unfortunately it doesn’t hit you the same way that you can make the immediate connection and make yourself stop. Which, when you think about it, is ridiculous from several angles: the candy is infinite, it’s never going to run out, so you don’t need to gorge! But then we justify ourselves as only a true addict would, that while the candy is infinite, the flavours are limited editions and always rotating, and what if I miss that one really one everyone is on?!"},{"id":48047670,"author":"latexr","month":"2026-05","story":"Vibe coding and agentic engineering are getting closer than I'd like","stance":"reference","why":"Boredom engages the default mode network and sparks curiosity.","quote":"Curiosity kicks in. Curiosity is a precursor to learning.","text":"> Also, when did we stop liking to learn? I suspect it happened when we achieved a level of such constant stimulation (there is a pocket computer always on us with infinite effortless distraction) that we’re never bored and never engage the default mode network. [LINK] [VIDEO] When you’re bored, your mind goes to places it wouldn’t otherwise go. Curiosity kicks in. Curiosity is a precursor to learning. Learning engages the brain and is fun. But it’s not fun all the time, some of it is challenging and frustrating (which is good, that’s the process that teaches you). When you have the digital equivalent to infinite candy and the brain equivalent to a sweet tooth, it’s hard to resist the siren’s call. The consequence is the brain equivalent to a stomachache—depression and loss of meaning—but unfortunately it doesn’t hit you the same way so you don’t make the immediate connection to make yourself stop. When you think about it, it’s ridiculous from several angles: the candy is infinite, it’s never going to run out, so you don’t need to gorge! But then we justify ourselves as only a true addict would, that while the candy is infinite, the flavours are limited editions and always rotating, and what if I miss that really good one everyone is on?! Then you miss it, is the answer. No one will be talking about it in fifteen minutes anyway."}]},"5KKDCp3OaMo":{"wy":"Cited as an expert's first-hand evidence that GPT translation is near human quality for distant language pairs.","q":{"x":"the basic quality of the translation [from Japanese to English] is as good as a human might do","i":35335590,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42896543,"author":"tkgally","month":"2025-02","story":"Notes on OpenAI o3-mini","stance":"reference","why":"Author of the talk commenting on LLM translation quality","quote":"","text":"As I explained in a sister comment, it is not possible to rate translation quality objectively, as opinions and positions about what constitutes a good translation vary. But in my tests of reasoning models since the release of o1-preview, they have not seemed as reliable as the straight nonreasoning versions of ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini. The translation process itself usually doesn’t seem to require the kind of multistep thinking those reasoning models can be good at. For more than a year, regular LLMs, when properly prompted, have been able to produce translations that would be indistinguishable from those of some professional translators for some types of translation. General-purpose LLMs are best for translating straight expository prose without much technical or organization-specific vocabulary. Results are mixed for texts containing slang, dialogue, poetry, archaic language, etc.—partly because people’s tastes differ for how such texts should be translated. Because most translators are freelancers, it’s hard to get a handle on what impact LLMs have been having on their workloads overall. I have heard reports from experienced translators who have seen work drop off precipitously and have had to change careers, while others report an increase in their workloads over the past two years. Many translation jobs involve confidential material, and some translators may be hanging onto their jobs because their clients or employers do not allow the use of cloud-based LLMs. That sa"},{"id":37525763,"author":"og_kalu","month":"2023-09","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence GPT translation is strong for distant language pairs","quote":"","text":">about machine translation and looked at some results of translating between English and French. It was difficult to find any fault with the translation. This has never been the case for distant language pairs till GPT. That is a pretty big change. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37526091,"author":"og_kalu","month":"2023-09","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Translator of 20 years on using GPT for Japanese-English","quote":"Translator for 20 years talking about using GPT for Japanese-English.","text":">but nobody in the very well funded translation industry has a GPT powered translation engine for the key reason that it's not ready for production. Translator for 20 years talking about using GPT for Japanese-English. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":35335590,"author":"bumbledraven","month":"2023-03","story":"ChatGPT Outperforms Crowd-Workers for Text-Annotation Tasks","stance":"reference","why":"Professional translator says GPT-4 Japanese-English is near human quality","quote":"the basic quality of the translation [from Japanese to English] is as good as a human might do","text":"Are you giving it multiple paragraphs to translate at once so that it has enough context for a good translation? If so, would you mind sharing a sample input and output that you found unsatisfactory? In \"Can GPT-4 translate literature?\" (Mar 18, 2023) [ [VIDEO] ], Tom Gally, a former professional translator and current professor at the University of Tokyo, said: > …the point is, to my eye at least, the the basic quality of the translation [from Japanese to English] is as good as a human might do, and with some relatively mild editing by a sensitive human editor, it could be turned into a novel that would be readable and enjoyable."}]},"AGXG5rNe_tI":{"wy":"Cited as the real-world example of an inescapable loop in Magic: The Gathering, in discussions of its Turing completeness.","q":{"x":"causing the game to wind up in a non-terminating loop of mandatory actions","i":15719375,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":38647771,"author":"thom","month":"2023-12","story":"Magic: The Gathering Is Turing Complete (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Classic example of an inescapable loop causing a draw","quote":"A classic example is this Luis Scott-Vargas game","text":"There’s never a time where players can’t interact - just passing priority or putting triggered abilities on the stack are actions. And each time this could happen the game checks state based actions to see if someone has won. That said, if both players have no options or just pass (which is more pronounced online) then you can end up in inescapable loops that cause draws. A classic example is this Luis Scott-Vargas game: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23467736,"author":"rcxdude","month":"2020-06","story":"How the Magic: The Gathering Color Wheel Explains Humanity","stance":"reference","why":"Oblivion Ring loop crashing MTGO","quote":"there's an example of it being used on MTGO, which doesn't end well","text":"That rule is exactly where the the construction used becomes interesting: it's built in such a way so that if the turing machine halts, the player who set it up wins the game. If it doesn't, by the rule above, it's a draw. So it's fundamentally impossible to tell the result of an arbitrary setup (and in practice you could set it up such that it halts iff some unknown conjecture is true). Regarding the oblivion ring loop, there's an example of it being used on MTGO, which doesn't end well (basically seems to crash the server and/or client): [VIDEO] And here's the paper on the latest turing machine construction in mtg: [LINK]"},{"id":23468083,"author":"saghm","month":"2020-06","story":"How the Magic: The Gathering Color Wheel Explains Humanity","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"somebody would try it out online just to see what happens!","text":"> Regarding the oblivion ring loop, there's an example of it being used on MTGO, which doesn't end well (basically seems to crash the server and/or client): [VIDEO] Oh wow, I should have realized that somebody would try it out online just to see what happens! I'm not surprised it ends up with some sort of clash; given that it's Turing complete, the game engine obviously can't detect whether a current state will end up halting or not... > And here's the paper on the latest turing machine construction in mtg: [LINK] Awesome, thanks! I'll definitely check that out"},{"id":15719375,"author":"ubernostrum","month":"2017-11","story":"Magic: The Gathering Is Turing Complete (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Pro player triggers a non-terminating loop in MTGO","quote":"causing the game to wind up in a non-terminating loop of mandatory actions","text":"There's also the infamous video of (well-known professional player) Luis Scott-Vargas \"breaking\" MTGO by causing the game to wind up in a non-terminating loop of mandatory actions: [VIDEO] For those who are confused, the card involved there is Oblivion Ring: [LINK] Its effect is that, when it comes into play, you choose a non-land card in play that gets exiled (removed from play) for as long as Oblivion Ring stays in play, then gets returned once Oblivion Ring leaves play. This game devolves into a situation where there are three Oblivion Rings and no other non-land cards, so: Ring 1 exiles Ring 2, which returns Ring 3, which exiles Ring 1, which returns Ring 2, which exiles Ring 3, which returns Ring 1, which exiles Ring 2, and so on to infinity. The rules state that when a loop like this occurs (which is not often in real play), the game is an automatic draw."}]},"CHgUN_95UAw":{"wy":"Quoted as the go-to joke whenever a monopoly or big tech company ignores customers, from Verizon to Google to Apple.","q":{"x":"Like the old SNL sketch, Apple doesn't care. They don't have to.","i":13480225,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":32120480,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2022-07","story":"Comcast: Simulating shitty network connections so you can build better systems","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"We don't care. We don't have to. We're the phone company.\" -Lilly Tomlin as Ernestine","text":"[VIDEO] \"So the next time you complain about your phone service, why don't you try using two Dixie Cups with a string? We don't care. We don't have to. We're the phone company.\" -Lilly Tomlin as Ernestine"},{"id":22308379,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-02","story":"Rotary Cellphone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"You could define a class called RingyDingyEnumerator! [LINK] Speaking of \"old school\", I love watching Joan Rivers shamelessly plug MCI, after which she audaciously asks Ernestine how old she is and grills her about her sexuality: [LINK] [LINK] > In 1970, AT&T offered Tomlin $500,000 to play her character Ernestine in a commercial, but she declined, saying it would compromise her artistic integrity. In 1976, she appeared on Saturday Night Live as Ernestine in a Ma Bell advertisement parody in which she proclaimed, \"We don't care, we don't have to...we're the phone company.\" The character later made a guest appearance at The Superhighway Summit at UCLA on January 11, 1994, interrupting a speech being given on the information superhighway by then-Vice President Al Gore. She appeared as three of her minor characters in a 1998 ad campaign for Fidelity Investments that did not include Ernestine or Edith Ann. In 2003, she made two commercials as an \"updated\" Ernestine for WebEx. phone company sketch - we dont care - Tomlin [VIDEO] Ernestine's House Call - Saturday Night Live [LINK] Ernestine the telephone operator calls General Motors [LINK] Lily Tomlin as Ernestine the Telephone Operator [LINK]"},{"id":14733992,"author":"nikanj","month":"2017-07","story":"It's been 191 days since Google Drive worked for me","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Somehow it's starting to feel like Google is the Bell of the 21st century","text":"Somehow it's starting to feel like Google is the Bell of the 21st century, and this sketch has stood the test of time surprisingly well [VIDEO]"},{"id":13480225,"author":"draugadrotten","month":"2017-01","story":"iOS and MacOS Developers Can Now Respond to Reviews","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Like the old SNL sketch, Apple doesn't care. They don't have to.","text":"> Now how about tackling fake reviews? From apples point of view, why? If an app gets fake positive reviews, it will sell more, and give Apple higher income. If an app gets fake negative reviews, the developer will just bump the version, the reviews will be forgotten, and there will be no negative effect to Apple. Like the old SNL sketch, Apple doesn't care. They don't have to. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13004670,"author":"throwanem","month":"2016-11","story":"Android user locked out of Google after moving cities","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"before a robot came along that could do the same job cheaper","text":"\"We don't care. We don't have to. We're the phone company.\" - Lily Tomlin [1], before a robot came along that could do the same job cheaper [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12838604,"author":"mdip","month":"2016-10","story":"CenturyLink to Buy Level 3 for $34B","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I was an employee for Level 3, coming from the Global Crossing acquisition, for around 17 years, choosing to leave early last year (and unlike most former Level 3/Global Crossing employees, this choice was not forced upon me). Internally (and by, internally, I mean within my team, not within management or anyone who makes decisions of this nature), we'd always seen CenturyLink as an interesting prospect for merger. The two companies' footprints and businesses appeared to compliment each other. It was generally dismissed out of hand because of the consumer side of CentryLink. Level 3 (and even less so with Global Crossing), focused on carriers and Fortune 50-100 businesses as their core and shied away from the more expensive, less profitable consumer facing pieces. A bit of history for those who weren't around in the 90s: When thinking CenturyLink, think Qwest (and commercials about the little motel in the desert with \"Cable TV\" replaced by media services delivering every television show and movie produced in the history of ever). They were one of the formerly local telecoms that expanded into long distance/fiber/internet after the 1996 telecom deregulation[0]. Level 3's business is Carrier and Enterprise with much of the Enterprise piece coming from the Global Crossing side of it because, at the time, we effectively couldn't compete with Level 3. We'd come in to bid a project at a price we could eek out a small profit on and would be undercut because they owned far more local"},{"id":12105248,"author":"mdip","month":"2016-07","story":"State of LTE Advanced","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Their attitude was best summed up by a Saturday Night Live skit[1]","text":"Very interesting read, particularly in where the service providers are expanding their network. It speaks a lot to the each company's priorities and confirms a bit of my personal thoughts on each of the companies. Verizon - I had Verizon service from 1996 through 2008 and the one thing you couldn't really complain about where I lived was reception. Billing issues and customer hostile behavior were the reason I ditched them [0] but there wasn't a moment I can recall grabbing my phone and not having a solid signal everywhere I traveled in the US. They were laser focused on providing a modern, reliable network. Quickly moving to increase capacity - they've always had a pretty solid focus on providing a solid network. AT&T - I have a personal hatred for this company after numerous problems with services other than mobile. Their attitude was best summed up by a Saturday Night Live skit[1], \"We're the phone company. We don't care.\" I've never used their services if I had a choice (that was probably the single biggest reason I didn't purchase an iPhone, originally) because every time I've been forced to use one of their offerings, a variety of service related issues (not helped by their customer service department) has made them unavailable for long periods of time. It's disappointing to me that they seemed to think it would be a good idea to halt wireless expansion and redirect that money to the DirecTV acquisition. It seems that couldn't have come at a worse time considering the r"},{"id":10905849,"author":"bsder","month":"2016-01","story":"Dear GitHub","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Lily Tomlin's Phone Company Sketch","text":"Distributed revision control users whining about centralized repository lacking features. Ummm ... anybody getting the irony here? And, from a GitHub business perspective, why do I hear Lily Tomlin: \"We don't care. We don't have to.\" Everybody anointed GitHub as \"the chosen one\" over strenuous objections from some of us that creating another monopoly for open source projects is a bad idea . Pardon me for enjoying some Schadenfreude now that GitHub leveraged the open-source adoption into corporate contracts and now doesn't have to give two shits about open source folks. Lily Tomlin's Phone Company Sketch: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10677249,"author":"ghaff","month":"2015-12","story":"Towns want Verizon investigated for abandoning networks through neglect","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"We don't care. We don't have to. We're the phone company.","text":"In part, because of the existence proof of doing it that way. No, it's not as simple as saying that AT&T was bad. They established highly reliable landline service. I also hear that Bell Labs did a couple of interesting things in its history. :-) (AT&T wasn't nationalized but it was a regulated monopoly.) However, you also weren't allowed to so much as provide your own telephone until AT&T was forced to allow it by the government. And phone service, especially outside of a small local area, was shockingly expensive for a very long time. (Yes, some of this is because of technology but AT&T also had little incentive and little interest in cutting rates absent competition.) For the short video version, cue Lily Tomlin: We don't care. We don't have to. We're the phone company. [VIDEO]"}]},"IkwaV6k6BmM":{"wy":"HN uses it as evidence that D has unusually good C++ interop and as Walter Bright's account of the challenges D is tackling.","m":[{"id":19753085,"author":"maga_2020","month":"2019-04","story":"Flutter desktop shells","stance":"reference","why":"Walter Bright on D's challenges matching C++","quote":"talking about the challenges that D is tackling","text":"Another alternative, I think is D-language bindings to QT (or betterC which is a subset of D allowes to use D with C runtime library, while retaining D-s class system, templates and memory safety). [1] [LINK] D's team is doing an incredible job of matching C++ compiler specific ABIs, name mangling/etc -- just so D users can use C++ libraries (including templates) directly from D without loosing the features of the language. [2] [LINK] Here is also an older video of Walter Bright (2015) talking about the challenges that D is tackling [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18758956,"author":"armitron","month":"2018-12","story":"Creating a Non-Trivial Lisp Game in 2018","stance":"reference","why":"D comes closest to native C++ interop","quote":"The language that comes closest is D I think","text":"Rust does not have native C++ interop. C ABI is different to what clasp has done (native C++ interop). All widely used Common Lisp implementations have seamless C ABI interop through their FFIs / CFFI [1]. Clasp goes above and beyond that and natively interfaces with C++. LTO works with mixed Common Lisp and C++ code. Exception handling works as expected. You can mix Common Lisp and C++ stack frames. Correct me if I'm wrong but Rust does not have anything equivalent. The language that comes closest is D I think [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13218700,"author":"qznc","month":"2016-12","story":"D Future","stance":"reference","why":"Shows D's C++ interop as a reason to use it","quote":"it does C++ interop better than any other language","text":"Reasons for D: It is a way out of the C++ tar pit, because it does C++ interop better than any other language. D needs to continue to outrun C++ evolving, though. See [LINK] or [VIDEO] It has powerful meta-programming. It is effectively the opposite of Go, which does not even have generics. This certainly provides foot shooting as well, so other languages (e.g. Rust) are more conservative with that. D examples: [LINK] Those are the two reasons, which D has ahead of all other languages (ok, not Lisp wrt meta-programming). If you compare it to another specific language, there are more things to talk about."}]},"KEDrMriKsFM":{"wy":"Cited in threads on personal flight and hoverboards as evidence that jet-powered personal flight devices really exist.","q":{"x":"The \"Flyboard Air\" is also interesting [0] (and almost definitely not a hoax!).","i":13993909,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13993909,"author":"J-dawg","month":"2017-03","story":"Iron Man-like flight suit by a British entrepreneur","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"The \"Flyboard Air\" is also interesting [0] (and almost definitely not a hoax!).","text":"The \"Flyboard Air\" is also interesting [0] (and almost definitely not a hoax!). The inventor was recently in the news [1] saying he would have to leave France because the aviation authorities were cracking down and limiting his ability to test the craft. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":12711064,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-10","story":"Hyperloop One Raises $50M, Hires Former Uber CFO","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Moller makes the same claims over and over, and adjusts the proposed completion date on the web site year after year to be about three years in the future. (Long discussion of this on the Wikipedia talk page.) He's been in trouble with the SEC for overclaiming to investors. His product has been \"real soon now\" since the 1970s. Here's his 1974 brochure.[1] Full-scale production was to begin by the end of 1976. Moller was trying to build a VTOL powered by Wankel engines. His company was trying to build Wankels with much better power to weight ratios than usual. As usual, this resulted in much reduced engine lifespan. (Classic tradeoff, as everybody in drag racing knows.) He's been able to build a vehicle that can get off the ground, and hover a bit, tethered from a crane, for 2-3 minutes. Pure-thrust VTOL is quite possible with jet engines. The aircraft that do it, such as the Harrier and the F-35, are fighters, which are mostly engine anyway. It can be done, but not at low cost or high payload or high efficiency. Jet engines remain expensive. Despite many attempts, small, cheap, reliable jet engines remain out of reach. Below 6-passenger bizjet size, reliable engines don't seem to get much cheaper. There are cheap high-powered small jet engines with model aircraft levels of reliability and a life of 100 hours or so. That's how the Flyboard Air does it.[2] Not being stupid, they always fly this over water at low altitudes. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11849564,"author":"claar","month":"2016-06","story":"Personal flight, once a pipe dream, is within our reach","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Looks like a nice competition: Jetpack Aviation JB-9/JB-10 (from this article): [LINK] * Specs from [LINK] : - Up to 10,000+ feet - Top speed > 100mph (>160kph) - Max flight time: 10+ minutes Flyboard Air: [VIDEO] * Specs from [LINK] : - Up to 10,000 feet - Top speed of 93.2 mph (150 km/h) - Max flight time: 10 minutes"},{"id":11474898,"author":"ck2","month":"2016-04","story":"Hoverboard Teardown","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Now figure this one out: [VIDEO] (volume warning) Best theory so far it is it just a quadcopter with some greenscreen video photoshopping But if it is somehow real, wow. Imagine THOSE all over your city. That is the same guy who make the water based one that attached to jetskis I just cannot fathom any portable powersource that would produce that much energy for that much weight over 10 minutes. Even large scale RC engines have weight limits below a fully grown man."}]},"NU-fTr-udZg":{"wy":"Mostly linked by a company in its recurring hiring posts as a tech talk showing its approach, rather than recommended by commenters.","m":[{"id":24344095,"author":"swilson24","month":"2020-09","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (September 2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Company tech talk on trace driven development","quote":"","text":"LightStep | San Francisco or Remote | [LINK] LightStep builds observability tools for proactively monitoring software in today's microservices-driven world. We write for other engineering teams, and are the only company that develops observability tools at the scale and complexity that enterprises need them. We're especially looking for people-minded engineers who are excited about shipping useful, meaningful products for the developer community. To learn more about our diverse, thoughtful team; our engineering culture; and whether this is the right place for you, read our Key Values profile: [LINK] You can also check out some recent blog posts and tech talks: * \"How we write code at LightStep\" [LINK] * \"Trace driven development\" [VIDEO] * \"How GCS processes cloud functions 4x faster than AWS\" [LINK] Our open positions: All Roles: [LINK] * Engineering Manager (SF) * Software Engineer (remote friendly) * Software and Reliability Engineer (SRE) / DevOps (remote friendly) * Technical Account Manager (SF) * Technical Account Manager (Remote – East Coast) We're big believers in growth and that a career path can take many different shapes and forms; with that being said, for remote candidates, we do request prior professional experience in your discipline/field of interest. Tech Stack: d3.js/React/Redux single page webapp, Go backend Email swilson[at]lightstep[dot]com for inquiries."},{"id":21685263,"author":"lmejia-santana","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (December 2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Company tech talk on trace driven development","quote":"","text":"LightStep | San Francisco or Remote | [LINK] LightStep builds observability tools for proactively monitoring software in today's microservices-driven world. We write for other engineering teams, and are the only company that develops observability tools at the scale and complexity that enterprises need them. We're especially looking for people-minded engineers who are excited about shipping useful, meaningful products for the developer community. To learn more about our diverse, thoughtful team; our engineering culture; and whether this is the right place for you, read our Key Values profile: [LINK] You can also check out some recent blog posts, tech talks, and demo: * Play with our product for free! [LINK] * \"How we write code at LightStep\" [LINK] * \"Trace driven development\" [VIDEO] * \"How GCS processes cloud functions 4x faster than AWS\" [LINK] Our open positions (Hiring multiple roles but actively seeking EM): * All Roles: [LINK] * Engineering Manager (SF) - high priority: [LINK] We are not able to sponsor new H1B visas at this time. Tech Stack: d3.js/React/Redux single page webapp, Go backend Email Luis[at]lightstep[dot]com for inquiries."},{"id":21131004,"author":"akyaky","month":"2019-10","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (October 2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Company tech talk on trace driven development","quote":"","text":"LightStep | SF or Remote | [LINK] LightStep builds observability tools for proactively monitoring software in today's microservices-driven world. We write for other engineering teams, and are the only company that develops observability tools at the scale and complexity that enterprises need them. We're especially looking for people-minded engineers who are excited about shipping useful, meaningful products for the developer community. To learn more about whether this is the right place for you, read our Key Values profile: [LINK] You can also check out some recent blog posts, tech talks, and demo: * Play with our product for free! [LINK] * \"How we write code at LightStep\" [LINK] * \"Trace driven development\" [VIDEO] Select open positions: * All Roles: [LINK] * Engineering Manager (SF) - high priority: [LINK] * Software Engineer (remote friendly): [LINK] * Software and Reliability Engineer (SRE) / DevOps (remote friendly): [LINK] * Technical Account Manager (SF, NYC): [LINK] We are not able to sponsor new H1B visas at this time. Tech Stack: d3.js/React/Redux single page webapp, Go backend Email austen[at]lightstep[dot]com for inquiries."},{"id":20870638,"author":"akyaky","month":"2019-09","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (September 2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Company tech talk on trace driven development","quote":"","text":"LightStep | SF, NYC, Remote | [LINK] LightStep builds observability tools for proactively monitoring software in today's cloud-native world. We write for other engineering teams, and are the only company that develops observability tools at the scale and complexity that enterprises need them. We're especially looking for people-minded engineers who are excited about shipping useful, meaningful products for the developer community. To learn more about our diverse, thoughtful team; our engineering culture; and whether this is the right place for you, read our Key Values profile: [LINK] You can also check out some recent blog posts and tech talks: * \"How we write code at LightStep\" [LINK] * \"Trace driven development\" [VIDEO] * \"How GCS processes cloud functions 4x faster than AWS\" [LINK] Our open positions: * All Roles: [LINK] * OSS Engineer (remote friendly): [LINK] * Software Engineer (remote friendly): [LINK] * Software and Reliability Engineer (SRE) / DevOps (remote friendly): [LINK] We're big believers in growth and that a career path can take many different shapes and forms; with that being said, for remote candidates, we do request prior professional experience in your discipline/field of interest. We are not able to sponsor new H1B visas at this time. Tech Stack: d3.js/React/Redux single page webapp, Go backend Email austen[at]lightstep[dot]com for inquiries."},{"id":19801448,"author":"akyaky","month":"2019-05","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (May 2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Company talk on trace driven development","quote":"","text":"LightStep | San Francisco, New York City, or Remote | Onsite or Remote | [LINK] LightStep builds observability tools for proactively monitoring software in today's microservices-driven world. We write software for other engineering teams, and are the only company that develops observability tools at the scale and complexity that enterprises need them. To learn more about who we are, our engineering culture, and whether this is the right place for you, read our Key Values profile: [LINK] You can listen to us discuss why performance is a shape not a number ( [LINK] ), why we need to rethink the traditional three pillars of observability ( [LINK] ), and trace driven development ( [VIDEO] ). Our open positions: * See All Open Roles: [LINK] * Engineering Manager: [LINK] * Full Stack Product Engineer: [LINK] * OSS Engineer: [LINK] * Software Engineer: [LINK] * Software and Reliability Engineer (SRE) / DevOps: [LINK] * (First!) Sales Engineer in NYC: [LINK] ^we have an account exec, SDR, and small eng presence in NYC already so you have company! * Technical Account Manager in SF: [LINK] ^even if you haven't been a TAM before, software engineers that crave more customer facing work and cross functional impact can be really successful in this role Tech Stack: d3.js/React/Redux single page webapp, Go backend Email austen[at]lightstep[dot]com for inquiries."}]},"S63Cy64p2lQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as the introductory talk from the Open Source Ecology founder in threads on tractors, repair rights and self-sufficiency.","q":{"x":"The TED Talk was really cool","i":47868356,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47868356,"author":"mynameisash","month":"2026-04","story":"Alberta Startup Sells No-Tech Tractors for Half Price","stance":"reference","why":"Talk from the Open Source Ecology founder.","quote":"The TED Talk was really cool","text":"Years ago, there was a TED Talk[0] from the guy that started Open Source Ecology[1]. The TED Talk was really cool, but I haven't really followed what they did. It sounded promising to have open-source technology for use in this space. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":27442934,"author":"VMtest","month":"2021-06","story":"Farmers Deserve the Right to Repair Their Tractors","stance":"reference","why":"Open Source Ecology founder on building and repairing own machines.","quote":"they are really doing a great job repairing on their own","text":"Not sure if anybody has mentioned this regarding open source hardware, they are really doing a great job repairing on their own 1. [LINK] 2. [LINK] | Open Building Institute - Introductory Video 3. [LINK] | Marcin Jakubowski 4. [VIDEO] | Civilization starter kit | Marcin Jakubowski | TEDxKC"},{"id":8392793,"author":"quick111","month":"2014-10","story":"On a farm in Missouri, a radical experiment in self-sufficiency (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"His TED talk -","text":"His TED talk - [VIDEO] His OSE - [LINK] Looks like the initial ambitions have been scaled back to what they use to be, at least in time frame."}]},"TFEvkmvIjVo":{"wy":"Cited as evidence that Chinese robotaxis handle difficult edge cases in self-driving debates.","q":{"x":"AutoX has some of the best edge case handling I've seen.","i":29531577,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":35709441,"author":"Animats","month":"2023-04","story":"BYD says self-driving tech is more valuable in factories than cars","stance":"reference","why":"Shows AutoX driverless operation in Shenzhen","quote":"AutoX operates a fleet of over 1000 driverless cars in Shenzhen.","text":"Especially since AutoX operates a fleet of over 1000 driverless cars in Shenzhen.[1][2] BYD was licensing NVidia's self-driving car technology. So apparently that doesn't work. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29531577,"author":"mygoodaccount","month":"2021-12","story":"Alarmed by Tesla’s self-driving test, state legislators demand answers from DMV","stance":"reference","why":"Driverless robotaxi handling conservative Asian urban village traffic","quote":"AutoX has some of the best edge case handling I've seen.","text":"Slightly off topic, but it's fun to see company culture reflected in the algorithm behaviour. There are thousands of clips of Tesla's Fatal Self Driving being far too aggressive for American roads ( [LINK] ). Meanwhile, companies like AutoX have the problem of being far too conservative for Asian roads ( [VIDEO] ). AutoX has some of the best edge case handling I've seen."},{"id":29101751,"author":"ximeng","month":"2021-11","story":"Waymo will start testing self-driving cars in New York City","stance":"reference","why":"Chinese robotaxi handling difficult scenarios, though imperfectly.","quote":"these Chinese self-driving cars are handling quite difficult scenarios.","text":"[VIDEO] Clearly not perfect in how it handles this as it doesn’t really back up or go onto the sidewalk but these Chinese self-driving cars are handling quite difficult scenarios. It’s like there’s a phase transition from theoretical ideal driving to ignoring street markings and navigating based on how much space there is around you and intentions of surrounding vehicles. Looks difficult but not impossible. There are similar videos showing Teslas handling double parked vehicles and oncoming traffic too."}]},"WY6KkRsS26M":{"wy":"Used as a joke or reference whenever politicians' 'cyber' buzzword comes up.","q":{"x":"32C3 press conference montage from around that time","i":29309586,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29309586,"author":"st_goliath","month":"2021-11","story":"Cryptographers are not happy with how you’re using the word ‘crypto’","stance":"reference","why":"Press conference montage of politicians' cyber buzzword.","quote":"32C3 press conference montage from around that time","text":"Yes, that was the time when politicians and managers for some reason suddenly decided to exhume that 90ies buzzword (32C3 press conference montage from around that time: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":45905028,"author":"martin_a","month":"2025-11","story":"Laptops with Stickers","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Have a look at this cybervideo for example","text":"I think the CYBER thing is a joke from the German part of the internet. Our government (and companies alike), liked to cyber-everything. Cyber as a word alone or prefix became a joke and that might be the origin. Have a look at this cybervideo for example: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14850679,"author":"kaybe","month":"2017-07","story":"U.S. Defense Budget May Help Fund “Hacking for Defense” Classes at Universities","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"May I interest you in some cyber?","text":"May I interest you in some cyber? [VIDEO] (better if you understand German) I think we are in the process of re-appropriating the word actually. One of my best purchases this year was a roll of yellow tape with 'cyber' printed in black. It presumably provides great cyber defense if one covers a computer with it. And I think that is fantastic social commentary on the word."}]},"X3-hOigoxHs":{"wy":"Cited as a critique whenever HN discusses the show's depiction of nerd culture and STEM men.","q":{"x":"Here are Pop Culture Detective's video essays highlighting misogynistic and toxic-masculine behaviour","i":19985552,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":38621700,"author":"tempusr","month":"2023-12","story":"What we watched: Netflix releases global viewing figures","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Overall, I believe the show is a terrible role model for how men who are in STEM fields should behave","text":"Sure, but the show displays toxic masculinity as a dorky thing that \"nerds\" just do. Overall, I believe the show is a terrible role model for how men who are in STEM fields should behave to others who do not fit the stereotype. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":38627294,"author":"Kye","month":"2023-12","story":"What we watched: Netflix releases global viewing figures","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Pop Culture Detective did a short set of videos on it that matched my first impressions","text":"Pop Culture Detective did a short set of videos on it that matched my first impressions: [VIDEO] [LINK] I don't know how to mesh that with Wil Wheaton speaking so fondly of his time on the show in his book. Even the updated one where he talks about his past views and behavior with exceptional self-awareness."},{"id":19985346,"author":"nydel","month":"2019-05","story":"“The Big Bang Theory” Normalized Nerd Culture","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I find this piece on toxic masculinity in The Big Bang Theory much more interesting than the show or this article","text":"I find this piece on toxic masculinity in The Big Bang Theory much more interesting than the show or this article: [VIDEO] (The Adorkable Misogyny of The Big Bang Theory by Pop Culture Detective) I'm happy for people who can enjoy The Big Bang Theory but I find it difficult personally."},{"id":19985552,"author":"anonymfus","month":"2019-05","story":"“The Big Bang Theory” Normalized Nerd Culture","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Here are Pop Culture Detective's video essays highlighting misogynistic and toxic-masculine behaviour","text":"It is not just Sheldon who is extremely sexist in their group. Here are Pop Culture Detective's video essays highlighting misogynistic and toxic-masculine behaviour of all these \"nerd\" characters: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":15993070,"author":"alistproducer2","month":"2017-12","story":"Kids in ‘Netflix Only’ Homes Saved from 230 Hours of Commercials a Year","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"check this video on how the show perpetuates misogyny","text":"Just in case anyone still (or ever liked) BBT, check this video on how the show perpetuates misogyny. I was a fan, but after watching this it totally changed my perspective on the show. [VIDEO]"}]},"Y-Elr5K2Vuo":{"wy":"Quoted jokingly when someone's bad behavior was learned from the very people criticizing it.","m":[{"id":41533830,"author":"duxup","month":"2024-09","story":"Facebook scraped every Australian adult user's public posts to train AI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"We're going to create a really bad AI and get upset by that fact only to discover that ... we all made it that way. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7863591,"author":"brownbat","month":"2014-06","story":"Jimmy Wales: Europe’s \"Right To Be Forgotten\" A \"Terrible Danger\"","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It reminds me of this cheesy anti-drug PSA","text":"> As a european, a find this downright insulting and imperialist. Suggesting Europe go literally copy the language of American documents was poor form, granted. As to the broader issue, though, I worry there's less room for polite deference. The situation is basically equivalent to a European citizen calling me up and asking me a question, asking if I know something, and the European government insisting that I lie. An offline analogue would be if I had a friend in Germany asking for dating advice. The friend might ask, \"I can't get a sense of if this is a good idea or not. You know Jeffrey, has he ever, say, raped or murdered anyone?\" \"Why, no, I have no information to suggest Mr. Dahmer is not a fine upstanding citizen,\" I'd be forced to reply of the infamous Milwaukee Cannibal. I don't feel like a foreign government should be allowed to compel me to lie to its citizens, even by omission. I've heard Stephen Fry rather charmingly suggest that Americans maintain an unhealthy obsession with free speech. Typically, that man can convince me any premise through simple assertion. But on this, I live up to my American stereotype, and stubbornly differ on the gravity of the issue. Mostly, I find the ground sufficiently covered by Milton's \"Areopagitica\" and Mill's \"nearly everything.\" Speaker's corner seems a fun European embrace of the concept. I'm not sure where in Europe you're from, but I'm sometimes surprised at concern from the UK. It reminds me of this cheesy anti-drug PSA: ["},{"id":4996651,"author":"VaedaStrike","month":"2013-01","story":"Make the Metric system the standard in the United States","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I learned it by watching you Dad!","text":"Ironic that a post designed to attempt to rid the world of obfuscation and confusion has a UI that commits the cardinal sin of auto loading the signature list so that it's virtually impossible to get to the footer without letting all the signatures load. :) Reminds me of getting classes on water conservation in High School only to walk outside of class and see that they are watering the High School's parking lot. How very often the government evokes the classic hypocritical parent of the old anti-drug campaign commercial of the 1980's where the son finally breaks and yells at his Father \"I learned it by watching you Dad!\" [VIDEO]"}]},"bMJaMy-0ChA":{"wy":"HN commenters use it as an example of segregated bike infrastructure and of how quiet and pleasant car-free streets are.","q":{"x":"infrastructure for bikes should be well-kept year round, regardless of local conditions[2].","i":27671001,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":43442424,"author":"Mawr","month":"2025-03","story":"Congestion Pricing Is a Policy Miracle","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Dutch streets as a better environment than US ones.","quote":"","text":"> So make cars pollute less Physically impossible to have them pollute less than methods of mass transportation. Mathematically, even. Just divide the number of people carried per pollution caused by cars vs trains, bikes, or even buses. There's nothing you can do to make cars competitive. > Figure out how to filter microplastics from our food and water \"Just figure it out\", ok. But even then, an ounce of prevention is worth the pound of a cure. > Figure out what's causing asthma in children A little random but ok. I'm guessing grinding up rubber and metal particles into the air right next to where said children live isn't the best for their lungs. > Not sure how obesity is related. Maybe improve the food supply? 1. Wake up 2. Walk 30 feet to your car 3. Sit as you drive to work 4. Spend 8 hours at your desk 5. Walk 30 feet to your car 6. Sleep 7. Why am I fat?! Maybe improve the food supply?! > A lot of these are solved with electric cars which are increasingly more competitive. Virtually none are, an electric car is still a car, see point 1. > Why force people to regress towards a worse product rather than address these technical issues? Highly dependent on your definition of worse; I'd argue the sort of streets and environments you can find in the Netherlands [1][2][3] are way better than in the US. Not everything is a technical issue that can simply be solved by better engineering. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO] [3]: [LINK]"},{"id":43479204,"author":"CHB0403085482","month":"2025-03","story":"Stoop Coffee: A simple idea transformed my neighborhood","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There were towns and cities BEFORE cars","text":"There were towns and cities BEFORE cars [VIDEO] You can also wave to your neighbours passing by in their cars."},{"id":38271468,"author":"Mawr","month":"2023-11","story":"Vehicles with higher, more vertical front ends pose greater risk to pedestrians","stance":"reference","why":"Quiet Dutch cycling streets as an example of low noise.","quote":"You can even hear birds chirping at 4:29.","text":"You're comparing living in a car dependant city vs living in car dependant suburbs. It's not going to be meaningfully different. You mention the constant noise, but what do you think causes it? To see what everyone means by car dependency, you'd need to see the few places that aren't. Here's what a 100% car-free island looks like: [LINK] Here are some progressive European cities that have started to undo the damage that's been done by cars: • Freiburg, Germany: [LINK] • Haarlem, Netherlands: [LINK] • Paris, France: [LINK] Check out these background noise levels: • A very busy mixed-use street in Amsterdam: [LINK] • Exiting the train station in Delft, Netherlands: [LINK] • Cycling streets in the Netherlands: [VIDEO] . You can even hear birds chirping at 4:29."},{"id":27671001,"author":"estebank","month":"2021-06","story":"Cities Are Not Loud: Cars Are Loud, and Places Without Cars Are Quiet [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Segregated bike infrastructure in the Netherlands.","quote":"traffic should be segregated[1]","text":"> I’m unwilling to have someone who forgot to put winter tires on slide over me and my bike Part of the argument is that traffic should be segregated[1] and infrastructure for bikes should be well-kept year round, regardless of local conditions[2]. If a car can slide in snow and run you over, a distracted driver can do that on ideal conditions as well. If the roads are not in a ridable condition, that means that bike infrastructure is not being prioritized, usually because it is not considered a valid commuting mechanism. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"}]},"dxIPcbmo1_U":{"wy":"Referenced in threads about futuristic interfaces, exotic OSes and Linux visualization as the famous movie FSN moment.","m":[{"id":31109676,"author":"tacticaldev","month":"2022-04","story":"HUDS and GUIs: an inspiration resource site featuring Future User Interfaces","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Let's not forget the scene from Jurassic Park where the young female uses FSN","text":"Let's not forget the scene from Jurassic Park where the young female uses FSN to navigate a unix file system to save the day. \"I know this\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":22784721,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-04","story":"New Jersey needs COBOL programmers for their unemployment claims system","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sun made SPARCstations, not SGI, who used MIPS, not SPARC, and just called them workstations. Then there was the SGI Indy, which was an SGI Indigo without the go. ;) The 3d desktop file system navigator software running on the SGI workstation was actually a thing that SGI shipped, by Joel Sherrill <joel@sgi.com>, called SGI Fusion, or FSN! Original URL of binary distribution: ftp://ftp.sgi.com/sgi/fsn/fsn.tar.Z Now archived at: ftp://ftp.funet.fi/pub/unix/sgi/bin/fsn.tar.Z [LINK] [LINK] >File System Navigator (fsn; pronounced \"fusion\") is an experimental application to view a file system in 3D, made by SGI for IRIX systems. >Even though it was never developed to a fully functional file manager, it gained some fame after appearing in the movie Jurassic Park in 1993. In a scene in the film, the character Lex Murphy, played by Ariana Richards, finds a computer displaying the interface. She exclaims \"It's a UNIX system! I know this!\" and proceeds to restart the building's access control system, locking the control room's doors. After the release of the film, some perceived the visualization as an example of media misrepresentation of computers, citing the computer game-like display as being an unrealistic Hollywood mockup. FSN – the IRIX 3D file system tool from Jurassic Park [LINK] SGI Fusion - FSN 3d File System Navigator SGI File System Navigator, otherwise known as Fusion. Running on IRIX 6.5 on an Octane. If you have an SGI, a binary of this program is available at ftp.sgi.c"},{"id":37495115,"author":"voxadam","month":"2023-09","story":"Interactive Map of Linux Kernel","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"For anyone that doesn't get the rest of the reference","text":"I really should have provided a couple of refs. For anyone that doesn't get the rest of the reference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32601165,"author":"babypuncher","month":"2022-08","story":"What have we lost? – Demo of exotic OSes (2021) [video]","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"With the upgrades to WSL in Windows 11 you can run fsv[1] with minimal hassle and finally get the true UNIX experience[2]. 1: [LINK] 2: [VIDEO]"}]},"eiyfwZVAzGw":{"wy":"Used purely as a joke reference in unrelated threads.","m":[{"id":49172842,"author":"marky1991","month":"2026-08","story":"All of Winona Police Department's Flock cameras cut down and stolen","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"What do you mean by 'removed from society'? Do you really mean you think someone should get a life sentence for stealing some cameras? Do you mean they should be executed? Something else? And then you say 'to set an example'. By that, do you mean 'they should be punished in excess of the harm they caused, to deter others from doing the same'? If so, by its very nature, that's obviously unjust and immoral. Your reaction sounds far more third-world-country than the original act you're responding to. 'Straight to jail': [VIDEO] It sounds like a desire to turn the country into an authoritarian paradise. In my opinion, such opinions are unamerican."},{"id":45145003,"author":"coolspot","month":"2025-09","story":"A computer upgrade shut down BART","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Too high. I think NK transit system is incomparably safer and cleaner than BART. Riding without a ticket? Jail. Littering on the platform? Straight to jail, right away! Doing any violent crime in NK transit? Believe it or not - death by firing squad. Here is a quick overview of how the system works: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21758240,"author":"ransom1538","month":"2019-12","story":"Facebook fired an employee who was paid bribes to reactivate banned ad accounts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of this Parks and Rec!","text":"\"What more do you want? Criminal prosecution for violating Facebook's internal policy?\" Reminds me of this Parks and Rec! [VIDEO]"},{"id":21621505,"author":"cheez","month":"2019-11","story":"TikTok: Cheerfulness and censorship","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Well said. I like Twitter but god forbid I say anything against anything on the mainstream media networks, I get banned. On my 4th banned account now. 99% of what I tweet is completely technical. 1% gets me banned, no matter how reasonable or unemotional language is used. Like this: [VIDEO]"}]},"gn7T599QaN8":{"wy":"HN cites it as an example of early research into room-scale wireless power transfer.","q":{"x":"There is already early-stage research into long-range wireless charging","i":22023035,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22023035,"author":"judge2020","month":"2020-01","story":"FBI Surveillance Vendor Threatens to Sue Tech Reporters for Crime of Journalism","stance":"reference","why":"Early research on long-range wireless charging","quote":"There is already early-stage research into long-range wireless charging","text":"I mean, the issue with cameras on everyone is that they all require small batteries, meaning always recording with them isn't really practical. There is already early-stage research into long-range wireless charging [VIDEO] so maybe 15+ years in the future it would be practical to have always-recording devices."},{"id":19260300,"author":"stuntkite","month":"2019-02","story":"How does the Hololens 2 matter?","stance":"reference","why":"Video linked alongside a long discussion of spatial computing","quote":"","text":"That is doable right now. I am only sort of being glib here. I just want to outline what's involved in what people think is a simple task in a spatial system. TLDR: As of right now, your wildest dreams are pretty possible. In the next 2 years, how we compute is going to get strange. Nothing is nearly as simple in an XR environment as a traditional computing one and currently most people don't really want what they'd ask for. Building a simple (useful) XR app makes launching a web product look like assembling a lego set vs. landing on the moon. --------- Imagenet[0] works just fine for the object identification. You would probably want to use RGBd sensors like the kinect[1] or Intel Realsense[2] instead of regular cameras, but tracking like what the Vive[3] uses could also work. The thing you just proposed would involve a network of server processes handling the spatial data and feeding extracted relevant contextual information to a wireless headset at a pretty crazy rate. Just to give you an idea a SLAM[4] point cloud from a stereo set of cameras or a cloud from a Kinect2 or Realsese produces a stream of data that is about 200mb a second. Google Draco[5] can compress that and help you stream that data at 1/8 the size without any tuning. Extracting skeletal information from that is really something that only Microsoft has reliably managed to deliver and it's at the core of the Kinect/Hololens products. NuiTrack[6] is the next best thing, but registering a human involves a T-Po"},{"id":13663314,"author":"jf","month":"2017-02","story":"Demo of Short-Range Wireless Power Transfer","stance":"reference","why":"Demo videos for wireless power transfer research","quote":"","text":"If you're having trouble loading the page, here is the direct link to the PDF: [LINK] Here are the YouTube videos: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"hGo5bxWy21g":{"wy":"HN uses it as a joke reference, posting it in threads about meetings, procedure and corporate behavior.","m":[{"id":43043580,"author":"anigbrowl","month":"2025-02","story":"US prosecutor in New York resigns after order to drop mayoral case","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"for those who don't get the reference","text":"Very much so. Sassoon's resignation letter describes the Acting Attorney General (prior to Pam Bondi's recent confirmation) as objecting to a DoJ lawyer taking notes in the meeting with Adams' lawyers, and demanding those notes be handed over when exiting the meeting. [VIDEO] for those who don't get the reference"},{"id":32625220,"author":"gorgoiler","month":"2022-08","story":"Robert's Rules of Order (1876)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"STRINGER: Yo, what is that? SHAMROCK: [scribbling on a legal pad] The Roberts Rules say we gotta have minutes for the meeting right? These the minutes. STRINGER: Is you takin’ notes on a criminal f*** conspiracy?! What the ** is you thinking man?! [VIDEO]"},{"id":29440546,"author":"SahAssar","month":"2021-12","story":"Square is now called Block","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Every time emails leak from facebook/google/amazon showing unflattering things I think of the end of this scene: \"Is you taking notes on a criminal fucking conspiracy? What the fuck is you thinking!?\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":5102125,"author":"spindritf","month":"2013-01","story":"Steve Jobs threatened patent suit to enforce no-hire policy: filing","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> \"Schmidt responded that he preferred it be shared 'verbally, since I don't want to create a paper trail over which we can be sued later?'\" he said, according to the court filing. The HR director agreed. [VIDEO]"}]},"hou0lU8WMgo":{"wy":"Posted as a joke reply when someone makes a pedantic but technically accurate point.","m":[{"id":44444058,"author":"latexr","month":"2025-07","story":"Figma files for proposed IPO","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> You can define any space as \"not crowded\" if you narrow it down enough. I mean, you’re technically correct¹ but that’s not exactly helpful because the inverse is also true: You can define any space as “crowded” if you widen it enough. That is to say it is perfectly plausible I am wrong and the space was crowded, but that argument doesn’t really prove or disprove it. > That was the point really, they entered a crowded space & re-defined it with their offering. Especially if you allow someone in the space to redefine it. At that point all bets are off and anyone can claim anything. ¹ The best kind of correct: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19949432,"author":"nickdrozd","month":"2019-05","story":"I Got a Knuth Check for 0x$3.00","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Tuh, you and me both. Then again, it's been said that \"technically correct\" is \"the best kind of correct\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":7067672,"author":"jfarmer","month":"2014-01","story":"Super successful companies","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"And yet, @kyro and I and I suspect many others were able to understand perfectly well what @sama meant. The original comment > what does \"ruthless\" mean nowadays in valley newspeak? or did i miss a memo about \"nice\"..? reads as if the commenter didn't even attempt to understand what @sama meant. Instead, it appears as if he saw that what @sama wrote was technically inconsistent and glommed onto that without actually engaging with the essay. If the commenter was simply offering up copyediting advice, well, he could've left out the color (\"valley newspeak\", etc.). As it stands, the comment is utterly boring and, I suspect, reflects the attitude @kyro meant to highlight with the phrase \"forced stupidity.\" \"Technically correct\" is not actually the best kind of correct ( [VIDEO] )."}]},"j0UnS3jHhAA":{"wy":"Cited as an early, amusing example of LLM-driven command-line toys, with commenters sharing similar experiments.","m":[{"id":30735193,"author":"O_H_E","month":"2022-03","story":"Fish 3.4.0","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter replicated the demo; waitlist now ended.","quote":"I remember that I was able to replicate this last year","text":"Yup, right here [VIDEO] I remember that I was able to replicate this last year, not 100% sure why I'm not using it daily tho. :D One thing to note, the limited waitinglist he talks about in the video is done, and now you can sign up and \"pay\" for ~NLP~...ahem CLP tokens as OpenAI calls them"},{"id":29196819,"author":"vfinn","month":"2021-11","story":"Useful sed scripts and patterns","stance":"reference","why":"AI-powered shell that guesses bash commands.","quote":"I made an OpenAI-powered Linux shell that guesses your bash command","text":"Waiting for an AI powered shell, or something similar, so that one can avoid wasting time on these trivial tasks that require too much attention if solved with traditional methods: \"I made an OpenAI-powered Linux shell that guesses your bash command\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":28193447,"author":"riveducha","month":"2021-08","story":"OpenAI's Codex sure knows a lot about HN [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Similar GPT-3 bash toy that generated a Rickroll URL.","quote":"I made a similar little GPT-3 toy (for Linux/bash) that also ended up generating a Rickroll URL.","text":"I made a similar little GPT-3 toy (for Linux/bash) that also ended up generating a Rickroll URL.[0] In vague cases, GPT-3 functions approximately as well as a markov chain - it will give you the most “probable” sequence of tokens. There’s some implementation details regarding how it deals with tokenization, but unless you really increase the randomness (“temperature”) you’re likely to get very popular YT video IDs. In other cases, it will “hallucinate” things like URLs, UUIDs, hashes, and other things that are basically a random string of characters. In my experience it will make UUIDs that have the right number of characters but seem suspiciously non-random and don’t fit any of the defined UUID formats. Fun stuff. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"jgYYOUC10aM":{"wy":"HN commenters use it only as a joke reference when a thread's scheme or fraud sounds like the sketch.","m":[{"id":47428094,"author":"vlovich123","month":"2026-03","story":"North Korean's 100k fake IT workers net $500M a year for Kim","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of the Key & Peele sketch","text":"Reminds me of the Key & Peele sketch: [VIDEO] > Once employed in a full-time role, fake workers are often very successful, since they sometimes have multiple people helping them to produce their work, with the hope of getting a promotion and gaining more privileged access to the IT systems. I think the \"fake\" part is the long term play to get enough privilege to presumably perform a cybersecurity attack. But less \"fake\" and more \"spy\" from the description - the outlined scheme is literally what spies agencies do."},{"id":41375449,"author":"yieldcrv","month":"2024-08","story":"Hackers infect ISPs with malware that steals customers' credentials","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"North Koreans are currently funding their nuclear program by…. passing interviews at US tech companies and … actually doing the work KYC is a joke when everybody’s ID can be used to make a valid account and receive payments reminds me of this bank heist skit, where they just get employed at a bank for 30 years [VIDEO]"},{"id":40003656,"author":"bko","month":"2024-04","story":"Harvard will require test scores for admission again","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think standardized tests correlate very highly (perhaps highest of any measure) to performance. I regularly see schools in the US with a 10% math proficiency and 40% literacy proficiency with a 99% graduation rate. What's up with that? Standardized tests are the most fair and simplest way to evaluate students. Plus gaming the test is what exactly? Hiring a tutor and spending hours studying, much of it alone? And it's not a shock that success on studying and doing well on exams correlates to college where you're evaluated on how well you study and do on exams It reminds me of that Key and Peele sketch where they're planning a long con bank heist but by the end it's obvious they're just working a job and saving for retirement [VIDEO]"},{"id":32998125,"author":"ryandrake","month":"2022-09","story":"Someone is pretending to be me","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Whenever I read these kinds of super-complicated scams, I can't help but think if the scammer would have instead invested all that time and effort into legitimate tech and interviewing skills, he/she could have just come in the front door! It's like the person who spends hundreds of hours putting together the perfect exam cheating scheme, where they could have instead put in half of that time actually studying! Or, in video form, the Kay & Peele Bank Heist[1] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31499843,"author":"jrandm","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: How would you make $100k fast?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminded me of this Key & Peele sketch","text":"> might be easier [...] just working at a bank Reminded me of this Key & Peele sketch: [VIDEO] Robber 2: So how do we get the money? Robber 1: That's the beauty of it, bro. They deposit the money into our bank accounts, week after week, month after month. They're not even gonna know they're being robbed! And then 20-30 years later, we walk out the front door like nothin' even happened."},{"id":30833437,"author":"MengerSponge","month":"2022-03","story":"Reinstating our SAT/ACT requirement for future admissions cycles","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The SAT is a test of your academic preparation, not Raven's progressive matrices. That you can study for it is not inherently a bad thing. Portions that are highly susceptible to coaching are bad, and that's why there aren't analogies any more. If students learn the vocabulary and practice the math to do better on the test, at some point it just becomes the Key and Peele Heist sketch: \"That's called a job!\" [VIDEO]"}]},"kUS9uvYyn3A":{"wy":"Cited as the standard example of algorithmic redistricting when gerrymandering and electoral reform come up.","q":{"x":"See for example the Shortest Splitline Algorithm","i":14642180,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14642180,"author":"NortySpock","month":"2017-06","story":"Delaney Introduces Bill to End Gerrymandering, Reform Elections","stance":"reference","why":"Public neutral algorithm for drawing districts.","quote":"See for example the Shortest Splitline Algorithm","text":"I'd prefer the algorithm if it was public and neutral (or at least consistent). See for example the Shortest Splitline Algorithm: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13605360,"author":"ClayShentrup","month":"2017-02","story":"This is the best explanation of gerrymandering you will ever see","stance":"reference","why":"Algorithmic redistricting as a cure for gerrymandering.","quote":"Another cure to Gerrymandering is algorithmic redistricting, which I'm supportive of.","text":"PR mitigates Gerrymandering because it reduces the amount of \"discardable\" surplus votes. Imagine you have 100 single-winner districts that are each roughly 50/50 Dem/GOP, with the slight edge breaking 50/50, so that your legislature has 50 reps from each party. Now we redistrict to pack a bunch of Dems from the GOP-majority districts into one super-Dem district, shaving down their margins in numerous districts. We could conceivable get 99 GOP reps even though the population is 50/50 Dem/GOP. That super-Dem district \"discards\" or \"wastes\" 50% of the votes of the Dems inside it, because they're surplus votes in excess of what they need to win the district. Now imagine you switch to 20 5-winner districts elected with PR. The Dems could win all five seats with conceivably 83.3% of the vote (5 candidates getting 16.6% each means they get all the votes). So the most you can possibly wast is 16.7% of the votes instead of 50%. > It only replaces districts with parties No. Most PR systems still have districts. Exceptions like Israel, where the whole country is one big district, tend to be the poster children of PR gone wrong. Plus there are PR systems where you vote for candidates, not parties. Single Transferable Vote in Australia's Senate is an example. You just rank the candidates (although many voters blindly follow the guidance of their preferred party). Another cure to Gerrymandering is algorithmic redistricting, which I'm supportive of. [VIDEO] As for Score Voting (aka Range V"},{"id":3965889,"author":"zanny","month":"2012-05","story":"Germany's Pirate Party Looks to Win More Seats","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I always think that the best way to combat the unwillingness of the commoner to actively engage in politics is to bring them to the commoner. People behave very differently when comparing communities to even just the town or city they live in. They behave to their neighbor much differently than someone a 15 minute drive from, and absolutely differently from someone they never see. Given that (in the US at least) approximately 1.5~ in 10 people are public sector workers (I forgot where I got that statistic, but anything > 1 in 50 would suffice), you can easily go full republic and have small communities of 1,000 people (psychological analysis studies seem to lean towards something in the 700 - 1300 range being the maximum number of people any one individual can actively relate to at a human level) who elect a singular representative amongst themselves every year (with no re-elections, and at such a small scale, the politics of campaign finance break down completely - everyone should know each other.) You simply select, emotionally, the person amongst yourselves you feel best represents you individually, and those that want to take the local seat can debate one another in forum with whoever is interested. The rest can be expected to take sides, but the beauty of this idea is that as long as people remain mobile, you can expect common political interests to attract one another, and as long as you can preserve individual mobility (which is much easier in the modern world) you can"}]},"kx1Jxk6kjbQ":{"wy":"HN cites it in methane and permafrost threads as an early warning that still looks relevant.","q":{"x":"This is from 8 years ago but it is very relevant today","i":28153594,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":35668310,"author":"pier25","month":"2023-04","story":"Methane may not warm the Earth quite as much as previously thought","stance":"reference","why":"Shakhova on methane emissions years ago.","quote":"Scientists such as Natalia Shakhova have been studying this for years.","text":"> Scientists fear that as warming triggers thaw of permafrost in the Arctic regions, this could also lead to increased methane emissions Could? This is already happening. Scientists such as Natalia Shakhova have been studying this for years. This is a video of her from 10 years ago. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32312649,"author":"pier25","month":"2022-08","story":"Net-zero commitments could limit warming to below 2 °C","stance":"reference","why":"2013 interview with Shakhova.","quote":"Here's an interview with her in 2013","text":"It is true. In fact if you look at the source for that graph [1] you'll see it's a paper from 2009 and they used old 2007 IPCC data for their models. The IPCC has not taken feedbacks into account until their sixth report of which the synthesis hasn't been published yet [2]. In 2009 feedbacks were still not well understood. In fact Natalia Shakhova didn't publish her seminal paper on methane in the Arctic until 2010. Here's an interview with her in 2013: [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":28153594,"author":"NiceWayToDoIT","month":"2021-08","story":"Climate change: Curbing methane emissions will 'buy us time'","stance":"reference","why":"Older warning still relevant.","quote":"This is from 8 years ago but it is very relevant today","text":"Let say we succeed to stop methane leakage from cows (algae feed, etc.) and also leakage from coal, oil and gas extraction. How are we going to stop Siberian permafrost melting (it has huge amounts of methane ice) it has potentially 100 to 1000Gt (currently 5Gt in atmosphere CH4) which is enough to tip the temperature to 2-3C? [LINK] [LINK] This is from 8 years ago but it is very relevant today: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24916742,"author":"NiceWayToDoIT","month":"2020-10","story":"'Sleeping giant' Arctic methane deposits starting to release, scientists find","stance":"reference","why":"Early warning about permafrost methane.","quote":"This was warning 7 years ago","text":"In case your line is not sarcasm, we knew about it long time ago. This was warning 7 years ago: [VIDEO] Current amount of methane in atmosphere approximately 5Gt, methane stored in permafrost amounts from 100 - 1000 Gt ... which if it melts means Game Over with instant K.O."},{"id":20013787,"author":"freebear","month":"2019-05","story":"Unexpected Surge in Global Methane Levels","stance":"reference","why":"Risk of abrupt methane release in Arctic shelf.","quote":"The risk of abrupt methane release in the east siberian arctic shelf","text":"The risk of abrupt methane release in the east siberian arctic shelf: [VIDEO] This graph is more fun but not up to date [LINK]"},{"id":12455829,"author":"patrickmn","month":"2016-09","story":"Permafrost ‘bubbles’ are leaking methane 200 times above the norm","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with a methane researcher.","quote":"Very ominous.","text":"Related interview with Natalia Shakhova from the International Arctic Research Center: [VIDEO] Very ominous."}]},"lUQ_0MubbcM":{"wy":"Used as a source for the argument that US support for Israel is systematic and strategic, and for Chomsky's view of Palestine as resistance to colonialism.","q":{"x":"Noan Chomsky says the issue of Palestine is resistance against colonialism.","i":38830333,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":48737804,"author":"lr0","month":"2026-06","story":"The US ambassador had Belgian police stop our reporting","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why US support of Israel is systematic.","quote":"it's quite systematic and there are so much literature on that.","text":"The US \"obsession\" with Israel is not \"random\" or arbitrary at all, it's quite systematic and there are so much literature on that. [LINK] [VIDEO] [Why the US. supports Israel / Noam Chomsky]"},{"id":38830333,"author":"helixfelix","month":"2024-01","story":"Maersk ship hit by missile in the Red Sea","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky on Palestine as resistance against colonialism.","quote":"Noan Chomsky says the issue of Palestine is resistance against colonialism.","text":"> \"Hamas is playing the land grab card against Israel\". What do you mean by that? Comparing Russia with Hamas seems like a massive distortion of reality, not sure you meant to do that. Israel is occupying internationally recognized Palestinian lands of West Bank and a blockade on Gaza. Just yesterday the Israeli foreign minister suggested sending Israeli settlers into Gaza illegally and drive Palestinians out for good. Hamas is a terrorist organization, who kills civilians, but let's not pretend that the IDF is any better. And the US is a colonial power of the 20th and 21st century. It just hasn't done it the same way as the Europeans of 17,18th,19th century, mostly because there are different tools available now: culture export, dependence on oil, dollar as reserve currency, global free trade, and the rise of corporations. Noan Chomsky says the issue of Palestine is resistance against colonialism. [1] [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":19140272,"author":"abalone","month":"2019-02","story":"Private Mossad for Hire","stance":"reference","why":"Source for US strategic interests in Israel.","quote":"","text":"The idea that an \"all-powerful small ethnic Jewish lobby\" can trick the corporate elite of America for decades to do things against their interests is, putting aside its anti-semitic implications, utterly ridiculous. It is a way of deflecting blame away from the U.S. government itself.[1] The strategic interests are obvious: Israel is one of the largest military powers in the middle east and a means to project U.S. power over it. This was observed by the Pentagon as early as the late 1940s.[2] This is why the U.S. has given so much financial, military and diplomatic aid to Israel, one of the effects of which has been to build up its tech industry. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"lncLOEqc9Rw":{"wy":"Quoted as a comedic aside in threads on homelessness and housing, where commenters note it lands on the same conclusion as serious articles.","q":{"x":"he also made a bit on homelessness and came to the same conclusion the article did","i":28100093,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":46537149,"author":"beeflet","month":"2026-01","story":"Eat Real Food – Introducing the New Pyramid","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin bit on declaring war on homelessness.","quote":"It reminds me of carlin's act about how everything we do has to be contextualized into war","text":"Those DEMOCRAT SOYBOYS are gonna hate this, but I'm gonna say it anyways. Today we're joining the WAR on protein- ON THE SIDE OF THE PROTEIN. It's an idiocracy bit, the continual flanderization of the USA. It reminds me of carlin's act about how everything we do has to be contextualized into war: we can't just solve homelessness, we have to declare WAR on homelessness ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":28100093,"author":"Chiron1991","month":"2021-08","story":"Homelessness is about housing","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin bit on homelessness reaching same conclusion as the article.","quote":"he also made a bit on homelessness and came to the same conclusion the article did","text":"Speaking of George Carlin, he also made a bit on homelessness and came to the same conclusion the article did: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15526906,"author":"icebraining","month":"2017-10","story":"An alternative to burial and cremation gains popularity","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That was the great George Carlin","text":"Some comedian used to joke about the best land being taken up by golf courses and cemeteries. That was the great George Carlin: [VIDEO]"}]},"ny_OPsxHQmU":{"wy":"Linked as a quick visual reference for resolution and screen-door effect differences between popular VR headsets.","q":{"x":"Resolution comparison between Index, Quest 2, and Reverb G2","i":27532940,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":33485898,"author":"chaostheory","month":"2022-11","story":"Ask HN: Working in a VR Headset?","stance":"reference","why":"Through-the-lens comparison of G2, Index and Quest 2.","quote":"Through the lens comparison g2 vs Index vs Quest 2","text":"I feel that the bare minimum headset that can handle work is the HP Reverb G2. It’s the only headset with a high enough resolution under $1000 (you can find it under $300 on eBay). There’s also Pimax, but their QA and customer support is terrible and you need to deal with base stations. Through the lens comparison g2 vs Index vs Quest 2 [VIDEO]"},{"id":33168112,"author":"nomel","month":"2022-10","story":"Meta Quest Pro","stance":"reference","why":"Through-the-lens view showing screen door effect across headsets.","quote":"","text":"> this is arguably a worse piece of hardware than Valve's Index Could you expand on this? Even the Quest 2 has considerably lower screen door effect [1]. The quest pro has double the valve index PPD (~14 vs ~32). The selling point of the Index is FOV. 90 degrees is plenty for work. 1. Through the lens: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27532940,"author":"cowgoesmoo","month":"2021-06","story":"Facebook will start putting ads in Oculus Quest apps","stance":"reference","why":"Through-the-lens resolution comparison of Index, Quest 2 and Reverb G2.","quote":"Resolution comparison between Index, Quest 2, and Reverb G2","text":"I also have the CV1 and am looking to upgrade. The Index has better tracking (using lighthouse) and a larger FOV than the Quest 2 but much worse resolution. If you want the inside-out tracking and higher resolution of the Quest 2 but don't want to deal with Facebook, check out the HP Reverb G2. It's pricier than the Quest 2 but still much cheaper than the Index. Resolution comparison between Index, Quest 2, and Reverb G2: [VIDEO] Resolution comparison between CV1, Vive, and Quest 2: [LINK]"}]},"vZw35VUBdzo":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference for denying that something obviously defunct is dead.","m":[{"id":38948956,"author":"guytv","month":"2024-01","story":"The golden age of Kotlin and its uncertain future","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"No no he's not dead, he's, he's restin'!","text":"\"Scala isn't dead\" reminded me of this one \"No no he's not dead, he's, he's restin'!\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":22842769,"author":"aurizon","month":"2020-04","story":"Engineering Tests to Evaluate the Feasibility of Solar Pavement Technology","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this dog is as dead as Monty Python's parrot","text":"Thanks for that! A valuable comparison to the official glowing reports of the 'valuable lessons'. Looks like a cover of pure diamond would resist the scratches, and in the netherland, their groos national product would enable them to cover a few square meters a year. This is a dog that never hunted, not a paw hit the ground, this dog is as dead as Monty Python's parrot [VIDEO]"},{"id":20235610,"author":"programd","month":"2019-06","story":"Getting 2FA Right in 2019","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"like arguing about the beautiful plumage of the dead parrot","text":"This is the most insightful post in the entire thread. Thank you for that. Most of the discussion here is about TOTP, which at this point is like arguing about the beautiful plumage of the dead parrot. TOTP for professional 2FA is a walking corpse, pushing up the daisies, wouldn't squawk if you put 10K volts through it [1]. If you're a company seeking to secure your infrastructure all your employees and contractors should be using U2F hardware keys to access your network. Period, end of discussion. Same for admin access to any external SaaS dependencies - and you should be loudly complaining if your SaaS does not yet support hardware keys. And if you're a startup and even a solo developer, start looking at supporting WebAuth so you're not caught with your pants down later, especially if you want to sell to other businesses. Business to consumer TOTP is a more complicated issue. The future is clearly hardware keys, tied to devices like phones, but the support is not yet all there. So you're going to have to support TOTP for a while yet, since it's better then bare passwords. But you should be making plans to move to hardware U2F ASAP, and the earlier you do it the easier the transition will be later when you will have to mandate it for all your users for liability and CYA reasons. The looming shadow over all this is account recovery, which is not a solved problem in Business to Consumer space (IT/HR can sort you out to get back on your corp network if you lose your keys). Ther"}]},"xDcvPf78g1k":{"wy":"Cited as an ironic example of YouTube receiving a free expression award, and as a showcase of the public dislike count before it was hidden.","q":{"x":"YouTube has been awarded a Free Expression Award from an organization sponsored by... YouTube.","i":27728266,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29178113,"author":"ainar-g","month":"2021-11","story":"Making the dislike count private across YouTube","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Currently at 56,502 dislikes vs 226 likes.","text":"Rewind is kind of old news. Now that time YouTube CEO Susan Wojcicki received a Free Expression Award from an organisation sponsored by YouTube[1] is more recent and something else completely, ratio-wise. Currently at 56,502 dislikes vs 226 likes. Which is a ratio of over 250×. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29184475,"author":"throwaway2077","month":"2021-11","story":"Making the dislike count private across YouTube","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"it is unacceptable that goodthink content that promotes correct opinions gets ratioed and ridiculed. the writing was on the wall for a while. this was probably the final nail in the coffin: [VIDEO] kudos to our billionaire overlords, celebrities and their first world cosmopolitan yuppie congregation for looking out for us unwashed, uneducated plebeians. without their guidance, we'd be lost."},{"id":29186670,"author":"throwaway2077","month":"2021-11","story":"Making the dislike count private across YouTube","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"go glimpse it in all its glory before it gets memoryholed","text":"the writing was on the wall for a while. this was probably the final nail in the coffin: [VIDEO] go glimpse it in all its glory before it gets memoryholed"},{"id":28691407,"author":"0xy","month":"2021-09","story":"YouTube's CEO says free speech is a 'core value' even after removing videos","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"YouTube is the home of censorship, and any proclamations of \"free speech\" are merely corporate marketing.","text":"Susan Wojcicki was awarded a \"Free Expression Award\" from a company sponsored by... YouTube. [1] This isn't surprising. YouTube is the home of censorship, and any proclamations of \"free speech\" are merely corporate marketing. I don't know how software engineers at YouTube justify to themselves that they're working for a free speech company, given they can easily see the code that \"deboosts\" content for political reasons, and other censorship. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27728266,"author":"0xy","month":"2021-07","story":"YouTube Censors Nobel Prize Winner Professor Satoshi Omura","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"YouTube has been awarded a Free Expression Award from an organization sponsored by... YouTube.","text":"Previously YouTube incompetently censored discussion of lab leak theory, allowing misinformation to fester on the platform. [1] YouTube's Trust and Safety team are actively malicious at this point. This was no error. They regularly censor correct information based on the current thinking among the orthodoxy, which I highly suspect is operating in a cartel manner to censor like-minded topics (hence why FB also sloppily censored the same topic). Don't worry though, YouTube has been awarded a Free Expression Award from an organization sponsored by... YouTube. [2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26881877,"author":"ainar-g","month":"2021-04","story":"YouTube CEO Susan Wojcicki Gets 'Freedom Expression' Award Sponsored by YouTube","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"At the time of me writing this comment, the ratio is 53 likes to 15,486 dislikes.","text":"Here[1] is the video. At the time of me writing this comment, the ratio is 53 likes to 15,486 dislikes. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26885900,"author":"beshrkayali","month":"2021-04","story":"YouTube CEO Susan Wojcicki Gets 'Freedom Expression' Award Sponsored by YouTube","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"In the video, she calls speech they don’t agree with “censorship” and actual censorship “freedom of expression”. If this isn’t a prime example of doublespeak I don’t know what is. [VIDEO]"}]},"yuEBBwJdjhQ":{"wy":"Cited in debates about slurs, censorship and platform moderation as proof such content has long existed on YouTube.","m":[{"id":40041822,"author":"5555624","month":"2024-04","story":"'We're the last bastion of rental': video stores resisting rise of streaming","stance":"reference","why":"Official SNL channel still hosts Word Association sketch.","quote":"The Official SNL Channel still has \"Word Association","text":">My understanding is that the official Saturday Night Live clips on YouTube have been silently edited to remove \"problematic\" skits. The Official SNL Channel still has \"Word Association\" ( [VIDEO] ) available; so, maybe there's some hope."},{"id":36883328,"author":"hotpotamus","month":"2023-07","story":"Sinead O’Connor has died","stance":"reference","why":"SNL clip with Chevy Chase and Richard Pryor trading racial slurs.","quote":"Here's an SNL clip from way back where Chevy Chase and Richard Pryor trade racial slurs","text":"Here's an SNL clip from way back where Chevy Chase and Richard Pryor trade racial slurs [VIDEO] I'm not a big fan of the whole, \"you couldn't do that today\", but you sure couldn't do that today. I think you weren't supposed to do it then either which made it subversive comedy."},{"id":34628034,"author":"34611","month":"2023-02","story":"Firearms channels embrace Rumble as YouTube starts censoring videos","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"You can find SNL sketches that use the N word on youtube.","text":"> Also also: are movie trailers that depict a gun being loaded with a > 30 rd magazine now banned? Or movie trailers that show any kind of modified weapon? How about video game footage? Rules do not apply to establishment corporate entities. You can find SNL sketches that use the N word on youtube. Youtube rents videos on their platform that has racist, etc language. Late night \"comedy\" shows could use vulgar terms/jokes without getting demonetized, banned, etc. It's just you can't. It's about reigning in the voice of the little guys and protecting corporate interests. Remember when youtube was about giving a platform to \"you\" - the little guys? The funny thing is that these guys support banning tiktok and giving youtube a greater monopoly. They, of all people, should be demanding more options. Where are they going to go once rumble does the same thing. After all, rumble is controlled by the same interests that control youtube. SNL skit: [VIDEO]"}]},"ztsi0CLxmjw":{"wy":"Cited as the go-to example of hyperbolic geometry made intuitive through VR.","q":{"x":"reminds me of the demonstration of hyperbolic geometry in VR by Henry Segerman","i":30009972,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30224649,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Why isn't computer programming a major part of the arts?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of non-Euclidean computer art.","quote":"","text":"none-2D surface things: * 1D: MIDI (music) * 3D: CNC mill/ 3D Printer * 4D: VR (animated) * outright Non-Euclidian: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30009972,"author":"thealig","month":"2022-01","story":"Geometry from Another Universe","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstration of hyperbolic geometry in VR.","quote":"reminds me of the demonstration of hyperbolic geometry in VR by Henry Segerman","text":"reminds me of the demonstration of hyperbolic geometry in VR by Henry Segerman ( [VIDEO] ). there is a browser demo too where you can 'fly' through the small world ( [LINK] )"},{"id":21333745,"author":"indigochill","month":"2019-10","story":"Geometry, Algebra, and Intuition (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"VR demo of non-Euclidean geometry.","quote":"You may have seen the \"Non-euclidian virtual reality\" video floating around YouTube","text":"I'm cautiously optimistic about VR as a tool for teaching and understanding math up to three dimensions. You may have seen the \"Non-euclidian virtual reality\" video floating around YouTube ( [VIDEO] )."}]}});
